    "md5": "83924b05acb5370f8347175ebfcc810f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka eyes world-first universal basic AI pilot to plug digital dollar drain\n\nPresidential Secretariat directs Digital Economy Ministry on UBAI pilot: founder\n\nFree Sovereign Engram app to run up to 80% of everyday AI work on personal laptops\n\nSolar-powered PicoDC edge nodes to handle heavier workloads, local assembly planned\n\nThe Presidential Secretariat has directed the Digital Economy Ministry to take forward the world\u2019s first national pilot for universal basic AI (UBAI), an initiative to be run in Sri Lanka and Australia to secure digital sovereignty and retain capital onshore, Anthosa and Sovereign Stack Founder Chamara Somaratne said.\n\nSomaratne said the directive (Ref: PS/PRD/PR/12/03/09/02/230663) was issued this week after two years of representations to President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who also holds the Digital Economy portfolio and Senior Presidential Adviser on Digital Economy Dr. Hans Wijayasuriya.\n\n\u201cNow this is actually formally sanctioned by the president himself,\u201d he said.\n\nThe pilot targets what Somaratne describes as a severe macroeconomic distortion. He estimated Sri Lanka spends about US $ 600 million (over Rs.180 billion) a year on foreign digital services, outstripping what he cited as a roughly US $ 550 million food import bill.\n\nThe outflow spans cloud services, software platforms ranging from Microsoft 365 to ride-hailing apps and the \u201cattention economy\u201d of digital advertising. Australia faces the same pressure on a larger scale, spending about A$ 35 billion a year on the US technology, he said.\n\nSomaratne, an Australia-based Sri Lankan systems engineer, brings a 30-year career across deep-tech and corporate strategy. A mechatronics engineering graduate of the University of New South Wales, he spent a decade in semiconductor manufacturing and five years in financial services, followed by 15 years in management consulting. That included leading the strategy, transformation and innovation practices at Wipro and DXC Consulting."
  },
