    "md5": "25f03060c09696072760816ea1d2f650",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1620,
    "chunk_text": "An El Ni\u00f1o like no other\n\nNishanthi Gunarathna stands in front of her home in Kadurugaswewa. Days after this photo was taken a wild\u00a0elephant broke into the house looking for food and water\n\nNishanthi Gunarathna was at her tea stall with her three children when she heard villagers screaming and the loud clatter of metal roofing sheets being ripped apart. She ran towards her mud-thatched house and watched in horror as a wild elephant towered over her one bedroom home and destroyed it. Nishanthi says she has not seen a wild elephant as tall as this one before.\n\nIt was around 6.30 in the evening. Her children just returned to the tea stall after lighting the oil lamp to the Lord Buddha at their home, just a few hundred meters away.\n\n\u201cWe screamed and made loud noises hoping to scare the elephant away. It didn\u2019t even flinch. It broke into the house and ate the rice bags that were stored inside,\u201d Nishanthi said, adding that wild elephants are increasingly venturing into the village. \u201cWe live in constant fear,\u201d she said.\n\nNishanthi, her husband and children have now moved into the small mud-thatched tea stall. Her children\u2019s uniforms and books were destroyed by the wild elephant.\n\nSince the drought-like weather began months ago, life in Kadurugaswewa village in Polonnaruwa district has become exceedingly difficult. The reservoir and wells have dried up, and vegetation has withered away leaving parched land and brittle plants. Now the villagers have to compete with wild animals for what little is left. The dry conditions that are causing this are linked to the ongoing effects of El Ni\u00f1o."
  },
