    "md5": "7b4a549398d9830a2d4a46150f968143",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "In Kadurugaswewa, an extremely rural village, women now depend on what they call a \u2018water hole\u2019, a stagnant stretch of a once-flowing waterway that remains after the rest of the water has dried up, to fetch water in for cleaning and cooking. Under the scorching sun, the women collect water in containers and use wheelbarrows to carry the heavy loads back home.\n\n\u201cAfter the cattle leave, we have to wait until the mud and debris settle to collect the water,\u201d W. A. Nadeesha, a mother of three, said.\n\n\u201cOur children fall sick bathing in the \u201ccattle\u201d water. They get rashes, eye infections and diarrhea. Many families have already left our village because it is very difficult to live. Now there are abandoned houses everywhere,\u201d she said.\n\nWith her husband bedridden with cancer, Nadeesha is left to manage the household and collect water on her own. Nadeesha is also the breadwinner of her family, earning a living by caring for cattle for a fee.\u00a0 Nadeesha walks long distances to find food and water for the animals in her charge. She said her work has become even herder as the heat gets unbearable during the afternoon.\n\nBandara Manike echoed her experience. \u201cSweet potato wines, lime trees, and other vegetable plants have all died. There\u2019s not even manioc leaves to eat. It has been three months since our well dried up. Now we have to collect water from the water hole. We are farmers but we can\u2019t grow anything now,\u201d she said. She worries that the water hole will dry up too.\n\nKeerthi Rathna, a paddy farmer, said his harvest had failed. \u201cThe plants died just when it was time for the grains to grow,\u201d he said.\n\nBringing some relief to the villagers, local donors started bringing bottled waters to the village. Unfortunately, the water distributed by the government does not reach the village."
