    "num": "dailymirrorlk-367f5409",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "Salesforce pivots from AI hype to real biz value in SL\u2019s digital push",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Salesforce-pivots-from-AI-hype-to-real-biz-value-in-SLs-digital-push/273-350231",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789366753.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-367f5409-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f3e501244d9b01657898954259ab912",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Salesforce pivots from AI hype to real biz value in SL\u2019s digital push\n\nSalesforce Managing Director for India and Sri Lanka Mankiran Chowhan\n\nAs Sri Lanka navigates a critical phase of economic recovery, both the state and corporate sector are aggressively looking toward a digital-first future. Recognising this pivotal juncture, global customer relationship management software leader Salesforce hosted its first-ever Innovation Day in Colombo on August 31, signalling a deepening commitment to the island nation.\n\nThe gathering marked a decisive departure from the superficial buzz surrounding artificial intelligence (AI), focusing instead on tangible, bottom-line business impacts.\n\nLeading this strategic engagement was Salesforce Managing Director for India and Sri Lanka Mankiran Chowhan, who sat down with Mirror Business on the sidelines of the event to discuss how the Sri Lankan enterprises can harness connected data, modernise legacy operations and scale sustainably.\n\nFor the past two years, the global boardrooms have been captivated by the rapid emergence of generative AI. However, Chowhan observed that the honeymoon period of casual experimentation has come to an end. Corporate leadership is now demanding measurable financial returns rather than novelty demonstrations.\n\n\u201cToday, we live in a world where AI is no longer an experiment,\u201d Chowhan noted.\n\n\u201cEighteen to 24 months ago, when ChatGPT came for the first time, everybody was experimenting with it. We were all doing video editing, AI prompts or our children were using it for homework. Most organisations were also experimenting with AI to see what was right. But now we are at a point where we know that AI is here to stay and AI is valuable.\n\nThe question is: how do you use AI in the context of your business to get some value? And you are going to get value when you work on real problems, when you work on a bottom-line or a top-line problem.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-367f5409",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-367f5409",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "Salesforce pivots from AI hype to real biz value in SL\u2019s digital push",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Salesforce-pivots-from-AI-hype-to-real-biz-value-in-SLs-digital-push/273-350231",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789366753.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-367f5409-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "69f699ea63d9cc6817193fe76012a888",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1908,
    "chunk_text": "The question is: how do you use AI in the context of your business to get some value? And you are going to get value when you work on real problems, when you work on a bottom-line or a top-line problem.\u201d\n\n\u201cJust doing fancy emails or making things look pretty is not the real value of AI in the corporate world. How do you leverage AI for meaningful top-line and bottom-line impact? That is why we are seeing a lot of customers no longer do just the pilots but ask: how do I attack a real problem with AI?\u201d\n\nIllustrating this shift, Chowhan pointed to Salesforce\u2019s internal operations, where the software giant acted as its own test case. By deploying autonomous service agents to manage repetitive customer inquiries, the company achieved an 86 percent first-call resolution rate. This operational efficiency eliminated wait times for clients and allowed the enterprise to repurpose substantial headcount into high-value strategic functions.\n\nFurthermore, deploying intelligent agents to qualify inbound sales leads ensured that opportunities were no longer lost to capacity constraints, transforming human limitations into blended human-plus-machine productivity.\n\nFor Sri Lankan corporate entities aspiring to scale, Chowhan identified a pervasive structural bottleneck: fragmented digital infrastructure. Companies frequently invest in specialised software applications in isolation, creating data silos that prevent leadership from obtaining a cohesive picture of their operations.\n\n\u201cWhen you have scale ambition, agility becomes very important and agility cannot sit on disparate, siloed systems. The systems need to be interconnected,\u201d Chowhan emphasised.\n\n\u201cYou are able to unlock the power of data and technology when the data speaks to itself.\u201d\n\nUnlocking this connectivity is critical to fostering customer loyalty, an imperative Chowhan illustrated using the local retail landscape."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
