    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking as the Chief Guest, Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilaka assured the shipping community that the government is actively responding to industry feedback regarding regulatory frameworks. Acknowledging the concerns raised by the local shipping community, he announced the imminent formation of a new industry committee to evaluate these issues, with cabinet approval expected this week.\n\nBoth Minister Karunathilaka and Dr. Dissanayake took the opportunity to praise the critical role of NVOCCs in driving the country\u2019s maritime success. Minister Karunathilaka highlighted that SLANA members are crucial to the national logistics framework and commended their ongoing contribution to the broader Sri Lankan economy.\n\nDr. Dissanayake echoed these sentiments, noting that NVOCCs operate at the vital intersection between ship operators and cargo owners, making them indispensable to the operational resilience of the Port of Colombo.\n\nHowever, Dr. Dissanayake cautioned that the global shipping landscape is rapidly consolidating, placing terminal operators and NVOCCs increasingly at the mercy of major global players.\n\n\u201cGlobally, all container ships put together, what is the total shipboard capacity? It\u2019s 34.6 million TEUs. Of that 34.6 million TEUs, the top 10 shipping lines control 84.5 percent. The top five shipping lines control 65.2 percent,\u201d he pointed out.\n\nThe top five carriers by capacity are MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM, COSCO and Hapag-Lloyd.\n\n\u201cThe bottom line is that ports, terminal operators, NVOCCs, are at the mercy of these five shipping lines,\u201d he added.\u00a0 Noting that these leading carriers have transformed from simple ship operators into comprehensive supply chain service providers equipped with massive hardware advantages, he urged SLANA members to shift their operational roles.\n\n\u201cSLANA\u2019s role should change from representing NVOCC agents, to supply chain orchestrators enabling Sri Lanka\u2019s next-generation logistics,\u201d he advised."
