    "md5": "bbdb784ef69563f1c9aa8d4c0051f884",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1862,
    "chunk_text": "Tourism authority mounts cleanliness drive as Pekoe Trail passes into state ownership\n\nTrail pass income to fund waste collection by\u00a0 plantation communities under new non-profit mgt. model\n\nSLTDA engages Finance Ministry to share tourism levy with local councils, taps US$ 100mn WB project\u00a0 for recycling and Clean Sri Lanka project to fund tourist-friendly eco washrooms\n\nMoves follow latest tourist survey identifying cleanliness as the destination\u2019s weakest link, despite high overall satisfaction\n\nThe Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) will take ownership of the Pekoe Trail this month under a new non-profit model that funnels trail pass income to local government authorities to fund waste collection in tea plantation communities.\n\nThe trail handover serves as the focal point for a sweeping national cleanliness programme assembled by the authority, which also spans visitor washrooms at key sites, a proposed revenue-sharing model for the tourism development levy, and a US$ 100 million World Bank-funded recycling initiative in the North and East.\n\nThe measures advance a wider repositioning of the destination toward experience-based and sustainable tourism, aimed at repeat visitors and higher-yielding segments. Crucially, they address the destination\u2019s clearest vulnerability, identified in the authority\u2019s latest survey of departing foreign tourists. General cleanliness ranked weakest among all ten service attributes tested. Nearly one in ten tourists rated cleanliness poorly, a stark contrast to the near-perfect scores awarded for general welcome and hospitality.\n\nSLTDA Chairman Suranjith Wevita told Mirror Business the Pekoe Trail\u2019s route dictated its unique funding design, acting as a much-needed economic intervention for local councils that lack a formal tax base to manage waste in marginalized estate sectors."
