    "md5": "e2816e41ec75c7365c3c61063e2e2c9c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond the Mugshots: The Real Scandal of SriLankan Airlines\n\nThe arrest itself risks becoming the political headline while deeper institutional failure disappears from view (Pic courtesy of BBC News)\n\nThe arrest of SLPP National Organiser and Parliamentarian Namal Rajapaksa over alleged corruption linked to SriLankan Airlines\u2019 Airbus procurement has revived one of the most troubling chapters in the national carrier\u2019s history.\u00a0 Predictably, some welcome the arrest as proof the Government takes corruption seriously, while others see a politically motivated probe. Both reactions miss the main point.\n\nThe Airbus affair is ultimately about establishing who was responsible for exposing billions of dollars of public money, whether illegal payments occurred, and why the system failed to stop it. If found guilty, individuals involved, including Namal Rajapaksa, must face the maximum punishment under the law. Sri Lanka deserves those answers, absolute justice, and a system in which tomorrow\u2019s corruption is much harder to commit.\n\nThe Airbus affair should not be reduced to whether one politician is guilty or innocent. The central question is: how did a loss-making state airline become involved in aircraft transactions worth billions of dollars, and why have the institutions responsible for protecting public money repeatedly failed to establish accountability? That is the real scandal.\n\nThe controversy dates back to 2013, when SriLankan Airlines entered into arrangements involving Airbus A330-300 and A350-900 aircraft in a fleet-renewal programme valued over US$2.5 billion. For a struggling airline, commitments on that scale exposed vast public funds. The cancellation of some arrangements reportedly resulted in penalties exceeding US$100 million. Sri Lanka paid a heavy price for decisions made by custodians of a national asset."
  },
