    "md5": "9f2848c23c27b22dd58c76d7302b6caf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Time to flush black money out of the system\n\nColombo, Sep. 14 (Daily Mirror) - Start with a managed demonetization and withdrawal of the Rs. 5,000 note, make it harder to hoard illicit cash, conduct anonymous transactions and keep money beyond the reach of the formal economy. Bring hidden cash into the banking system, widen the tax net, curb bribery and illicit transactions and strike at Sri Lanka\u2019s shadow economy. This is not an untested experiment. Our big brother India and several other countries have already travelled this road, giving Sri Lanka a tested international playbook and the lessons to phase out its highest-value note.\n\nSri Lanka stands at a rare inflection point. Having clawed its way back from the worst economic collapse in its post-independence history, the country is now under intense scrutiny \u2014 from the IMF, from credit rating agencies, and from its own citizens \u2014 to prove that the reforms of the past two years were not merely a survival exercise, but the foundation of a fundamentally more honest economy. It is in this context that policymakers should seriously examine a measure that has quietly worked in other jurisdictions.\n\nWithdrawing the Rs. 5,000 note, the highest denomination in circulation.\n\nThe Hidden Cost of High-Value Cash. Every economy has a shadow twin the undeclared, undocumented, untaxed transactions that run parallel to the formal one. In Sri Lanka, this shadow economy has long been sustained by the convenience of high-denomination currency. Large notes are compact, anonymous, and untraceable \u2014 precisely the qualities that make them ideal for hoarding unaccounted wealth, facilitating smuggling, and greasing informal channels such as undiyal-style money transfers that bypass the banking system entirely."
