    "md5": "6d761860bd25e87ac56fd2e17e3785fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1624,
    "chunk_text": "The Hidden Cost of High-Value Cash. Every economy has a shadow twin the undeclared, undocumented, untaxed transactions that run parallel to the formal one. In Sri Lanka, this shadow economy has long been sustained by the convenience of high-denomination currency. Large notes are compact, anonymous, and untraceable \u2014 precisely the qualities that make them ideal for hoarding unaccounted wealth, facilitating smuggling, and greasing informal channels such as undiyal-style money transfers that bypass the banking system entirely.\n\nFirst, the IMF program demands it. Sri Lanka\u2019s Extended Fund Facility is explicitly tied to governance and revenue-mobilization benchmarks. A tax-to-GDP ratio that has historically lagged regional peers cannot be fixed through new taxes alone if the base itself remains hidden in cash. Withdrawing the highest note is a structural nudge toward formalization one that costs the government nothing to implement and could meaningfully widen the tax net.\n\nSecond, public trust demands it. A visible, credible move against unaccounted wealth would resonate as substance, not symbolism, in a political climate still hungry for accountability.\n\nThird, the digital rails already exist. The scaffolding for a smooth transition is more developed today than it was for comparable reforms elsewhere.\n\nIndia has run the experiment twice, with sharply different designs and sharply different results.\n\n2016: the shock therapy model. When India abruptly demonetized its Rs. 500 and Rs. 1,000 notes overnight, roughly 86% of currency in circulation by value vanished from legal use with almost no warning."
