    "chunk_size_bytes": 1637,
    "chunk_text": "The takeaway for Sri Lanka is unambiguous. If the goal is to curb unaccounted cash without repeating India\u2019s 2016 trauma, the design should borrow directly from India\u2019s 2023 playbook a clear withdrawal announcement, an extended and well-publicized exchange window, continued legal-tender status during the transition, multiple deposit/exchange channels (including for those without easy access to bank branches), and no sudden invalidation of the note\u2019s value. The objective is not to punish cash holders overnight it is to make holding large sums outside the banking system progressively less convenient, while giving legitimate holders every reasonable opportunity to formalize their money.\n\nIt would not eliminate such activities altogether, but it could substantially increase the inconvenience, risk and physical volume involved in conducting large illicit cash transactions, while pushing more legitimate economic activity towards traceable banking and digital channels.\n\nThe benefits could also extend to the external economy. Greater use of regulated channels could help redirect remittances and foreign currency away from informal networks and into the official banking system, supporting foreign-exchange liquidity and reserves.\n\nIncreased bank deposits could strengthen financial-system liquidity and potentially expand the pool of funds available for productive lending, business expansion and investment. At the same time, greater adoption of digital payments would create clearer transaction trails, improve financial transparency and accelerate Sri Lanka\u2019s transition towards a modern, less cash-dependent economy."
