    "md5": "d3a43ad019c04dd285f6f890c1eb8d39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1645,
    "chunk_text": "Increased bank deposits could strengthen financial-system liquidity and potentially expand the pool of funds available for productive lending, business expansion and investment. At the same time, greater adoption of digital payments would create clearer transaction trails, improve financial transparency and accelerate Sri Lanka\u2019s transition towards a modern, less cash-dependent economy.\n\nSeveral countries have already moved to eliminate or reduce their highest-value banknotes as part of broader efforts to modernize their payment systems and make large anonymous cash transactions less convenient. Denmark offers one of the most relevant recent examples. In 2023, it announced the withdrawal of its 1,000-kroner note its highest denomination. Danish authorities specifically pointed to the disproportionate use of the note in financial crime.\n\nThe European Central Bank similarly stopped issuing the \u20ac500 note because of concerns that it could facilitate illicit activity, while Canada has removed its CAN$1,000 bill from legal-tender circulation. Nigeria\u2019s Naira 2022\u201323 currency exercise also sought to tackle the enormous volume of cash being hoarded outside the banking system. This will protect the ordinary citizens and the legitimate business and minimize the financial crime in the future.\n\nIf Sri Lanka is serious about fighting corruption, black money, tax evasion, underworld cash and illicit transactions, take away one of their greatest convenience.\n\n\u201cDon\u2019t keep taxing the people already inside the system and the genuine citizens\u201d Go after the hidden economy instead of repeatedly squeezing compliant taxpayers."
