    "md5": "d579126ca58749b70137beeac3b8fef9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1797,
    "chunk_text": "In an age in which people are bombarded with information, his message gives greater meaning to the objective of the summit, which promotes the idea that reading matters not simply because books contain knowledge, but because they give people something to think about and talk about.\n\n\u201cWe are having a conversation with somebody who lived 500 years ago,\u201d Pathirana said, describing the extraordinary reach created by writing and books.\n\nThe question of what happens to reading when machines can increasingly do the reading for us was placed at the centre of the Pick a Book 2026 campaign.\n\nPathirana\u2019s answer was to look back at what writing and reading have enabled humans to do: preserve thoughts, share knowledge across generations and engage in conversations with people who lived centuries before us.\n\nFounded seven years ago as a network of book clubs, Pick a Book is now attempting to turn its community into a much larger reading ecosystem.\n\nPick a Book Founder Ragulan Tharmakulasingam believes the rise of artificial intelligence makes the proposition more urgent.\n\n\u201cWe are living through a moment where machines can read everything and understand almost none of it, and where attention has become the scarcest thing any of us owns,\u201d he said. \u201cIn that world, reading is no longer a pastime. It is the human edge.\u201d\n\nThe movement has already moved beyond simply encouraging people to buy and read books. Members discuss and debate them, exchange them, donate them and gift them, turning reading from a solitary activity into a social one.\n\n\u201cPick a Book is a platform of possibilities, where books open doors to knowledge, meaningful connections, and the lifelong journey of becoming your best self,\u201d Ragulan said, adding that its next phase is considerably more ambitious."
  },
