    "md5": "7ef0c0cd7183db6740a98d33270a645c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "Westminster\u2019s time is coming to an end, Welsh, Scottish, Northern Irish leaders say\n\nBBC - The first ministers of Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland have declared that \"Westminster's time is coming to an end\".\n\nThe nationalist leaders from Plaid Cymru, Sinn F\u00e9in and the SNP - who want their countries to leave the United Kingdom - signed an agreement on Monday which said no Westminster government had the right to \"block democracy\".\n\nBut while Scotland's John Swinney and Northern Ireland's Michelle O'Neill called for referendums at a joint press conference, Wales' Rhun ap Iorwerth declined to give a timetable for one in his country.\n\nLabour and Reform accused Plaid of wanting to break up the UK.\n\nSwinney and ap Iorwerth's governments later backed a separate agreement agreed to work together on pressing UK government on \"fairness\" in funding and powers.\n\nFor the first time since devolution began, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland all have first ministers who want to leave the United Kingdom.\n\nAll three met on Monday as representatives of their parties. Sinn F\u00e9in president Mary Lou McDonald also attended.\n\nThe memorandum of understanding said: \"For people watching across these islands - for people watching around the world - there could be no clearer sign that Westminster's time is coming to an end.\n\n\"No Westminster government has the right to block democracy or undermine the principle that our people will decide their own future.\n\n\"We recognise that our parties have different constitutional positions and that each nation will determine its own future in its own way and in its own time.\"\n\nPlaid has ruled out an independence referendum before the next Senedd election in 2030.\n\nAt the press conference, ap Iorwerth said there was a \"growing recognition that Westminster is not working\" and \"power genuinely needs to be brought closer to people and communities\"."
  },
