    "md5": "c7d39ba9d42fb402c0eab4e5fb33f369",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "Asked by BBC Wales to clarify what the memorandum of understanding meant for Wales, and whether it meant asking Westminster not to block a Welsh independence referendum, ap Iorwerth said it was \"up to the people of Wales to decide its own future\".\n\nThe Welsh government does not have the power to hold such a referendum, but ap Iorwerth told the press conference he wanted to see that devolved.\n\nLater on Monday, Swinney and ap Iorwerth signed an agreement between the Welsh and Scottish governments.\n\nThe agreement, which is not legally binding, said it would \"press the case for greater fairness for Wales and Scotland in areas such as the operation of the UK funding arrangements and powers over our constitutional future\".\n\nIt said the two governments would work together on a range of issues, including tackling child poverty and maximising the benefits of their relationships with the European Union.\n\nAt a second press conference, Swinney declined to offer support for reform of the Barnett Formula - the system that determines how Wales is funded.\n\nWelsh ministers have long called for the Barnett funding formula to be reformed, claiming it disadvantages Wales.\n\nBefore he became prime minister, Burnham made the case for change, but on his recent visit to Wales he played down the prospect of reform.\n\nSwinney said: \"Financial arrangements across the United Kingdom vary and by constituent part of the United Kingdom. We all have to put forward the issues and the interests and the perspectives in each of our countries.\"\n\nAp Iorwerth said: \"There are flexibilities that Scotland already have right now as a minimum to be handed to Wales, is something that is important to us.\"\n\nDuring Prime Minister's Questions on Wednesday, Burnham appeared to indicate a second referendum could be held in Scotland if there was clear public support for a vote."
  },
