    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Ap Iorwerth said: \"There are flexibilities that Scotland already have right now as a minimum to be handed to Wales, is something that is important to us.\"\n\nDuring Prime Minister's Questions on Wednesday, Burnham appeared to indicate a second referendum could be held in Scotland if there was clear public support for a vote.\n\n\"I would say that it is exactly the same situation in Scotland,\" he added.\n\nThe apparent softening in tone was welcomed by Swinney before Burnham clarified that another Scottish referendum remained \"off limits\".\n\nA referendum on Scottish independence in September 2014 saw the country vote to remain part of the UK by a margin of 55% to 45%.\n\nAp Iorwerth, who was appointed the first non-Labour first minister of Wales after May's Senedd election, had ruled out pushing for a referendum on Welsh independence during his party's first term in government.\n\nHis government intends to set up a national commission to \"lay the foundations\" for future plans for Welsh independence.\n\nIn the meantime, Welsh ministers are seeking extra powers to be transferred to Wales, including over policing and youth justice - which Burnham has said he is \"open\" to the idea of.\n\nAp Iorwerth said Burnham had \"a lot to learn\" about \"how devolution to a nation is rather different to devolution to a region or a city of England\".\n\n\"I want to have a positive relationship with him, I believe he wants to have a positive relationship with me,\" he said.\n\nThe UK government said: \"The prime minister is committed to delivering change across all four nations of the United Kingdom and believes strongly in the union.\"\n\nThe devolved leaders will meet the prime minister next month.\n\nLabour leader in Wales, Ken Skates, said: \"Plaid have given people nothing since the election.\n\n\"They clearly want to use power to break up Britain, weaken Wales and spend their time obsessing over more powers for their politicians.\""
