    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "SDB bank recognised as Banking Sector Runner-Up at NBEA\n\nSDB bank has been recognised as the Banking Sector Runner-Up at the National Business Excellence Awards, reaffirming its position as a purpose-driven financial institution committed to sustainable growth, responsible banking and meaningful social impact.\n\nOrganised by the National Chamber of Commerce of Sri Lanka, the National Business Excellence Awards is one of the country\u2019s most respected platforms for recognizing organisations that demonstrate excellence across business performance, governance, sustainability, innovation and value creation.\n\nThe recognition reflects SDB bank\u2019s continued progress in combining sound financial performance with a strong commitment to national development. Through its operations, the Bank continues to support financial inclusion, women\u2019s empowerment, small and medium-sized enterprise development, digital accessibility and the advancement of communities across Sri Lanka.\n\nCommenting on the achievement, Manoj Akmeemana, Acting Chief Executive Officer of SDB bank said, \u201cBeing recognised as the Banking Sector Runner-Up is an important acknowledgement of the progress we continue to make as a purpose-driven financial institution. This recognition reflects the dedication of our employees and the continued trust placed in us by our customers, shareholders, business partners and communities.\u201d\n\nHe added, \u201cOur focus extends beyond financial performance. We remain committed to creating sustainable economic and social value by supporting entrepreneurs, SMEs, individuals and communities while strengthening responsible banking practices across our operations.\u201d\n\nEnvironmental, Social and Governance principles remain central to SDB bank\u2019s strategy and operating philosophy. The Bank continues to strengthen its customer-focused solutions, digital capabilities and community-centered initiatives to improve access to financial services and enable broader participation in the formal economy."
  },
