    "chunk_text": "Was BRICS treated as a genuine foreign-policy priority? Were India, China, Russia, Brazil, South Africa and the newer members systematically engaged? Were Sri Lankan missions given a coordinated lobbying strategy? Did we explain not merely why Sri Lanka wants BRICS, but why BRICS should want Sri Lanka?\n\nOther countries of different sizes, regions and economic weight secured Partner Country status. They advanced. Sri Lanka did not.\n\nFor a smaller country like Sri Lanka, the answer should not be fewer relationships but more. We need more markets, more financing options, more partnerships and more seats at more tables.\n\nBRICS should not be viewed as choosing one geopolitical camp over another. It should be another economic and diplomatic bridge consistent with Sri Lanka\u2019s independent, balanced foreign policy.\n\nCloser engagement could strengthen relations simultaneously with India, our closest neighbour and critical economic partner; China, a major investor and trading partner; and Russia, an important political, economic and tourism partner. Sri Lanka\u2019s national interest is served by engaging all major powers through multi-alignment, economic pragmatism and national interest.\n\nThe economic stakes are equally important. BRICS is increasingly connecting economies through trade, investment, business partnerships, start-ups, technology and wider market access.\n\nThe wider shift is unmistakable. The Global South is rising.\n\nDeveloping and emerging economies are becoming more economically powerful, politically influential and strategically important. The Global South is no longer merely watching the world order it is increasingly shaping it.\n\nSri Lanka also sought membership of the New Development Bank. For a country recovering from sovereign default and requiring substantial infrastructure investment, expanding access to long-term development finance should be a strategic priority. This is not a choice between the IMF, World Bank, Asian Development Bank or BRICS institutions."
  },
