    "md5": "ff13de3904427416ead78ec7ac1ea969",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "The Delhi BRICS\u2019 summit: Discussing key Global South issues while managing contradictions\n\n(Left to right) Chinese President Xi Jinping, Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi,\u00a0 Russian President Vladimir Putin and\u00a0 Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian during the 18th BRICS Summit in New Delhi. (Photo courtesy@narendramodi/IG via PTI Photo)\n\nThe 18th BRICS summit held at New Delhi on September 12\u201313 managed to resolve sharp internal contradictions and produced a joint declaration, which was the best that could be, given the complexities of international politics, individual members\u2019 concerns and ambitions, and above all the need to keep the organisation together in the face of unprecedented challenges from the United States under the mercurial President Trump.\n\nThe Delhi Declaration of 2026, (DD for short) was forthright in demanding much-needed changes in world governance. It called for gender and country inclusivity in all global decision-making bodies such as the UN, IMF and World Bank.\n\n\u201cWe reiterate our commitment to ensuring greater and more meaningful participation and representation of Emerging Markets and Developing Countries (EMDCs), as well as Least Developed Countries (LDCs), especially from Africa, Asia and Latin America and the Caribbean, in global decision-making processes and structures and making them better attuned to contemporary realities,\u201d the DD said.\n\nIt also called for achieving equitable geographical representation in the Secretariat of the UN and other international organisations in a timely manner, particularly by addressing the persistent under-representation of developing countries, including at senior and decision-making levels, as well as increasing the role and share of women, especially from the Emerging Markets and Developing Countries (EMDCs), at all levels of leadership."
  },
  {
