    "md5": "43489fba8167c0c061d41623b4d5823a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "CA Sri Lanka submits comprehensive proposals for National Budget 2027\n\nAdvocates compliance-led revenue growth, digital transformation, taxpayer fairness and a more competitive business environment\n\nThe Institute of Chartered Accountants of Sri Lanka (CA Sri Lanka) submitted its comprehensive proposals for the National Budget 2027 to the Secretary to the Ministry of Finance, Dr. Harshana Suriyapperuma, on 7 September.\n\nThe proposals were submitted by CA Sri Lanka President Tishan Subasinghe, accompanied by Members of the Council, Alternate Chairman of the Faculty of Taxation Prasad Dasanayaka, and Chief Executive Officer Lakmali Priyangika.\n\nThe 16-page proposals, developed through extensive consultations with members and deliberations at the Institute\u2019s 5th Annual Economic and Tax Symposium, present a comprehensive roadmap for strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s fiscal position through improved compliance, digitalisation, formalisation and taxpayer confidence, while supporting investment and sustainable economic growth.\n\nPresenting the proposals, President Subasinghe emphasised that Sri Lanka\u2019s next phase of fiscal reform must move beyond repeated increases in statutory tax rates.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka has made commendable progress in restoring macroeconomic stability and rebuilding fiscal credibility, but we cannot sustain this momentum through excessive reliance on tax rate hikes alone,\u201d Subasinghe said.\n\n\u201cOur focus must now shift towards better capturing the economic activity already taking place across the country and bringing more of it into the formal tax system. We need to broaden and strengthen the tax base through improved compliance, better use of information and more predictable tax administration. The Institute believes that by prioritizing digitalisation, formalisation and taxpayer confidence, we can mobilise revenue fairly and efficiently while fostering an environment conducive to investment and sustainable growth,\u201d he said."
  },
