    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "While recognising the fiscal impact, CA Sri Lanka believes the measure could stimulate economic activity and improve disposable income amid continuing inflationary pressures. The proposals therefore emphasise that the fiscal cost should be considered alongside the potential economic benefits of increased consumption and formal economic activity.\n\nTo support formalisation and sustainable growth, CA Sri Lanka proposes a tiered corporate income tax structure based on annual turnover.\n\nMedium enterprises: annual turnover up to Rs. 1 billion; and\n\nMicro businesses should be able to maintain simplified records, while small and medium-sized businesses could progressively adopt standardised digital templates. The objective is to encourage businesses to formalise and grow without excessive compliance requirements inadvertently pushing smaller enterprises into informality.\n\nThe Institute also recommends that the Central Bank encourage financial institutions to rely, where appropriate, on audited financial statements and tax returns when assessing credit applications, thereby reducing the need for SMEs to incur additional costs to satisfy lending requirements.\n\nCA Sri Lanka further calls for the urgent expedition of Development Banks\u2019 concessionary lending facilities for SMEs, with access linked to the submission of audited accounts. This would provide much-needed support to businesses facing current cash-flow constraints while simultaneously encouraging stronger financial reporting practices.\n\nThe CA Sri Lanka proposals also call for stronger and more transparent capital gains assessments, including a framework under which valuations prepared by accredited independent professionals are formally considered by the Inland Revenue Department (IRD).\n\nFor tourism, CA Sri Lanka recommends a dedicated Digital Tourism Booking Framework to address tax-base erosion associated with non-resident digital platforms facilitating accommodation bookings in Sri Lanka."
