    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s allocation sits half a percentage point below the ceiling on any single country. Vietnam, Egypt, Morocco, Pakistan, Bangladesh and Kazakhstan each carry the full 8 percent weighting, with Nigeria close behind Sri Lanka at 7.4 percent, as per J.P. Morgan\u2019s index research dated September 14. Taken together, the seven markets at or near the cap make up 55.5 percent of the index, a share that climbs to 62.9 percent once Nigeria is added.\n\nInclusion matters because international investors tend to gravitate towards bonds that feature in such benchmarks. While FTSE Russell has operated a comparable index since 2021, J.P. Morgan\u2019s indices carry greater prominence among emerging market fund managers, who also use them to measure their own performance. Analysts estimate tradeable local currency emerging market debt has trebled over the past decade to around US$ 1 trillion, meaning the new index would cover roughly a third of that pool.\n\nEconomists also expect the index to help deepen local currency debt markets, an objective long promoted by the World Bank and the IMF to reduce the risk of debt crises triggered when currency collapses leave governments unable to service dollar debt. Angola, which features in the index, cited this as one reason for opening its US$ 18.6 billion domestic bond market more widely last week.\n\nJ.P. Morgan, in its note, likened many of these markets to core emerging markets in the early 2000s, pointing to high nominal carry, improving market infrastructure and periodic volatility. According to the World Bank, frontier economies are home to a fifth of the global population yet account for just 3.1 percent of global capital flows and less than 5 percent of global GDP. However, their populations are projected to grow by 800 million over the next 25 years, more than the rest of the world combined."
