    "chunk_size_bytes": 1640,
    "chunk_text": "The regime protection and impunity extended to wheeler-dealers and corrupt regime operators were a way of life in Sri Lankan politics; it has, for the first time, faced a decisive assault. The de facto bipartisan consensus defending regime holders, save minor political sideshows, had been dutifully followed by both the UNP and the Rajapaksa regime. While the JVP/NPP administration\u2019s sincerity in combating corruption, or for that matter corruption under its predecessors, is yet to be determined, in this particular incident it carries no political baggage. Stakes may be different if the government chose to pursue the VFS global Visa scam.( The government, neither otherwise activist CIABOC had chosen not to, nor has the opposition SJB, true to the old bipartisan consensus.)\n\nThere is also a time-tested Sri Lankan political script that transforms a crook into a political victim and a hero. Namal Rajapaksa quotes Nelson Mandela from his remand prison. The government, however, has a duty to defend the integrity of the judicial process and the credibility of the CIABOC and other investigating apparatus. Political slander against the judicial process and CIABOC should be tried under Libel laws. The differentiation between mala fide and legitimate criticism, at times, might be hard to establish, and the onus of that should be placed on the courts themselves, rather than letting vested interests run roughshod over the independent state institutions. Those were the same sins the JVP was culpable of in the past, but that dubious practice has to be held accountable before the court if Sri Lanka is to build its institutions."
