    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6805609acffdb713b9c8a033a06ce120",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Cabinet Subcommittee on El Ni\u00f1o orders disaster drills, public awareness drive\n\nPreparedness drills to be piloted first in landslide-prone Kandy, Badulla, Matale and Nuwara Eliya\n\nPublic awareness campaign to run via electronic and print media as dry conditions turn to rain over next two months\n\nDivisional Secretariat-level operational centres to be set up ahead of anticipated floods and landslides\n\nProgress of ongoing relief measures for those already affected by El Ni\u00f1o conditions reviewed\n\nThe Special Cabinet Subcommittee and Officials\u2019 Committee appointed to address the impact of the El Ni\u00f1o climate phenomenon met for the fifth time at the Presidential Secretariat on 14 September, 2026, chaired by Minister of Environment Dr. Dhammika Patabendi.\n\nWith El Ni\u00f1o-driven weather expected to shift from the current dry spell to wetter conditions over the next two months, the committees discussed measures to prepare for the change.\n\nThe committees decided to raise public awareness of potential floods and landslides linked to increased rainfall, and to conduct disaster preparedness drills to familiarise communities with the appropriate response in an emergency.\n\nInitial drills were ordered for the districts of Kandy, Badulla, Matale and Nuwara Eliya, identified as most vulnerable to landslides, with instructions that the necessary measures be taken without delay.\n\nThe meeting also agreed to run a public awareness program through electronic and print media, and discussed preparing communities in advance by establishing operational centres at Divisional Secretariat level.\n\nThe committees additionally reviewed progress on relief measures already under way for those affected by the prevailing El Ni\u00f1o conditions, as well as plans for measures ahead."
