    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "Misinformation on Aesthetic studies misleads society: experts\n\nColombo, Sep. 16 (Daily Mirror) - \u201cThere is no use of the Arts, one can prosper by becoming a doctor or an engineer. Don\u2019t think of studying the Arts but try to become a doctor or an engineer. This has been the mindset of most parents in Sri Lanka over the years and they have not encouraged their children to study the Arts. However, one must remember that the Arts could do much more than anyone could imagine. This was the message given by some Sri Lankan academic experts at a media briefing recently.\n\n\u201cThe Arts is used from birth to death though many will not imagine. A child is exposed to music from the time of birth. The first song a child hears is a mother\u2019s lullaby. When a doctor looks at an X -ray, he actually looks at a work of art which depicts the inside of a human body. It is the same when it comes to an ultrasound scan. These works of art are done using modern technologies. Therefore, the Arts is everywhere,\u201d Vice Chancellor of the University of Visual and Performing Arts, Professor. Rohan Netthsinghe said.\n\n\u201cSri Lankan peasants used cattle for paddy cultivation. How many farmers use cattle today?. Obviously very few machinery such as \u2018 Boothaya\u2019 is used in farming today. Some rituals performed by indigenous people are dying. The future generations will get to know about these traditions only through research papers. This is why research on the Arts is essential. This alone creates opportunities in the Arts though some argue that the Arts is of no use,\u201d he added.\n\nProfessor Nethsinghe together with the Registrar of the University of Visual and Performing Arts, Dr. Chani Imbulgoda and Director, Centre for Research and Creative Work and Knowledge Dissemination of the University, Prof. Tharanga Dandeniya then announced a special programme UVPA PRAXIS 2026, an international conference to be held at the University premises on September 16th to the 18th."
  },
