    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "Cabinet nod for Rs. 400 m project to mark boundaries of major irrigation tanks countrywide\n\nBoundary demarcation at Kandy, Kantale and Tissa tanks already completed as Irrigation Department moves to protect water sources from encroachment\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved the continued implementation of a project to precisely mark the boundaries of major tanks under the control of the Department of Irrigation, using boundary posts installed on the ground, as part of a broader effort to conserve water sources affected by current land use patterns.\n\nThe initiative was launched using an allocation of Rs. 100 million provided to the Agriculture, Livestock, Lands and Irrigation Ministry for 2025 under the \u2018Clean Sri Lanka\u2019 initiative.\n\nWork began in 2025 on 21 selected tanks identified as being under various forms of pressure, with boundary marking and pillar installation fully completed that year for three tanks in Kandy, Kantale and Tissa.\n\nA further 48 tanks have been identified under the project proposal approved by the Department of National Planning, with work on these yet to commence and scheduled for completion in the coming years.\n\nCabinet Spokesman and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa said yesterday the estimated total cost of the project for the 2025\u20132028 period covering the surveying of remaining tanks, the setting and installation of boundary stones, and tree planting has been calculated at Rs. 400 million.\n\n\u201cThe Cabinet has approved the continued implementation of the project through the Department of Irrigation to complete the remaining work within this timeframe,\u201d he said at the weekly post-Cabinet meeting media briefing.\n\nThe proposal to this effect was submitted by the Agriculture, Livestock, Lands and Irrigation Minister K.D. Lal Kantha."
  },
