    "md5": "4fd9bc92289c24b64ded8e8510ec79f8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "Rabies bill could derail dog vaccination drive, raise human rabies risk: PHIs\n\nColombo, Sep. 16 (Daily Mirror) - The proposed Rabies Eradication and Dog Registration Bill could potentially disrupt Sri Lanka\u2019s dog vaccination programme and increase the risk of human rabies, the Sri Lanka Public Health Inspectors Association (SLPHIA) has warned.\n\nIn a letter to President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, the association urged the Government to review the Bill, saying several of its provisions could create practical difficulties in maintaining the country\u2019s existing community-based rabies control programme.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Ministry of Health has identified dog vaccination as a key component of its rabies prevention strategy. The country\u2019s National Strategic Plan targets dog vaccination coverage of more than 75 per cent and zero dog-mediated human rabies deaths by 2026.\n\nThe association said Sri Lanka\u2019s dog rabies vaccination programme had a long history, dating back to 1953, and was currently implemented through the Ministry of Health\u2019s Public Health Veterinary Service, District Rabies Control Units and Medical Officer of Health (MOH) areas.\n\nPublic Health Inspectors (PHIs), it said, play a key role in taking vaccination services to communities, including vaccination of domestic dogs, stray dogs and dogs found around schools, temples, public places and government and private institutions.\n\nThe association also said special vaccination programmes were conducted in areas where dog bites or suspected rabies cases were reported, based on surveillance carried out at the location.\n\nAccording to the PHIs, the proposed legislation could create a major obstacle to this system.\n\nThe association specifically raised concerns over Section 4 of the Bill, which it said would require dog vaccination to be carried out by a veterinarian or under a veterinarian\u2019s supervision."
  },
