    "chunk_text": "According to the PHIs, the proposed legislation could create a major obstacle to this system.\n\nThe association specifically raised concerns over Section 4 of the Bill, which it said would require dog vaccination to be carried out by a veterinarian or under a veterinarian\u2019s supervision.\n\nThe association therefore warned that the existing vaccination activities could be forced to stop once the proposed law comes into effect unless an alternative mechanism is established.\n\nThe PHIs said this was particularly concerning given the link between sustained dog vaccination and the long-term reduction in human rabies deaths in Sri Lanka.\n\nAccording to the Ministry of Health, 20 laboratory-confirmed human rabies deaths were reported in 2024, while dogs remained the main reservoir and primary source responsible for most human rabies deaths that year.\n\nThe World Health Organization has also identified dog vaccination and post-exposure prophylaxis as key public health interventions behind Sri Lanka\u2019s substantial reduction in human rabies deaths, from 377 deaths in the mid-1970s to 31 in 2021.\n\nThe association further referred to a change made in 2018, when responsibility for dog vaccination was transferred to veterinarians of the Department of Animal Production and Health and District Rabies Control Units under the Health Ministry were discontinued.\n\nIt claimed that only about six per cent of dogs were vaccinated under that arrangement in 2018 and that the programme was subsequently returned to the Health Ministry in 2019.\n\nThe association said District Rabies Control Units were then reactivated and vaccination activities resumed, with the Health Ministry working towards reducing human rabies deaths and ultimately eliminating dog-mediated human rabies.\n\nThe PHIs also raised concerns over the proposed Bill\u2019s enforcement mechanism."
  },
