    "md5": "5d012a62768de059ec3fdf2726209a2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1451,
    "chunk_text": "The association said District Rabies Control Units were then reactivated and vaccination activities resumed, with the Health Ministry working towards reducing human rabies deaths and ultimately eliminating dog-mediated human rabies.\n\nThe PHIs also raised concerns over the proposed Bill\u2019s enforcement mechanism.\n\nThe association argued that excluding PHIs could create difficulties in enforcing the proposed law at grassroots level and responding to complaints from the public.\n\nIt also questioned the practicality of requiring Medical Officers of Health to undertake all field-level activities, claiming that powers vested in an MOH under the proposed legislation could not be delegated to a PHI or another officer.\n\nThe proposed legislation is intended to consolidate Sri Lanka\u2019s existing Rabies Ordinance and Dog Registration Ordinance, make rabies vaccination of dogs mandatory, strengthen dog population control and clarify the responsibilities of institutions involved in rabies control and dog registration. Cabinet approved its publication in the Gazette before submission to Parliament.\n\nThe association said it supported efforts to eradicate rabies but urged the Government to amend the proposed legislation to ensure that the law is practical at grassroots level and does not disrupt the existing vaccination system.\n\nIt requested President Dissanayake to review the Bill and introduce the necessary amendments before it is implemented."
