    "md5": "6fff715eb253ee31e3eeefa3f7579f82",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1607,
    "chunk_text": "Strictly theoretically, 2029 should be something of a slam-dunk for Sajith, because the unprecedented growth of poverty and inequality should provide a socioeconomic majority for him if he deploys the franchise he holds exclusively, i.e., that of the developmentalist-populist paradigm of his father President Premadasa. Today\u2019s political narrative is also about \u2018Fathers and Sons\u2019.\n\nBut that\u2019s only theoretically. The reality is that Sajith Premadasa is a social democratic centrist who leads a party which is not. The SJB is a centre-right party with a rightist caucus. Having been under Ranil Wickremesinghe\u2019s leadership, it has no experience of a party and politics chosen by popular vote to lead the country. It therefore lacks the political instincts and reflexes to fight and win. It rejects attempts to change or induct new elements, and inhabits its centre-right cocoon with the only idea buzzing around being an SJB-UNP bloc with a \u2018rightist\u2019 ideological identity.\u00a0 An SJB-UNP bloc isn\u2019t intrinsically bad, but is colossally inadequate\u2014as surgically argued by Kusum Wijetilleke, SJB Working Committee member and Advisor on Political Economy to Sajith Premadasa. Wijetilleke has written what I consider the best think-piece I have read in the local press this year so far. (https://www.ft.lk/columns/Centre-right-Wrong-about-everything/4-797138)\n\nAny SJB-UNP convergence can decisively attract votes only if such a convergence takes place on the basis of a shift to progressive centrism/social democracy and the revival of President Premadasa\u2019s pro-people development paradigm."
  },
