    "description": "Cabinet clears Rs. 46 b Mudun Ela Drainage Project to tackle chronic flooding in Peliyagoda, Kelaniya",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Cabinet-clears-Rs-46-b-Mudun-Ela-Drainage-Project-to-tackle-chronic-flooding-in-Peliyagoda-Kelaniya/56-797340",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1789602881.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-17-dailyftlk-6214882c-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "56b533c8b82adfd2f976fdeedb3a0308",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1568,
    "chunk_text": "Cabinet clears Rs. 46 b Mudun Ela Drainage Project to tackle chronic flooding in Peliyagoda, Kelaniya\n\nDecade-long project, backed by JICA and SLLDC feasibility studies, aims to free up flood-prone land for future development\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved the implementation of the \u2018Mudun Ela Storm Drainage and Environment Improvement Project\u2019, a large-scale initiative aimed at resolving chronic storm water drainage problems affecting the Peliyagoda and Kelaniya areas within the Mudun Ela sub-catchment zone.\n\nThe project, proposed by the Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation (SLLDC), will be implemented in phases over the period 2027 to 2036, at an estimated total cost of Rs. 46,190.6 million.\n\nThe initiative has been designed based on two key studies: a Pre-Feasibility Study of the Mudun Ela Basin Zone conducted by the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) in 2023, and a subsequent Feasibility Study carried out by the SLLDC in 2024.\n\nStorm water drainage issues in the Mudun Ela sub-catchment area have long posed a significant obstacle to development in Peliyagoda and Kelaniya, with recurring flooding affecting both ongoing economic activities and future development potential in the area.\n\nOnce implemented, the project is expected to provide a comprehensive solution to the area\u2019s drainage problems, minimising the damage and disruption caused by floods to current development projects and economic activity. It is also expected to free up land currently constrained by flood risk, making it available for future development."
  },
