    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3c6e4ef33075fd0fe7ab1ac092fc4ecc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "These successive shocks have weakened financial capacity and human capital, constrained investment in machinery and equipment, delayed digitalisation and AI adoption, and disrupted manufacturing and production. They have also affected service reliability, quality standards and businesses\u2019 ability to fulfil contractual and performance obligations.\n\nBuilding on the PwC-FCCISL Business Resurgence Study, FCCISL developed a five-chapter strategic initiative and submitted five proposals to the President and the Finance Ministry for National Budget and policy consideration:\n\n1.Integrated Strategic Framework for Data-Driven Governance \u2013 GovData-SL: Connecting public institutions through integrated data, digital systems and evidence-based decision-making.\n\n2.Public Service Transformation Fund: Reskilling and upskilling public officials to strengthen efficient, digitally competent and citizen-centred service delivery.\n\n3.National Business Recovery and Entrepreneurial Resurgence Framework: Establishing national and district-level mechanisms to resolve business difficulties, facilitate finance, support distressed enterprises and protect employment.\n\n4.National Public-Private Consortium for Infrastructure and Economic Development: Mobilising private capital, professional expertise and technology for infrastructure and economic development.\n\n5.Galle District Strategic Development Plan: A regional business resurgence and diversification pilot, adaptable for replication across other districts.\n\nPwC-FCCISL Business Resurgence Study Project Chairman Ruwan De Silva, led the research and consolidation of the interconnected reform framework. These proposals reflect the longstanding need for coordinated institutional support and practical business recovery mechanisms.\n\nFCCISL has also prepared and submitted \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Path to Inclusion in the World Bank\u2019s Business Ready (B-READY) Index,\u201d supporting coordinated business-environment reforms."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
