    "chunk_text": "The unions said the dispute had remained unresolved for nearly two years and rejected a request from the Ministry Secretary for additional time to reach a final decision.\n\nThey said that the strike was not aimed at opposing the Government but at rebuilding the Railway Department, improving operational efficiency and restoring a reliable service for passengers.\n\nThey also accused the administration of failing to adequately engage with trade unions representing railway employees.\n\nUnion representatives said several groups within the Department, including station masters, controllers, drivers, cabinmen, shunting staff, workshop employees and station staff, had sought discussions on issues affecting both employees and passengers but had not been given adequate opportunities to raise their concerns with the General Manager.\n\nThe Railway Controllers Union has separately backed the strike, saying its demands include the immediate and unconditional withdrawal of the request for an apology from the Station Masters\u2019 Union secretary and the removal of the General Manager.\n\nThe Railway Technological and Operating Service Union has also announced that it would join the joint trade union action, citing concerns over the administration of the Department and delays in processing railway employees\u2019 pension matters.\n\nThe union said the head of the Railway Department should possess expertise suited to a highly specialised engineering and operational institution, warning that continued administrative experimentation could further affect the service.\n\nIt said it would participate in the strike to draw the attention of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to the issues faced by railway employees and the Department.\n\nMeanwhile, Minister Bimal Rathnayake has maintained that he cannot agree to the demand for the removal of the General Manager."
