    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "94519876f2040fdee48072207a2f37fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "Supreme Court rules judges liable for income tax\n\nColombo, September 17 (Daily Mirror)- The Supreme Court has dismissed three appeals filed by associations representing judicial officers, holding that judges of the High Court, District Courts, Magistrates\u2019 Courts and Labour Tribunals are liable to pay income tax and that Advance Personal Income Tax (APIT) could be deducted from their remuneration under the Inland Revenue Act.\n\nThe five-member Supreme Court Bench comprising Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena and Justices Shiran Gooneratne, Janak De Silva, Mahinda Samayawardhana and Arjuna Obeyesekere delivered the judgment. The appeals were dismissed without costs.\n\nThe appeals were filed against a November 2, 2023 judgment of a divisional bench of the Court of Appeal, which had dismissed three writ applications filed by associations representing judicial officers. The associations represented High Court Judges, District Judges, Additional District Judges, Magistrates, Additional Magistrates and Presidents of Labour Tribunals.\n\nJustice Janak De Silva, delivering the principal judgment, held that the application of the Inland Revenue Act to the judicial officers represented in the appeals did not violate the Constitution.\n\nHe further held that the Act did not create an employer-employee relationship between judicial officers and the relevant respondents. Instead, it recognised, for taxation purposes, the factual role of the Secretary to the Ministry in paying the salaries of judicial officers and identifying the person responsible for deducting APIT.\n\nJustice De Silva observed that although the independence of the judiciary was a foundational constitutional principle, judicial officers remained subject to taxation imposed by law where such taxation was of general application, non-discriminatory and not specifically directed at judges or the judiciary."
  },
