    "chunk_size_bytes": 1799,
    "chunk_text": "The market has since transformed. Sri Lanka had about 235 listed companies, with a market capitalisation of around 100 billion and the rupee traded at Rs.89 to the US dollar. Today, 290 companies are listed, with a market capitalisation of Rs.8 trillion, while the exchange rate stands at Rs.328.\n\nThe number of listed companies has grown by around 23 percent, while the market capitalisation has risen eightyfold in rupee terms.\n\nThe SLID now represents more than 1,000 members. Over the past year, the institute hosted Sri Lanka Corporate Director Summit 2026, which brought together more than 400 chairpersons, directors and senior business leaders alongside over 20 international speakers and launched the SLID ACCA National Corporate Director Awards to recognise excellence in stewardship. It has also pursued initiatives such as Women on Boards and the Board Ready Directors directory.\n\nWeerakkody closed his address with the words of the late Ratan Tata, whom he had met on several occasions: \u201cGood governance is not about rules. It is about doing the right thing when no one is watching.\u201d\n\nHe told the gathering, \u201cThe way forward is clear and well lit. All we need is the courage to walk that path.\u201d\n\nMeanwhile, Secretary to Prime Minister G. Pradeep Saputhanthri, attending as the chief guest, pointed out that Sri Lanka is undertaking important economic and structural reforms as it rebuilds from the most difficult economic crisis in its recent history. At the same time, artificial intelligence and other technologies are changing businesses, institutions and the way decisions are made.\n\n\u201cFor directors, technology can no longer be left entirely to technical specialists. It has become a question of governance, accountability and institutional responsibility,\u201d he observed."
