    "md5": "67de59829b8e72cee6bed147f63e85ac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1789,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s 4.2% growth conceals two-speed recovery\n\nGrowth slowed from 5.0% in corresponding quarter of 2025, with industry expanding 7.3% and services growing 2.7%\n\nUnderlying composition showed recovery becoming heavily reliant on construction, mining, financial services and imports\n\nConstruction, credit-supported consumption and other interest-sensitive activities that drove 2Q growth could face increasing resistance as monetary tightening filters through economy\n\nThe national economy grew 4.2 percent in the second quarter; however, a surge in imports and construction-linked activity masked the contractions across agriculture and key export industries, which points to an increasingly uneven recovery as the tighter monetary conditions begin to weigh on demand.\n\nGrowth slowed from 5.0 percent in the corresponding quarter of 2025, with industry expanding 7.3 percent and services growing 2.7 percent, while agriculture contracted 2.3 percent, according to the Census and Statistics Department.\n\nThe underlying composition showed the recovery becoming heavily reliant on construction, mining, financial services and imports. At the same time, apparel, tea, rice, fishing and petroleum refining contracted, while tourism-related activity lost considerable momentum.\n\nConstruction expanded 13.9 percent and mining and quarrying surged 17.4 percent, making them two of the strongest contributors to industrial growth.\n\nManufacturing, which accounts for 16.1 percent of GDP, grew at a more moderate 3.2 percent, slowing from 3.8 percent a year earlier.\n\nThe divergence was also evident within manufacturing. Wood products, non-metallic mineral products, furniture and machinery recorded double-digit growth, largely reflecting the revival in construction and domestic investment."
  },
