    "md5": "c0e7f2bdf6cef4d5dace2225882c7e98",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "The divergence was also evident within manufacturing. Wood products, non-metallic mineral products, furniture and machinery recorded double-digit growth, largely reflecting the revival in construction and domestic investment.\n\nThe growth pattern coincided with a sharp acceleration in imports. The total import expenditure increased 47.3 percent to Rs. 2.15 trillion during the quarter, while the import volumes rose 20.5 percent.\n\nThe consumer-goods import expenditure climbed 41.4 percent to Rs.515.42 billion, with the volumes surging 44.3 percent.\n\nThe export performance was considerably weaker. Although the export earnings increased 19.0 percent in rupee terms, the total export volumes grew only 1.8 percent. The industrial export volumes rose 2.5 percent, while the agricultural export volumes declined 2.6 percent.\n\nThe gap affirms that the recovery generated a much stronger increase in the import demand than in the export capacity, raising questions over its sustainability as Sri Lanka resumes foreign debt repayments and seeks to rebuild the external buffers.\n\nThe Central Bank had already warned in its July monetary policy review that the external current account had moved into deficit from April, mainly because the higher fuel import costs widened the merchandise trade deficit while the tourism earnings slowed.\n\nIt also observed that the demand conditions had strengthened and expected the monetary policy tightening undertaken in May, together with the other measures, to moderate the credit growth and demand pressures over the coming months.\n\nThe GDP data provide further evidence supporting that caution. Financial services expanded 7.7 percent during the quarter, with the total deposits and gross loans and advances growing 13.7 percent.\n\nHowever, the borrowing costs were also higher. The Average Weighted Lending Rate increased to 12.16 percent in 2Q26, from 11.56 percent a year earlier, while the Average Weighted Deposit Rate rose to 7.20 percent, from 6.93 percent."
