    "chunk_text": "However, the borrowing costs were also higher. The Average Weighted Lending Rate increased to 12.16 percent in 2Q26, from 11.56 percent a year earlier, while the Average Weighted Deposit Rate rose to 7.20 percent, from 6.93 percent.\n\nAgriculture presented the sharpest contrast to the industrial recovery, contracting 2.3 percent, after growing 2.5 percent a year earlier.\n\nRice production declined 15.1 percent, while marine fishing contracted 10.1 percent. Tea declined 1.9 percent, reflecting a 2.0 percent fall in made-tea production and weaker demand from the key markets, including Iran.\n\nThe agricultural weakness also carried direct cost-of-living implications. Freshwater fishing and aquaculture contracted 61.0 percent, while the producer and retail prices increased 48.7 percent and 44.9 percent, respectively.\n\nMarine fish production fell 10.0 percent, with the retail prices for coastal and deep-sea fish rising 14.4 percent and 13.2 percent.\n\nThe combination of reduced food production and stronger consumer demand adds to the inflation challenge. The GDP implicit price deflator increased 6.9 percent in the quarter, while the National Consumer Price Index rose 5.6 percent.\n\nServices, which account for more than half the economy, expanded 2.7 percent, slowing from 4.0 percent a year earlier.\n\nAccommodation, food and beverage services grew 2.9 percent, down sharply from a 12.0 percent growth in 2Q25.\n\nTourist arrivals fell to 405,939, from 445,768 a year earlier, a decline of nearly 9 percent, as the conflict in the Middle East disrupted travel and weakened the tourism earnings.\n\nTransportation grew 3.1 percent, supported by an 8.9 percent increase in port cargo and a 12.0 percent expansion in transport-support and warehousing activities. However, air passenger kilometres declined 15.8 percent and water transport contracted 6.1 percent."
