    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a3320eaae313a6106514ed064a043b28",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "These individuals cannot be genuine supporters of the SLPP. Instead, it seems that the event organisers simply brought in whoever they could find to fill the venue. The disruptive behaviour of these highly intoxicated crowds did absolutely nothing to restore the party\u2019s reputation.\n\nIn Namal\u2019s absence, organisers brought his father, former President Mahinda Rajapaksa, to the stage. The frail, elderly leader could neither walk nor stand properly, presenting a deeply pitiful sight. Witnessing this, many citizens took to social media to urge the SLPP to let the elderly leader live out his final days in peace, heavily criticising his children for exploiting their ailing father for political survival.\n\nThe speeches delivered at the rally explicitly exposed the inherent weakness and fragmentation of the opposition while none of the problems faced by the people were discussed.\n\nWhile National Freedom Front (NFF) leader Wimal Weerawansa, Pivithuru Hela Urumaya (PHU) leader Udaya Gammanpila, and Jaffna District Independent MP Ramanathan Archchuna attended the event, other groups like the Sri Lanka Freedom Party (SLFP), the New Democratic Front (NDF), and the United National Party (UNP) \u2014 all of whom had closely co-organised anti-government rallies with the SLPP in recent months\u2014had completely been excluded.\n\nWhen Namal Rajapaksa was arrested, the UNP issued an official statement condemning it as a political vendetta aimed at disrupting the SLPP rally. During the UNP\u2019s 80th annual convention on September 5, UNP leader Ranil Wickremesinghe even indicated that Namal is one of them, highlighting that the latter is the grandson of D.A. Rajapaksa, one of the UNP\u2019s founding members.\n\nThis marks a glaring contradiction: the initial investigations into the Airbus scandal were launched in 2015 under UNP-led government before being shelved. Now that the current administration has revived the case and executed the arrest, the UNP ironically decries it as political victimisation."
  },
  {
