    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2961685519a05afdf5b8d3898b775aaf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1649,
    "chunk_text": "Furthermore, this shows that if Mahinda Rajapaksa genuinely cared about resolving the ethnic problem when he was at the peak of his power,\u00a0 he could have succeeded, as his supporters who cheered a man who publicly claimed to be a \u201cTiger\u201d wouldn\u2019t have opposed any of the Tamil demands.\n\nUltimately, the rally intended to spark a massive opposition wave has instead backfired, exposing an alliance riddled with catastrophic ideological flaws and contradictions.\n\nThis issue is not confined to the SLPP. The wider opposition is trapped in a loop of contradictions and lack of direction. UNP oscillates between working with the SLPP and trying to forge a coalition with the Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB). Opposition figures like Dayasiri Jayasekara argue that they must form a grand coalition against the government by welcoming absolutely anyone\u2014corrupt individuals, financial fraudsters, or racists\u2014while simultaneously claiming that this alliance is necessary to fight government corruption.\n\nThe opposition operates entirely without a long-term plan, merely reacting to events as they happen. These opposition leaders formed a coalition called \u201cMaha Jana Handa\u201d (The Great People\u2019s Voice) on October 27th last year, followed by another rally in Nugegoda on November 21. They boastfully announced a nationwide campaign claiming that these were the beginning of a long struggle against the government, yet not a single subsequent rally materialised. Wickremesinghe in November last year promised to spearhead 1,000 rallies across the island, which also failed to happen. This is the pathetic reality of Sri Lanka\u2019s opposition today."
  },
