    "chunk_id": "2026-09-17-dailymirrorlk-fc13b7e3-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3fbf1f5b2b7bfe7933f444d30f0fbdbc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "I am thoroughly enjoying this season, I think probably a bit more than Season 1, because things are running a bit smoother now. The entire crew understands the production a lot better, and I believe it will just continue to get better as we go through.\n\nSeason 2\u2019s been good. It\u2019s different. If I talk about the contestants, there is a different attitude and a different type of person. They are outwardly more confident. In terms of cooking skills, some are at a similar level and some may be stronger, but it is a completely different group of human beings. Each one brings their own nuances. That is what makes good television, and we are trying to make a really good television show.\n\nSeason 1 was a trial. We went through lots of pain and hardship, but also lots of success. Season 2 now is solidifying MasterChef Sri Lanka on the global platform as something Sri Lanka can do as well, which is why I\u2019m here. In fact, other countries are now looking at Sri Lanka and wanting to bring their own teams here to produce and film, because they see what we\u2019ve built. I\u2019m very happy and proud to be part of this.\n\nQCould you explain how the challenge that featured pork was structured?\n\nPeter Kuruvita: The way the challenges work in MasterChef is that they are not simply the judges\u2019 decision. The challenge is developed by the production team and the food producer, and approved by the director. Sometimes we only see the challenge on the morning of filming, and sometimes it is a surprise. We knew about this challenge, but we also insisted that no one would be forced to use pork. That was something we knew from the beginning. The first question was, \u201cWhat are the other proteins?\u201d It was a Chinese challenge, and pork is commonly used in many Chinese dishes. Two days earlier, I had shown the contestants how to make a dozen different Chinese sauces, so the challenge followed from that lesson."
  },
