    "md5": "5a4f2c64dad2d88e74f3d961b3f380c3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "Peter Kuruvita: The way the challenges work in MasterChef is that they are not simply the judges\u2019 decision. The challenge is developed by the production team and the food producer, and approved by the director. Sometimes we only see the challenge on the morning of filming, and sometimes it is a surprise. We knew about this challenge, but we also insisted that no one would be forced to use pork. That was something we knew from the beginning. The first question was, \u201cWhat are the other proteins?\u201d It was a Chinese challenge, and pork is commonly used in many Chinese dishes. Two days earlier, I had shown the contestants how to make a dozen different Chinese sauces, so the challenge followed from that lesson.\n\nThe underneath of that mystery box had about 12 ingredients, and they only had to use six. So, it was their choice. We didn\u2019t say you have to use pork. There is no way in the world anyone was ever forced to do anything.\n\nQHow was Shabnam\u2019s dish assessed after she chose to use tofu?\n\nPeter Kuruvita: The reason Shabnam was at the bottom had nothing to do with her beliefs. It was because the dish did not meet the required standard. She would have been eliminated that day if another contestant had not served undercooked pork.\n\nIt is an international competition, and the ingredients were included as part of the challenge. Black pork curry is one of Sri Lanka\u2019s best known pork dishes, but we understand that some people eat pork and others do not. There was no issue in the judgment. We were firm because the dish did not meet the requirements. Shabnam tried to recreate a pork dish using tofu, and that approach did not work as intended. Tofu and eggs is a classic Chinese dish. She could have scrambled the tofu, made it with eggs and added one of the 12 sauces I taught her."
  },
