    "md5": "94cb26f7d8d887f947021556c3864219",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "It is an international competition, and the ingredients were included as part of the challenge. Black pork curry is one of Sri Lanka\u2019s best known pork dishes, but we understand that some people eat pork and others do not. There was no issue in the judgment. We were firm because the dish did not meet the requirements. Shabnam tried to recreate a pork dish using tofu, and that approach did not work as intended. Tofu and eggs is a classic Chinese dish. She could have scrambled the tofu, made it with eggs and added one of the 12 sauces I taught her.\n\nThis production includes people from different racial and religious backgrounds. There are 58 of us travelling to the Maldives, representing many parts of Sri Lankan society. The team includes Christians, Hindus, Muslims, Buddhists and atheists.\n\nWhat really upsets me is seeing comments saying it was because she wore a hijab. It had nothing to do with that. In Season 1, we had a similar situation, and the contestants worked around it and decided not to use the ingredient. As judges, we questioned it, but we were rightly corrected. It is an international show, and the range of ingredients has to remain broad.\n\nSavindri Perera: To add to that, MasterChef is an international franchise, and there are standards that we are bound to uphold, including culinary standards. At the same time, we understand that faith, religious beliefs, allergies and dietary requirements have to be considered. There is no single solution that will suit every contestant in every challenge.\n\nFor example, MasterChef Australia has featured Amina Elshafei and Samira El Khafir, Muslim women who wore hijabs during the competition. One of them was eliminated following a pork dish, not because of the ingredient itself, but because the execution failed. Narmadha did not use pork, produced a good dish and did not end up at the bottom."
  },
