    "chunk_size_bytes": 1962,
    "chunk_text": "Some people interpreted the comments as being insensitive to Muslim contestants. That was not our intention. It is important to understand how a production operates and the support that is available to contestants on set.\n\nQHow does MasterChef Sri Lanka accommodate contestants with different religious, cultural, dietary and medical requirements?\n\nPeter Kuruvita: We have three producers who translate every word on the cooking line for Tamil speaking contestants, so the audience does not see that support. We film for anywhere from eight to 14 hours a day. Contestants may need to sit down, rest or receive medical assistance, and we have a medical team there to support them. Contestants have prepared Jaffna dishes, biryani and wattalapam, and we have also had vegetarian challenges. We have tried to cover the full spectrum.\n\nQWhat would you like viewers to remember when discussing the contestants and their decisions?\n\nSavindri Perera: I think what people need to understand is that there is no agenda. The intention is to put out a good show and look after our contestants.\n\nPeter Kuruvita: That is something I really want everyone to understand. Please stop being mean to our contestants. They come from many different backgrounds, and online bullying can have a serious effect on them and their families. People may accept what they read on social media as the truth, and that can create real problems in their lives.\n\nQWhat do you hope MasterChef Sri Lanka will ultimately achieve?\n\nPeter Kuruvita: We are travelling around the world. A couple of days ago it was France, and now we are going to the Maldives. We want to put out a great show, showcase our culture and show what Sri Lanka is capable of doing on an international stage.\n\nSavindri Perera: It\u2019s an inclusive programme where every community, every faith, and every region of Sri Lanka can bring their food to the contest and share it with the world. That is what MasterChef is all about."
