    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a54a5906fb78d8007af2cd92b976b761",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI\u2019m a Tiger but not an LTTE Tiger\u201d - Archchuna\n\nColombo, September 17 (Daily Mirror) - Jaffna District MP Ramanathan Archchuna defended his use of the word \u201ctiger\u201d, saying he would not consider it an offence to identify himself with the animal, while denying that he had referred to a \u201cTiger diaspora\u201d.\n\n\u201cIf someone asks me whether I am a tiger, I accept it. My favourite animal is the tiger. Some people like elephants and some like snakes; I like tigers,\u201d Archchuna said.\n\nHe said that he had never referred to an \u201cLTTE diaspora\u201d and rejected suggestions that his reference to a tiger indicated support for the LTTE.\n\n\u201cEven my personal mobile phone background image is a tiger. Sinhala people say they are lions. We never say that saying \u2018lion\u2019 is wrong,\u201d he said.\n\nArchchuna said he had only referred to the \u201cdiaspora\u201d while explaining that the term included Sri Lankan Tamils, Muslims and Sinhalese living overseas.\n\n\u201cI never said the word \u2018Tiger diaspora\u2019. I only said diaspora. Our President may not understand English,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said his reference was to funds received from Sri Lankans living overseas for the country\u2019s rebuilding programme.\n\n\u201cThe country received funds for rebuilding Sri Lanka. Those funds were received from the diaspora. That was why I said the government took money from the diaspora and is deceiving us,\u201d he said.\n\nArchchuna also questioned whether his comments could reasonably be interpreted as support for the LTTE.\n\n\u201cDo you think I use the LTTE tiger logo, join the LTTE and think about taking up weapons again? I am not like that,\u201d he said.\n\nThe MP, who identified himself as a doctor, said he had served in hospitals including Kalubowila and Peradeniya and had treated Sinhala patients throughout his career.\n\n\u201cI have been a doctor for the past 40 years. If I represented the LTTE, I would not have been able to provide medicine to Sinhala people,\u201d he said."
  },
