    "language": "en",
    "md5": "737f6074c99ae15a581beee04278dfbf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1786,
    "chunk_text": "e) Supporting Sri Lanka\u2019s national commitment to achieving 70% renewable electricity generation by 2030 and carbon neutrality in the electricity sector by 2050.\n\nf) Significantly minimising the disruptive effects of El Ni\u00f1o on Sri Lanka\u2019s energy sector.\n\nSri Lanka, like many nations in the Indian Ocean, is highly vulnerable to climate variability. Monsoon shifts, droughts, and cyclones can disrupt electricity generation and place immense strain on national infrastructure. In such circumstances, BESS is not merely a technical solution it is a resilience strategy.\n\nStored energy can keep hospitals functioning during blackouts, ensure schools remain open, and power emergency services when conventional generation falters. It can provide backup capacity during droughts that reduce hydropower output, or during storms that damage transmission lines.\n\nBy investing early, Sri Lanka positions itself as a climate-resilient island nation, setting an example for other Asian countries facing similar vulnerabilities. From Bangladesh\u2019s floodplains to the typhoon-prone Philippines, the lesson is clear: storage is not optional; it is essential. The catastrophic Himalayan flash floods that struck Nepal and Tibet in August 2026 triggered by the collapse of a glacier above the Lhende river on the Tibetan side, which dammed the waterway before the surge tore through border settlements in Nepal\u2019s Rasuwa district and into Tibet\u2019s Gyirong Port killed hundreds and left thousands more missing across both sides of the border, with scientists linking the collapse to warming-driven destabilisation of mountain permafrost and Nepal\u2019s own climate officials and foreign minister explicitly attributing the disaster to climate change and calling for a coordinated global response."
  },
