    "md5": "35e2cc6850eeb3d90f4c48ee5b234ff2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "By investing early, Sri Lanka positions itself as a climate-resilient island nation, setting an example for other Asian countries facing similar vulnerabilities. From Bangladesh\u2019s floodplains to the typhoon-prone Philippines, the lesson is clear: storage is not optional; it is essential. The catastrophic Himalayan flash floods that struck Nepal and Tibet in August 2026 triggered by the collapse of a glacier above the Lhende river on the Tibetan side, which dammed the waterway before the surge tore through border settlements in Nepal\u2019s Rasuwa district and into Tibet\u2019s Gyirong Port killed hundreds and left thousands more missing across both sides of the border, with scientists linking the collapse to warming-driven destabilisation of mountain permafrost and Nepal\u2019s own climate officials and foreign minister explicitly attributing the disaster to climate change and calling for a coordinated global response.\n\nEnergy security is inseparable from economic security. Sri Lanka\u2019s dependence on imported fuels exposes the nation to international price volatility and foreign exchange pressures.\u00a0 In 2025, fuel imports accounted for 20\u201322% of total merchandise imports, costing the country USD 4 billion annually (World Bank, 2025).\u00a0 Today, this vulnerability is even more pronounced.\u00a0 Global crude oil prices remain elevated, with Brent trading near $108 per barrel and WTI at $102.78 per barrel (September 14, 2026). WTI stands for West Texas Intermediate a grade of crude oil that serves as a major global benchmark for oil pricing, and is often used as a reference point in energy sector analysis, including discussions on fuel costs, power generation economics, and broader energy security. This sharp rise underscores the urgency of reducing reliance on imported fuels and highlights the strategic importance of the newly commissioned Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) in Galle."
