    "md5": "2616974af6a34ef58fe70263acfd6db2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1884,
    "chunk_text": "Energy security is inseparable from economic security. Sri Lanka\u2019s dependence on imported fuels exposes the nation to international price volatility and foreign exchange pressures.\u00a0 In 2025, fuel imports accounted for 20\u201322% of total merchandise imports, costing the country USD 4 billion annually (World Bank, 2025).\u00a0 Today, this vulnerability is even more pronounced.\u00a0 Global crude oil prices remain elevated, with Brent trading near $108 per barrel and WTI at $102.78 per barrel (September 14, 2026). WTI stands for West Texas Intermediate a grade of crude oil that serves as a major global benchmark for oil pricing, and is often used as a reference point in energy sector analysis, including discussions on fuel costs, power generation economics, and broader energy security. This sharp rise underscores the urgency of reducing reliance on imported fuels and highlights the strategic importance of the newly commissioned Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) in Galle.\n\nBy reducing fuel imports, Sri Lanka also strengthens its balance of payments and shields its economy from external shocks creating a more attractive environment for foreign investment and reinforcing the virtuous cycle of growth and innovation.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s energy transition carries profound geopolitical significance. In the Indian Ocean, energy security is inseparable from maritime trade and strategic competition. By reducing dependence on imported fuels, Sri Lanka strengthens its sovereignty and reduces exposure to external shocks.\n\nThe vision must extend beyond national borders. South Asia is one of the fastest-growing energy markets in the world, with energy demand projected to grow at 6% annually, while fossil fuels still account for 70% of the region\u2019s primary energy supply (International Energy Agency, 2025). This imbalance underscores the urgency of scalable renewable solutions."
