    "language": "en",
    "md5": "659afeae494171ac3bee7670024ce8f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1727,
    "chunk_text": "Q The UN has consistently pressured Sri Lanka to repeal the PTA. The incumbent government has instead proposed the Protection of State against Terrorism Act. What are your thoughts regarding this new Act?\n\nQ The report also mentions excavations at the Chemmani mass grave and states that it is a critical test for justice. Your thoughts?\n\nThey invited Volker T\u00fcrk to the country, took him to Jaffna, showed him how the excavations are taking place. There were 583 sets of skeletons that were discovered. All this was done to establish a rapport with the High Commissioner for Human Rights. so that he would understand the situation and the government briefed him fully. Then a proposal was brought before the UN General Assembly in New York to extend the term of office of the high commissioner for another three years. 144 countries supported the high commissioner, 10 countries opposed and 13 countries abstained. Sri Lanka was one among the 13 countries that abstained. Where is the consistency in our foreign policy? We invite him, show him Chemmani, establish a rapport with him and then we don\u2019t support him when his term is being extended. Do we know what we are doing? Is there any compatibility of our actions from day to day? There seems to be some doubt as to whether there was a deliberate decision by the foreign affairs minister to withhold support from Volker T\u00fcrk or whether there was some failure in communication. Is it that we just didn\u2019t focus on it and take a decision? But now, look at the consequences.\n\nQ The High Commissioner\u2019s report urged Sri Lanka to establish an independent prosecutor\u2019s office separate from the Attorney General\u2019s Department. What do you have to say about this demand?"
