    "chunk_text": "It is an age-old problem and it\u2019s not something that can be fixed overnight. There\u2019s no need to reinvent the wheel. There\u2019s so much material that is available. There have been so many commissions, committees and so on which have done valuable work. What is required is a fresh evaluation of the abundant material that is already in existence. Not to add one more leaf to the thicket, but to make a comprehensive appraisal of the already existing material. That\u2019s what the government promised to do. But nothing has happened.\n\nQThe government is determined to pass the controversial 22nd Amendment. But you have been quite critical about this move.\n\nYes. We very badly need investments in our country. Thanks to what Ranil Wickremesinghe did when he was President. The negotiations which he carried out with the IMF there is some degree of stability with regards to the economy. But there\u2019s no growth because there are no investments. Serious investors including foreign banks, foreign companies would consider the independence of the judiciary among other factors; about how credible and reliable the judiciary is. Because when disputes arise they want to take those disputes to an impartial body. If there are doubts with regards to the integrity and objectivity of the judiciary there could be a strong disincentive to investments. The 22A among other deleterious consequences will discourage serious foreign investors from coming into the country.\n\nQ The High Commissioner\u2019s report also refers to arbitrary arrests, torture, detentions and custodial deaths which portray a negative picture about the country\u2019s human rights situation. What are your comments?"
