    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "WIM puts talent, workplaces and women-led enterprise at heart of global competitiveness\n\nBusiness & Leadership Night 2026 brings three platforms under one roof\n\nDr. Segera calls for shift from academic credentials to skills, innovation and execution\n\n\u201cSri Lanka must move beyond academic qualifications and domestic recognition to build the skills, leadership, innovation and international exposure needed to compete in the global economy,\u201d Women in Management Global (WIM) Founder and Chairperson Dr. Sulochana Segera said.\n\nShe made the observation at the launch of WIM \u2013 The Business & Leadership Night 2026, which will bring together three flagship platforms focusing on women-led enterprise, next-generation leadership and responsible workplaces on 9 December.\n\nThe landmark event will unite the second Women MSME Awards 2026, the Respectful Workplace Audit & Certification and the second NextTier Mentorship & Awards 2026, as WIM seeks to connect business recognition with leadership development and internationally aligned workplace practices.\n\nSegera said Sri Lanka needed to rethink how it develops and positions its human capital, arguing that academic qualifications alone were no longer sufficient in a rapidly changing business environment.\n\n\u201cA degree is important, but a degree alone cannot determine success in today\u2019s global economy, stressing that skills, discipline, innovation, leadership, technological adaptability, international exposure and the ability to execute were becoming increasingly important.\n\nShe said Sri Lanka must also examine whether its relatively educated population was being effectively translated into greater economic productivity and global competitiveness.\n\n\u201cIt is time for Sri Lanka to move beyond simply participating in the global economy and start competing,\u201d Segera stressed."
  },
