    "md5": "322e788a6c7a9a6b727c30ffe0f18b35",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "She said Sri Lanka must also examine whether its relatively educated population was being effectively translated into greater economic productivity and global competitiveness.\n\n\u201cIt is time for Sri Lanka to move beyond simply participating in the global economy and start competing,\u201d Segera stressed.\n\nSegera said the Business and Leadership Night was an extension of this ambition, bringing entrepreneurship, leadership development and responsible workplace practices together while providing greater visibility for Sri Lankan talent and corporate excellence.\n\nShe also called for greater economic participation by women, particularly given their strong representation within higher education, arguing that Sri Lanka could not afford to leave a significant segment of its talent outside productive economic activity.\n\n\u201cIf we don\u2019t get them into economic participation, we can\u2019t move forward,\u201d she said.\n\nHowever, Segera stressed that advancing women should not be treated solely as a women\u2019s issue and called for greater involvement from men, businesses and other stakeholders.\n\n\u201cLeadership has no gender. It\u2019s the way we approach it,\u201d she noted.\n\nNextTier Mentorship & Awards Chairman Dr. Rohantha Athukorala said rapid technological and economic changes were fundamentally altering traditional employment and career pathways, increasing the importance of mentorship and continuous skills development.\n\nAthukorala pointed particularly to artificial intelligence, which he said was already transforming how businesses operate, market themselves and access information.\n\nHe said the changing environment meant both young people entering employment and established professionals needed to continuously reassess their capabilities and understand where new opportunities were emerging.\n\nNextTier seeks to address this gap by exposing participants to areas including AI, analytics, FinTech, project management, recruitment, leadership, management and macroeconomics."
  },
