    "md5": "d9ebd328ed3ae5b412ca677f4e124dbe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1235,
    "chunk_text": "The Government's long-term goal is to make care an important part of national plans, programmes and budgets, while reducing the burden on families and improving women's access to employment and economic opportunities.\n\nSri Lanka has strengthened its commitment through regional cooperation, including participation earlier this year in the SAARC Transform Care South Asia Dialogue in Nepal, organised by SAARC and UN Women with the support of the Republic of Korea.\n\nThe dialogue contributed to a regional Action Plan on transforming care systems.\n\nThe Deputy Minister said countries in the region could learn from each other's experiences in areas such as childcare, eldercare, disability inclusion, public investment, data systems and social attitudes.\n\n\u201cNo country has all the answers,\u201d she said, stressing the importance of regional cooperation in finding practical solutions.\n\nShe said Sri Lanka was ready to work with the Republic of Korea, UN Women and regional partners to strengthen care systems.\n\n\u201cIf care makes our economies possible, sustains our families, and advances women\u2019s empowerment, then the question before us is not whether we can afford to invest in care. It is whether we can afford not to,\u201d she said."
