    "md5": "539df3651df8861c96124e09fcd87c7a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018No dogs left behind\u2019 key to ending rabies: Vet\n\nColombo, September 18 (Daily Mirror) - Eradicating human rabies in Sri Lanka would be impossible without eliminating animal-mediated rabies, Veterinary Surgeon and Animal Welfare Coalition (AWC) Executive Member Dr. Chamith Nanayakkara said, challenging concerns raised by the Public Health Inspectors\u2019 (PHI) Union over the proposed new rabies legislation.\n\nResponding to claims that the proposed Elimination of Rabies and Registration of Dogs Bill could disrupt the country\u2019s dog vaccination programme, Dr. Nanayakkara said Sri Lanka was approaching the end of the 73rd year of its national rabies eradication programme, which began in 1953, yet vaccination coverage remained around 50 per cent.\n\nHe said dog bites accounted for nearly 95 per cent of animal-mediated rabies cases and that 20 human deaths were recorded due to rabies in 2024.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara questioned why the national rabies eradication programme remained under the Health Ministry when vaccination coverage had failed to reach the 70 per cent threshold recommended by the World Health Organization (WHO).\n\nHe said the responsibility for controlling other zoonotic diseases remained with the Department of Animal Production and Health (DAPH), while effective control of the canine population was essential to maintaining adequate vaccination coverage.\n\nAccording to him, the disruption of the national sterilisation programme during the past three years had further aggravated the problem.\n\nHe also criticised the expenditure on post-exposure treatment for people bitten by animals, saying nearly Rs. 1 billion in public funds was being spent on such treatment without sufficiently involving the nearly 700 DAPH veterinary surgeons stationed at regional veterinary offices to address the source of the problem."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
