    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a885b1f11cf0437e5128897212319c94",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1172,
    "chunk_text": "He further alleged that the Health Ministry had not updated its database with vaccination and sterilisation figures from that period despite repeated reminders, while continuing to attribute shortcomings to the DAPH.\n\nAt the same time, Dr. Nanayakkara acknowledged the contribution of PHIs to rabies control at grassroots level.\n\nHe said the proposed system was based on the One Health approach, which seeks to bring together human health, animal health and environmental interventions.\n\nUnder the proposed arrangement, veterinarians would have primary responsibility for eliminating animal-mediated rabies, while PHIs could continue educating pet owners and property owners about the importance of vaccinating dogs kept on private and public premises, including offices and schools.\n\nThe proposed Bill also places responsibilities on local authorities for dog registration and the management of stray dogs, including sterilisation and vaccination measures.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara\u2019s comments come amid an ongoing debate over how responsibilities for rabies prevention should be divided among the Health Ministry, veterinary authorities, local government institutions and PHIs."
  },
