    "description": "England crush Sri Lanka to seal T20 series win",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/England-crush-Sri-Lanka-to-seal-T20-series-win/108-350558",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789702801.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-18-dailymirrorlk-c81b983e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "99f04783225dd1cc81431e369026f585",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "England crush Sri Lanka to seal T20 series win\n\nBBC - England's impressive T20 form continued as they thrashed Sri Lanka by six wickets in Cardiff to seal the series with a match to spare.\n\nTom Banton's unbeaten 52 ensured England made light work of chasing a below-par 146, reaching the target in just 12.1 overs.\n\nJos Buttler and Aneurin Donald raced to an opening stand of 51 in 4.3 overs before they both fell in the space of four balls for 30 and 21 respectively, but England's middle order followed suit in their aggression.\n\nCaptain Harry Brook needed to score 49 to break Tilak Varma's record of 336 T20 international runs without being dismissed, and he looked on course, whacking three sixes in his 23 off just eight balls, before he was brilliantly caught by a diving Eshan Malinga.\n\nBanton then took on the charge, clubbing four sixes in his 24-ball knock as England remain unbeaten in the shortest format this summer.\n\nBrendon McCullum's side have now won 22 of their past 25 completed T20s since the start of last summer - a win percentage of 88%, with India (73%) the next best among full-member nations.\n\nEngland's fluency juxtaposed Sri Lanka's innings as the tourist batters struggled again, limping to 145-9 after being bowled out for just 135 in the opener on Tuesday.\n\nThere will be added frustration from this innings, though, as their openers started so brightly by reaching 55-0 from the first five overs.\n\nBut Liam Dawson took two wickets in the final over of the powerplay to halt their momentum and the middle order failed to recover, losing 9-90 thereafter and adding just 12 from a dire final four overs.\n\nDawson finished with 3-32 and Jamie Overton 2-23, while Sonny Baker impressed again with his pace and control in a miserly spell of 1-15, including a wicket maiden in the 19th over.\n\nThe series concludes at Old Trafford on Saturday and is followed by three one-day internationals."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
