    "md5": "03b919f44faa15440d220cf7a364f768",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "Born after 2010; No tobacco for life?\n\nColombo, September 18 (Daily Mirror) - A proposal to create Sri Lanka\u2019s first Tobacco-Free Generation could prevent everyone born from 2010 onwards from ever legally purchasing tobacco, as health advocates warn of the long-term human and economic cost of tobacco use.\n\nThe proposal, backed by the Alcohol and Drug Information Centre (ADIC), would permanently prohibit the sale of tobacco products to people born on or after January 1, 2010. Unlike the current age-based restrictions, the proposed policy would follow the generation throughout its lifetime.\n\n\u201cEstablishing a Tobacco-Free Generation to prevent the sale of tobacco products to individuals born after 2010\u201d is among the tobacco-control measures recommended by ADIC.\n\nIf introduced, those born in 2010 would turn 21 in 2031 without gaining the legal right to buy cigarettes or other tobacco products. The protected group would then expand with each succeeding generation.\n\nHowever, Executive Director Sampath De Seram told the Daily Mirror that the mechanism to identify whether a person was born after 2010, which will be introduced by the Health Ministry, will come into effect in 2031.\n\nHe said a mechanism would be introduced to identify such persons at the point of sale by checking their identity documents or through any other technical facility to be announced by that time.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Medical Association (SLMA) first formally proposed the policy to the Health Minister in 2024 and resubmitted it in 2025. In 2026, the National Authority on Tobacco and Alcohol (NATA), together with the SLMA, again submitted the proposal to the Minister.\n\nADIC said stronger and more progressive tobacco-control measures were needed to protect present and future generations from tobacco-related harm. In its 2026 campaign, the organization highlighted the Tobacco-Free Generation policy alongside measures including stronger taxation, plain packaging and safeguards against tobacco industry interference."
  },
