    "md5": "1b8c3c962bee20baaeab217453e80f99",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "ADIC said stronger and more progressive tobacco-control measures were needed to protect present and future generations from tobacco-related harm. In its 2026 campaign, the organization highlighted the Tobacco-Free Generation policy alongside measures including stronger taxation, plain packaging and safeguards against tobacco industry interference.\n\nADIC\u2019s latest tobacco-industry interference report, meanwhile, cites National Authority on Tobacco and Alcohol figures putting annual tobacco-related deaths at approximately 20,000, while ADIC surveys have estimated daily cigarette expenditure at around Rs. 520 million.\n\nADIC has also warned that tobacco industry efforts to attract young people can contribute to the creation of new consumers. Its tobacco trend survey has identified the need for measures to prevent or delay smoking initiation, including the implementation of a Tobacco-Free Generation.\n\nThe proposed policy is based on the idea that preventing new generations from entering the tobacco market could gradually reduce tobacco use and its associated health costs rather than relying solely on helping established smokers quit.\n\nSri Lanka already prohibits tobacco sales to people under 21 under the National Authority on Tobacco and Alcohol Act. NATA says its mandate includes discouraging tobacco use, particularly among children, by restricting access to tobacco products.\n\nThe proposal would therefore represent a major shift from an age-based restriction to a birth-year-based restriction, with its impact growing as each new generation reaches adulthood.\n\nInternationally, the Maldives has adopted a generational tobacco restriction, while the United Kingdom has also legislated for restrictions based on date of birth. Other countries have considered similar \u201csmoke-free generation\u201d measures."
