    "md5": "87bc7a9ad7f88785c26bb24b2724f070",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Turning to the link between tariffs and geopolitics, he observed that the general consensus was that the US tariff regime was shaped not only by domestic compulsions, such as revenue generation and helping American manufacturers compete, but also by a carrot-and-stick approach.\n\nVietnam, meanwhile, faced a 40 percent surcharge linked to transshipment activity. The surcharge was not levied after Vietnam agreed to a rigorous supervision mechanism, with direct consent from the US side, to prevent goods produced in other countries from being transshipped through Vietnam to the US market.\n\nSamarasinghe also highlighted the US decision to negotiate on a one-on-one basis rather than within multilateral frameworks. From the start of the talks, it was made clear that the WTO and other multilateral trade agreements Sri Lanka had signed were not relevant to the tariff negotiations, he recalled.\n\nOn supply chains, he drew attention to Bangladesh\u2019s agreement with the US, under which Bangladesh would receive a zero-rated quota, against the 10 percent it currently pays, if it shifted to sourcing raw materials such as cotton and fibre from the US.\n\n\u201cThat was very attractive, and this is a clause that Sri Lanka also would like to have because Bangladesh is one of our strongest competitors,\u201d he said, adding that Sri Lanka wanted to retain its competitive position against rival exporters.\n\nMany countries secured favourable rates by committing to major purchases and investments in the US. One East Asian country, likely Japan, agreed to buy US$ 8 billion worth of US agricultural products and, for the first time in its history, allowed US rice imports. Another, likely South Korea, agreed to invest billions of dollars in revitalising the US shipbuilding industry, while a third, likely Taiwan, agreed to shift semiconductor production to the US."
  },
