    "md5": "45e0ebbe2faa7f9838e08800544c4844",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "No more home visits; Ex-Presidents, politicians must report to investigators\n\nColombo, Sep. 18 (Daily Mirror) - Public Security Minister Sunil Watagala said even former Executive Presidents and senior politicians were now required to appear before law enforcement agencies when summoned for questioning, instead of having investigators visit their residences to record statements.\n\nHe said the change reflected the Government\u2019s commitment to ensuring that the law was applied equally to everyone, regardless of political status or position.\n\nWatagala said the Government had taken firm steps to ensure the independence of state institutions and uphold the rule of law, attributing recent law enforcement successes to the depoliticization of key institutions.\n\nHe said institutions including the Criminal Investigation Department (CID), Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC), Attorney General\u2019s Department and the Judiciary were now able to carry out their duties independently without political interference.\n\nHe said the Government had established a political authority that would not interfere with these institutions, enabling public officials to perform their duties independently and effectively.\n\nWatagala said the equal application of the law was a key political commitment of the Government, particularly in dealing with corruption and the alleged theft of state property.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka had moved away from a system where the application of the law was influenced by political status, party affiliations or personal connections.\n\nReferring to Article 12(1) of the Constitution, Watagala said every person was entitled to equal protection of the law.\n\nHe said there had been a significant change in the way investigations were conducted, noting that powerful individuals could no longer expect investigators to visit their homes to obtain statements simply because of their positions."
  },
