    "chunk_size_bytes": 2006,
    "chunk_text": "Rail strike ends tonight amid Rs.60m loss; unions warn of fresh action\n\nColombo, Sep. 18 (Daily Mirror) - The two-day railway strike is scheduled to end at midnight today, but railway trade unions warned that they could launch further industrial action if their grievances remain unresolved, claiming the strike has already caused losses exceeding Rs.60 million.\n\nThe Railway Station Masters\u2019 Association and other unions told a press conference that there was still no satisfactory solution to their demands, despite the disruption caused to railway services across the country.\n\nThey said only around 90 train services were scheduled to operate today, with several of them already cancelled, including six services on the Upcountry route and a number of regional trains.\n\nThe unions estimated that the Railway Department had incurred more than Rs.40 million in losses during the two-day strike, while the overall loss was said to have exceeded Rs.60 million.\n\nThey claimed that only two of Sri Lanka\u2019s 196 railway stations\u2014Veyangoda and Ragama\u2014had issued tickets during the strike, despite efforts by the authorities to maintain services.\n\nAccording to the unions, ticket issuing, parcel transportation and seat reservation services were not carried out at the remaining railway stations.\n\nThe unions said their demand for the removal of the Railway General Manager was linked to the daily cancellation of train services and the failure to provide lasting solutions to technical problems affecting railway operations.\n\nThey said railway authorities had been working continuously for the past 48 hours to maintain train operations, adding that the General Manager had now become directly involved in addressing the situation.\n\n\u201cIf work is carried out like this every day, the railway service can function properly,\u201d they said.\n\nRejecting allegations that the strike was politically motivated, the unions said they would not be deterred by criticism from trade unions supporting the Government."
  },
