    "md5": "8bcd542712ffcce965121877640d29c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "England: Harry Brook (c), Ben Duckett, James Coles, Will Jacks, Gus Atkinson, Liam Dawson, Jamie Overton, Tom Banton, Jos Buttler, Jordan Cox, Aneurin Donald, Jofra Archer, Sonny Baker, Saqib Mahmood, Josh Tongue, Adil Rashid.\n\nWith England chasing a series clean sweep and Sri Lanka aiming to avoid a whitewash, the final T20I presents both sides with contrasting objectives at Old Trafford.\n\nEngland fast bowler Brydon Carse will face no further police action after an investigation into an alleged assault in Derby was closed.\n\nDerbyshire Police confirmed that the investigation into an alleged assault in Derby city centre was dropped following an incident in the early hours of August 23. Carse was initially arrested on suspicion of being drunk and disorderly before being de-arrested after officers took his details.\n\nHowever, Carse will remain subject to an investigation by the Cricket Regulator. The 31-year-old was previously left out of England\u2019s squad for the third Test against Pakistan and the white-ball series against Sri Lanka following the incident.\n\nThe England and Wales Cricket Board confirmed that Carse remains available for selection, while head coach Brendon McCullum said the fast bowler needs to \u201cget his house in order\u201d before returning to international cricket.\n\nThe BCCI has authorised its office-bearers to decide whether to extend Ajit Agarkar\u2019s tenure as chairman of selectors or appoint a replacement following its 95th Annual General Meeting (AGM) on Friday.\n\nAgarkar\u2019s current contract is set to expire on October 4, with the board also looking to fill five vacancies on the junior selection committee.\n\n\u201cThe office-bearers have been authorised to take decisions on matters concerning the selection committees,\u201d a senior BCCI official said after the meeting."
