    "md5": "2651e4834447dc5943169c71d633764a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "New Zealand and Sri Lanka explore new education partnerships to build skilled workforce\n\nNew Zealand and Sri Lanka have taken an important step towards building stronger education and workforce development ties, with senior education leaders, government representatives and industry stakeholders from both countries coming together in Colombo to explore new models of collaboration that support skills development, graduate employability and economic growth.\n\nHosted by Education New Zealand Manapou ki te Ao (ENZ), the Applied Learning and Industry Connect event marked the first phase of a broader strategy to establish sustainable institutional partnerships and grow transnational education opportunities between New Zealand and Sri Lanka.\n\nThe event, held in Colombo at the Shangri-La Hotel on 24 August, focused on identifying practical partnership opportunities that strengthen workforce capability, enhance trainer capacity and support the delivery of high-quality, industry-relevant education and training.\n\nDavid Pine, New Zealand High Commissioner to Sri Lanka, delivered the inaugural address, reaffirming New Zealand\u2019s commitment to strengthening education partnerships that contribute to skills development, innovation and economic prosperity.\n\nThe event comes at a significant time for Sri Lanka as it advances reforms across its higher education and Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) sectors to better align skills development with national economic priorities.\n\nParticipants from both countries examined vocational education systems, shared best practice in applied learning and industry engagement, and explored pathways for collaboration in programme delivery, trainer capability development and workforce readiness.\n\nDiscussions were supported by expert presentations, facilitated roundtables and networking sessions, including contributions from the Asian Development Bank on Sri Lanka\u2019s evolving skills ecosystem and opportunities for international collaboration."
  },
