    "chunk_size_bytes": 1843,
    "chunk_text": "Houthis at wailing gate:  Iran war\u2019s turning point\n\nIn 1991, when Saddam Hussein\u2019s Iraq came under attack from United States\u2011led coalition forces, some 120 Iraqi fighter jets landed in Iran to avoid being hit. Iran did not return the aircraft. After all, it was Iraq that wronged Iran by launching the nine-year war that caused the deaths of nearly 600,000 Iranians.\n\nAs though the surprise gift was not enough, Iran saw pro\u2011Iranian Shiites capturing government power in elections held under 2003 US occupation and afterward. Call it divine reward or natural justice \u2014 Iran stood to gain. Iraq the enemy became Iraq the friend.\n\nFast forward to 2026. Iran was wronged once again when the US-Israeli combined force launched a surprise attack on Iran on February 28. And once again it stands to gain from a development in a direction it least expected to yield such a military advantage as it has now gained in the lightning battleground victories of Yemen\u2019s pro\u2011Iranian Ansarallah group led by the Houthis. Iran\u2019s position in its war against the far superior military might of the US has been reinforced by Houthi victories. The Houthis now control the Bab al\u2011Mandab waterway and the strategically crucial nearby islands in the Red Sea. They have captured the Red sea city of Mocha and are advancing in other directions.\n\nIn asymmetrical wars, the unexpected happens. Battlefield conditions can turn in favour of the underdog.\n\nSuch battlefield surprises neither the US nor Israel seemed to have taken into consideration when they rushed to attack Iran and triggered a war without giving much thought to adverse global ramifications that could ensue. Oil prices, particularly diesel, have been skyrocketing since the fall of Bab al\u2011Mandab, or the Gate of Tears. True to its meaning, the whole world wails over rising prices."
  },
