    "chunk_text": "Robert Knox: Chronicler par excellence\n\nThe quip that \u201conce the mud is washed off, the man working in a paddy field is fit to be king\u201d which famously evolved into the colloquial paraphrase, did not originate from a Lankan familiar with local parlance. It came instead from Robert Knox, an Englishman, who spent nineteen years as a prisoner in the then Kandyan kingdom before escaping and writing his celebrated work \u201cAn Historical Relation of the island Ceylon.\u201d It noted that \u201ctaking a ploughman from Udunuwara or Yatinuwara and washing the dirt off would make him fit for kingship.\u201d\n\nAlthough he limited the leadership potential of the farmer to two specific regions, Knox\u2019s remark has endured for centuries. In fact, it re-echoed recently in Parliament when the mounting crisis of\u00a0 paddy cultivators ignited a heated debate illustrating the continued usage of Knox\u2019s quote.\n\nPublished in London in 1681, \u201cAn Historical Relation of the island Ceylon,\u201d was the oldest major English language book which broadened the comprehension of the\u00a0 Lankan way of life. It provided European readers with an unprecedented insight into Sri Lankan society, culture and the economy during the seventeenth century long before the British established control over the island.\n\nWhat makes Knox\u2019s episode particularly significant is that his captivity\u00a0 \u00a0which led him to write the book was linked with a major turning point in Lankan history. In fact, if not for the unforeseen turn of events\u00a0 that occurred which made the reigning King, Rajasinghe 11 (1629-1687CE) ill-tempered, \u201cincreasingly moody, suspicious and tyrannical and caused him to develop a passion to imprison Europeans,\u201d Knox would not have become a victim of royal wrath, and circumstances would not have led him to write the celebrated book."
