    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "What makes Knox\u2019s episode particularly significant is that his captivity\u00a0 \u00a0which led him to write the book was linked with a major turning point in Lankan history. In fact, if not for the unforeseen turn of events\u00a0 that occurred which made the reigning King, Rajasinghe 11 (1629-1687CE) ill-tempered, \u201cincreasingly moody, suspicious and tyrannical and caused him to develop a passion to imprison Europeans,\u201d Knox would not have become a victim of royal wrath, and circumstances would not have led him to write the celebrated book.\n\nAs to his fate, Knox, in his autobiography recalled: \u201cThe Ann being hit by a storm, was forced ashore\u2026..that fatal voyage in which I lost my father and myself and the prime of my time\u2026\u2026\u201d\u00a0 Knox\u2019s father and the crew of sixteen sailors were awaiting repairs in the Kottiyar Bay in the east coast not knowing that they had arrived during a period of political turmoil. An ambitious strategy employed by King Rajasinghe to expel the Portuguese from areas under their rule had misfired. The failure of his political gamble resulted in a major transition in\u00a0 Lankan political history. The move in fact, led\u00a0 Portugese rule in Sri Lanka\u2019s coastal area to be replaced by the Dutch.\n\nThe unexpected development in which the Dutch kept the Portuguese territory they won instead of handing them over to the King as promised, transformed the King to a \u201cdisgruntled terror\u201d as Knox described. The new development led people to coin the phrase \u201cgiving ginger and receiving chillies in return.\u201d\n\nKnox in his book described the situation vividly:\u00a0 \u201cGreat vexation they gave the King by their irruptions into his Dominions. Great mischief they did him, the often times great loss on their sides. Great battles have been lost and won between them with great destruction of men on both parts. But being greatly distressed, at last he (Rajasinghe) sent and called in the Hollanders to his aid.\u201d"
