    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "93edf5306d3bb45fbfbff2547d86147b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "Developing climate-resilient crop varieties is one of the highest priorities. Future crops must tolerate drought, heat, salinity, flooding and emerging pest pressures. Advances in genomics, biotechnology, and molecular breeding provide unprecedented opportunities to accelerate crop improvement.\n\nEqually important is the restoration of soil health. Healthy soils improve water retention, increase carbon sequestration, enhance nutrient availability, and provide resilience against climatic extremes. Practices such as conservation agriculture, composting, agroforestry, and integrated nutrient management can contribute substantially to sustainable food production.\n\nAnother promising area is biofortification\u2014the development of crop varieties with enhanced nutritional value. Recent research has identified wheat varieties capable of accumulating significantly higher concentrations of zinc in grain. Understanding the genetic and physiological mechanisms controlling nutrient uptake and grain loading offers opportunities to develop nutritionally superior crop varieties capable of combating hidden hunger.\n\nSri Lanka is uniquely positioned to contribute to this global effort. Despite its relatively small land area, Sri Lanka possesses remarkable climatic and ecological diversity. The country contains more than 50 microclimatic zones, creating a natural laboratory for studying climate adaptation across diverse agricultural systems.\n\nAgriculture remains central to the nation\u2019s economy and livelihoods. Nearly half of Sri Lanka\u2019s population depends directly or indirectly on agriculture. Consequently, improving agricultural productivity and resilience could have transformative impacts on economic growth, rural development, and\u00a0 food security.\n\nEven modest improvements can generate substantial benefits. For example, increasing average rice yield by just one tonne per hectare could create hundreds of millions of dollars in additional economic value while strengthening national food security."
  },
  {
