    "chunk_id": "2026-09-19-dailymirrorlk-f0cbd531-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cd01fc56e75b050903ac661d3739ee25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "Even modest improvements can generate substantial benefits. For example, increasing average rice yield by just one tonne per hectare could create hundreds of millions of dollars in additional economic value while strengthening national food security.\n\nOne of the greatest barriers to effective climate action is communication. Important scientific findings often remain confined within academic journals and technical reports. Many farmers, policymakers, educators, and members of the public do not have access to this information or may struggle to interpret complex scientific concepts.\n\nAt the same time, misinformation is spreading rapidly through social media and other digital platforms. False narratives about climate change can undermine public trust and delay necessary action. This is where journalists and science communicators play a critical role.\n\nThe media serves as the bridge between scientific knowledge and public understanding. Journalists have the power to translate complex scientific evidence into accessible information that communities can understand and act upon. Effective communication can increase awareness, influence behaviour, strengthen policy support, and accelerate adaptation efforts.\n\nClimate communication must therefore become a national priority. Scientists, educators, policymakers, journalists, and community leaders must work together to ensure that accurate, practical, and locally relevant information reaches society.\n\nThe future depends on three interconnected actions: reducing greenhouse gas emissions, restoring and protecting natural ecosystems, and strengthening climate communication and public engagement.\n\nClimate change is not merely an environmental issue. It is fundamentally a challenge to food security, health, economic development, biodiversity, and human wellbeing. The solutions exist within science, innovation, and collective action. The task before us is to transform knowledge into action before the window of opportunity closes."
  },
