    "md5": "99a952b76881d616414e69f6abababc6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "The high-level forum brought together over 100 government officials, experts, and academics from China, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, the Maldives, and Pakistan. Key discussions centered on climate change, marine environmental protection, ocean disaster monitoring, early warning systems, disaster risk reduction, and capacity building. The Sri Lankan delegation accompanying the Minister included Ministry Secretary Dr. B.K. Kolitha Kamal Jinadasa, National Aquatic Resources Research and Development Agency (NARA) Chairman Dr. Sanath Hettiarachchi, NARA Director General Dr. K.H.M.L. Amaralal, and Senior Oceanography Researcher Dr. W.N.C. Priyadarshani.\n\nEmphasizing the importance of regional collaboration, the Minister noted that shared threats such as climate change, marine pollution, resource depletion, and Illegal, Unreported, and Unregulated (IUU) fishing extend beyond national borders. Consequently, he called for enhanced joint research, data sharing, marine observation, and disaster early warning mechanisms among South Asian countries and China to safeguard ocean resources while expanding livelihoods for coastal communities.\n\nAddressing ongoing legislative changes, Minister Chandrasekar pointed out that both Sri Lanka and China are actively reforming their respective fisheries management frameworks. China\u2019s broad revisions to its Fisheries Law are scheduled to come into effect on May 1, 2026, while Sri Lanka is currently drafting a comprehensive new Fisheries Act to replace the Fisheries and Aquatic Resources Act No. 2 of 1996. Expressing gratitude for China\u2019s ongoing technical assistance and training programs, he urged the expansion of these initiatives into emerging fields such as blue finance, artificial intelligence (AI) in marine technology, and ocean science."
