    "chunk_size_bytes": 2025,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis partnership represents far more than a management agreement. It is a commitment to the future of Trincomalee\u2019s tourism industry,\u201d Jeyakumaran stated. He expressed confidence that the collaboration would bring economic benefits to the Eastern Province, create employment and encourage further investment into the region.\n\nThe 46-room resort sits on the beachfront along the boardwalk, with an outdoor pool, a children\u2019s pool, a beach bar, spa facilities and a conference centre with two meeting rooms.\n\nThe owner will invest a minimum of Rs.60 million to Rs.65 million in the refurbishment, covering linen, air conditioning and bathrooms, while the operator project manages the works to ensure the finished product meets its standard. The outlay works out to roughly Rs.1.3 million per key. Cader pointed out that the existing rooms were already in sound condition, which keeps the bill relatively modest for an upgrade of this scale.\n\nBeyond the rooms, the partners have lined up upgraded dining and leisure offerings, professional management systems, stronger international and domestic marketing, and new experiences aimed at both leisure and business travellers.\n\nThe deal was not originally conceived as a pure management contract. The two sides had first planned a 50-50 investment, but the owner\u2019s larger expansion ambitions went beyond what the operator was prepared to co-fund, so the arrangement was reworked. Jeyakumaran now retains full ownership and carries the capital cost.\n\n\u201cSo we said you invest, we will manage,\u201d Cader explained, noting that the investor had studied the group\u2019s Colombo operation closely before committing.\n\nThe operator does not lend its name lightly, he revealed. Properties that fall short of its benchmark would trade under their existing names with a \u201cmanaged by\u201d tag, and the four-star upgrade is what cleared the Trincomalee resort to carry the brand outright. \u201cIf we think it is good for our standard, then we are okay to give our name,\u201d Cader remarked."
