    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f14c39c098cbb7ecae68da632d239ad7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "CIPM sets \u2018Human Edge in Age of AI\u2019 as NHRC 2026 theme\n\nThe Chartered Institute of Personnel Management (CIPM) Sri Lanka will host National HR Conference (NHRC) 2026 this October, at Monarch Imperial, under the theme \u2018The Human Edge in the Age of AI\u2019, with Hatton National Bank PLC as Lead Event Partner.\n\nThe conference comes at a pressing moment for the employers. Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping the workplaces faster than any technology before it.\n\nThe World Economic Forum (WEF) projects that AI will create 170 million new roles this decade while displacing more than 90 million; yet, the displaced workers rarely fill the new positions. Closing that gap is a human responsibility, not a technical one and it sits at the core of NHRC 2026.\n\nThe theme rests on a clear proposition: human intelligence leads, while AI executes. AI can screen and recommend at extraordinary speed but people must steer it and carry the moral weight of decisions affecting others\u2019 lives.\n\nRegulators worldwide classify workforce-related AI as high risk and require human oversight, leaving HR professionals as the final authority.\n\nFor the profession, this is an elevation rather than a threat. Freed from heavy administration, HR can drive leadership, cultural change, reskilling and employee well-being. The WEF research shows the employers see skills gaps, not technology limitations, as the biggest barrier to transformation, making workforce readiness the real test of AI success.\n\nCIPM President and NHRC 2026 Technical Committee Chairman Dr. Neil Bogahalande explained that the real contest ahead is not between people and machines.\n\n\u201cIt is between the organisations handing over their judgment to technology and those strengthening it. Our technical agenda gives the HR leaders practical tools, from governance frameworks to reskilling. If delegates leave with the confidence to say no to a machine when it matters, we have done our job,\u201d he added."
  },
