    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "Gold may not have peaked yet as geopolitical risks rise: AIIB Treasurer\n\nGold prices may have further room to climb as heightened geopolitical uncertainty strengthens the precious metal\u2019s appeal as portfolio insurance, visiting Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) Treasurer Domenico Nardelli said, arguing that the market may not yet have reached its peak.\n\nNardelli observed that gold had gained increasing traction as investors sought greater diversification in response to a more fragmented and uncertain global environment.\n\n\u201cGold has intrinsic value and, as such, can be viewed as an insurance for any portfolio in almost any market situation, in particular when conflict and uncertainties are on the rise,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cIf that is true, I believe we may not have seen the peak in gold prices yet.\u201d\n\nHe shared his views while speaking at the inaugural session of the Reserve Management Conference 2026, held earlier this month.\n\nAccording to Nardelli, gold\u2019s traditional attributes, including the absence of credit risk, reasonable liquidity and its role as a hedge against inflation, continue to make it attractive to reserve managers and other institutional investors.\n\nHowever, he pointed out that the geopolitical backdrop was becoming an increasingly important driver of demand for the precious metal, as investors looked to reduce concentration across assets and jurisdictions.\n\nReserve managers globally are reassessing portfolio allocations against elevated geopolitical tensions and increased volatility across financial markets.\n\nNardelli noted that after a relatively uneventful decade between 2010 and 2020, gold began a pronounced rally following the COVID-19 pandemic, with the momentum gaining significant strength over the past two years.\n\nHe explained that the shift towards diversification was occurring as investors questioned the risks of concentrating assets within a single jurisdiction in an increasingly divided geopolitical landscape."
  },
