    "md5": "90702ce134837b0d94a37261e0cc8586",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1651,
    "chunk_text": "Tourism earnings cross US$ 2bn but trail revised target\n\nTourism earnings crossed US$ 2bn in August, a month later than in 2025, leaving US$ 2.14bn to be earned in four months to meet the revised US$ 4.2bn target\n\nLeisure travellers made up 70.84 percent of August arrivals, up from 59.22% a year earlier, while visits to friends and relatives rose to 14.09%\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tourism earnings crossed the US$ 2 billion mark in August, a month later than in 2025, but at US$ 2,061.1 million for the first eight months the sector remains well short of the pace needed to meet the revised US$ 4.2 billion annual target, as per Central Bank data.\n\nCumulative earnings for the period were 10 percent below the US$ 2,290 million recorded a year earlier, a shortfall of US$ 228.9 million, although arrivals declined by a comparatively modest 2 percent to 1,535,122.\n\nLast year, earnings had already reached US$ 2,031.1 million by the end of July. As a result, estimated earnings per visitor slipped to about US$ 1,343 from US$ 1,462, a decline of 8.1 percent.\n\nIn August alone, earnings rose 2.1 percent year-on-year to US$ 264.4 million, even as arrivals fell 3.3 percent to 191,704.\n\nThe weaker cumulative performance follows a marked change in the composition of arrivals since the escalation in the Middle East on 28 February, after which arrivals fell 19.8 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April, as per SLTDA data. European arrivals declined 13 percent to 685,968 over the eight months, whereas Asia-Pacific arrivals rose 11.5 percent to 737,296, displacing Europe as the largest source region and coinciding with the decline in spending per visitor."
