    "chunk_text": "The weaker cumulative performance follows a marked change in the composition of arrivals since the escalation in the Middle East on 28 February, after which arrivals fell 19.8 percent in March and 22.3 percent in April, as per SLTDA data. European arrivals declined 13 percent to 685,968 over the eight months, whereas Asia-Pacific arrivals rose 11.5 percent to 737,296, displacing Europe as the largest source region and coinciding with the decline in spending per visitor.\n\nLeisure travellers accounted for 70.84 percent of August arrivals, compared with 59.22 percent a year earlier, while the share of those visiting friends and relatives rose to 14.09 percent from 10.41 percent and MICE travel edged up to 8.01 percent from 7.51 percent. However, much of this change reflects a fall in unclassified responses to about 3 percent from 20 percent, which limits a direct comparison.\n\nMeanwhile, the 2026 revenue target was lowered in August to US$4.2 billion from US$5.0 billion, alongside a cut in the arrivals goal to 2.7 million. Meeting it will require US$ 2,138.9 million between September and December, an average of about US$ 535 million a month, roughly double August\u2019s receipts. Although arrivals and earnings typically rise in November and December with the onset of the winter season, even SLTDA\u2019s conservative scenario of 2,500,220 arrivals for the year, which implies 965,098 visitors over the remaining four months, would yield about US$ 3.39 billion at August\u2019s spending level, leaving a shortfall of some US$ 810 million."
