    "md5": "9b42cb549247d1be2a92521700153d58",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "BOI puts first-half realised FDI at US$ 450mn, confident of meeting US$ 1.5bn target\n\nRealised FDI of approximately US$ 450mn in first six months, equal to 30% of 2026 target\n\nSecond half needs US$ 1.05bn, more than twice the first-half pace\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Board of Investment (BOI) received approximately US$ 450 million in realised foreign direct investment (FDI) in the first six months of 2026 and is confident of meeting its US$ 1.5 billion target for the year, BOI Director General Dr. Sulakshana Jayawardena told Mirror Business.\n\nJayawardena stressed that the figure represented money actually received rather than commitments, explaining that an investment is counted as realised once the investor has brought in at least 10 percent of the project value, which he described as a basic requirement.\n\nHe noted that domestic\u00a0 investments during the period exceeded the foreign inflows.\n\nThe first-half figure amounts to roughly 43 percent of the US$ 1,057 million in FDI the BOI reported for the whole of 2025 and 30 percent of this year\u2019s target. At an average of about US$ 75 million a month, inflows have so far trailed 2025\u2019s monthly average of close to US$ 88 million by around 15 percent, which leaves the agency needing to secure some US$ 1.05 billion in the remaining six months, or about US$ 175 million a month, more than twice its first-half pace.\n\nHowever, Jayawardena remained upbeat on the outlook, pointing to a steady flow of applications that includes several large and sizeable investments alongside a number of smaller projects. The BOI has also compiled a country-wise breakdown of investment progress, he noted.\n\nThe latest figure is considerably higher than the approximately US$ 250 million in realised FDI he publicly cited for the period to June in early July, when he defined realised investments as those remitted into the country through Inward Investment Accounts."
