    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "54b907160a139182ad357c16fe0f9561",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1025,
    "chunk_text": "The tragedy of a Sri Lankan worker getting into serious legal trouble abroad should not be used to suggest that the host country has no right to enforce its laws. Nor should it be used to suggest that every consequence is automatically the responsibility of the Sri Lankan Government.\n\nA Sri Lankan working in a Muslim-majority country cannot reasonably assume that religious insults posted online will be treated in exactly the same way as they might be at home. The same principle applies in reverse: foreigners working in Sri Lanka are expected to respect Sri Lankan law and the country\u2019s religious and cultural sensitivities.\n\nThe Sivarasa case should therefore prompt two conversations, not one. Sri Lanka must examine whether its migrant workers receive sufficient information before departure. And Sri Lankans themselves must accept that freedom carries responsibility, when exercising it abroad.\n\nThat is not a lesson for the Foreign Ministry alone. It is a lesson in common sense for Sri Lankans travelling abroad."
  },
