    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Dangerous-shifting-world/172-350760",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789937714.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-9e2a72da-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d81ed5de9bf72ea749541dc0d5b21804",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "The last five decades of neoliberal policies \u2013 what are commonly known in Sri Lanka as open economy reforms \u2013 have led to both increased financialisation of the economies and repeated financial crises. In other words, the rapid rise of financial institutions including the encroachment of finance into every sector has produced mounting crises caused by increased sovereign debt and capital flight. Notable are the Latin American and African debt crisis of the 1980s, the East Asian crisis of 1997, the North Atlantic financial crisis of 2008, the Greek sovereign debt crisis of the 2010s, and the Global South debt crisis of the early 2020s; the latter as we know crippled our country. These are symptomatic of a global financial system that creates tremendous wealth for global financiers but leads to dispossession for the rest of the population.\n\nAs an engineer building fiber optic networks that came of age with the expansion of the internet in the late 1990s, I heard fantastic stories of how with communication moving at the speed of light, the internet was going to completely change the global economy. Production of goods were apparently secondary, and it was information technology that would determine everything. Start-ups and massive investments, and so many computer scientists and engineers, were all lured by this so-called historic change that came to be known as the dot-com boom. But alas, in 2000 the bubble burst with so many companies going under.\n\nTwenty-five years later, I sense similar sentiments and developments with the AI boom. The financial sector has been leveraged to the hilt for speculative investments in firms and data centres that for the most part are still loss making and can hardly show any real revenues. With every effort to hype up AI running out of steam, the time is ripe for a crash. But very few people seem to remember the past or learn from it. Indeed, finance moves quickly these days, but it also seems to make us forget quickly."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
