  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-487e9f89",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-487e9f89",
    "date_str": "2026-09-21",
    "description": "Sri Lanka navigates path to implement  out-of-country voting",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Sri-Lanka-navigates-path-to-implement-out-of-country-voting/131-350764",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789944144.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-487e9f89-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "050561c8bccd7eaf3e6b4017ac25f923",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka navigates path to implement  out-of-country voting\n\nEven though OCV was discussed during the 2024 Presidential Election, Sri Lanka is still in its preliminary stages\n\nTwo-day regional conference held in Colombo to discuss persisting challenges regarding voting rights for overseas Sri Lankans\n\nInstrument that addresses political participation of immigrants is the Intl. Convention for the Rights of Migrant Workers\n\nDespite having over 1.5 million citizens working overseas, Sri Lanka is one among many South Asian countries that is yet to implement Out-of-Country Voting (OCV). Studies indicate that in South Asia, millions of people relocate annually due to factors driven by employment, education, family circumstances, safety concerns or environmental pressures. But, apart from a few exceptions, electoral frameworks across the region require people to be physically present at the polling station on the day of the election. Even though OCV was discussed during the 2024 Presidential Election, Sri Lanka is still in its preliminary stages. In this backdrop, a two-day regional conference was recently held in Colombo to discuss international best practices and persisting challenges regarding voting rights for overseas Sri Lankans.\n\nAt this conference, jointly organised by the Elections Commission of Sri Lanka and the International Foundation for Electoral Systems (IFES), representatives from Australia, Bangladesh, Maldives, Nepal and the Philippines shared their practical experiences and successful mechanisms for securing voting rights for citizens living abroad.\n\nModern democracies are evolving and human mobility is reshaping societies, economies and political systems across the globe. There was a time when elections were conducted within the boundaries of countries. People\u2019s democratic rights too were exercised within these boundaries."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-487e9f89",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-487e9f89",
    "date_str": "2026-09-21",
    "description": "Sri Lanka navigates path to implement  out-of-country voting",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Sri-Lanka-navigates-path-to-implement-out-of-country-voting/131-350764",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789944144.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-21-dailymirrorlk-487e9f89-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d8be8b53cd037f257c4895fc702d37ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Modern democracies are evolving and human mobility is reshaping societies, economies and political systems across the globe. There was a time when elections were conducted within the boundaries of countries. People\u2019s democratic rights too were exercised within these boundaries.\n\nAccording to IFES\u2019 Regional Director Vasu Mohan, around 304 million people were living outside the borders of their countries as per 2024 statistics. \u201cMobility is becoming an increasingly important feature in modern society. This is an issue significant for countries with large populations abroad. So what are their rights and how are their rights going to be expressed? There is no single international answer, but many countries have developed their own approaches,\u201d he said in his remarks.\n\nMohan said that the only international instrument that talks about political participation and voting rights of citizens living abroad is the International Convention for the Rights of Migrant Workers. \u201cBut even this convention specifies that it must be done \u2018in accordance with national legislation.\u2019 So national constitutions and domestic legislations are going to be the overarching framework for this process. The question is not how we move ballots across borders, but how we move democratic rights, because people are already moving. IFES doesn\u2019t recommend any country to either implement or not implement OCV. These are sovereign decisions completely rooted in each country\u2019s own experience. What we do instead is facilitate sharing of international best practices, particularly in south-south collaboration and regional collaboration among Asia-Pacific nations,\u201d he added.\n\nSpeaking at the inaugural event, Sri Lanka Elections Commission member Ameer Faiz said that Sri Lanka is ready to implement OCV. \u201cBut what lies in between implementation and intention is the legal authority to proceed. The difficulty has never been the principle; difficulty is in the details,\u201d he said."
