    "md5": "8fbcfa0ccae2747f2eca7df7f7d40bf4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "Supreme Court rules transit passengers subject to Customs jurisdiction\n\nColombo, Sep. 21 (Daily Mirror) - The Supreme Court has dismissed a long-running claim by an Indian national seeking the return of 50 slabs of gold seized at the Bandaranaike International Airport in 1991, holding that transit passengers are also subject to the jurisdiction of Sri Lanka Customs.\n\nThe Supreme Court held the view that a transit passenger carrying a restricted or prohibited item falls outside the jurisdiction of Sri Lanka Customs is contrary to the provisions of the Exchange Control Act and the Customs Ordinance.\n\nThe judgment was delivered by Justice Arjuna Obeyesekere, with Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena and Justice Mahinda Samayawardhena agreeing with him. The Court made the determination while allowing an appeal filed by the Attorney General.\n\nThe Supreme Court delivered this judgment brings to a close a legal battle that began 35 years ago when Angelo Sebastian Pereira, then 37 and based in Dubai, arrived on an Air Lanka flight from Dubai bound for Madras.\n\nPereira was carrying 5,850 grams of gold equivalent to more than 700 sovereigns concealed in a false bottom of a black briefcase. He was stopped at the entrance to the transit lounge by an Assistant Superintendent of Customs. The gold was forfeited under the Exchange Control Act, the Import and Export Control Act and the Customs Ordinance. A fine of three times the value was initially imposed; it was later mitigated to Rs. 100,000, which Pereira paid.\n\nPereira maintained he was only a transit passenger, that the gold was destined for India and that he had never \u201carrived\u201d in Sri Lanka in the legal sense. He later claimed ownership after initially telling Customs officers the gold belonged to a third party in Dubai and that he was merely a courier for a fee."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
