    "md5": "baaabca82c80c6b309b82ff133575e85",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "Police to introduce on-site drug testing for drivers within three months - IGP\n\nColombo, Sep. 21 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka Police is to introduce specialised on-site drug testing kits within the next two to three months to crack down on the growing trend of motorists driving under the influence of narcotics, Inspector General of Police (IGP) Priyantha Weerasuriya said.\n\nHe said Police have observed changes in the factors contributing to fatal road accidents, with driving under the influence of drugs becoming an increasing concern alongside drunk driving.\n\n\"While drunk driving was previously the primary focus of law enforcement, driving under the influence of narcotics is now the most frequently observed cause of severe accidents For example, regarding the road accident that occurred last week in Mattakkuliya, we conducted that test, and upon checking, it was found that the individual had used narcotics,\" he said.\n\nA recent pilot operation targeting buses in and around Colombo found that more than 5 percent bus drivers had been operating vehicles under the influence of narcotics, the IGP said.\n\nIGP Weerasuriya said that unlike alcohol use, which can often be detected by police officers at the scene, suspected narcotics use requires medical testing. Police have therefore made drug testing mandatory for drivers involved in serious accidents.\n\n\"While a police officer can immediately detect alcohol consumption on the spot, to confirm driving under the influence of narcotics, we must present the individual to a medical doctor and conduct a test. For that, as an alternative for the police in the future, we are taking steps to purchase the necessary testing kits. Regarding that, I believe we will adopt that methodology in another two to three months,\" the IGP said.\n\nHe added that driving under the influence of alcohol or drugs, reckless driving, speeding and violations of traffic laws remain among the major factors contributing to fatal road accidents in Sri Lanka."
  },
