    "md5": "64a663925dea549957f899606cf88d83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1655,
    "chunk_text": "He added that driving under the influence of alcohol or drugs, reckless driving, speeding and violations of traffic laws remain among the major factors contributing to fatal road accidents in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe IGP said that Sri Lanka continues to record more than 2,500 fatal road accidents annually, with 2,549 fatal accidents reported in 2025.\n\nAccording to the IGP, 2025 also saw 7,830 accidents involving serious injuries, more than 11,151 causing minor injuries and nearly 5,900 accidents resulting in property damage.\n\nHe said 1,849 fatal accidents have been recorded so far in 2026. Compared with the same period last year, the number of fatal accidents has increased by eight, while the number of deaths has risen by 16.\n\nPedestrians remain the most vulnerable group, with many being killed while standing by the roadside or using roads for various purposes, the IGP said.\n\nMotorcyclists are the second most vulnerable group. He said that an increasing number of motorcyclists are dying after crashing into roadside obstacles rather than in collisions with other vehicles.\n\nPillion riders and cyclists have also recorded a significant number of deaths, with 199 and 195 fatalities respectively during the period under review.\n\nThe IGP said three-wheelers are also frequently involved in accidents, with 3,515 accidents involving three-wheelers recorded. He said that motorcycles and three-wheelers account for a large proportion of vehicles on Sri Lankan roads.\n\nMeanwhile, authorities have identified 712 accident-prone locations across the country following a nationwide survey conducted in response to the rising number of serious road accidents."
  },
