    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5f81584f97cdc36297e18bb2cb498200",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1831,
    "chunk_text": "China \u2013 Sri Lanka launches Media Cooperation Action Plan\n\nAcademy of Contemporary China and World Studies Vice President Fan Daqi,\u00a0\u00a0Chinese Ambassador\u00a0Wei Huaxiang,\u00a0Mass Media Deputy Minister Kaushalya Ariyarathne,\u00a0University of Colombo Prof. Tissa Ravinda Perera,\u00a0University of Kelaniya Head of Department of Mass Communication Prof\u00a0Manoj Jinadasa\n\nSri Lanka and China have agreed on an action plan to take media cooperation into a broader and more practical phase, with greater emphasis on joint reporting, content sharing, professional exchanges to encourage young journalists and the use of new technologies, including artificial intelligence.\n\nThe China-Sri Lanka Media Cooperation Action Plan was launched at the China-Sri Lanka Media Cooperation and Exchange Seminar 2026 held in Colombo, recently.\n\nThe Action Plan aims to deepen media ties through stronger institutional cooperation, joint reporting and content sharing, professional and young journalist exchanges, and greater collaboration among media organisations, universities and think tanks.\n\nIt also places emphasis on AI and digital technologies, including AI-assisted translation, digital content production, data journalism and visualisation, while encouraging both countries to expand their participation in Global South and international media cooperation and establish a continuing mechanism to review progress and develop new areas of partnership and deepen people-to-people understanding between Sri Lanka and China.\n\nMeanwhile, the importance of strengthening people-to-people connection through a close media network between China and Sri Lanka to explore new forms of cooperation for a shared future was extensively discussed at the seminar with the participation of academics, senior experts on international relations and media professionals."
  },
  {
