    "md5": "a45a0ef2cab3173a520b569cd2261e9e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, the importance of strengthening people-to-people connection through a close media network between China and Sri Lanka to explore new forms of cooperation for a shared future was extensively discussed at the seminar with the participation of academics, senior experts on international relations and media professionals.\n\nHealth and Mass Media Deputy Minister Kaushalya Ariyarathne in her opening remarks said stronger media cooperation between Sri Lanka and China should go beyond the exchange of news and programs to deepen people-to-people understanding.\n\nShe said journalists and media organisations had an important role in helping audiences to understand each other\u2019s societies, experiences and development journeys. \u201cMedia is no longer simply a means of transmitting information. It has become a powerful instrument for building understanding between societies and strengthening people-to-people relations,\u201d she said.\n\nAriyarathne, reflecting on her recent visit to China as part of a workshop for Sri Lankan parliamentarians, said that it had given her valuable insights into the country\u2019s experience in governance, economic development, infrastructure modernisation, technological innovation and green development.\n\nShe said the program, which included visits to Beijing, Shenzhen and Guangdong, also provided an opportunity to engage with Chinese institutions and better understand the country\u2019s development experience.\n\n\u201cI found these exchanges particularly valuable in understanding how knowledge, technology and effective communication can contribute to national development,\u201d she said.\n\nEmphasising that the trust as the foundation for a meaningful communication, she also highlighted professional exchanges, joint reporting and content production as practical ways to strengthen the relationship between the two countries."
  },
