    "md5": "20b93a1d9c89cbd8c64d0eca419a0ae2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "Emphasising that the trust as the foundation for a meaningful communication, she also highlighted professional exchanges, joint reporting and content production as practical ways to strengthen the relationship between the two countries.\n\nHighlighting the long history of China\u2013Sri Lanka relations, referring to the ancient Silk Road and the Rubber-Rice Pact as important chapters in the relationship, Chinese Ambassador Wei Huaxiang said that the coming years will include major diplomatic anniversaries, providing an opportunity to build on the historical relationship.\n\n\u201cNext year, we will celebrate the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic ties between China and Sri Lanka, and the 75th anniversary of the signing of the Rubber-Rice Pact. China-Sri Lanka relations will face a historic opportunity to build on the past achievements and chart the future,\u201d he said\n\nAccording to Ambassador Wei, this has created a more important scenario than ever for the Chinese and Sri Lankan media to serve as a bridge for telling new stories of sincere mutual assistance, everlasting friendship, and win-win cooperation toward a shared future between China and Sri Lanka.\n\nHe said that it would help to carry forward China-Sri Lanka friendship and enhance people-to-people exchanges.\n\n\u201cIn the global media landscape, the voices of developing countries remain relatively weak. The media of China and Sri Lanka have a responsibility to deepen cooperation and strengthen coordination in international communication,\u201d he said.\n\nAmbassador Wei stressed the importance of both nations speaking with a common voice on issues such as climate change, poverty reduction and development, and global governance, while effectively countering misinformation and misleading narratives."
  },
