    "md5": "56df5923ac66f0395a09209de009ddd4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1834,
    "chunk_text": "Prof. Jinadasa stressed that China\u2019s technological and economic advances could offer useful lessons for Sri Lanka\u2019s media sector, while Sri Lanka could contribute by bringing its own communication traditions and philosophical perspectives into the partnership.\n\nCalling for a model of cooperation based on exchange, understanding and mutual learning, rather than one-way communication, he also emphasised the need of the media in both countries placing people and human values at the centre of their work in an increasingly algorithm-driven environment.\n\n\u201cGreater digital connectivity should be matched by stronger human connectivity, with media cooperation helping audiences understand one another and strengthening people-to-people relations. We are all connected by digital connectivity, though we are not connected with humanity. We have to reconnect our human values to the media,\u201d Prof. Jinadasa explained.\n\nUniversity of Colombo Prof. Tissa Ravinda Perera delivering the keynote address said that China\u2019s engagement with Sri Lanka and the region had become an important part of contemporary regional cooperation, particularly through infrastructure development and connectivity under the Belt and Road Initiative.\n\n\u201cThe media has a key responsibility to ensure that such development cooperation is communicated responsibly. Citizens need accurate information about the objectives, opportunities, challenges, social implications and long-term significance of major projects,\u201d he said, adding that media organisations should help audiences understand development beyond political slogans or isolated headlines.\n\nHe emphasised that the media could explain how infrastructure projects connect with national economic strategies, regional trade, investment, employment, technology and the everyday lives of communities."
  },
