    "chunk_text": "He emphasised that the media could explain how infrastructure projects connect with national economic strategies, regional trade, investment, employment, technology and the everyday lives of communities.\n\n\u201cInternational reporting should therefore be based on verified information, reliable sources and professional journalistic standards. The media should provide sufficient context when reporting on complex economic, political and development issues. Inaccurate or incomplete reporting can create confusion and undermine public trust,\u201d he noted.\n\nAcademy of Contemporary China and World Studies Senior Research Fellow Dr. Chen Wenling focused on China\u2019s modernisation and its cooperation with developing countries while highlighting China\u2019s transformation from a relatively poor country at the time of its founding in 1949 into the world\u2019s second-largest economy.\n\nShe attributed this transformation to the leadership of the Communist Party of China and the sustained efforts of successive generations of Chinese people, leaders and entrepreneurs.\n\nExplaining China\u2019s modernisation process involving a very large population looking at common prosperity including cultural development, ecological protection and development.\n\n\u201cChina\u2019s development experience should be understood in the context of each country\u2019s own circumstances,\u201d Dr. Chen said, mentioning China's involvement in development cooperation through the Belt and Road Initiative and other projects in countries including Sri Lanka.\n\nAt the panel discussion on Deepening media ties between China and Sri Lanka through people-to people connectivity,\u00a0 International News of China Daily Deputy Director Shao Xinying emphasised that journalists in China and Sri Lanka have much in common and that media cooperation can strengthen mutual trust."
