    "description": "UN flags closed legislative process, poor drafting; calls Online Safety Act \u201cunimplementable\u201d",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/UN-flags-closed-legislative-process-poor-drafting-calls-Online-Safety-Act-unimplementable/56-797587",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1790037775.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-22-dailyftlk-2ea73f42-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cb0513e55055e44a3ded98d1b8cbc937",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "UN flags closed legislative process, poor drafting; calls Online Safety Act \u201cunimplementable\u201d\n\nUN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche (left) with Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa\n\nUN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche flags legislative process which lacks consultations with stakeholders affected by proposed changes to laws\n\nOnline Safety Act introduced by previous Govt. singled out as law written without input from people who understand the internet and badly written piece of legislature, making it unimplementable\n\nCanada\u2019s Parliamentary Committee system cited as model where all affected stakeholders must testify before a law is passed\n\n22nd Amendment process flagged for insufficient consultation on changes to Constitution amid concerns on transitional measures and conflict of interest\n\nUN Resident Coordinator in Sri Lanka Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche said the country\u2019s legislative process is developed in a closed manner, warning that laws are frequently drafted without adequate technical or public input, in a discussion with Opposition Leader Sajith Premadasa, a video of which Premadasa has released.\n\nFranche said the biggest challenge was not only a matter of democratic process, where laws are developed solely between the Ministry, the Legal Draftsman, and the Attorney General, but also that laws often end up badly written as a result.\n\nHe cited the Online Safety Act (OSA), enacted under the previous Government, as a case in point. \u201cOne could have a debate about the substance, and if this is good or bad, but it\u2019s also a very, very bad law, because it is absolutely unimplementable,\u201d he said, adding that those who drafted it knew very little about the internet.\n\nFranche said this was not an isolated case but a pattern across Sri Lanka\u2019s legislative process, and called for greater political input and broader stakeholder engagement in drafting laws."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
