    "md5": "0a76a0f77f64d015d39fe119b0169295",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1522,
    "chunk_text": "14 districts on landslide alert as rains persist; flood warnings extended\n\nRed Warnings issued for six of 14 districts at landslide risk\n\nFlood warnings for Kuda Ganga and Gin Ganga basins extended by 24 hours\n\n38 schools, mostly in Central Province, closed yesterday due to weather\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s National Building Research Organisation (NBRO) has reported around 35 minor land instability incidents over the past three days amid heavy rainfall, warning that the situation could worsen if rains continue.\n\nRainfall over the past three days had exceeded 300 mm, and continued intensity at this level could trigger landslides, rock-falls, subsidence and slope failures. No major incidents had been reported so far, with only minor occurrences recorded to date, authorities said.\n\nFourteen districts are currently at risk of landslides, with Red Warnings issued for six of them. Authorities said the number of districts under Red Warning could rise if rainfall intensifies, and urged residents in high-risk areas previously identified by Divisional Secretariats to evacuate to safe locations without delay.\n\nThe Irrigation Department said significant rainfall continued across the Western, North Western and Sabaragamuwa provinces and the Galle, Matara, Kandy, Nuwara Eliya and Matale districts, though intensity had eased slightly. The upper catchment areas of the Kelani River had received 100mm to 125mm of rainfall as of yesterday morning, while most other river basins recorded 50mm to 80mm over the preceding 24 hours."
