    "md5": "11a794ec8375e5a1793433eb81765c89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "ITF announces landmark Colombo AI Conference hosted by NUSS\n\nStephen Cotton - General Secretary\u00a0 - ITF and\u00a0 Boa Athu - CEO NUSS\n\nThe International Transport Workers\u2019 Federation (ITF), in partnership with the National Union of Seafarers of Sri Lanka (NUSS), announced a ground-breaking conference on artificial intelligence, to be held in Colombo.\n\nThis landmark event marks ITF\u2019s first AI-focused conference worldwide and the first of its kind to be hosted in Sri Lanka, underscoring ITF\u2019s commitment to advancing technology, safety, and sustainable practice across the global\n\nITF is a global federation representing seafarers, dockworkers, and other transport workers, with a membership base exceeding 8 million workers across more than 150 countries.\n\nThis event will bring together leading voices from labour, industry, and technology to explore AI\u2019s impact on safety, operations, workforce development, and governance.\n\nGlobal collaboration on AI ethics and safety: Establish guiding principles for AI deployment that protect workers\u2019 rights, privacy, and safety at sea and on land.\n\nWorkforce development and training: Share best practices for upskilling and reskilling seafarers and transport workers to thrive in an AI-enabled environment.\n\nOperational efficiency and safety: Examine AI-driven solutions for routing, logistics, maintenance, and safety monitoring while ensuring robust human oversight.\n\nPolicy and governance: Promote inclusive policy frameworks that balance innovation with social protection and fair labour standards.\n\nNetworking and partnership opportunities: Foster cross-border collaboration among unions, employers, technology providers,\n\nOrganized by ITF and hosted by NUSS, the conference will feature keynote addresses from international labour leaders, technical sessions, and panels addressing the practical implications of AI adoption in maritime and transport sectors."
  },
