    "chunk_text": "The zone marks a second attempt at such a facility, as Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Ministry Secretary J.M. Thilaka Jayasundara acknowledged that an earlier effort with the Sri Lanka Ports Authority to set aside a separate industrial zone for maritime, boatbuilding and shipbuilding activities had collapsed.\n\n\u201cInstead of just establishing facilities, we really want to develop a separate zone with all facilities.\u201d\n\nSeparately, Jayasundara disclosed that the first inter-agency meeting on a marine hub at Kapparathota would be held this week, bringing together the relevant ministries, including the Industry and Fisheries, alongside the EDB and Board of Investment.\n\nAccording to Jayasundara, the EDB has already surveyed the site and development is expected to commence by 2027.\n\nJayasundara noted that the boat manufacturing industry earned US $ 53.7 million in export revenue in 2025, while the earnings for the January to July 2026 period stood at around US $ 25 million. If sustained, that pace would bring full-year earnings to roughly US $ 43 million, below the 2025 level. Against this backdrop, she pressed the industry to define its share of the government\u2019s US $ 36 billion export target for 2030.\n\n\u201cSo, where is the gap and what is the way forward?\u201d she questioned, before asking the chamber, \u201cOut of that US $ 36 billion, what is your portion? What is your contribution?\u201d\n\nEDB Chairman Mangala Wijesinghe urged the industry to rethink how it sees itself, arguing that \u201cthe marine-based industry should no longer be viewed simply as a traditional maritime sector\u201d.\n\n\u201cWe are closely working with the relevant chambers to fulfil the identified gaps and are targeting more than US $ 500 million in export revenue from this sector,\u201d Wijesinghe noted."
