    "md5": "b854ffa869d1ed460f83230911237841",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1194,
    "chunk_text": "The NCPI inflation trajectory has risen from 2.4 percent in January to 1.6 percent in February, 2.4 percent in March, 4.7 percent in April, 5.4 percent in May, 6.5 percent in June, 7.2 percent in July and 8.1 percent in August.\n\nFresh fish recorded the largest upward contribution, followed by dried fish, green chillies and big onions. Fresh fruits, dried chillies, milk powder, vegetables, eggs and chicken also pushed the prices higher from a year earlier. Some relief came from the lower prices for coconuts, rice and ginger. The acceleration in the annual rate came even though the prices increased only modestly from July. The overall NCPI rose 0.2 percent month-on-month, with food contributing 0.18 percentage points to the increase and the non-food items 0.08 points. The monthly price increases remain relatively contained, while the YoY inflation rate is rising rapidly, as the low-price base of 2025 drops out of the comparison. The August numbers therefore highlight not only to the renewed foodprice pressure but also to a progressively less favourable base effect feeding into headline inflation. The NCPI stood at 223.9 in August, up from 223.4 in July and 207.2 a year earlier."
