    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "Committee proposes community service, work camps to ease prison overcrowding\n\nColombo, Sep. 22 (Daily Mirror) - Officials have proposed referring minor offenders serving sentences of less than four years, or those unable to pay relatively small fines, to community service or work camps through expedited judicial procedures as part of measures to ease severe overcrowding in Sri Lankan prisons.\n\nThe proposal was discussed at a meeting of the Sectoral Oversight Committee on Governance, Justice and Civil Protection, chaired by MP Dr Najith Indika, at the Parliamentary complex. It was proposed that such cases be dealt with through expedited judicial procedures.\n\nThe committee examined a range of legal, administrative and infrastructure measures aimed at addressing the growing pressure on the country\u2019s prison system.\n\nOfficials said the high number of suspects held in remand custody, compared to convicted prisoners, was a major factor contributing to prison overcrowding.\n\nThey also pointed to delays in obtaining Government Analyst reports in drug-related cases as a major issue, with some suspects reportedly remaining in remand custody for periods ranging from 18 months to two years while awaiting the reports.\n\nThe committee discussed amending existing laws to allow persons arrested for minor drug offences or drug use to be referred to rehabilitation centres following a medical assessment, instead of being incarcerated.\n\nThe committee also called for the rapid introduction of remote video conferencing facilities for court proceedings involving inmates.\n\nOfficials noted that the daily transportation of prisoners to courts places a significant burden on prison staff and creates additional security risks. They said virtual court proceedings could help reduce staff shortages, minimise transportation-related risks and streamline judicial proceedings, subject to the necessary amendments to the legal framework."
