    "md5": "638ab4e0a1e3b2f9af893e336c92ba68",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "Russian Embassy hosts polling station for State Duma election\n\nColombo, Sep. 22 (Daily Mirror) - A polling station had been set up at the Russian Embassy in Colombo last Sunday for Russian nationals, particularly embassy staff members, to cast their ballots at the election to the State Duma, the Russian Parliament.\n\nRussia had done it in its other embassies and consulates. The State Duma serves as the lower house of Russia\u2019s Federal Assembly, the national parliament. It comprises 450 members elected for five-year terms.\n\nRussian Ambassador Levan Dzhagaryan, who shared his views on the election conducted after four and a half years of war with Ukraine said it was very significant because it was the first elections conducted after the beginning of special military operation.\n\nHe expressed optimism that a major part of the Russian population would support the policy of his President to strengthen his country\u2019s sovereignty and independence.\n\n\u201cWe are ready to protect our values against threats and dangers coming from outside, first of all from European countries,\u201d he said.\n\nAsked whether they are concerned about the election, he said, \u201cWe feel that there are some attempts from outside to undermine the process, including hacker attacks against our internet infrastructure and intentions to create provocations. But we are on alert,\u201d\n\n\u201cThe main problem is the situation in the Russian regions close to the border with Ukraine, as well as the Donbass region, because they are under attack. The Ukrainian regime does not want to have elections in its own country. I would like to underline once again that the so-called President of Ukraine, Zelensky, is illegitimate because his tenure expired long ago.\n\nThey don't want to have elections, while at the same time they want to undermine elections in my country. All these attempts will fail,\u201d he said."
  },
