    "description": "Navy builds Sri Lanka\u2019s first waveless referee boat",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Navy-builds-Sri-Lankas-first-waveless-referee-boat/108-350904",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790071636.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-22-dailymirrorlk-d1232cf9-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1fbb87540e2ad80f2ddde89c932c5edf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1321,
    "chunk_text": "Navy builds Sri Lanka\u2019s first waveless referee boat\n\nColombo, Sep. 22 (Daily Mirror) - The Sri Lanka Navy has designed and built the country\u2019s first waveless referee boat for rowing and canoeing competitions at the Navy Water Sports Centre in Diyawanna, Battaramulla.\n\nThe Naval Boat Building Yard (NBBY) constructed the boat and delivered it to the centre in April 2026. The locally built catamaran is now in operational use during water sports events.\n\nThe project is part of the Navy\u2019s Strategic Plan 2030, known as NAVSTRAT-2030, which focuses on self-reliance, technological development and strengthening local engineering capabilities.\n\nDesigned and approved by the Directorate of Naval Design in accordance with naval engineering standards, the vessel successfully completed performance trials before being deployed.\n\nThe high-stability catamaran has been specifically designed to minimise wake generation while operating alongside competitors. This is important in rowing events, where surface waves can affect athletes\u2019 safety, timing and the fairness of races.\n\nThe vessel, bearing hull number NC/WL/001, is 5.53 metres long and 2.30 metres wide, with a draft of 0.16 metres and a total weight of 0.45 tonnes. It is powered by a 25-horsepower petrol outboard engine and can accommodate up to two people."
  },
