    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Was-negligence-behind-former-Ampara-hospital-directors-death/108-350921",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790090918.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-22-dailymirrorlk-1d3f3f4a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ffd8bb08a0748026692de71bc01bfa5b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "Was negligence behind former Ampara hospital director\u2019s death?\n\nColombo, Sep. 22 (Daily Mirror) - A Health Ministry investigation into the treatment of a patient who later died at the Sri Jayewardenepura General Hospital has raised serious questions over the medical and nursing procedures followed during the patient's treatment, according to the investigation report.\n\nMedical expert Dr. Chamal Sanjeewa has called on the Health Secretary to make the findings of the investigation public, saying the report contains several matters that require the attention of the health authorities.\n\nThe patient, identified in the report as a former Director of the Ampara Hospital, Dr. Lanka Jayasinghe had been admitted to the Sri Jayewardenepura General Hospital in July 2020 and received treatment at several units, including the Emergency Treatment Unit (ETU), wards and the Intensive Care Unit (ICU).\n\nAccording to the investigation report, the inquiry examined the treatment provided to the patient, subsequent complications and the decisions taken by medical and nursing staff during the patient's stay in hospital.\n\nParticular attention had been paid to the procedure involving Percutaneous Endoscopic Gastrostomy (PEG) feeding. Statements obtained from doctors and nurses reportedly included differing details concerning the procedure, the timing of feeding and the monitoring of the patient's condition.\n\nThe report also examined changes in the patient's blood pressure, complaints of chest pain, cardiac investigations, Troponin tests and ECG examinations before the patient was subsequently transferred to the ICU.\n\nIt further considered the treatment provided after the patient developed breathing difficulties and other complications.\n\nThe investigation had also examined the coordination between doctors and nursing staff, the response to emergencies and hospital administrative procedures."
  },
