    "md5": "3b0040dd0098bf7378812f08d992990b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1748,
    "chunk_text": "Tribute to Nihal Rodrigo and family: A friendship beyond boundaries\n\nAmbassador Nihal Rodrigo (back row in a black suit) at the 1979 Non\u2011Aligned Summit in Havana, along with Sri Lanka\u2019s President J.R. Jayewardene, Cuban leader Fidel Castro UN Secretary General Kurt Waldheim\n\nIn many ways my friendship with Nihal went against the current. I was quite a loud-mouthed human rights activist in my youth and I would mumble, grumble and express outrage at Sri Lanka\u2019s human rights violations. When I got carried away Nihal\u2019s humour and innate tolerance\u00a0 would always bring me back to base\n\nIn an era of vicious polarisation my relationship with Nihal and Chitra Rodrigo and their children is unique because it crosses socially imposed boundaries. It is a testament to a friendship that is bonded in a nostalgic Sri Lankan identity before ethnic nationalisms became prominent. Through them I developed a love for Southern cuisine (especially nelun Ala, polos and del), Sarathchandra plays, Chitrasena ballets and Sri Lankan painting. Whenever Sarathchandra plays opened in Colombo I was always taken to the best seats in the house and Chitra would whisper the storyline.\n\nThey took me to meet George Keyt. I was young then, starry eyed and Keyt told me I had a face like the full moon. Nihal watching my expression cautioned me by whispering that he says that to everyone! A sketch of Radha Krishna done during that encounter hangs in my house. Nihal himself was a painter whose beautiful women all resembled different versions of his wife Chitra. My relationship with the Rodrigos was close and signified the promise of independence where everything was possible and we dreamed of the beautiful futures\u00a0 so captured by the art of that period."
  },
