    "chunk_text": "They took me to meet George Keyt. I was young then, starry eyed and Keyt told me I had a face like the full moon. Nihal watching my expression cautioned me by whispering that he says that to everyone! A sketch of Radha Krishna done during that encounter hangs in my house. Nihal himself was a painter whose beautiful women all resembled different versions of his wife Chitra. My relationship with the Rodrigos was close and signified the promise of independence where everything was possible and we dreamed of the beautiful futures\u00a0 so captured by the art of that period.\n\nIf Nihal had a public persona and a passion it was as Sri Lanka\u2019s leading advocate for the non- aligned movement\n\nIn many ways my friendship with Nihal went against the current. I was quite a loud-mouthed human rights activist in my youth and I would mumble, grumble and express outrage at Sri Lanka\u2019s human rights violations. When I got carried away Nihal\u2019s humour and innate tolerance\u00a0 would always bring me back to base. At that time, I was also constantly attacked in the newspapers. My mother would get agitated and besides family she would call on Neelan and Sithie Tiruchelvam and Nihal and Chitra. They would come immediately. Chitra says Nihal went to advise and calm down my mother while she accompanied him for my mother\u2019s specialty cheese toast .\n\nIf Nihal had a public persona and a passion it was as Sri Lanka\u2019s leading advocate for the non- aligned movement. In this we were united. His life and service paralleled the early growth of the movement. He was on Sri Lanka\u2019s delegations at every Non-Aligned Summit since the Colombo Summit in 1976. At that summit Sri Lanka became the chair of the movement. Nihal co-ordinated its UN activities and was elected Chairperson of the Political Committee at the 11th NAM summit.\n\nNihal was very Sri Lankan but like many in his generation, inspired by the Indian freedom struggle he also had a South Asian identity"
