    "chunk_text": "If Nihal had a public persona and a passion it was as Sri Lanka\u2019s leading advocate for the non- aligned movement. In this we were united. His life and service paralleled the early growth of the movement. He was on Sri Lanka\u2019s delegations at every Non-Aligned Summit since the Colombo Summit in 1976. At that summit Sri Lanka became the chair of the movement. Nihal co-ordinated its UN activities and was elected Chairperson of the Political Committee at the 11th NAM summit.\n\nNihal was very Sri Lankan but like many in his generation, inspired by the Indian freedom struggle he also had a South Asian identity\n\nNihal was very Sri Lankan but like many in his generation, inspired by the Indian freedom struggle he also had a South Asian identity. He read widely on South Asian history and culture. He had close friendships with so many South Asian diplomats. The aesthetic of his house also shows this bias. Th paintings are Sri Lankan but the statues and artifacts are from all over South Asia. It therefore seems quite natural that he would end his career as the Secretary General of SAARC but with a divided and polarised membership\n\nWhile geopolitics and foreign policy were Nihal\u2019s public face, he was a very creative person and loved creativity in others\n\nNihal was also one of the first to realise the importance of our relationship with China while he was ambassador there. We know so little about this great power and Nihal gained encyclopaedic knowledge while he was there that he would share with friends, colleagues and at international conferences. Relations with China would blossom during his time while China itself was undergoing a major transformation from being underdeveloped to a global economic power. Nihal witnessed this transformation taking place and felt there were many lessons for Sri Lanka. Nihal was very much part of the \u201cAsia Rising\u201d school given his passion for the aspirations of both South Asia and China."
  },
