    "chunk_text": "Nihal was also one of the first to realise the importance of our relationship with China while he was ambassador there. We know so little about this great power and Nihal gained encyclopaedic knowledge while he was there that he would share with friends, colleagues and at international conferences. Relations with China would blossom during his time while China itself was undergoing a major transformation from being underdeveloped to a global economic power. Nihal witnessed this transformation taking place and felt there were many lessons for Sri Lanka. Nihal was very much part of the \u201cAsia Rising\u201d school given his passion for the aspirations of both South Asia and China.\n\nNihal was also a lover of books. He was deeply disturbed by the burning of the Jaffna Library. For him it was a civilisational violation and he became one of the main voices fighting for its restoration. In 1997 a presidentially appointed committee co-chaired by Foreign Minister Lakshman Kadirgamar and Mangala Samaraweera gave Nihal the opportunity and restoration work began in 1997. The foreign Minister held discussions with British Library officials and a film was made to gather assistance for the Jaffna library. Between 2001-2003 it was restored with new structures and books though the precious original manuscripts could never be replaced.\n\nWhile geopolitics and foreign policy were Nihal\u2019s public face, he was a very creative person and loved creativity in others. He loved music and opera and in his last years music would keep him calm and contented. Nihal\u2019s secret passion were old movies, especially those with Humphrey Bogart as a hero. He must have seen Casablanca a 100 times. He was an unabashed romantic. Nihal and Chitra\u2019s love story with Nihal stealing the Harischandra beauty was also testament to that fact."
