    "md5": "155c7866a8e834bb44f9f07e93cb1dbf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has little room for tax hikes or cuts: IPS\n\nUrges shift towards direct taxes; warns fiscal consolidation has squeezed productive public investment\n\nSri Lanka should resist both further tax rate increases and premature tax cuts in Budget 2027, and instead widen the tax net and strengthen compliance as the country enters a critical phase of fiscal consolidation, the Institute of Policy Studies of Sri Lanka (IPS) has recommended.\n\nSetting out policy priorities for the upcoming Budget, the think tank said the next phase of revenue mobilisation should focus less on changing headline tax rates and more on addressing weaknesses in the tax base, exemptions, informality and compliance, particularly among high-income earners and the self-employed.\n\n\u201cFurther tax rate increases are not recommended, but tax cuts are premature because of challenges such as uneven compliance, informality, and exemptions,\u201d IPS said.\n\nThe recommendation places the tax burden at the centre of the Budget 2027 debate, as policymakers face the difficult task of preserving the fiscal gains achieved since the economic crisis while creating sufficient space for investment and growth.\n\nGovernment revenue has risen sharply from 8.2 percent of GDP in 2022 to 16.6 percent in 2025, according to IPS. However, the tax structure remains heavily tilted towards consumption, with direct and indirect taxes accounting for a 40:60 share.\n\nIPS argued that revenue policy should increasingly shift towards direct taxation by broadening the tax base, reducing exemptions, improving compliance among self-employed and high-income earners and accelerating the digitalisation of tax administration.\n\nIt also backed wealth taxation as part of a more equitable revenue framework, arguing that those with a greater ability to pay should contribute a larger share.\n\nThe recommendations come as Sri Lanka faces a tighter fiscal framework in 2027 following temporary easing this year."
  },
