    "md5": "32cb384f7611444b4353348a551ecdf1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "This creates a difficult policy trade-off for Budget 2027: maintaining sufficient revenue to protect debt sustainability while ensuring fiscal consolidation does not continue to crowd out expenditure capable of lifting the economy\u2019s productive capacity.\n\nIPS said expenditure rationalisation should therefore move beyond simply controlling spending and focus on improving the Government\u2019s capacity to select and execute investments.\n\nThe concern over investment execution is also reflected in the IMF\u2019s assessment. The Fund has said Sri Lanka needs to resolve bottlenecks in spending execution and improve public investment management while maintaining fiscal discipline.\n\nSri Lanka substantially outperformed its fiscal targets last year, recording a primary surplus of 5.4 percent of GDP in 2025, against the programme target of 2.3 percent. However, the IMF has cautioned that part of the performance reflected elevated motor vehicle-related revenue and under-execution of capital expenditure, highlighting the importance of developing a broader and more durable revenue base.\n\nFor 2027, IMF projections envisage government revenue and grants at around 15.1 percent of GDP, a primary balance of 2.3 percent and central government debt at 93.5 percent of GDP. Real GDP growth is projected at 3.2 percent.\n\nAgainst that backdrop, IPS\u2019 prescription is effectively for Budget 2027 to shift the emphasis of fiscal consolidation from higher rates towards better collection and a broader, more progressive tax base, while protecting expenditure capable of supporting productivity and longer-term growth.\n\nThe institute said the broader fiscal strategy must continue to protect macroeconomic stability while confronting high public debt, constrained fiscal space and weak investment in productivity-enhancing sectors."
