  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-939cc396",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-939cc396",
    "date_str": "2026-09-23",
    "description": "Sri Lanka back in \u2018B\u2019 league",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-main/Sri-Lanka-back-in-B-league/245-350938",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790126548.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-939cc396-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2c0426680b69c39ee25629c850378457",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka back in \u2018B\u2019 league\n\nFour years after Sri Lanka\u2019s economic collapse pushed its sovereign rating into default territory, the country has climbed back into the single-\u2018B\u2019 category, earning international recognition for a recovery built on higher taxes, tighter spending and painful economic reforms.\n\nFitch Ratings yesterday upgraded Sri Lanka\u2019s Long-Term Issuer Default Ratings to \u2018B-\u2019 from \u2018CCC+\u2019 and assigned a Stable Outlook, signalling that the immediate danger of another sovereign default has receded, even as the country remains a high-risk borrower burdened by heavy debt and limited foreign-exchange protection.\n\nThe upgrade is more than a symbolic vote of confidence as it strengthens Sri Lanka\u2019s credibility among international investors, could gradually improve financing conditions for the government and domestic companies, and supports Colombo\u2019s ambition to return to global bond markets in 2027.\n\nIt does not, however, amount to an economic clean bill of health.\n\nSri Lanka remains several steps below investment grade, while its debt and interest costs are substantially higher than those of similarly rated countries. Its foreign reserves cover less than three months of external payments, and debt repayments are set to increase sharply after 2028.\n\nThe upgrade therefore marks a transition from crisis management to credibility management. Sri Lanka has demonstrated that it can stabilise an economy after default. Its next challenge is to preserve that stability when external financing becomes more difficult, debt repayments rise and domestic political pressure to loosen fiscal policy intensifies.\n\n\u201cThe upgrade reflects the implementation of macro-stabilisation policies, underpinned by structural reforms, which have eased external financing risks and provide a degree of resilience to shocks,\u201d Fitch said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-939cc396",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-939cc396",
    "date_str": "2026-09-23",
    "description": "Sri Lanka back in \u2018B\u2019 league",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-main/Sri-Lanka-back-in-B-league/245-350938",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790126548.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-939cc396-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fdb44e78e0d0cc4cb49b3ef92e0d197a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe upgrade reflects the implementation of macro-stabilisation policies, underpinned by structural reforms, which have eased external financing risks and provide a degree of resilience to shocks,\u201d Fitch said.\n\nIn April 2022, Fitch downgraded Sri Lanka to \u2018Restricted Default\u2019 as the country\u2019s foreign-exchange crisis deepened. It upgraded the sovereign rating to \u2018CCC+\u2019 in December 2024 and affirmed it in October 2025.\n\nThe latest upgrade to \u2018B-\u2019 shows that Fitch now considers Sri Lanka\u2019s credit profile to be more consistent with a sovereign facing a material, though no longer imminent, default risk.\n\nFor investors, the change provides an external validation of the country\u2019s economic adjustment and could help reduce the risk premium attached to Sri Lankan assets over time. It may also improve sentiment towards government securities, banks and corporates whose credit profiles are influenced by the sovereign rating.\n\nHowever, the immediate impact on borrowing costs may be limited as Sri Lanka remains in speculative-grade territory, and investors are likely to demand high returns until the country builds larger reserve buffers, lowers its debt burden and demonstrates several more years of policy consistency.\n\nThe \u2018B-\u2019 Country Ceiling also means the rating upgrade could create some room for improved assessments of Sri Lankan companies, although individual upgrades would depend on their own creditworthiness and exposure to sovereign risks.\n\nA sovereign rating upgrade does not automatically generate foreign direct investment either. Fitch identified Sri Lanka\u2019s continued difficulty in attracting FDI, expanding exports and reforming state-owned enterprises as constraints on medium-term growth.\n\nThe rating action is therefore likely to be most valuable as a confidence signal. It tells investors and lenders that Sri Lanka\u2019s economic direction has improved, while placing greater pressure on the government to avoid reversing the policies that supported the recovery."
  },
  {
