    "md5": "6771ab2b5d17613891e637bb0ab0a630",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1909,
    "chunk_text": "The Public Financial Management Act limits non-interest expenditure to 13 percent of GDP through 2031, providing a legal anchor for fiscal discipline. The limit has, however, been exceeded in 2026 due to cyclone reconstruction spending.\n\nFitch also warned that the risk of fiscal slippage could increase as the 2029 elections approach.\n\nGovernment debt is forecast to decline to 92.9 percent of GDP in 2026 from 96.7 percent in 2025 and fall to the low-80 percent range over the next five years.\n\nThe direction is positive, though Sri Lanka\u2019s debt burden remains far above the 54.7 percent median for sovereigns in the \u2018B\u2019 rating category.\n\nFitch expects the interest-to-revenue ratio to decline to 41 percent in 2026 from 45.6 percent in 2025 and a peak of 76.3 percent in 2023. Despite the improvement, the projected ratio remains more than three times the 12.7 percent median for \u2018B\u2019-rated countries.\n\nThis means more than Rs. 4 out of every Rs. 10 collected by the government is effectively absorbed by interest payments, leaving limited space for infrastructure, healthcare, education and social protection.\n\nSri Lanka has therefore improved its ability to service debt, though debt servicing continues to restrict its capacity to fund development.\n\nFitch expects higher energy prices to push the current account into a deficit of 1.2 percent of GDP in 2026, following three consecutive years in which Sri Lanka recorded an average surplus of 1.5 percent.\n\nThe US-Iran conflict has raised the energy import bill and temporarily weakened tourism inflows. Rising worker remittances have helped cushion the impact.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s dependence on imported fuel and fertiliser remains a structural weakness, leaving its external accounts exposed to geopolitical conflict and global commodity-price shocks.\n\nFitch expects the current account to return to near balance in 2027 as the energy shock eases."
  },
