    "md5": "2da8eab3f3d6f1a84dd70935bd32d962",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1119,
    "chunk_text": "Fitch said a follow-on IMF programme remains possible. Such an arrangement could offer a financing backstop and preserve reform discipline as debt repayments increase.\n\nFitch forecast economic growth to slow to 4.1 percent in 2026 from an average of 5 percent over the previous two years, before settling at slightly above 4 percent over the medium term.\n\nInflation is projected to average 6.3 percent in 2026, compared with negative 0.5 percent in 2025, driven by the global energy shock, El Ni\u00f1o conditions and domestic price pressures.\n\nFitch does not expect another monetary policy tightening after the Central Bank raised its key policy rate by 100 basis points to 8.75 percent in May. Inflation is forecast to fall in order to just below the Central Bank\u2019s 5 percent target in 2027.\n\nA further sovereign upgrade would require a substantial decline in government debt and interest costs, stronger growth and a large, sustainable increase in foreign-exchange reserves. Reduced fiscal discipline, weaker revenue collection, slower debt reduction or renewed external liquidity pressures could reverse the progress."
