    "md5": "bf0c0ede55cccfb568de16323adae1c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "Although International Monetary Fund (IMF) guided the country towards a relative recovery during the Ranil Wickremesinghe administration, people\u2019s anger towards the highly corrupt Rajapaksa regime, and later towards Wickremesinghe for collaborating with the Rajapaksas,\u00a0 created the \u201crevolutionary environment\u201d favourabe to the NPP, at the next Presidential and general elections in 2024.\n\nHe swiftly grasped that\u00a0 macroeconomic issues are the prime concerns that have to be addressed first instead of hurriedly implementing his election pledges. Hence, disregarding constant attacks by the Opposition, certain NGOs and\u00a0 social media over going back on its promises, the NPP government broadened the tax base and increased certain indirect and withholding tax rates while providing income tax relief by raising the tax-free threshold.\n\nThe finance ministry under\u00a0 Dissanayake\u2019s close watch kept a close tab on Sri Lanka Customs, the Department of Excise, and the Inland Revenue Department (IRD), the three principal agencies responsible for state tax and revenue collection, which resulted in the increase of state revenue considerably. This helped the government to face\u00a0 Cyclone Ditwah, the unprecedented natural disaster that hit the whole country in November last year and the West Asian crisis.\n\nConversely, disappointing\u00a0 Opposition political parties which predicted an economic collapse under the \u201cinexperienced\u201d NPP administration,\u00a0 Sri Lanka earned a record US$ 11.6 billion in 2025 from tourism, foreign remittances and consular services,\u00a0 and there are signs that those revenues taking a further upward trend this year. Export revenue in 2026 is also tracking a strong growth. These achievements are encouraging against the backdrop of the government\u2019s attempts to eradicate corruption and waste."
  },
