    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dd14db41bb1ac42e71938d32456f7496",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1402,
    "chunk_text": "On April 21, 2019, nine suicide bombers belonging to a home-grown Islamist extremist group, Jamathei Millathu Ibraheem (JMI), struck multiple churches and upscale hotels, killing 270 worshippers and tourists, and wounding five hundred people.\n\nThe SLTJ, one of the earliest purveyors of Wahabisation of Sri Lanka, was first established in the country as a branch of Tamil Nadu Tawheed Jammaath.\u00a0 The distinction between these groups\u00a0 is merely semantic, except that JMI exercised a sense of urgency in terrorist action at home, while the others vacillated, instead preferring to commit violence abroad. All these groups operated in the open.\n\nThe first mosque of SLTJ was set up in Beruwela in 2002.\u00a0 Mohammed Zahran Hashim, the ringleader of the Easter Sunday Attacks and the self-proclaimed local emir of Islamist State, split from SLTJ in 2012 and set up the National Tahweed Jammath (NTJ). In the same year, the first mosque of NTJ was set up in Kattankuddy. In the Parliamentary elections in 2015, every Muslim party in the East sought an audience with Zahran, who by then claimed to have a followership of 2000.\n\nEaster Sunday attacks were not a political conspiracy, as some elements in the government, catholic church and compromised investigators try to project. They were a product of home-grown radicalisation that had been fostered by political inaction and cheap electoral calculations."
  },
  {
