    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2b3c7593f20042166a593e2961630122",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "Easter Sunday attacks were not a political conspiracy, as some elements in the government, catholic church and compromised investigators try to project. They were a product of home-grown radicalisation that had been fostered by political inaction and cheap electoral calculations.\n\nRadicalisation is a long-haul process which, for the most its part, is a below-the-threshold campaign of non-violent indoctrination.\u00a0 It takes years or decades for non-violent extremism to mutate into violent manifestations. A decade before the July 7 2005 attacks in London, Islamists preached extremism in the East London mosques, and authorities looked the other way on the pretext of freedom of speech.\n\nNon-violent extremism is not always invincible to the naked eye. Societies and subcultures often betray the radicalisation process that is under way through their newly adopted norms, values, dress codes, schooling and self-isolation from the dominant value system. The Arabisation of some quarters of Muslim society, wider adoption of the burqa, mushrooming Islamic International schools and Wahhabi mosques were all early warnings of the influence of an implanted ultra-conservative Wahhabism. The successive governments at the time ignored those warnings.\n\nIf those signs were not forceful enough, barely months before the attacks, in December 2018,\u00a0 Buddhist statues in Mawanella were vandalised, and the police search for suspects led to a plantation in Wanathawilluwa, where a large cache of explosives and detonators were discovered. The Defence secretary acknowledged that around 50 Sri Lankans have gone to Islamic State-controlled territory in Syria. Ranil Wickremesinghe later famously claimed Sri Lanka did not have laws to punish the citizens who joined foreign terrorist groups.\u00a0 In the end, the suicide bombers struck after\u00a0 local authorities disregarded multiple intelligence warnings by the Indian intelligence agencies."
  },
