    "md5": "132e99405e4c1b92312e878ec1a5b86e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "If those signs were not forceful enough, barely months before the attacks, in December 2018,\u00a0 Buddhist statues in Mawanella were vandalised, and the police search for suspects led to a plantation in Wanathawilluwa, where a large cache of explosives and detonators were discovered. The Defence secretary acknowledged that around 50 Sri Lankans have gone to Islamic State-controlled territory in Syria. Ranil Wickremesinghe later famously claimed Sri Lanka did not have laws to punish the citizens who joined foreign terrorist groups.\u00a0 In the end, the suicide bombers struck after\u00a0 local authorities disregarded multiple intelligence warnings by the Indian intelligence agencies.\n\nStill, the Easter Sunday attacks were a premature violent manifestation of Islamic radicalisation. Despite the enormous tragedy it was, and the expanding tentacles of extremist ideology, Islamisation of the local Muslim society was still latent when\u00a0 hurried terrorists blew up churches and hotels. Since then, public scrutiny and the outrage within the Muslim community have rolled back the Wahhabist expansion in Sri Lanka.\n\nRecently, there is a growing fallacy that Islamist extremism has run out of steam. The proponents of this claim cite the defeat of Islamic State and the lull in the major terrorist attacks in the West. However, before the Islamic State, there was al-Qaeda, which momentarily fizzled away, driving followers to the hyper-extremist Islamic State. Ideologies, however warped they might be, do not just evaporate, especially if they entail primordial meanings. It would be a matter of years before the next phase of Islamist extremism announces its arrival, probably with a spectacular attack that galvanises its followers.\n\nMuslim community suffered a massive public backlash after the Easter attack, which, while unfortunate, is a predictable social reaction. It took years for the mistrust to fade away. But that might also have reversed the runaway Arabisation within the community."
  },
