    "date_str": "2026-09-23",
    "description": "Is Nature Finally Striking Back at Us?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Is-Nature-Finally-Striking-Back-at-Us/131-350945",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790130184.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-23-dailymirrorlk-bc3d8475-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "92325d85ed691a806efa44896ce0ad18",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1283,
    "chunk_text": "Yet we still talk rather confidently about \u201cmanaging wildlife\u201d, \u201cmanaging forests\u201d and \u201cmanaging natural resources\u201d, almost as though nature were some badly run corporation waiting for humans to take charge. Sometimes I wonder whether it is we who require more management.\n\nComing back to where we started, I don\u2019t really believe nature is striking back at us because nature is not a person and is inanimate. What we are experiencing is something less dramatic, but perhaps more concerning. We are beginning to live with the consequences of interfering with immensely complex natural systems, that we still only partly understand.\n\n(All images are from the internet with due acknowledgment to original owners)\n\nPerhaps our greatest mistake has therefore been to imagine that we are somehow masters of this planet, standing above nature and deciding how much of it we can use, modify or destroy. In reality we are very recent arrivals, living within a natural system that operated for billions of years before us, and will probably continue for billions of years after us.\n\nSo perhaps nature is not striking back at us after all. We are now beginning to experience some of those consequences and, perhaps, what we are seeing is simply nature presenting us with the bill."
  },
