    "md5": "5427121ff01ccaf1a75d474228f278d4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "IMF urges Sri Lanka to stay the course on economic reforms\n\nColombo, Sep. 23 (Daily Mirror) - An International Monetary Fund (IMF) staff team has concluded a visit to Sri Lanka, with discussions focusing on recent economic developments, progress under the IMF Extended Fund Facility (EFF) programme and policy recommendations under the 2026 Article IV Consultation.\n\nThe IMF team, led by Evan Papageorgiou, was in Sri Lanka from September 10 to 23, 2026. At the conclusion of the mission, the IMF said Sri Lanka\u2019s economy had remained resilient despite successive shocks, with economic activity expanding by 4.2% in the second quarter of 2026.\n\nThe IMF noted that Sri Lanka had recorded 11 consecutive quarters of economic growth. However, headline inflation rose to 8% year-on-year in August, largely reflecting the impact of higher global oil prices. The Fund said inflation expectations remained broadly anchored.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s gross official reserves had increased to US$6.9 billion by the end of August, while the IMF said banks remained well capitalised and profitable. It also noted that fiscal performance during the first half of 2026 was strong and that the country\u2019s debt restructuring was largely completed.\n\nThe IMF, however, warned that Sri Lanka continues to face downside risks linked to uncertainty over the duration and intensity of the conflict in the Middle East, global trade policies and the impact of El Ni\u00f1o.\n\nThe Fund called for continued efforts to strengthen fiscal and external buffers, maintain price stability and advance governance reforms while strengthening social safety nets for vulnerable groups.\n\nThe IMF also recommended developing a medium-term revenue strategy to improve tax collection while making the tax system more efficient and equitable. It called for broadening the tax base, reviewing tax exemptions and incentives, and strengthening revenue administration."
