    "md5": "a6f331a7ad9a8814c76158f01b472c7d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "Sectoral Oversight Committee approves 22A, Judicature Amendment Bills\n\nColombo, Sep. 23 (Daily Mirror) - The Sectoral Oversight Committee on Governance, Justice and Civil Protection has approved the proposed 22nd Amendment to the Constitution and the Judicature (Amendment) Bills, which are scheduled for debate in Parliament on September 24 and 25.\n\nThe committee approved the Bills following consideration at a meeting held yesterday (22) under the chairmanship of MP Najith Indika, the Communications Department of Parliament said.\n\nAttorney General, President\u2019s Counsel Parinda Ranasinghe, officials from the Attorney General\u2019s Department, the Ministry of Justice and National Integration, and the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department participated in the meeting.\n\nThe Attorney General briefed the committee on the Supreme Court\u2019s determination on the two Bills, which was announced to Parliament by Speaker Jagath Wickramaratne on September 22. Committee members also expressed their views on the proposed legislation.\n\nThe proposed amendments seek to expand judicial capacity, improve access to appellate justice at the regional level, expedite the disposal of pending cases, retain experienced judges and strengthen the process for appointing judges.\n\nAn amendment to Article 137 of the Constitution has been proposed to increase the number of Court of Appeal judges from 19 to 24. The establishment of regional Courts of Appeal is intended to expedite the disposal of pending cases and reduce the need for litigants to travel to Colombo.\n\nThe Bills also propose measures to increase judicial capacity at the Provincial High Court level. Provincial High Courts exercise original criminal jurisdiction over serious offences, while Civil High Courts have exercised appellate jurisdiction since amendments introduced in 2006."
