    "description": "Energy Minister provides timeline for oil, gas licensing round",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Energy-Minister-provides-timeline-for-oil-gas-licensing-round/34-797685",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1790213104.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-dailyftlk-6bd191ba-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e96ac8a44c518e7199530839852ccd09",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1549,
    "chunk_text": "Energy Minister provides timeline for oil, gas licensing round\n\nEnergy Minister Anura Karunathilaka yesterday gave Parliament a detailed timeline for Sri Lanka\u2019s ongoing oil and gas exploration licensing round, setting out the schedule and commercial terms under which the country hopes to secure binding investment in its offshore acreage by the end of 2027.\n\nSpeaking during a parliamentary debate, Energy Minister Karunathilaka said bids called in August would remain open for six months, with the Government hoping to close bidding by February or March 2027. Bids would then be evaluated through the remainder of 2027, with negotiations opening with successful bidders and licensing agreements finalised from October 2027, with the process expected to conclude by the end of that year.\n\nUnder the terms disclosed by the Minister, companies bidding for exploration and production rights through the Petroleum Development Authority of Sri Lanka (PDASL) would be expected to pay a signature bonus of around $ 5,000, described as the norm for the round, while also submitting production, royalty and profit-sharing terms as part of their bid for evaluation. Investors are also required to submit development plans covering the full cycle from exploration through to production.\n\nSuccessful bidders will be granted six months to mobilise and three years to explore, an exploration period that can be extended up to eight years. Production must commence within that eight-year window, after which successful bidders will hold the asset for 20 years."
  },
