    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "81c1f80b0ebb8bcb38f38ed4363c082c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1923,
    "chunk_text": "Development banking needs tax relief and sharper aim to take off, says Bingumal Thewarathanthri\n\nBanking taxes absorbing 50-60% of profits seen deterring capital into development finance\n\nFiscal incentives needed at outset, a stance at odds with IMF thinking\n\nTighter directed lending and targeted agri-finance urged, citing India\u2019s model\n\nSri Lanka will struggle to draw fresh capital into development banking unless the state offers fiscal incentives to offset the taxes that swallow as much as 60 percent of the bank profits, a stance that cuts against the International Monetary Fund (IMF) thinking, Standard Chartered Bank Sri Lanka Chief Executive Officer Bingumal Thewarathanthri said.\n\nThewarathanthri, who is also Vice Chairperson of the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce, was speaking on a panel on Sri Lanka\u2019s financial landscape, at the 49th Annual Meetings of the Association of Development Financing Institutions in Asia and the Pacific, hosted by DFCC Bank, in Colombo this week.\n\nHe framed the debate less around who should own a development bank than around why anyone would fund one.\n\n\u201cThe reality is, why should anybody put money into a market where the banking tax is something like 50 to 60 percent of your profits?\u201d he asked.\n\nAt that rate, an investor keeps between Rs.40 and Rs.50 of every Rs.100 earned before tax. Thewarathanthri contended that such a return would struggle to attract foreign capital, whether into a new development bank, a public-private partnership or a dedicated development-banking subsidiary of an existing lender. He was candid that his prescription sits uneasily with the country\u2019s IMF programme.\n\n\u201cThis is completely against the IMF thinking but the reality is that a lot of countries, when they started some of these, have given those,\u201d he acknowledged.\n\nHe added that the tax rates are typically raised to normal banking levels as institutions and markets mature."
  },
