    "language": "en",
    "md5": "abb0dfb356d97a4354d07f9975d47346",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis is completely against the IMF thinking but the reality is that a lot of countries, when they started some of these, have given those,\u201d he acknowledged.\n\nHe added that the tax rates are typically raised to normal banking levels as institutions and markets mature.\n\nHowever, he cautioned that capital alone would not fix a model whose past lending, in his view, followed the priorities of the funders rather than those of the economy.\n\n\u201cOur business strategy was always driven by the DFIs\u2019 objectives. We have not looked at what the country needs, what are the country\u2019s economic pillars,\u201d he observed.\n\nHe stressed that where the state backs such institutions, it is the taxpayers\u2019 money that must be steered into the right sectors.\n\nThewarathanthri stressed that the decisions on which sectors, segments, demographics and geographies to finance must be grounded in data, warning against the lending choices shaped by instinct or individual preference.\n\n\u201cIt can\u2019t be based on gut feeling; it can\u2019t be based on someone\u2019s thinking,\u201d he told the gathering.\n\nHe went on to cite portfolio-based lending, digital platforms and tools such as IoT and blockchain as means of monitoring the borrowers without relying on constant physical visits.\n\nPointing out that several banks and non-bank financial institutions already extend collateral-free credit through programme-based loans and community cross-guarantees, he expressed confidence that the state-backed credit guarantee scheme was gaining traction, although he conceded he had no data on its take-up.\n\nAs per the available data, the National Credit Guarantee Institution, which commenced operations in January 2025, had issued 2,000 guarantees, facilitating over Rs.14 billion in SME loans by July this year, averaging roughly Rs.7 million per loan, while it expects to double its beneficiaries to 4,000 by end-2026."
  },
