    "language": "en",
    "md5": "622f9a90377a9576fa6eca8248000d0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "As per the available data, the National Credit Guarantee Institution, which commenced operations in January 2025, had issued 2,000 guarantees, facilitating over Rs.14 billion in SME loans by July this year, averaging roughly Rs.7 million per loan, while it expects to double its beneficiaries to 4,000 by end-2026.\n\n\u201cWhen there is funding available, people just get into it and grow mangoes or something like that. Then suddenly you realise you have no market for it,\u201d he remarked, recalling the watermelons and mangoes sold at depressed prices in markets around Kurunegala and Bibile.\n\nMeanwhile, he urged the Central Bank to take a firmer hand in directing credit. He set Sri Lanka\u2019s 10 percent priority sector requirement against the Reserve Bank of India\u2019s (RBI) target of around 40 percent. The two figures are not strictly comparable.\n\nThe RBI\u2019s 40 percent spans all priority sectors, whereas the 10 percent he cited is Sri Lanka\u2019s mandatory allocation to agriculture, a requirement dating back to 2008. Even on a like-for-like basis, however, India\u2019s agriculture sub-target of 18 percent is close to double Sri Lanka\u2019s.\n\nThe banks in India that miss their targets, which he noted include global lenders such as Standard Chartered, must place the shortfall in designated funds such as the Rural Infrastructure Development Fund managed by NABARD.\n\n\u201cThose are good practices that have worked for India. We have to all agree, it\u2019s 6, 7 percent growth; it has worked for India,\u201d he pointed out.\n\nAgriculture, he argued, is where directed finance could do the most good. He put the sector\u2019s share of GDP at 6 to 7 percent and its share of the labour force at 27 percent. The official data point to a narrower, though still stark, gap."
  },
