    "language": "en",
    "md5": "de67319ece494ee775e017c4ac9abc24",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1962,
    "chunk_text": "Agriculture, he argued, is where directed finance could do the most good. He put the sector\u2019s share of GDP at 6 to 7 percent and its share of the labour force at 27 percent. The official data point to a narrower, though still stark, gap.\n\nRaising agriculture\u2019s contribution to 12 to 13 percent of GDP would, in his reckoning, lifts much of the farming population out of poverty. Many farmers, he noted, live on less than Rs.17,000 to Rs.18,000 a month per person. That range brackets the DCS official poverty line, which rose to Rs.17,679 per person per month in July 2026 or about Rs.590 a day.\n\nHe added that a quarter of Sri Lankans live in poverty and a further quarter are vulnerable, leaving half the population exposed.\n\nBeyond incomes, he cast the sector as a question of national security in a multipolar world unsettled by the Middle East crisis. He recalled the wheat export curbs during the Russia-Ukraine war and India\u2019s periodic bans on crop exports.\n\n\u201cGetting food security right is critical, forget the development and growth,\u201d he remarked.\n\nSuch gains, he insisted, can come only through technology and mechanisation, from geo-fencing and climate projections to crop monitoring. These tools, he said, must be tailored to farms that typically span one to two acres and support several families.\n\nHe saw room for the banks to go well beyond the 10 percent now directed to agriculture, pointing to the export potential for coconut, cinnamon, fruit and organic produce in the nearby markets such as the UAE and Singapore.\n\nHis call for state incentives sat alongside a note of caution from fellow panellist and DFCC Bank Director and Chief Executive Officer Thimal Perera. Perera argued that any development bank must be run independently, free of political interference and able to stand on its own feet.\n\n\u201cOtherwise, it becomes a burden on the taxpayer at the end of the day, if the development banks are loss-making,\u201d Perera warned."
