    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c34fd3efc08653cf69a9bd23b5bcbdad",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1720,
    "chunk_text": "Venue shortage cost Sri Lanka an Indian film festival:  Deputy Tourism Minister\n\nMICE share of arrivals up from 5% in 2024 to around 10-11%, figures estimated\n\nSri Lanka recently lost a major Indian film festival after the organisers\u2019 preferred venue, the Nelum Pokuna, proved unavailable, underlining the venue and accommodation gaps that continue to hold back the country\u2019s push for international business events, Tourism Deputy Minister Prof. Ruwan Ranasinghe said on Tuesday.\n\n\u201cVery recently one top group from India for a film festival met me and they came for the Nelum Pokuna but it was not available and they cancelled,\u201d Prof. Ranasinghe revealed at the media briefing for Sri Lanka MICE Expo 2026.\n\nHe cited connectivity, room inventory and the availability of suitable venues and logistics as key constraints facing the meetings, incentives, conferences and exhibitions (MICE) segment.\n\nHowever, the government has opened Temple Trees for events, while the developments in Port City Colombo are expected to add capacity and a process is underway to bring the facilities in Mattala into MICE use.\n\nSri Lanka Convention Bureau (SLCB) Chairman Dheera Hettiarachchi put the MICE share of total tourist arrivals at about 5 percent in 2024, when the current leadership took charge, a figure that has since doubled to 10 percent.\n\n\u201cOur expectation is to grow it to 11 percent by the end of this year,\u201d he said.\n\nProf. Ranasinghe placed the current share at around 11 percent but acknowledged the limits of the data.\n\n\u201cOf course, these statistics are some approximations, where we don\u2019t have the exact statistics pertaining to MICE business. But there is an increase, a clear increase,\u201d he explained."
  },
