    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "Prof. Ranasinghe placed the current share at around 11 percent but acknowledged the limits of the data.\n\n\u201cOf course, these statistics are some approximations, where we don\u2019t have the exact statistics pertaining to MICE business. But there is an increase, a clear increase,\u201d he explained.\n\n\u201cIf we take the top 10 MICE destination cities globally, except Sydney and Melbourne, all the other eight are in the Asian region. So, Sri Lanka faces strong competition in this business as well,\u201d Prof. Ranasinghe warned.\n\nHe also disclosed that the SLCB, a team of just nine members, operates on an annual budget of Rs.360 million, of which more than Rs.110 million is being invested in this year\u2019s expo. That is nearly a third of its yearly allocation or roughly Rs.1 million for each hosted buyer.\n\nMeanwhile, Hettiarachchi observed that the sixth edition had drawn more than 600 applications from MICE professionals and media representatives worldwide.\n\n\u201cSince its beginnings in 2021, the expo has evolved from a virtual initiative into an international platform,\u201d he added.\n\nThe figures presented by SLCB Marketing Manager Malkanthi Welikala showed that the bureau received 587 hosted buyer applications from 49 countries. With more than 700 buyers already hosted across previous editions, only new applicants were considered and after 81 repeat applications were excluded, 104 buyers from 24 countries were selected from the 506 evaluated, roughly one in five.\n\n\u201cIndia is the main market for MICE tourism and because of that reason, we have selected 40 percent from India,\u201d Welikala pointed out.\n\nA further 17 journalists from 11 countries were chosen from 79 media applications, bringing the international contingent to participants from 27 countries.\n\nThe delegates will arrive on October 4 or early on October 5, with the B2B meetings scheduled for October 6 and familiarisation tours of the Cultural Triangle and other areas on October 7 and 8, before the programme closes on October 9."
