    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "53bac7392df081565211a173e8e461a4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "Apparel slump tests Sri Lanka\u2019s US $ 12bn export run\n\nMerchandise exports increased 3.63% to US $ 9.41bn, while services exports grew 6.59% to US $ 2.60bn\n\nOverall gain came despite a 6.76% fall in apparel and textile earnings to US $ 3.34bn\n\nAugust merchandise exports fell 1% from a year earlier to US $ 1.27bn, while estimated services exports rose 13.97% to US $ 331.87mn\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s exports crossed US $ 12 billion in the first eight months of 2026, although the declining apparel and tea earnings showed that the growth remained uneven across two of the country\u2019s biggest export industries.\n\nTotal exports rose 4.26 percent from a year earlier to an estimated US $ 12.01 billion, the provisional data released by the Export Development Board (EDB) showed yesterday.\n\nMerchandise exports increased 3.63 percent to US $ 9.41 billion, while services exports grew 6.59 percent to US $ 2.60 billion.\n\nThe overall gain came despite a 6.76 percent fall in apparel and textile earnings to US $ 3.34 billion. Exports from the sector declined to all three of its major markets: the United States, European Union and United Kingdom.\n\nTea earnings fell 7.56 percent to US $ 948.45 million, with bulk tea and tea packets both recording declines.\n\nGrowth elsewhere helped offset those losses. Electrical and electronic component exports surged 57.49 percent to US $ 440.31 million over the eight months. Spices and essential oils rose 24.09 percent to US $ 355.78 million, while food and beverage exports increased 19.99 percent to US $ 462.52 million.\n\nThe EDB estimated that ICT and business process management exports grew 9.76 percent to US $ 1.15 billion.\n\nAugust merchandise exports fell one percent from a year earlier to US $ 1.27 billion, while the estimated services exports rose 13.97 percent to US $ 331.87 million. That lifted total exports for the month by 1.77 percent to US $ 1.60 billion."
  },
