    "chunk_size_bytes": 1184,
    "chunk_text": "August merchandise exports fell one percent from a year earlier to US $ 1.27 billion, while the estimated services exports rose 13.97 percent to US $ 331.87 million. That lifted total exports for the month by 1.77 percent to US $ 1.60 billion.\n\nTea earnings declined 7.76 percent to US $ 130.92 million. Exports to the Middle East, a key tea market, fell 41.05 percent, according to the EDB.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s largest single merchandise export market, the United States, recorded a 13.43 percent drop in purchases to US $ 263.24 million in August. Exports to the United States were down 2.09 percent at US $ 1.97 billion for January to August.\n\nIndia, meanwhile, moved ahead of the UK as Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest export destination, with purchases rising 10.15 percent to US $ 776.62 million over the eight months.\n\nEDB Chairman and Chief Executive Officer Mangala Wijesinghe said sustaining the export growth would require stronger competitiveness and a broader range of products and markets.\n\n\u201cGreater value addition, innovation, technology adoption, market diversification and deeper integration into global value chains will be critical to sustaining export growth,\u201d he said."
