    "time_ut": 1790229738.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-dailymirrorlk-e765b34a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "03fdaa2aac487b6971e549f936d21ea6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "Fitch upgrade paves way for investment-led growth; DFIs urged to go beyond lending: CB Governor\n\nFollowing Fitch\u2019s sovereign rating upgrade, Sri Lanka is now strengthening its financing architecture to convert the hard-won macroeconomic stability into sustainable investment-led growth, a shift in which the development finance institutions (DFIs) must move beyond traditional lending to mobilise capital and share risk, according to Central Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe.\n\nDr. Weerasinghe delivered the keynote address at the inauguration of the 49th Association of Development Financing Institutions in Asia and the Pacific (ADFIAP) Annual Meetings in Colombo, hosted by DFCC Bank PLC.\n\nHe noted that the restoration of stability after the economic crisis had laid a stronger foundation for the country\u2019s next phase of development.\n\n\u201cThe progress has been recognised by international agencies and by the upgrade of Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating by Fitch Ratings to B- yesterday. The focus is now on transforming this stability into sustainable investment-led growth,\u201d he stressed.\n\nFitch on Tuesday lifted Sri Lanka\u2019s Long-Term Issuer Default Ratings to B-, from CCC+, with a Stable Outlook, crediting the macro-stabilisation policies and structural reforms for easing the external financing risks.\n\nHowever, the agency expects the government debt to stand at 92.9 percent of GDP this year. That is down from 96.7 percent in 2025 but still nearly 1.7 times the 54.7 percent median for the B-rated countries.\n\n\u201cIn this regard, Sri Lanka is strengthening its financing architecture for a more resilient and sustainable economy,\u201d Dr. Weerasinghe added.\n\nHe pointed to the National Climate Finance Strategy 2025 to 2030, launched last October, to integrate climate priorities into fiscal planning and investment decisions.\n\nHe also cited the Central Bank\u2019s implementation of the Green Finance Taxonomy, which classifies environmentally sustainable activities to guide investment."
  },
