    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0d98113286828b0378eede6e16d90a1c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "He pointed to the National Climate Finance Strategy 2025 to 2030, launched last October, to integrate climate priorities into fiscal planning and investment decisions.\n\nHe also cited the Central Bank\u2019s implementation of the Green Finance Taxonomy, which classifies environmentally sustainable activities to guide investment.\n\nTurning to the institutions gathered in Colombo, Dr. Weerasinghe called on the DFIs to play a more catalytic role in channelling that capital.\n\n\u201cThe role of the DFIs, like the members of this association, should go beyond traditional lending. They need to increasingly act as catalysts for mobilising capital, sharing risks and accelerating development impacts,\u201d he urged.\n\nHe added that blending concessional and commercial finance could draw broader participation from development partners and private investors.\n\nHe cautioned that the geopolitical tensions, trade fragmentation, climate risks and concerns over energy and food security were testing regional resilience.\n\nHe also noted that financial integration in Asia and the Pacific remains relatively underdeveloped despite the resilient intra-regional investment. Interoperable payment systems are already advanced in ASEAN through cross-border QR payments and real-time linkages and they are now taking shape in South Asia, he observed.\n\nMeanwhile, ADFIAP Chairman and Agricultural and Rural Development Bank of Cambodia Chief Executive Officer Dr. Kao Thach made a parallel case for the DFIs to rethink how they measure success. He argued that the region\u2019s financing needs for climate action, SMEs and underserved communities far exceed what the public budgets or individual institutions can provide.\n\n\u201cThe measure of a development finance institution should no longer be only how much it can lend from its own balance sheet. The more strategic question is how much additional capital it can unlock, mobilise and direct towards development priorities,\u201d he emphasised."
