    "md5": "6cca192c4ae4827157bcec7b8318f266",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "The burden of road traffic accidents in Sri Lanka\n\nRecords indicate that people between 15-45 years of age are more vulnerable to road accidents\n\nRoad accidents have been identified as the biggest manmade disaster in post-war Sri Lanka. According to statistics recorded by the Sri Lanka Medical Association (SLMA), over 2000 individuals have succumbed to road accidents in 2025 alone. Sri Lanka Police statistics indicate that over 1400 people have died as a result of road accidents during the first six months of this year. This shows that despite repeated awareness and new laws coming into place, the issue perhaps lies elsewhere.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s socioeconomic context following the economic crisis in 2022 demands an individual to at least juggle between two jobs to earn a decent living. For an individual living by him or herself, depending on one salary is insufficient given the responsibilities and other expenses that need to be covered. Therefore men, especially, opt to do voluntary jobs such as taking hires on ride-hailing apps or going on food deliveries once office hours are over. But, by the time they start their temporary jobs, they are already too exhausted. According to statistics issued by Sri Lanka Police Traffic Management and Road Safety Division over 740 motorcycle-related deaths have been recorded during the first six months of 2026.\n\nThis applies to three-wheeler drivers and bus drivers as well. The Ella-Wellawaya bus accident in September 2025 claimed the lives of 16 people while injuring 18 others. In June this year, a head-on private bus collision in Lunugamwehera injured 15 people. On September 19, a road accident claimed the life of the Dambulla National School principal."
  },
