  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-24-dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d",
    "date_str": "2026-09-24",
    "description": "Why are ministers  still complaining about bureaucratic bottlenecks?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Why-are-ministers-still-complaining-about-bureaucratic-bottlenecks/172-351033",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790231408.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "729a2888f1d630502c74d6e8fb11976d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2008,
    "chunk_text": "Why are ministers  still complaining about bureaucratic bottlenecks?\n\nFormer Elections Commissioner Mahinda Deshapriya warned of the damage the \u2018deep state\u2019 could do if it is not kept in check\n\nThe public grants a mandate to the government during elections to execute its policies and programmes. No official has the right to subvert or act against this popular democratic mandate\n\nThe Deputy Minister of Transport and Highways, Dr. Prasanna Gunasena, has echoed the complaints made some time ago by Ministers K.D. Lalkantha and Sunil Handunnetti regarding the bureaucracy\u2019s lack of cooperation with government programmes.\n\nIn December 2024 \u2013 right after the landslide victory of the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) at the Parliamentary election -- Minister Lalkantha accused these state officials of creating bottlenecks to implementing the government\u2019s policies and plans.\n\nMinister Handunnetthi remarked that officials who grew accustomed to following the corrupt policies and practices of previous regimes are hesitant to abandon them. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake also warned state officials during a speech at a meeting in April last year, saying, \u201cChange your practices, or else we will change them.\u201d\n\nAnd now, Dr. Gunasena has stated during a recent rally that although the (NPP) obtained government power through the 2024 Presidential and Parliamentary elections, it has not yet fully acquired state power. He made these remarks while responding to criticisms from some quarters that the government\u2019s performance is inadequate.\n\nIt is obvious that the policies and laws formulated by any government must be implemented through\u00a0 state officials. A majority of the officials now serving within Sri Lanka\u2019s state bureaucracy were appointed by previous governments. Many of those holding senior positions politically assisted the parties that ruled the country in the past,\u00a0 and secured those posts as political appointees. Consequently, they may not favour the NPP government."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-24-dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d",
    "date_str": "2026-09-24",
    "description": "Why are ministers  still complaining about bureaucratic bottlenecks?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Why-are-ministers-still-complaining-about-bureaucratic-bottlenecks/172-351033",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790231408.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-dailymirrorlk-dda2bd9d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0a2160f983355b205d3d44b17ee42d6e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "It is obvious that the policies and laws formulated by any government must be implemented through\u00a0 state officials. A majority of the officials now serving within Sri Lanka\u2019s state bureaucracy were appointed by previous governments. Many of those holding senior positions politically assisted the parties that ruled the country in the past,\u00a0 and secured those posts as political appointees. Consequently, they may not favour the NPP government.\n\nHowever, in a practical sense, the Deputy Minister\u2019s assertion appears valid. Despite many factors contributing to the voters\u2019 choice of political parties at elections, it is widely accepted that the public votes a party into power based on its policies and programmes. Therefore, once that party takes office, it is obligated to fulfill the policies, promises, and plans it presented to the electorate.\n\nThis can only be achieved through the officials operating within the administrative machinery. Therefore, the independence of state officials is relative. No matter what their personal political affiliations are,\u00a0 or which past regime appointed them, officials must take all possible measures to execute the policies and plans of the incumbent government.\n\nHence, there is a clear justification when a new government takes office and appoints new officials to top positions within state corporations and institutions. Even if these appear to be political appointments, there is nothing wrong with appointing trustworthy officials to senior posts to execute people\u2019s mandate. One cannot implement government\u2019s policies by leaving officials who oppose the administration in those critical roles. Yet, the possibility of this view leading to nepotism and cronyism is also not to be ruled out."
  },
