    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8642a106d074a2868046a10bd1d9f4ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "Hence, there is a clear justification when a new government takes office and appoints new officials to top positions within state corporations and institutions. Even if these appear to be political appointments, there is nothing wrong with appointing trustworthy officials to senior posts to execute people\u2019s mandate. One cannot implement government\u2019s policies by leaving officials who oppose the administration in those critical roles. Yet, the possibility of this view leading to nepotism and cronyism is also not to be ruled out.\n\nThis is likely what Ministers Lalkantha, Handunnetthi, and Deputy Minister Prasanna Gunasena are referring to. Furthermore, Prasanna Gunasena pointed out that if the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) had come to power through the insurrections of 1971 or 1988, this problem would not have arisen. This is also true: if a party captures power through an armed revolution or a rebellion, bureaucrats are unlikely to push back.\n\nThe public grants a mandate to the government during elections to execute its policies and programmes. No official has the right to subvert or act against this popular democratic mandate.\n\nAt the same time, if officials genuinely perceive that certain government actions could severely harm the nation or its people, channels should exist for them to raise their concerns directly with the President or relevant Ministers.\n\nHowever, in the past, neither did ministers come forward to point out the shortcomings of presidents, nor did bureaucrats step up to critique the flaws of presidents or ministers. When Gotabaya Rajapaksa, upon taking office, decided to slash taxes equivalent to one-third of state revenues, no one opposed it. Even former minister Bandula Gunawardane, hailed as an economic expert, justified and defended the move."
  },
