    "md5": "ed57b18dfb4adf653d70c86f1cbe90a3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "However, in the past, neither did ministers come forward to point out the shortcomings of presidents, nor did bureaucrats step up to critique the flaws of presidents or ministers. When Gotabaya Rajapaksa, upon taking office, decided to slash taxes equivalent to one-third of state revenues, no one opposed it. Even former minister Bandula Gunawardane, hailed as an economic expert, justified and defended the move.\n\nOn the other hand, certain past leaders managed to bully and control not only the state machinery but the judiciary as well. Public officials were regularly coerced into carrying out illegal or inappropriate acts. President Ranil Wickremesinghe publicly threatening the officials who hesitated to convert the historic Nuwara Eliya post office into a hotel is a classic example.\n\nFor this reason, conflicts between the bureaucratic apparatus and political leadership seldom occurred in the past. But now, the situation has changed. Opportunities for corruption within the state machinery are shrinking. Against this backdrop, officials might cause friction, delays, and complications in executing government plans. It is then that the ministers complain that they lack real state power. Former Election Commission Chairman Mahinda Deshapriya highlighted the danger of this dynamic on his Facebook page last year.\n\nDeshapriya noted in that post that if the \u2018deep state\u2019 is not kept in check, what happened to the governments of Patrice Lumumba (Congo), Sukarno (Indonesia), and Salvador Allende (Chile) will happen here as well. All of these progressive leaders were ousted from power through internal armed coups.\n\nThis is precisely what Deshapriya was warning against. However, the fact that ministers are complaining about bureaucratic bottlenecks even two years after assuming power suggests that something is fundamentally missing within the government."
  },
