    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ed4343d0ed5a92205c9cafb0b762b671",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1776,
    "chunk_text": "No regrets whatsoever, accused still hold onto their ideology: ASG\n\nDelivering the judgment, High Court Judge Navaratne Marasinghe observed that a conspiracy does not require all those involved to be physically present together or to have directly communicated with one another. He explained that an agreement to commit an offence could be formed either expressly or by implication and that individuals who joined the conspiracy at different stages could nevertheless be considered parties to the same conspiracy.\n\nMarking one of the darkest chapters of cruelty in Sri Lanka\u2019s recent history, the Colombo High Court Trial-at-Bar comprising Judges Navaratne Marasinghe, Ramanathan Kannan and Sujeewa Nishshanka yesterday imposed sentences of rigorous imprisonment on 15 accused in the Easter Sunday attacks case.\n\n- Accused No - 1, 9, 10, 16 and 18 - 220 years of rigorous imprisonment each.\n\n- Accused - 2, 3, 4 and 5 - 240 years of rigorous imprisonment each.\n\n- Accused - 6, 7, 11, 15 and 19 - 200 years of rigorous imprisonment each.\n\nThe Court held that the prosecution had failed to establish the charges against nine accused beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nThe 17th accused, Yasin Bawa Abdul Rauf, died in prison during the course of the trial.\n\nThe Court imposed separate sentences in respect of the various incidents and charges.\n\nIn the case of the first accused, Mohamed Ibrahim Mohamed Naufer alias Naufer Moulavi, two individual charges were also considered, for which he was sentenced to 20 years of rigorous imprisonment on each charge.\n\nFor the attack planned against St. Anthony\u2019s Church, Kochchikade, the accused were sentenced to,\n\n- 20 years in respect of the persons killed in the attack; and\n\nThe sentences relating to that incident are to run concurrently."
  },
