    "md5": "cade056e8ab8416733827f75a25ae3e9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "For the attack planned against St. Anthony\u2019s Church, Kochchikade, the accused were sentenced to,\n\n- 20 years in respect of the persons killed in the attack; and\n\nThe sentences relating to that incident are to run concurrently.\n\nIn respect of the second bombing targeting the Shangri-La Hotel, separate 20-year terms were also imposed in relation to the conspiracy, deaths and injuries.\n\nThe Court ordered that the relevant sentences relating to each incident be served concurrently within that incident, while the sentences relating to the separate incidents would commence after the completion of the sentence imposed for the preceding incident.\n\nAccordingly, the first accused, Naufer Moulavi, was sentenced to a total of 220 years of rigorous imprisonment.\n\nSimilar sentencing orders were made against the other convicted accused in respect of the individual incidents and the separate charges applicable to each of them.\n\nThe Court further ordered that, in accordance with Section 4 of the Prevention of Terrorism Act, the immovable and movable property of the accused convicted under the relevant provisions would be forfeited to the State.\n\nThe order also prevents the transfer or sale of such property from the date on which the sentence takes effect.\n\nThe trial at the Colombo High Court Trial-at-Bar Case No. TAB 2972/21, which commenced on October 10, 2023, concluded on August 24 this year.\n\nGovernment Analyst reports, DNA reports and medico-legal reports relating to the persons who died in the attacks were also produced as evidence by the prosecution.\n\nAnother significant feature of the trial was that 2,076 productions, including explosives, chemicals used to manufacture explosives, swords and firearms, were produced by the prosecution before the Court.\n\nAccordingly, Trial-at-Bar President Judge Navaratne Marasinghe commenced delivering the judgment."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
