    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ac050538dfe14b0c392ff917217b4062",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1843,
    "chunk_text": "Another significant feature of the trial was that 2,076 productions, including explosives, chemicals used to manufacture explosives, swords and firearms, were produced by the prosecution before the Court.\n\nAccordingly, Trial-at-Bar President Judge Navaratne Marasinghe commenced delivering the judgment.\n\nThe present case against the accused concerns eight suicide bomb attacks carried out on April 21, 2019, by eight Muslim religious extremists led by Zahran Hashim, targeting St. Anthony\u2019s Church in Kochchikade, Kotahena, St. Sebastian\u2019s Church in Katuwapitiya, Zion Church in Batticaloa, the Shangri-La Hotel in Colombo, three tourist hotels and the Tropical Inn restaurant.\n\nSubsequently, before the special three-member High Court appointed by the Chief Justice in August 2021, the Attorney General filed this case against all 25 accused on 23,270 indictments.\n\nThis case will also enter world history as a case filed with the highest number of charges against an accused or a group of accused in the world.\n\nDuring the trial, the proceedings had to be postponed due to the accused not having legal representation.\n\nAccordingly, during the trial, the accused were informed that they could represent themselves and were also given relevant instructions.\n\nAs many of the accused conducted lengthy cross-examinations of witnesses regarding their evidence during the trial, the case had to be heard until after 10 p.m.\n\nAs the accused personally faced the trial, arrangements were made to provide them with all the necessary materials, including pens and pencils.\n\nThe president of the High court trial-at-Bar stated that the Court had paid special attention to the fact that the accused had conducted extremist lectures before the attacks, conducted weapons training workshops and maintained safe locations to carry out such activities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
