    "language": "en",
    "md5": "45588ec88e408dfe51b1732ea4127824",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "The president of the High court trial-at-Bar stated that the Court had paid special attention to the fact that the accused had conducted extremist lectures before the attacks, conducted weapons training workshops and maintained safe locations to carry out such activities.\n\nColombo High Court Judge Navaratne Marasinghe,\u201cThe indictment against the accused in this case alleges that they had committed offences punishable under the provisions of the Prevention of Terrorism Act by conspiring to carry out the attacks, aiding and abetting, propagating extremist ideas and creating disharmony among races.\n\nThe Court has paid special attention to the fact that the accused conducted extremist lectures before the attacks, conducted weapons training workshops and maintained safe locations to carry out such activities.\n\nPersons involved in a conspiracy do not necessarily have to be standing in a line at the same place. A person can become involved in a conspiracy directly or indirectly, at the beginning, in the middle or at the end. Anyone who becomes involved in such a manner is a party to the conspiracy.\n\nThe Court also considered the statements made in defence by each accused during the trial.\n\nThe Court rejects and dismisses the defence statements of the first, second, third, fourth and fifth accused. The Court observes that those statements had been made after due consideration due to the seriousness of the charges levelled against them.\n\nThe Court also paid special attention to a video recording contained in a pen drive, which was produced before the Court by the prosecution.\n\nIt was revealed before the Court that eight persons, including Zahran, were present in the relevant video recording and that they had been clearly identified from the footage."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
