    "md5": "4700a4ae5ba5cd941ba85953deb6442e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1924,
    "chunk_text": "The first accused, Mohamed Ibrahim Mohamed Naufer alias Naufer Moulavi, stated that according to the teachings of Islam, it was not appropriate to make requests from authorities. Therefore, he said that he would accept any sentencing order imposed by the Court.\n\n\u201cMy Lord, in this case, During this trial, I felt as though I wished the hands of the clock would turn faster. That is how distressing the examination of this evidence was.\n\nCan one group of people take away the happiness of others? When Zahran was giving those lectures, there was a T-56 and a sword. Is this what religion means? Can others not follow another religion? \u2018We are going to carry out a series of attacks tomorrow. We must go happily.\u2019\n\nPeople were peacefully sitting in churches and praying on Easter Sunday for the resurrection of Jesus Christ. It was a very happy day for those people.\n\nThose who were having meals at hotels - none of these people had done anything wrong to anyone. Now, although those people are alive, they are like dead people. Those in wheelchairs have no sense of what happened. What happened to these completely disabled people?\n\n\"None of them have any regret. They are still holding on to their ideology.\u201d She said.\n\nShe further made submissions to Court by highlighting Supreme Court judgments, including Sumandiran v. Harsha Ilupitiya, and explained the matters that should be considered by Court when imposing sentences.\n\nShe also referred to the judgment relating to the Central Bank bomb attack, in which Prabhakaran was sentenced to 200 years of imprisonment. The incident took place in 1996 and the judgment was delivered by Court in 2002.\n\nHighlighting this case, she stated that the then counsel appearing for the Attorney General\u2019s Department in that case was the president of the Trial-at-Bar, Judge Navaratne Marasinghe, who was also one of the judges hearing the present Easter Sunday attacks case."
  },
