    "md5": "357960406a2ed838a77cabdfe88414a9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1669,
    "chunk_text": "The ruling continued: \"The record lacks factual support for Defendants' contention that the revocation of Plaintiffs' hard passes will in fact protect national security or that national security will be endangered if the Court orders their passes reinstated while this litigation proceeds.\"\n\nIn its defence of the ban, the Trump administration cited a number of articles published by the outlets, including some about construction at the White House, including work on a new ballroom.\n\nIn its original filing, the Trump administration cited declarations from defence department officials supporting the ban - but the judge said they had \"no direct knowledge of how reporters operate in the White House, or about any of the facts at issue here\".\n\nTed Boutrous, the lawyer representing the news outlets, told CNN: \"This is a strong ruling vindicating freedom of the press, due process and the rule of law.\"\n\nOn Monday, major US TV networks pulled out of White House pool duties in protest at the ban. The arrangement sees media organisations share material with each other to save on costs and space when broadcasting presidential events to the American public.\n\nForty-nine media organisations - including CBS News, The New York Times and Fox News - also filed a legal brief in support of CNN, MS NOW and Politico's lawsuit.\n\nTrump frequently criticises news organisations, and his administration has launched legal action against a series of outlets, including the New York Times, the Wall Street Journal and the BBC.\n\nSome of the lawsuits have resulted in media organisations agreeing to pay multi-million-dollar settlements. Trump's lawsuit against the BBC is ongoing."
