    "md5": "66ce51fbefb53676e7282c81e5e50397",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1275,
    "chunk_text": "She said delays in justice had affected the lives of ordinary citizens while also benefiting an elite, as prolonged investigations and legal proceedings had contributed to a cycle in which accountability was weakened whenever governments changed.\n\nShe cited international examples, noting that the United Kingdom increased the judicial retirement age from 70 to 75 in 2021, while Hong Kong increased it from 65 to 70 in 2019.\n\nThe Prime Minister also rejected concerns that the amendment would give the Executive special powers over the Judiciary or alter the balance of power among the institutions of government.\n\nShe said the amendment should therefore be debated in the context of the Government\u2019s broader efforts to strengthen the independence, efficiency and accessibility of the justice system.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya also said growing public confidence in the possibility of building stronger institutions and a more accountable system of governance was an indication of the transformation taking place in the country.\n\nShe cited the response of young people following the recent railway strike, including those who purchased additional tickets after services resumed, as a small but meaningful indication of a growing sense of responsibility towards public institutions."
