    "md5": "20548f1f3fad6eb0e4efb8102017fd0b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "PickMe CEO Jiffry Zulfer said the company\u2019s focus was on making the different parts of a Book Fair visit work together more smoothly. \u201cFor visitors, the experience begins before they reach the BMICH and continues until they get home. Being able to buy an entry ticket through PickMe, use a QR code to enter the fair and arrange transport through the same app brings greater convenience to that entire journey. This year, we are strengthening that experience with a dedicated pickup arrangement at Gate 3, making it easier for visitors and drivers to find each other when it is time to leave,\u201d he said.\n\nFor the journey home on PickMe, the dedicated waiting area at Gate 3 will accommodate up to 10 vehicles at a time, with vehicles rotating as drivers collect passengers and depart. The arrangement gives drivers a designated place to wait within the premises and is intended to encourage more drivers to serve the Fair.\n\nPickMe will place wayfinders and assistants at key points inside the venue to assist the visitors with the pickup arrangements and direct them towards Gate 3. App notifications and social media updates will provide further guidance, helping visitors plan their departure while still inside the exhibition.\n\nThe initiative follows PickMe\u2019s experience at previous Fairs, where congestion, multiple exits and one-way traffic on surrounding roads made pickups difficult. While vehicle availability will depend on demand, the dedicated waiting area and on-site coordination are designed to make connecting passengers with the drivers straightforward and departures more convenient.\n\n\u201cAfter spending time browsing and buying books, visitors may be leaving with several bags, children or elderly family members. A clearly identified pickup point, with support on the field available to guide them, can make a meaningful difference. It also helps the drivers registered on the PickMe app collect passengers with greater ease,\u201d Zulfer said."
  },
