    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "This is where the coordination-versus-transformation distinction is sharpest. Sri Lanka ranked 96th of 190 economies on the World Bank\u2019s \u201ctrading across borders\u201d pillar in 2020 [17]. In the World Bank\u2019s Logistics Performance Index, Sri Lanka\u2019s overall ranking improved from 92nd to 73rd of roughly 139 countries between the 2018 and 2023 rounds \u2014 yet its customs sub-ranking moved in the opposite direction, slipping from 77th to 84th over the same period [18]. A country whose broad logistics environment is improving while its customs administration specifically regresses is not suffering from a missing piece in a puzzle; it is showing that a core piece of institutional machinery is actively deteriorating relative to the rest of the system around it.\n\nThe preference-dependent export model built between 1977 and 2005 was well suited to a quota-protected trading world that no longer exists; reassembling its components more efficiently does not address the fact that Vietnam and Bangladesh have since built cost and scale advantages Sri Lanka cannot now match on the same terms.\n\nSri Lanka has not lacked planning documents: the Export Development Board (1977), the National Export Strategy (2014, then 2018\u201322) and the current National Export Development Plan (2026\u201330, targeting 14\u201315% of GDP and $36 billion in exports by 2030) represent three successive planning cycles across nearly five decades without the underlying export share ever recovering its year-2000 peak.\n\nCustoms and border administration \u2014 identified by both this paper and the original commentary as central \u2014 has regressed on the specific sub-metric that measures it, even while adjacent logistics indicators improved, suggesting the institution itself needs restructuring rather than better placement within a wider plan."
