    "chunk_size_bytes": 1359,
    "chunk_text": "Customs and border administration \u2014 identified by both this paper and the original commentary as central \u2014 has regressed on the specific sub-metric that measures it, even while adjacent logistics indicators improved, suggesting the institution itself needs restructuring rather than better placement within a wider plan.\n\nReplace, rather than incrementally digitise, legacy customs procedures with a risk-based, largely automated clearance system, with the customs-specific LPI sub-score (not only the aggregate score) tracked as the accountability metric.\n\nConvert Board of Investment incentives from open-ended, low-labour-cost tax holidays to time-bound, performance-linked support tied to export revenue, technology transfer and measured productivity gains \u2014 consistent with the East Asian model but adapted to current global value chains.\n\nBuild institutional continuity into trade-strategy leadership (a standing, professionally staffed body rather than a minister-dependent initiative) so that strategy does not depend on the tenure or survival of individual political figures, as it did between 1977 and 1994.\n\nSet diversification targets benchmarked explicitly against the trajectories of Vietnam and Bangladesh since 2010, rather than against Sri Lanka\u2019s own historical export levels, since the latter measures recovery, not competitiveness."
