    "chunk_text": "After 22A, NPP Govt to begin work on new Constitution\n\nColombo, Sep. 25 (Daily Mirror) - Despite the Government\u2019s sudden move to enact the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution, which provides for an increase in the retirement age of superior court judges, the introduction of a new Constitution, a key election promise of the ruling National People\u2019s Power (NPP), will come under formal discussion within the party next year, an official said.\n\nThe NPP, in its election manifesto, 'A Thriving Nation, A Beautiful Life, promised to repeal the current Constitution and replace it with a new Constitution, scrapping the executive presidential system.\n\nHowever, the \"Government opted to amend the Constitution first through a piecemeal approach by introducing the 22nd Amendment, a move that drew flak from many quarters, including the Mahanayake Theras.\n\nOpposition parties have also resisted the enactment of the new Bill.\n\nThe vote on the Bill is due to be taken this evening in Parliament, with wide anticipation that the Government, which commands a two-thirds majority, will secure its passage through Parliament conveniently.\n\nAsked about the current status of Constitution-making, NPP Secretary Dr. Nihal Abeysinghe said a serious discussion on a new Constitution would begin next year, with a focus on the fate of the Provincial Council system.\n\nDr. Abeysinghe said the discussion would begin with a plan to conclude the process as early as possible.\n\n\u201cWe have to discuss it among ourselves first. The new Constitution and the Provincial Council system are the two matters which are yet to be touched upon,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said past constitution-making proposals would be studied as part of this exercise to explore whether they could be incorporated into the NPP\u2019s thinking.\n\nThe JVP, the key constituent of the NPP, has been advocating the abolition of the executive presidency for decades."
