    "md5": "0d58da9937c140dc794cd962db692e89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1655,
    "chunk_text": "Rainwater harvesting in Sri Lanka: How to start at home before the dry months\n\nRainwater harvesting is the practice of collecting the rain that falls on your roof, storing it in a covered tank or drum, and using it later. In Sri Lanka, it can cut water bills, help households through dry spells, and ease pressure on drains. A single 100 m\u00b2 roof collects about 2,000 litres from one heavy shower.\n\nThe timing matters this year. As of September 19, drought has affected 169,071 people from 51,959 families across 10 districts, according to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC). Monaragala is the worst hit, with more than 42,000 people affected, and water bowsers are now supplying over 142,000 people.\n\nThe Meteorology Department puts the chance of a strong El Ni\u00f1o developing between October and December at 98 percent. The DMC expects heavier rain from late September, followed by a significant water shortage between January and March 2027. The rain of the coming weeks may be the last good rain for months, and most of it will be lost.\n\nRainwater harvesting means collecting the rain that falls on your roof instead of letting it disappear into the stormwater drains. At its simplest, you need a roof, a gutter and a container. Sri Lankan villages have done this for generations, often with nothing more than a barrel under the roof's edge during the rains.\n\nA more complete system adds a first-flush diverter, a fitting that throws away the first and dirtiest water off the roof (dust, leaves, bird droppings) before the cleaner water flows into your tank. If you plan to drink the water, you also add a filter. Neither needs specialised equipment."
