    "md5": "6801b70f6a702d07c8ce3ced6bf5b102",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "A more complete system adds a first-flush diverter, a fitting that throws away the first and dirtiest water off the roof (dust, leaves, bird droppings) before the cleaner water flows into your tank. If you plan to drink the water, you also add a filter. Neither needs specialised equipment.\n\nSchools are another large, untapped source. In 2024, speaking at a seminar organised by the Institution of Engineers Sri Lanka (IESL), Ariyananda said Colombo has about 340 schools with an average roof area of 1,000 m\u00b2. By her estimate, those roofs could collect 548,800 cubic metres of rainwater a year. \"If we calculate the value of water at 60 rupees a cubic meter, it comes to 32,928,000 rupees,\" she said.\n\nThe law already points in this direction. Sri Lanka adopted a National Rainwater Harvesting Policy in 2005, and regulations under the UDA (Amendment) Act No. 36 of 2007, published in Gazette No. 1597/08 in 2009, require rainwater harvesting for many new buildings in UDA areas. Implementation is still incomplete, though.\n\nWhat are the benefits of rainwater harvesting for Sri Lankan homes?\n\nThe first benefit is money. Many household jobs don't need drinking-quality water, such as watering the garden, washing the car, mopping floors and flushing toilets. Rainwater can handle all of them, so you use less piped water and your bill comes down.\n\nThe second is security. If supply in your area is intermittent, or if the dry spell forecast for early next year arrives as expected, a full tank keeps you going until the tap comes back on or the bowser arrives.\n\nThere is a neighbourhood benefit too. In a heavy downpour, every litre sitting in your tank is a litre that isn't flooding already overloaded drains on your street.\n\nOver the long term it simply makes sense. Our rain comes in seasons, but we need water every day. Saving some of the wet months' surplus for the dry months is one of the easiest ways a household can become more water secure, year after year."
  },
