    "md5": "8c6e94a6915a3efe014de2e63e82b067",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1956,
    "chunk_text": "Over the long term it simply makes sense. Our rain comes in seasons, but we need water every day. Saving some of the wet months' surplus for the dry months is one of the easiest ways a household can become more water secure, year after year.\n\nFigure 1: How the five parts connect, from roof to tap. Every opening marked in red needs a tight lid or fine mesh.\n\nTo work out how much rainwater a roof can collect, multiply the roof area in square metres by the rainfall in millimetres and by a runoff factor of 0.8 to 0.9 for tile or metal roofs. The answer is in litres. A 100 m\u00b2 roof collects about 2,000 litres from a single 25 mm shower.\n\nLitres collected = roof area (m\u00b2) \u00d7 rainfall (mm) \u00d7 runoff factor\n\nThe runoff factor allows for water lost to splashing and evaporation. Use a lower figure for roofing materials other than tile or metal.\n\nOver a whole year, the same 100 m\u00b2 roof in an area that gets 2,000 mm of rain (normal for much of the wet zone) could collect between 160,000 and 180,000 litres. You won't need a tank anywhere near that big. The formula is there so you can size a tank to what your household actually uses, rather than guessing.\n\nFigure 2: The sizing formula, with two worked examples for a 100 m\u00b2 roof.\n\nA drum is the cheapest start. A repurposed drum with a mesh cover, placed under a downpipe, is a real system. A single 200-litre drum is enough to noticeably cut the water you use for the garden and cleaning. If you're unsure, begin here.\n\nA polyethylene tank is the middle ground. Poly tanks are light, quick to install and reasonably priced. They suit most homes that want more storage without any building work.\n\nConcrete or ferrocement lasts longest. Precast concrete and ferrocement tanks cost the most and need space, but they pay for themselves over time through lower water bills.\n\nChoose the downpipe that drains the largest section of your roof.\n\nSet a drum or tank under the downpipe on a firm, level base."
