    "md5": "8a2fa0de8799866ca00fe8f7b8c1c971",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "Concrete or ferrocement lasts longest. Precast concrete and ferrocement tanks cost the most and need space, but they pay for themselves over time through lower water bills.\n\nChoose the downpipe that drains the largest section of your roof.\n\nSet a drum or tank under the downpipe on a firm, level base.\n\nThis part is not optional. The National Dengue Control Unit has recorded more than 99,000 dengue cases and 76 deaths in Sri Lanka so far in 2026, with Gampaha and Colombo together accounting for over 39,000 of those cases. The mosquitoes that spread dengue breed in clean, still water, which is exactly what a rain tank holds. Even a small gap in a lid is enough for them to get in and lay eggs, putting your family and your neighbours at risk. These habits prevent that:\n\nCover every container completely. Every tank, drum and barrel needs a tight-fitting lid or fine mesh screen, with no gaps.\n\nScreen every opening. Put fine mesh over the inlet, the overflow pipe and the first-flush chamber as well as the top of the tank.\n\nKeep gutters clear. A blocked gutter holds pools of water on the roof where nobody sees them. Check and clean gutters regularly, especially after heavy rain.\n\nCheck your setup once a week. Walk around the system and look for gaps, sagging mesh, and pooled water nearby.\n\nScrub the tank walls when you clean it. Mosquito eggs can stick to the inside walls and survive after the water is gone, so rinsing isn't enough. Scrubbing also stops algae from building up.\n\nEmpty anything you can't cover. Buckets, basins, and pots collecting rain around the house should be emptied and turned upside down.\n\nLooked after this way, a covered tank is safer than the scattered puddles and open containers found around most homes.\n\nNot sure if it\u2019s dengue or seasonal flu? Read the full Daily Mirror guide here\n\nWhere can you learn more about rainwater harvesting in Sri Lanka?"
