    "md5": "2d7e6deecce6050f5b41de9ec0aab695",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "Looked after this way, a covered tank is safer than the scattered puddles and open containers found around most homes.\n\nNot sure if it\u2019s dengue or seasonal flu? Read the full Daily Mirror guide here\n\nWhere can you learn more about rainwater harvesting in Sri Lanka?\n\nOnly after treatment. A first-flush diverter gets rid of the dirtiest runoff, and drinking water should then be filtered and boiled. Most households that harvest rain use it for non-drinking purposes and keep municipal or well water for cooking and drinking.\n\nA house in Colombo with a 100 m\u00b2 roof can collect about 192,000 litres of rainwater a year, according to the Lanka Rain Water Harvesting Forum. For a family of four, that works out to about 130 litres per person per day.\n\nIt depends on the storage you choose. A basic drum and diverter costs relatively little. A ferrocement or concrete tank costs more up front but pays back over time through lower water bills.\n\nFor many new buildings, yes. Regulations under the UDA (Amendment) Act No. 36 of 2007, published in Gazette No. 1597/08 in April 2009, require rainwater harvesting for many new developments in UDA areas, with plots under 150 m\u00b2 (about six perches) exempt. The regulations also say storage tanks must be covered. A simple drum at an existing home needs no approval.\n\nAs a supplement, yes. Going into a dry spell with a full tank gives you a buffer, though it won't fully replace piped supply in a long drought.\n\nOnly if it is left open or neglected. A tight lid, mesh on every opening, clear gutters and a weekly check keep it safe.\n\nNow, while the rain is falling. With a water shortage forecast for January to March 2027, every shower you catch this season is water you won't have to buy or wait for later."
