    "md5": "9d54c580d7f0694da3bc75f561ade35e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "Tea export volumes fall through August as dollar prices weaken\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tea exports fell in August and over the first eight months of 2026, with higher average prices in rupees failing to offset lower shipment volumes, according to Sri Lanka Customs figures compiled by Forbes & Walker Research.\n\nAugust exports totalled 22.82 million kg, down 887,079 kg, or 3.7 percent, from 23.70 million kg a year earlier. The average free on board (FOB) value rose 5.8 percent to Rs. 1,910.27 per kg, but fell to US$5.74 from US$5.99 when expressed in dollars.\n\nFor January to August, exports declined 8.23 million kg, or 4.7 percent, to 166.32 million kg. The average FOB value increased to Rs. 1,837.70 per kg from Rs. 1,756.23, while its dollar equivalent slipped to US$5.71 from US$5.88.\n\nBased on the reported volumes and average FOB values, export earnings for the eight months were approximately Rs. 305.7 billion, compared with Rs. 306.6 billion a year earlier. The calculation indicates that the increase in the rupee price per kilogram did not fully compensate for the fall in shipments.\n\nTea packets made up the largest share of exports during the eight months, at 74.20 million kg, but also recorded the biggest volume decline, falling 4.67 million kg from a year earlier. Bulk tea shipments dropped 3.15 million kg to 69.74 million kg, while tea bag exports declined by about 686,000 kg to 16.57 million kg.\n\nInstant tea was the only category to post volume growth over the period, rising by nearly 490,000 kg to 2.73 million kg. Green tea exports fell by about 202,000 kg to 3.08 million kg.\n\nThe August figures showed a similar pattern. Tea bag shipments fell by 486,252 kg to 1.98 million kg, while bulk tea exports declined by 425,882 kg to 9.82 million kg. Green tea exports edged lower. Tea packets rose slightly to 10.31 million kg, and instant tea shipments increased to 302,750 kg."
  },
