    "chunk_size_bytes": 747,
    "chunk_text": "Over 53,000 people with kidney disease receive Rs. 10,000 monthly allowance: Minister\n\nColombo, Sep. 25 (Daily Mirror) - As many as 53,604 people suffering from kidney disease are currently receiving a monthly allowance of Rs. 10,000, Minister of Social Security Upali Pannilage told Parliament today.\n\nThe Minister made the statement in response to an oral question raised by Opposition MP Rohana Bandara regarding assistance provided to people affected by kidney disease.\n\nPannilage rejected the MP\u2019s claim that the payment of the allowance was being restricted, stating that there were no such restrictions.\n\n\u201cMost of the people receiving the special allowance are also beneficiaries of the Aswesuma welfare programme,\u201d the Minister said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d",
    "date_str": "2026-09-25",
    "description": "Development banks should return to equity stakes and board seats, says DFCC CEO",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Development-banks-should-return-to-equity-stakes-and-board-seats-says-DFCC-CEO/215-351126",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790317798.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "eef37f98555b1380061f944d03ec1662",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "Development banks should return to equity stakes and board seats, says DFCC CEO\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s gap lies in access to finance rather than inclusion\n\nLenders urged to move from collateral to cash flow-based lending\n\nAny new development bank must be independent and commercially viable\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s development banks should return to taking equity stakes and board seats in the businesses they finance, as they once did, while lenders at large must shift from collateral to cash flow-based lending to reach start-ups, DFCC Bank Director/Chief Executive Officer Thimal Perera stressed.\n\nPerera, who is also an ADFIAP Board Member, was speaking on a panel at the 49th Annual Meetings of the Association of Development Financing Institutions in Asia and the Pacific (ADFIAP), hosted by his bank in Colombo this week.\n\nHe drew a sharp distinction between two problems that are often treated as one.\n\n\u201cIf you look at Sri Lanka and we talk about financial inclusivity, I don\u2019t think we have an inclusivity issue, because everybody has access to a bank account. The issue we have is access to finance,\u201d he observed.\n\nSmall entrepreneurs, small businesses and start-ups, he pointed out, find it impossible to secure bank facilities, leaving them to rely on other means or venture capital. Banks, in his view, must find ways to assess such borrowers and lend to them at a reasonable risk and a reasonable price.\n\n\u201cWithout supporting that segment and getting new entrepreneurs and new businesses off the ground, this economy cannot really achieve the objectives that it set for itself,\u201d he warned.\n\nHe saw opportunities well beyond fintech, in areas such as agritech and pharmatech. He cited countries such as Germany, where start-up accelerators and financial support form part of a wider ecosystem, and contended that banks have a key role to play in building a similar one in Sri Lanka."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d",
    "date_str": "2026-09-25",
    "description": "Development banks should return to equity stakes and board seats, says DFCC CEO",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Development-banks-should-return-to-equity-stakes-and-board-seats-says-DFCC-CEO/215-351126",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790317798.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-1fc5e91d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "89b406837267130f8e92ef9f30d9ee72",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1421,
    "chunk_text": "He saw opportunities well beyond fintech, in areas such as agritech and pharmatech. He cited countries such as Germany, where start-up accelerators and financial support form part of a wider ecosystem, and contended that banks have a key role to play in building a similar one in Sri Lanka.\n\nGoing further, Perera identified the retreat of development banks from ownership and governance roles as the biggest gap in the system.\n\n\u201cIn the good old days, development banks used to participate in equity and had board seats, so they had an involvement in the running of the company,\u201d he recalled.\n\nThe International Finance Corporation (IFC), the World Bank Group\u2019s private sector arm, still held US$ 12,157 million in equity investments at the end of 2025. That is some 17 percent of its US$ 73,224 million disbursed portfolio, invested both directly in companies and through private equity funds. In Germany, which Perera cited as a model, KfW Capital, the venture arm of the state-owned development bank KfW, committed EUR 748 million in 2025. It has now invested around EUR 2.9 billion in 153 venture capital funds, reaching some 2,900 start-ups through fund managers rather than direct stakes of its own. That suggests equity participation remains part of the development finance toolkit, albeit in a changed form.\n\nMeanwhile, Perera held that the case for development banking in Sri Lanka had already been proven."
  },
