    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "The International Finance Corporation (IFC), the World Bank Group\u2019s private sector arm, still held US$ 12,157 million in equity investments at the end of 2025. That is some 17 percent of its US$ 73,224 million disbursed portfolio, invested both directly in companies and through private equity funds. In Germany, which Perera cited as a model, KfW Capital, the venture arm of the state-owned development bank KfW, committed EUR 748 million in 2025. It has now invested around EUR 2.9 billion in 153 venture capital funds, reaching some 2,900 start-ups through fund managers rather than direct stakes of its own. That suggests equity participation remains part of the development finance toolkit, albeit in a changed form.\n\nMeanwhile, Perera held that the case for development banking in Sri Lanka had already been proven.\n\n\u201cThen you are no different, and you are in fact at a disadvantage when compared to other commercial banks, because we had restrictions in terms of opening zero-cost CASA accounts and things like that,\u201d he reflected.\n\nDFCC\u2019s CASA ratio stood at 24.49 percent at the end of 2025, against an average of 32.9 percent for ten banks accounting for about 95 percent of the industry, as per an industry review by Boston Consulting Group. That places DFCC at roughly three-quarters of the industry level, a gap that still reflects its development banking legacy. The ratio edged up to 24.99 percent by the end of June 2026, or 29.42 percent when medium to long-term concessionary credit lines are included.\n\nDFCC completed its acquisition of Standard Chartered\u2019s wealth and retail banking business in Sri Lanka on 1 August 2026, taking on around 50,000 customers and 260 employees for a final premium of Rs.3,655 million. Standard Chartered Bank Sri Lanka Chief Executive Officer Bingumal Thewarathanthri shared the panel with Perera."
  },
