    "md5": "b98560b9650bb70204da3407d7990794",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1208,
    "chunk_text": "DFCC completed its acquisition of Standard Chartered\u2019s wealth and retail banking business in Sri Lanka on 1 August 2026, taking on around 50,000 customers and 260 employees for a final premium of Rs.3,655 million. Standard Chartered Bank Sri Lanka Chief Executive Officer Bingumal Thewarathanthri shared the panel with Perera.\n\n\u201cThese need to be run independently without political interference, they need to be commercially viable, they need to be able to stand on their own feet, because otherwise it becomes a burden on the taxpayer at the end of the day, if the development banks are loss-making,\u201d he maintained.\n\nFellow panellist and John Keells IT Director/Chief Executive Officer Ramesh Shanmuganathan argued that data and AI-driven models could help close the risk-assessment gap Perera described. He noted that many SME owners lack any credit history for lenders to assess.\n\n\u201cThere are better ways of doing it rather than going to a CRIB, where even an SME person may not have a record to even analyse the creditworthiness,\u201d he remarked.\n\n\u201cI think the argument for a proper, well-managed, profitable, independent development bank is there for everybody to see,\u201d Perera asserted.\u00a0 (NF)"
