    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Sri-Lanka-producing-tech-graduates-faster-than-it-creates-jobs-for-them-warns-Bingumal/215-351128",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790317995.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-a16a1790-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "43c3f0e4a29fc73f6a160e2170270943",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1500,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka producing tech graduates faster than it creates jobs for them, warns Bingumal\n\nAround 17,000 tech graduates a year entering a market with little room to absorb them\n\nYouth unrest risk flagged as graduates seek US$ 1,000 monthly salaries\n\nService export tax seen deterring global IT firms from scaling up\n\nSri Lanka is producing some 17,000 technology graduates every year without jobs to absorb them, a mismatch that risks breeding unrest among young people unless the country pivots decisively to the new economy, Standard Chartered Bank Sri Lanka Chief Executive Officer Bingumal Thewarathanthri warned.\n\nThewarathanthri raised the concern on a panel at the 49th Annual Meetings of the Association of Development Financing Institutions in Asia and the Pacific (ADFIAP), hosted by DFCC Bank in Colombo this week. He argued that the country\u2019s education pipeline had outpaced its capacity to create high-value jobs.\n\n\u201cWe are expecting something like 17,000 graduates in the tech space every year and we don\u2019t have a space to deploy them,\u201d he told the gathering.\n\nLeft unaddressed, he cautioned, the mismatch could leave a generation of qualified young people with expectations the domestic market is ill-equipped to meet. A US$ 1,000 monthly salary translates to roughly Rs.330,000 at current exchange rates.\n\n\u201cYou will have a huge youth unrest, depressed youngsters with a tech degree demanding a thousand dollar salary, and we don\u2019t have a place to deploy them,\u201d he remarked."
  },
  {
