    "md5": "952d17b5fad1eded80e91e6e64a3d51d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "Left unaddressed, he cautioned, the mismatch could leave a generation of qualified young people with expectations the domestic market is ill-equipped to meet. A US$ 1,000 monthly salary translates to roughly Rs.330,000 at current exchange rates.\n\n\u201cYou will have a huge youth unrest, depressed youngsters with a tech degree demanding a thousand dollar salary, and we don\u2019t have a place to deploy them,\u201d he remarked.\n\nUnemployment among those aged 15 to 24 stood at 19.5 percent in 2025, five times the national rate of 3.9 percent, while the rate for those aged 25 to 29 was 10.6 percent, as per Department of Census and Statistics (DCS) data. The issue of unemployment among educated youth across Asia was also highlighted in a recent IMF article, as reported earlier by Mirror Business.\n\nContrasting Sri Lanka\u2019s trajectory with those of India, China, Malaysia and Thailand, all of which he described as having done well in the new economy, Thewarathanthri argued that the growth model that carried the country thus far would not carry it further.\n\n\u201cThe new economy is not the traditional crop exports we did, or the graphite exports we did, of course apparel, and the labour force who sent us money to buy the cars. It\u2019s very different from what we have done so far, so what got us here will never get us there,\u201d he observed.\n\nIndustry 4.0 technologies, AI, data centres and tech exports, he contended, are where Sri Lanka should direct its attention if it is to put its graduates to work.\n\nMeanwhile, he traced part of the lost momentum to tax policy, recalling that the arrival of HCL had once signalled a wider opening for global IT firms.\n\n\u201cThere was a huge opportunity for Sri Lanka when companies like HCL came here, and there were other companies looking at Sri Lanka. We then put a tax on it and they kind of slowed down in terms of the number of desks,\u201d he pointed out, adding that the matter needs to be negotiated with the IMF."
  },
