    "chunk_size_bytes": 1097,
    "chunk_text": "WHEN SILENCE STOPS BEING SAFE\n\nThere is an idea in crisis communications that saying nothing is the safest option. Sometimes it is. But silence can create space for others to tell the story, and you must decide whether you can live with their version. Buckingham Palace faced that conundrum this week after Earl Spencer made a shocking allegation about King Charles in his new memoir about Diana. The Palace chose to answer, breaking with its usual reluctance to comment on books and ensuring that its response became a story in its own right.\n\nThis week on our BBC Radio 4 show, When It Hits the Fan, my co-host, David Yelland and I looked at why an organisation might knowingly give a story more attention by issuing a statement, what happens when private family disputes become public, and we look at the very different communications problem facing Ed Sheeran after controversy engulfed his North American tour.\n\nIn Swan Song: Diana, My Sister, Earl Spencer gives his account of a heated telephone conversation with the then Prince Charles in the days after Diana's death in 1997. Spencer says"
  },
