    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b88609dab4272e56578992054b00e851",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1480,
    "chunk_text": "This week on our BBC Radio 4 show, When It Hits the Fan, my co-host, David Yelland and I looked at why an organisation might knowingly give a story more attention by issuing a statement, what happens when private family disputes become public, and we look at the very different communications problem facing Ed Sheeran after controversy engulfed his North American tour.\n\nIn Swan Song: Diana, My Sister, Earl Spencer gives his account of a heated telephone conversation with the then Prince Charles in the days after Diana's death in 1997. Spencer says\n\nCommunications is not always about making a story disappear. Sometimes you have to accept that it will travel and concentrate instead on making sure your response travels with it. Spencer and the Palace are also playing by very different rules. Spencer can give interviews, defend his recollection and keep discussing the book. The King, as head of state, cannot sensibly engage in a running public argument with his former brother-in-law. Continued attention may help Spencer sell a new book; for the Palace, there is far more value in bringing the matter to a close. I do wonder whether the statement was slightly too interesting for that purpose. If you want a\n\nrebuttal to be reported once and then disappear, boring language can be useful. This was pointed, almost poetic and sufficiently unusual to invite analysis of every word. It did the job of challenging Spencer's account, but it also helped keep the story alight."
