    "chunk_text": "rebuttal to be reported once and then disappear, boring language can be useful. This was pointed, almost poetic and sufficiently unusual to invite analysis of every word. It did the job of challenging Spencer's account, but it also helped keep the story alight.\n\nEd Sheeran has faced a different problem on his North American tour. The controversy began after Macklemore, one of his support acts, used performances at New Jersey's MetLife Stadium to voice support for Palestinians and say, \u201cFree Palestine.\u201d Objections followed from some of the venues scheduled to host later dates, and Sheeran said he tried to broker a solution before the promoter ultimately removed Macklemore from the remaining shows. Other support acts subsequently withdrew in protest. Sheeran initially issued a statement setting out his account. Then at his Philadelphia concert on Saturday, he addressed the controversy again, apologised for mistakes and described the situation in Gaza as \u201ccatastrophic and unjustifiable.\u201d He also said he was speaking to people across the spectrum and acknowledged that he did not know enough. Watching him deliver that statement, I was struck by the attention paid to the fact that he appeared to be reading prepared words."
