    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "Ed Sheeran has faced a different problem on his North American tour. The controversy began after Macklemore, one of his support acts, used performances at New Jersey's MetLife Stadium to voice support for Palestinians and say, \u201cFree Palestine.\u201d Objections followed from some of the venues scheduled to host later dates, and Sheeran said he tried to broker a solution before the promoter ultimately removed Macklemore from the remaining shows. Other support acts subsequently withdrew in protest. Sheeran initially issued a statement setting out his account. Then at his Philadelphia concert on Saturday, he addressed the controversy again, apologised for mistakes and described the situation in Gaza as \u201ccatastrophic and unjustifiable.\u201d He also said he was speaking to people across the spectrum and acknowledged that he did not know enough. Watching him deliver that statement, I was struck by the attention paid to the fact that he appeared to be reading prepared words.\n\nGeopolitical questions that might once have sat mainly with government or specialist corporate teams can arrive suddenly in a stadium, a sponsorship agreement or a consumer campaign. Advisers do not need to become foreign policy experts, but they do need enough understanding to recognise when an apparently straightforward commercial decision sits inside a much larger political and cultural argument. Sheeran may now have reached the point where saying more simply creates another news event. All eyes are on the next PR battleground as he prepares to play at Robert Kraft\u2019s stadium this Saturday. What he does next may matter more than finding another way to explain what has already happened.\n\nFarzana Baduel is CEO of Curzon PR, President of the Chartered Institute of Public Relations, and co-presenter of When It Hits the Fan on BBC Radio 4. To follow Farzana Baduel on Instagram, scan the QR code below."
