    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "22nd Amendment to the Constitution: Arguments for and against\n\nColombo, Sep. 26 (Daily Mirror) - A central issue in the debate over Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed 22nd Amendment to the Constitution has been the retirement age and tenure of Superior Court judges, with arguments focusing on judicial independence, the retention of experienced judges and the possible impact on delays in the administration of justice.\n\nThe Supreme Court has determined that the Bill does not require approval at a referendum and can be passed by a special majority in Parliament. During the hearings, the Government and Government-backed intervenient petitioners highlighted what they described as the potential benefits of the proposed changes, particularly the contribution of senior judges\u2019 experience and expertise to judicial efficiency. However, the Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) and several opposition parties raised concerns over the possible implications for judicial independence.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, the Daily Mirror examines the principal arguments presented for and against the proposed 22nd Amendment during the Supreme Court proceedings.\n\nThe principal proposals of the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution are:\n\nIncrease the retirement age of Supreme Court judges to 67 years from 65.\n\nIncrease the retirement age of Court of Appeal judges to 65 years from 63.\n\nChief Justice would retire upon reaching 67 years or upon completion of six years from the date of appointment.\n\nSix-year limitation on the term of office of Chief Justice would not apply to incumbent Chief Justice.\n\nIncrease the maximum number of other judges of the Court of Appeal from 19 to 24.\n\nExtending retirement by two years would allow the judiciary to retain that experience rather than losing judges at 63 or 65.\n\nThe Government\u2019s central argument is that Sri Lanka has a substantial backlog of cases.\n\nKeeping experienced judges in service could provide additional judicial capacity while other reforms are implemented."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-dailymirrorlk-45c10dc4",
