    "md5": "4e26d17bf8fb7fbf2c5727eef784a04f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1731,
    "chunk_text": "The Government\u2019s central argument is that Sri Lanka has a substantial backlog of cases.\n\nKeeping experienced judges in service could provide additional judicial capacity while other reforms are implemented.\n\n4. Establishing sittings of the Court of Appeal outside Colombo to ease access to appellate justice\n\nThe Government has argued that the retirement age established decades ago should be reconsidered in light of increased life expectancy and changing social conditions.\n\nBASL argues that judicial retirement provisions are connected with the independence of the Judiciary\n\nIt says extending the tenure of judges, particularly sitting judges, could affect public confidence in judicial independence.\n\nBASL has described the proposal as a potential interference with judicial independence.\n\n2. The connection between retirement age and reducing case delays is disputed\n\nBASL argues that mere increase judges\u2019 retirement age would not solve case backlog\n\nOpponents argue that delays can also be addressed through: appointing additional judges, filling vacancies, increasing the number of courts, increasing court staff, improving infrastructure, digitalisation, and better case management.\n\nExtending the tenure of sitting judges could raise questions concerning judicial independence and the constitutional position of judicial power.\n\n4. Perception of an amendment designed to benefit particular individuals\n\nBASL has highlighted the fact that the incumbent Chief Justice would be among those potentially affected by the proposed changes.\n\nOpponents argue that the Judiciary must remain institutionally independent from the Executive and Legislature, and this is an attempt by the Executive to interfere with the Judiciary"
  },
