    "md5": "cff8f3fbdd6d0cb95a29d8cacd86270d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Anti-rabies drive: 1.7 million dogs vaccinated last year\n\nColombo, Sep. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka is stepping up efforts to prevent rabies by expanding dog vaccination, sterilisation and public awareness programmes, with nearly 1.7 million dogs vaccinated in 2025 as the country continues its push to reduce human deaths from the disease, according to the National Anti-Rabies Campaign of the Public Health Veterinary Services.\n\nThe number of dogs vaccinated reached a record 1,690,887 last year, up from 1,280,775 in 2024 and 1,317,422 in 2023. The increase has coincided with a decline in human rabies deaths, which fell from 28 in 2022 to 16 in 2023, 20 in 2024 and 14 in 2025.\n\nAccording to Dr Niroshan Gamage, Director of the National Anti-Rabies Campaign, dogs remain by far the main source of human rabies exposure in Sri Lanka. Laboratory surveillance conducted by the Medical Research Institute across multiple study periods has found that around 85% to 97% of confirmed rabies-positive samples come from dogs, while cats account for approximately 2% to 8%.\n\nThis makes mass vaccination (injections) of the canine population the main focus of Sri Lanka\u2019s rabies-control efforts. The programme covers owned as well as community and free-roaming dogs. Oral rabies vaccination is also used for dogs that are difficult to catch or handle. The need for continued vaccination remains significant, as Sri Lanka is still considered a canine-rabies-endemic country, with the disease claiming an estimated 20 to 30 lives each year and hundreds of thousands of people bitten by dogs annually.\n\nThe country\u2019s approach also extends beyond vaccination. Since 2007, Sri Lanka has followed a \u201cNo Kill\u201d policy, replacing dog culling with animal birth control measures such as sterilisation and, where appropriate, hormonal contraception to manage the free-roaming dog population."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
