    "language": "en",
    "md5": "49fde835f43b6c42e5255a7dce371034",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1274,
    "chunk_text": "The country\u2019s approach also extends beyond vaccination. Since 2007, Sri Lanka has followed a \u201cNo Kill\u201d policy, replacing dog culling with animal birth control measures such as sterilisation and, where appropriate, hormonal contraception to manage the free-roaming dog population.\n\nAnother key component is protecting people after exposure. Rabies is transmitted through the saliva of an infected animal, most commonly through a bite, although scratches or licks on broken skin or mucous membranes can also transmit the virus. Once clinical symptoms appear, the disease is essentially untreatable, making prompt post-exposure treatment critical.\n\nPost-exposure treatment for people bitten or otherwise exposed to potentially rabid animals is provided free of charge through government hospitals, forming the second major line of defence alongside preventing transmission among dogs.\n\nThe programme also relies on surveillance and coordination between veterinary and medical authorities, including the Medical Research Institute and the Department of Animal Production and Health, with technical support from international organisations such as the World Health Organisation (WHO), Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) and World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH)."
