    "md5": "b17b558e62f08d4c8b7f0497022d0d00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Northern Sea, Arabian Sea used as key drug smuggling routes to Sri Lanka\n\nColombo, Sep. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Drug trafficking networks are using different maritime routes to smuggle various types of narcotics and other contraband into Sri Lanka, with the Northern Sea and Arabian Sea emerging as key routes for different categories of illicit cargo, according to the Sri Lanka Navy.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Navy has seized illicit drugs estimated to be worth more than Rs. 55 billion through maritime and coastal anti-narcotics operations conducted so far this year, Navy Spokesman Commander Chaminda Walakuluge said.\n\nAccording to the Navy, around 300 suspects and 50 vessels allegedly linked to local and international drug trafficking networks have been taken into custody during the operations.\n\nDetailing the massive hauls recovered during these operations, the Navy Spokesman said that forces had intercepted over 1,080 kilograms of heroin, 590 kilograms of crystal methamphetamine (commonly known as Ice), and 256 kilograms of cocaine.\n\nIn addition to these highly addictive substances, the Navy confiscated over four million narcotic pills, 51 kilograms of hashish, and a staggering 55,000 kilograms (55 tonnes) of smuggled beedi leaves.\n\nA recent operation in the Gurunagar lagoon area resulted in the detection of around 800 kilograms of cannabis.\n\nDrug trafficking networks use different maritime routes to bring contraband into Sri Lanka. Cannabis and beedi leaves are mainly smuggled through the Northern Sea, while heroin, crystal methamphetamine and cocaine are primarily transported through the Arabian Sea.\n\nThe Navy has strengthened surveillance and interception operations in these areas to prevent illegal consignments from reaching the shore, he said.\n\nAlongside maritime enforcement, the Navy is also conducting community awareness programmes aimed at preventing drug abuse, particularly among young people."
