    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "Counsellors warn of growing sexual withdrawal among married men\n\nColombo, Sep. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Psychological counsellors have raised concerns about a growing pattern of sexual withdrawal among married men in Sri Lanka, saying a lack of intimacy is increasingly contributing to tension and conflict in relationships.\n\n\u201cMen in Sri Lanka are increasingly withdrawing from sexual relationships. Around 80% of those who seek counselling are women, and they report that their husbands gradually distance themselves from intimacy after marriage,\u201d a counsellor said.\n\nCounsellors said the issue is particularly observed among men aged 35 to 45. Financial difficulties, work-related stress, family responsibilities and emotional exhaustion were identified as factors that can affect sexual desire and intimacy.\n\nProlonged financial and economic pressures can also affect mental wellbeing and, in turn, a person's interest in intimacy, they said.\n\n\u201cIn the past, physical exhaustion from demanding work was often a major factor affecting men. Today, however, many face greater psychological and emotional stress. Mental pressure can negatively affect sexual desire in men, just as it can in women,\u201d the counsellor said.\n\nThe increasing use of mobile phones and other digital devices has also become a concern, with counsellors saying excessive screen time can reduce communication between spouses and contribute to emotional distance.\n\nThey also pointed to excessive pornography consumption as a factor that can create unrealistic expectations about sexual relationships. In some cases, embarrassment or discomfort in discussing such expectations with a partner can further affect communication and intimacy.\n\n\u201cOnline sex is easy, convenient and simple. There is no responsibility towards anyone, and it takes only a few minutes. Men may naturally choose the easier option, which can be a psychological tendency rather than a personal flaw,\u201d the counsellor said."
