    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ebbdde4dc7720c3675ec6b33849a5567",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1928,
    "chunk_text": "64% of pre-schoolers get 30+ minutes of daily screen time in SL, raising concerns - Survey\n\nColombo, Sep. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Excessive screen use among preschool children is raising concerns over their brain development, language and social skills, physical activity and overall health, with 64% of children spending more than 30 minutes a day on screens, according to a recent survey conducted by the Health Promotion Bureau in collaboration with the University of Colombo and UNICEF.\n\nThe figures were highlighted by Dr Anil Jasinghe, Secretary to the Ministry of Health and Mass Media, at a media briefing. The survey, conducted across four provinces - Southern, Western, Central and Northern - involved around 1,100 children, their parents and preschool teachers from 55 preschools.\n\nAccording to the survey, 64% of children spend more than 30 minutes a day on screens, while approximately 43% consume their meals while using a screen. Around 32% of the children also sleep for less than the recommended 10 to 11 hours a day.\n\nAccording to World Health Organisation recommendations, children below two years should not be exposed to screens, including televisions, mobile phones, tablets and computers. For children between two and five years, screen time should not exceed 60 minutes a day and should preferably be limited to 30 to 45 minutes per day, according to Dr Enoka Wickramasinghe, Consultant Community Physician and Head of the National Preschool Health Promotion Programme at the Health Promotion Bureau of the Ministry of Health and Mass Media.\n\nDr Wickramasinghe said excessive passive screen use could affect early childhood development, particularly as the first few years of life are a period of rapid brain growth and development. She explained that when children passively watch screens, they do not receive the same stimulation through their five senses as they would through active play and interaction."
  },
