    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bf84fc27084669bbd92888380fb01e89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "Dr Wickramasinghe said excessive passive screen use could affect early childhood development, particularly as the first few years of life are a period of rapid brain growth and development. She explained that when children passively watch screens, they do not receive the same stimulation through their five senses as they would through active play and interaction.\n\nShe said this could affect areas of development, including cognitive, language, motor, emotional and social development, as children require active experiences and interaction to develop these skills.\n\nDr Wickramasinghe also pointed to concerns raised by psychologists and psychiatrists over language development and behavioural problems among children who spend excessive amounts of time in front of screens.\n\n\u201cLanguage abilities can be poor, and language development can be reduced remarkably. There can also be behaviour problems, such as aggressive behaviour,\u201d she said.\n\nShe stressed, however, that screen use does not cause autism, while noting that children who spend excessive periods in front of screens may have fewer opportunities to develop social connections and social skills.\n\nAnother concern is the impact of prolonged screen use on children's physical activity. Dr Wickramasinghe said spending extended periods seated in front of screens contributes to sedentary behaviour, which may have longer-term consequences for children's health. According to her, children should engage in at least 180 minutes of physical activity a day, including around 60 minutes of vigorous activity. She noted that remaining seated for more than one hour is considered sedentary or inactive.\n\nFurther, reduced physical activity can increase the risk of overweight and obesity and contribute to non-communicable diseases such as diabetes, high blood pressure and heart diseases later in life, she said.\n\nShe also identified eye-related problems as another possible concern associated with excessive screen exposure among young children."
