    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "63e45a29a40c59694117ae539bd38791",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "The NPP masterfully exploited post-Aragalaya anti-corruption sentiments and the desire to break free from the ruling elites accumulated folly that culminated with the 2022 economic crisis and sovereign default.\n\nHowever, a significant portion of their parliamentary support is a \u201cprotest vote\u201d rather than a deeply rooted ideological alignment with any left-leaning socioeconomic policies. The NPP knows it.\n\nI was 28 years old in 1970 when the United Front Coalition beat the Dudley Senanayake led Government with a two thirds majority.\u00a0 I was 34 years old when JR\u2019s UNP beat Prime minister Sirimavo Bandaranaike\u2019s Government with a five sixth majority.\u00a0 I have witnessed unfolding history.\n\nHistory warns that supermajorities in Sri Lanka have always been a double-edged sword. Earlier Presidencies that wielded two-thirds majorities namely President JR\u2019s and Presidents MR and GR all succumbed to hubris, misinterpreting parliamentary dominance as absolute immunity, which ultimately led to policy overreach and public backlash.\n\nPresident Dissanayake\u2019s administration faces the monumental task of balancing its anti-corruption, social welfare rhetoric with the rigid fiscal constraints imposed by international lenders like the IMF.\n\nWith a parliamentary supermajority, the NPP can no longer point to legislative obstruction or deep-state interference to excuse slow progress.\n\nEvery policy failure, economic bottleneck, or delayed reform will rest squarely on their shoulders.\n\nThe 2024 parliamentary election undoubtedly re-mapped the contours of Sri Lankan politics, shattering long-standing barriers and giving the NPP an unprecedented tool for governance.\n\nThat said, the 159-seat supermajority is an institutional distortion produced by Opposition fragmentation and selective voter turnout, rather than a reflection of an absolute, monolithic national consensus."
  },
