    "md5": "9817d4fc5ef56279d4ffdf1bb4ac9068",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1576,
    "chunk_text": "NPP after two years: System change \u2013 or change of system?\n\nIf it merely replaces the people operating the old machinery, then \u2018system change\u2019 will eventually reveal itself to have been little more than system succession. The proposed judicial changes make that question particularly urgent. The NPP still has time. What it does with the extraordinary power Sri Lanka gave it will determine whether these first two years become merely the prologue to system change, or the beginning of another turn of the political wheel\n\nTwo years after the NPP swept into power, Sri Lanka is still neither the nation its then-opposition promised, nor the country that the present regime inherited. That may be both its achievement and its dilemma.\n\nTwo years ago, Sri Lanka gave Anura Kumara Dissanayake and the National People\u2019s Power an extraordinary mandate to change the system. First, with the Presidency in September 2024; and then, two months later, with a mammoth 159 of 225 parliamentary seats.\n\nPrevious presidents had possessed considerable power, but few executives had been handed such political capital to alter the machinery of the State, rather than merely occupy its driving seat.\n\nPerhaps the savvy summary is this: away from the precipice at which edge we were, but not yet towards that promised land of past and recent manifestoes. That initial accomplishment deserves quite a bit more credit than the Opposition may concede, and rather more criticism than the Government may welcome\u2026\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recent political history provides the context for that claim."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-dailyftlk-01564d3c",
    "num": "dailyftlk-01564d3c",
    "date_str": "2026-09-25",
    "description": "NPP after two years: System change \u2013 or change of system?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/NPP-after-two-years-System-change-or-change-of-system/4-797786",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1790359912.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailyftlk-01564d3c-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "004d6547057f6f146c9deb2e779c66b9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1847,
    "chunk_text": "Perhaps the savvy summary is this: away from the precipice at which edge we were, but not yet towards that promised land of past and recent manifestoes. That initial accomplishment deserves quite a bit more credit than the Opposition may concede, and rather more criticism than the Government may welcome\u2026\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recent political history provides the context for that claim.\n\nThe NPP inherited that recovery architecture. And its first and least glamorous achievement, therefore, was not to wreck it and ruin the country again.\n\nThere had been legitimate anxiety that a JVP-led Government might tear up the IMF agreement, increase spending beyond the State\u2019s means and attempt to cure austerity by spending money Sri Lanka did not possess. It did not. Instead, the NPP broadly continued the stabilisation program, while attempting to give it a more socially redistributive character.\n\nThat continuity was politically significant and nationally welcome. The IMF completed its combined fifth and sixth reviews in May 2026, releasing about $ 695 million, and affirming that the program remained broadly on track.\n\nDebt restructuring continued, reserves accumulated, and the economy grew by about 5% in both 2024 and 2025, with 1 2026 growth at 5.1%. Unemployment stood at 3.7%, while reserves reached $ 6.45 billion at end-June.\n\nBut let us not confuse inheritance with invention. The NPP did not manufacture this macroeconomic recovery from scratch. It inherited the IMF program and the stabilisation framework constructed under its predecessor. Its achievement was continuity without ideological rupture through an unexpected statecraft rather than revolutionary economics.\n\nThus kudos to the NPP! But it is not the same thing as creating a new economic model.\n\nAnd this is where their report card becomes considerably less comfortable."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-25-dailyftlk-01564d3c",
    "num": "dailyftlk-01564d3c",
    "date_str": "2026-09-25",
    "description": "NPP after two years: System change \u2013 or change of system?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/NPP-after-two-years-System-change-or-change-of-system/4-797786",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1790359912.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailyftlk-01564d3c-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ec71741468a765654b95704ceaf51682",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "But let us not confuse inheritance with invention. The NPP did not manufacture this macroeconomic recovery from scratch. It inherited the IMF program and the stabilisation framework constructed under its predecessor. Its achievement was continuity without ideological rupture through an unexpected statecraft rather than revolutionary economics.\n\nThus kudos to the NPP! But it is not the same thing as creating a new economic model.\n\nAnd this is where their report card becomes considerably less comfortable.\n\nBy August 2026, headline inflation had risen to 8%, with food inflation at 8.5%. The Middle East energy shock exposed Sri Lanka\u2019s continuing dependence on imported fuel, while the 2026 drought exposed the vulnerability of agriculture and water systems. Rainfall deficits in parts of Ampara and Monaragala reached extraordinary levels, followed by the more recent indiscriminate inundation of El Ni\u00f1o\u2019s advance-guard attack.\n\nThe truth be told \u2013 none of that can sensibly be blamed on the NPP. Governments, however, are ultimately judged by how they manage the shocks they encounter, not the eloquence of their erstwhile manifestoes.\n\nSo economically at least, the verdict after two years is reasonably clear: Sri Lanka has moved from near-collapse through tough recovery towards stability, but not from stability towards a quiet prosperity as before or anywhere near thriving.\n\nSri Lankan politics has long operated by virtue (or lack of it) of patronage. Politicians distributed State resources, cultivated loyalist networks and converted those articulated tentacles into self-serving electoral machinery. The NPP came to power promising to cleanse that culture.\n\nPolitical privilege is less ostentatious. Dynastic politics has receded. The public image of Government is markedly more austere. Politically powerful figures have faced investigation, prosecution and punishment. Public financial disclosure has received greater emphasis."
