    "md5": "4dafcf6d94d4da3adcb0b5fee646fce5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1779,
    "chunk_text": "Political privilege is less ostentatious. Dynastic politics has receded. The public image of Government is markedly more austere. Politically powerful figures have faced investigation, prosecution and punishment. Public financial disclosure has received greater emphasis.\n\nBut an arrest is not a conviction. And more importantly, anti-corruption politics becomes dangerous if it degenerates into selective prosecution. The real test is not how many former politicians are arrested and tried, but whether Sri Lanka constructs institutions strong enough that presidents no longer decide personally whom to prosecute.\n\nThe NPP inherited the 2023 Anti-Corruption Act rather than creating the framework from scratch, but it has used the machinery more aggressively. It has pursued old cases, elevated the prominence of CIABOC, increased transparency, and published the financial statements of major State-owned enterprises.\n\nThat last development is hardly thrilling cocktail-circuit conversation, but it is precisely the sort of boring institutional reform that matters. SOEs are the barometer of our systemic well-being.\n\nThe difficulty is that enforcement is not institutionalisation. Sri Lanka\u2019s structural ecosystem of political corruption still encompasses procurement, appointments, SOEs, land use, State concessions, tax exemptions, political financing, public contracts and discretionary governmental power.\n\nThe NPP has also begun moving from a resigned acceptance of impunity towards rigorous investigation of past misdemeanours. Yet, it has still to demonstrate that Sri Lanka can move from investigation to conviction, asset recovery and institutional bolstering.\n\nThe biggest broken promise remains the Executive Presidency: the NPP promised to abolish it; it has not."
  },
