    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "The NPP has also begun moving from a resigned acceptance of impunity towards rigorous investigation of past misdemeanours. Yet, it has still to demonstrate that Sri Lanka can move from investigation to conviction, asset recovery and institutional bolstering.\n\nThe biggest broken promise remains the Executive Presidency: the NPP promised to abolish it; it has not.\n\nThe same unfinished business extends to decentralisation. Provincial Councils remain unelected. The promised timetable for provincial elections has slipped, entangled in questions of electoral systems and delimitation.\n\nThis is not a minor administrative footnote. Democratic decentralisation was part of the promised system change.\n\nNor is the judiciary question reassuring. The Government has proposed constitutional changes involving the retirement age of senior judges and the number of judges in the higher courts. The administration says the purpose is to reduce judicial delays. Opposition parties, lawyers, unions and civil-society groups argue that the changes could compromise judicial independence.\n\nThe issue deserves close scrutiny precisely because of the NPP\u2019s original raison d\u2019\u00eatre. It does not follow that the proposed reforms are inherently corrupt or authoritarian. But if an administration uses an enormous parliamentary majority to increase its capacity to shape institutions that are supposed to constrain executive power, the appearance of institutional self-interest is itself dangerous.\n\nFor a movement elected to change the system, this is the red line to watch. That it is pressing the judges issue without ditching the executive albatross questions its bona fides big time.\n\nThe NPP achieved something politically important for a party rooted in the Sinhala south, by winning substantial Tamil support, including an unprecedented breakthrough in Jaffna."
  },
