    "md5": "47a8c0a402961547dabdc91fcc4271a9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "For a movement elected to change the system, this is the red line to watch. That it is pressing the judges issue without ditching the executive albatross questions its bona fides big time.\n\nThe NPP achieved something politically important for a party rooted in the Sinhala south, by winning substantial Tamil support, including an unprecedented breakthrough in Jaffna.\n\nThe Government has renewed investigations into cases including Prageeth Ekneligoda, Keith Noyahr and Lasantha Wickrematunge, and has permitted the Chemmani investigations to continue. The discovery of hundreds of skeletal remains at Chemmani has, however, created a particularly grave test of whether investigation can develop into a comprehensive accountability process.\n\nThe PTA per se presents another acid test. The Government promised reform of the counter-terrorism architecture, yet proposed replacement legislation has itself been criticised for retaining broad counter-terror powers. Changing the name of an institutional problem is not the same as solving it. And only a genuinely rights-compatible legal framework would turn the Government\u2019s rhetoric into reform.\n\nThe NPP did not abolish Aswesuma. It expanded payments, raising the minimum payment for poor households from Rs. 7,500 to Rs. 10,000 and the payment for the \u2018very poor\u2019 category to Rs. 17,500. Given Sri Lanka\u2019s fiscal constraints, targeted welfare is more realistic than universal subsidies, although targeting inevitably creates exclusions and administrative problems.\n\nHealthcare is less impressive. The manifesto envisaged Government health expenditure approaching 3% of GDP, but the 2026 allocation was around 1.9%."
