    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "The NPP did not abolish Aswesuma. It expanded payments, raising the minimum payment for poor households from Rs. 7,500 to Rs. 10,000 and the payment for the \u2018very poor\u2019 category to Rs. 17,500. Given Sri Lanka\u2019s fiscal constraints, targeted welfare is more realistic than universal subsidies, although targeting inevitably creates exclusions and administrative problems.\n\nHealthcare is less impressive. The manifesto envisaged Government health expenditure approaching 3% of GDP, but the 2026 allocation was around 1.9%.\n\nAgriculture presents the same tension between ideological ambition and inconvenient arithmetic. Fertiliser subsidies, irrigation rehabilitation, food security, domestic production, land mapping and tank restoration are all sensible priorities. But fragmented holdings, weak logistics, post-harvest losses, price volatility, inefficient State marketing, water vulnerability and climate change remain formidable obstacles. The 2026 drought has exposed these realities rather brutally. Now, the wreckage of heavy rain may complicate matters.\n\nEnergy offers greater structural promise. The expansion of solar power, including the Sampur project and investment in transmission and rooftop solar, matters; because Sri Lanka\u2019s vulnerability is not simply that oil is increasingly expensive: it is that our island imports much of the energy upon which its economy depends. Solar, wind, grid reform and storage are therefore not merely environmental policies; they are national-security policies. But a 2,000 MW promise is a long road from a project beginning to a transformed energy system.\n\nTourism and remittances continue to sustain the external economy, while the NPP has become more explicitly investment- and export-oriented than its campaign rhetoric might have suggested."
