    "chunk_size_bytes": 1989,
    "chunk_text": "Energy offers greater structural promise. The expansion of solar power, including the Sampur project and investment in transmission and rooftop solar, matters; because Sri Lanka\u2019s vulnerability is not simply that oil is increasingly expensive: it is that our island imports much of the energy upon which its economy depends. Solar, wind, grid reform and storage are therefore not merely environmental policies; they are national-security policies. But a 2,000 MW promise is a long road from a project beginning to a transformed energy system.\n\nTourism and remittances continue to sustain the external economy, while the NPP has become more explicitly investment- and export-oriented than its campaign rhetoric might have suggested.\n\nOne area in which the NPP has evolved considerably is foreign policy.\n\nThe old JVP ethos was nationalist and suspicious of foreign capital and major powers. Fears that a JVP-led Government might make Sri Lanka more insular and isolated have not materialised.\n\nInstead, the Government has pursued a pragmatic balancing policy. Relations with India have deepened while the relationship with China has been maintained, particularly in energy, investment, infrastructure, connectivity, maritime security and tourism.\n\nFor a small, strategically located, debt-constrained State, this pragmatism makes sense.\n\nThe greater challenge may lie closer to home: the civil service.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s problem has never simply been corrupt politicians. The machinery propping them up has itself become politicised, fragmented, inefficient and resistant to reform.\n\nThe NPP has begun digitalisation, institutional restructuring and attempts to improve administrative efficiency. But allegations of growing party influence over the bureaucracy are precisely what a movement promising a new political culture should take seriously.\n\nReplacing one patronage network with another, even a more disciplined one, is not system change. It is system continuity under new management."
