    "md5": "c26d71900883429fa55afd7933f51a5f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2008,
    "chunk_text": "The NPP has begun digitalisation, institutional restructuring and attempts to improve administrative efficiency. But allegations of growing party influence over the bureaucracy are precisely what a movement promising a new political culture should take seriously.\n\nReplacing one patronage network with another, even a more disciplined one, is not system change. It is system continuity under new management.\n\nWeaknesses: limited governing experience; shallow institutional reform; constitutional paralysis; incomplete reconciliation; an economic model stabilised more than reinvented; and the tension between expansive social ambitions and severe fiscal constraints.\n\nOpportunities: genuinely independent oversight institutions; a negotiated constitutional settlement; stronger productivity and exports; renewable energy and grid reform; digital Government; and the unusual opportunity created by simultaneous southern and minority electoral support to advance reconciliation.\n\nThreats: renewed inflation and external shocks; imported-energy vulnerability; climate stress; political overconfidence; institutional capture; and, most immediately, any erosion of judicial independence.\n\nThe central danger is that the NPP\u2019s greatest strength, its enormous parliamentary majority, could become its greatest weakness.\n\nA Government with overwhelming power has extraordinary capacity to reform institutions.\n\nSo, where are we? After two years, the broad picture looks like this.\n\nEconomically: Sri Lanka has moved from near-collapse to relative stability, but not yet from stability to prosperity.\n\nPolitically: the country has moved from establishment politics towards a noticeably different political culture.\n\nIn governance: the movement is from personalised patronage towards institutional accountability, but only a short distance towards that salutary goal.\n\nOn corruption: the country has moved from impunity towards investigation, but the harder work of conviction, asset recovery and prevention remains."
  },
