    "md5": "97ec13e64c17cb8b45f8b6f83ba6a523",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "In governance: the movement is from personalised patronage towards institutional accountability, but only a short distance towards that salutary goal.\n\nOn corruption: the country has moved from impunity towards investigation, but the harder work of conviction, asset recovery and prevention remains.\n\nSocially: welfare is more protective, but fiscal reality sharply limits ambition. Aswesuma is a tangible achievement; health and education remain works in progress.\n\nIn re reconciliation: progress on individual cases has not yet become a convincing national architecture.\n\nAs for foreign policy: pragmatism has prevailed, with India and China both meaningfully engaged.\n\nThat is a more than respectable record for a Government inheriting a bankrupt State, sovereign default, institutional dysfunction and an economy only beginning to recover. For a movement that promised system change, however, it is not yet enough.\n\nAnd perhaps that is the paradox that will ultimately define the NPP\u2019s first term.\n\nIts first phase, 2024-26, has essentially been about recovering and stabilising. On that front, the Government has broadly succeeded: it preserved the recovery, maintained the IMF framework, expanded welfare, began pursuing corruption cases and introduced a markedly different political style.\n\nThe second phase, 2026-29, is considerably harder. It requires constitutional reform, independent institutions, judicial independence, a professional civil service, higher productivity, diversified exports, energy security, meaningful education and healthcare investment, reconciliation, decentralisation, private-sector investment and technological modernisation.\n\nThat record remains largely unwritten. And here lies the acid test. The NPP was elected because Sri Lankans wanted to smash an odious system. Its greatest achievement so far has instead been learning to operate that system without immediately wrecking it."
  },
