    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign Secretary Ranaraja, in her opening remarks highlighted the enduring partnership between the two countries, recalling the foundational role both nations played in advancing major diplomatic initiatives most notably Non-Aligned Movement. Deputy Minister for Asia-Pacific and African Affairs Dr. Darmosumarto, in his response, welcomed the signing of the MoU as a structured framework for regular engagement and deepen cooperation between the two countries, while highlighting opportunities to expand collaboration across mutually beneficial areas.\n\nBoth sides also recognised the significant potential to deepen tourism cooperation and ease visa processes, fostering seamless travel and enhancing connectivity and identified strengthening educational and cultural cooperation for stronger people-to-people exchanges.\n\nThe Sri Lanka side reiterated the request for support of the Indonesian side for its bid to obtain ASEAN Sectoral Dialogue Partnership (SDP) status and to accede to the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP), which was duly acknowledged.\n\nThe two delegations agreed to organise a series of year-round activities to commemorate the 75th anniversary of diplomatic relations between Sri Lanka and Indonesia in 2027, emphasising that this historic milestone serves as a pivotal moment to celebrate shared achievements and explore new avenues to deepen bilateral ties.\n\nThe Consultations concluded with the decision to hold the 2nd Round of Consultations in Jakarta in 2028, reaffirming the shared commitment to maintaining regular dialogue and advancing bilateral cooperation.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Delegation included Sri Lanka Ambassador to Indonesia and ASEAN, Sashikala Premawardhane, Additional Secretary - Political\n\nAffairs M.R.K. Lenagala, Director General - South East Asia and Central Asia Chamari Rodrigo and senior Officials of the line agencies."
