    "chunk_id": "2026-09-25-dailyftlk-1f136eaa-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ab5b1b71e6026509aaa72e9af067672c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "Sajith says 22nd Amendment creating \u2018black present\u2019\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa yesterday called for the immediate withdrawal of the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution. He told Parliament that, although the Prime Minister speaks of a dark past, the amendment was creating \u2018a black present\u2019.\n\nSpeaking during the debate on the amendment, which raises the retirement age of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges, Premadasa said that while the Government claims it is good for the country and safeguards democracy, it is in reality anti-democratic. He said it degrades the self-respect of the people, violates fundamental rights and treats citizens as slaves in order to bring them under executive supremacy.\n\nHe likened the situation to Hans Christian Andersen\u2019s The Emperor\u2019s New Clothes, in which an emperor deceived by two fraudsters parades before the public in a garment that does not exist, while his entourage is too afraid to speak up.\n\nPremadasa said the amendment destroys the independence of the Judiciary, dismantles the separation of powers and eliminates the system of checks and balances. It would destroy the respect that 22 million citizens hold for the Judiciary and judges, turning the Judiciary into \u2018a tool and puppet of the executive\u2019, he said. He said the amendment deals a fatal blow to judicial independence, destroys the mechanism of justice delivery and buries the rule of law \u2018in the graveyard\u2019.\n\nRecalling past constitutional changes, he said executive powers were reduced by the 17th Amendment and reclaimed through the 18th, reduced again by the 19th and expanded by the 20th, which he said contributed to the country\u2019s bankruptcy, before the 21st restricted them once more. He said the tragedy of democracy is that some individuals raised their hands in support of all five amendments, and the public continues to vote for them."
  },
