    "md5": "ff3bf2bfa5699fe9f64b2c48320724da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "Recalling past constitutional changes, he said executive powers were reduced by the 17th Amendment and reclaimed through the 18th, reduced again by the 19th and expanded by the 20th, which he said contributed to the country\u2019s bankruptcy, before the 21st restricted them once more. He said the tragedy of democracy is that some individuals raised their hands in support of all five amendments, and the public continues to vote for them.\n\nCiting the case of Jagath Dias v. Upali Abeyratne, he said a three-judge Supreme Court Bench had held that any increase in the retirement age of judges must go to a referendum. He questioned why President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, who had insisted a referendum was mandatory for the 20th Amendment, now considered one unnecessary for the 22nd. The Supreme Court has determined that the 22nd Amendment does not require a referendum.\n\nPremadasa said the Government had promised to abolish the executive presidency on page 194 of its manifesto, \u2018A Thriving Nation, A Beautiful Life\u2019, and had received a mandate for it. Instead, he said, it was exerting pressure on judges, and was assassinating the Constitution and democracy through a \u2018constitutional conspiracy\u2019. Quoting John Locke, he said that where law ends, tyranny begins. He said the Justice Minister had denied any such plan on 11 June, 2026, but that the truth was exposed on 23 June.\n\nHe said those who now claim to protect the Judiciary should reflect on the treatment of former Chief Justice Dr. Shirani Bandaranayake. Those who stood by her at the time had later called for judges to be summoned before a Parliamentary Select Committee after a court order to hold local government elections, he said. He added that the Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) had defended judicial independence on both occasions. Responding to the Prime Minister\u2019s past remarks about former MP Prof. G.L. Peiris, he said the SJB was never part of that government."
  },
