    "date_str": "2026-09-26",
    "description": "The pros and cons of mandatory seat belt law",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/The-pros-and-cons-of-mandatory-seat-belt-law/172-351186",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790395412.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-26-dailymirrorlk-790e6509-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "83e97751cea1d64d6dfb32e3e46921ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1327,
    "chunk_text": "The pros and cons of mandatory seat belt law\n\nIt is common knowledge that the driver and the passenger or passengers sitting in the front of a vehicle should wear\u00a0 seat belts. This is because they are vulnerable to be thrown towards the windscreen due to an unexpected slamming on the footbrakes to avoid a head on collision. In such instances,\u00a0 the air bags too are supposed to work automatically. The passengers sitting on rear seats get thrown towards the back which is not as dangerous as most seats are cushioned. There is no urgent need of belts for passengers at the back. They must be free to get out in an emergency.\n\nInstead of saving the lives of the passengers they are made vulnerable to be exposed to danger when kept tied to seats with belts. They are unable to jump out in difficult circumstance such as a vehicle rolling over, catching fire, rolling down a precipice, skidding into a river/canal or reservoir,\u00a0 a tree falling on the vehicle body or caught up in a whirlwind.\n\nIt is up to the authorities to reconsider the futility of enforcing this law for the back seat passengers in buses. The previous position where the front seat rider/s and the driver requiring to wear the belt is fair enough. Why is this rush to implement this law when the government has to address other burning issues of the day?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-26-dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab",
    "date_str": "2026-09-26",
    "description": "Easter massacre \u2013 after seven years, the mystery continues",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Easter-massacre-after-seven-years-the-mystery-continues/172-351187",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790395604.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-26-dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "605a3264cef82d61b5c0858c0c4b9a89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1846,
    "chunk_text": "Easter massacre \u2013 after seven years, the mystery continues\n\nThe country was in disarray and no one appeared to be in control\n\nEaster Sunday in April 2019 will long be remembered by Lankan Catholics. On that day, quite out of the blue, as Christians were celebrating the holiest day in their calendar, suicide bombers blew themselves up in churches and three luxury hotels. Two-hundred and seventy people died and over 500 were injured.\n\nThe courts have found 15 persons guilty of the dastardly crime, while\u00a0 nine were acquitted. Yet Lankans are yet to understand why this country was picked to perpetrate the bloody atrocity. Christian and\u00a0 Muslim communities in this our common homeland have lived peacefully through the centuries. There had been no \u2018bad blood\u2019 so to say between the two communities.\n\nThe country had just come out of a bloody 30-year war. All communities were yearning for peace. Now there was every danger the mass killings would trigger another communal war - with Christians attacking their Islamic brethren. Thanks to the timely intervention of Catholic leader Malcolm Cardinal Ranjith,\u00a0 tempers were soothed and a calamity averted.\n\nThe Lankan government was unstable at the time. The president who also doubled as defence minister, was in open conflict with his prime minister. It led to a number of security lapses. Making matters even worse, the president was out of the country and the prime minister was out of the capital when the attacks occurred.\n\nThe rumour mills had a field day churning out fake news stories by the dozen. The rumour had it that Catholic ministers in the Cabinet had information regarding a possible attack and stayed away from churches on the day. When a particular minister announced that his father had advised him to stay away from church that day, it seemed to confirm the rumour."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-26-dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab",
    "date_str": "2026-09-26",
    "description": "Easter massacre \u2013 after seven years, the mystery continues",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Easter-massacre-after-seven-years-the-mystery-continues/172-351187",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790395604.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-26-dailymirrorlk-5b8e34ab-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a1f02fbda85daac518b49ff95f8af1bd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "The rumour mills had a field day churning out fake news stories by the dozen. The rumour had it that Catholic ministers in the Cabinet had information regarding a possible attack and stayed away from churches on the day. When a particular minister announced that his father had advised him to stay away from church that day, it seemed to confirm the rumour.\n\nPresidential Commissions of Inquiry, a Parliamentary Select Committee, and a number of administrative/ministerial review bodies inquired into the bombings, but nothing was made public. Speculation grew and suspicions increased. It was in this atmosphere of political instability that former Secretary Defence Gotabaya Rajapaksa announced his candidature for the presidential election of November 2019.\u00a0 After his election victory at the polls,\u00a0 however, little progress was made in bringing justice to the victims of the bombings. Allegations of a cover up by the president himself began to surface, after he began meddling with the work of officers inquiring into the Easter Sunday attacks.\n\nFollowing President Gotabaya\u2019s ouster from the presidency, Mr. Ranil Wickremesinghe was voted president by parliament. During his presidency,\u00a0 the Supreme Court found former president Maithripala Sirisena guilty of \u201cactively undermining\u201d the government, resulting in the \u201cserious lapses\u201d that allowed the terrorist attacks to happen.\n\nThe Attorney General also indicted the former Defence Secretary and former IGP before the Colombo Trial-at-Bar. But, the then Trial-at-Bar bench acquitted both accused, without calling for their defence after the prosecution concluded leading its evidence.\n\nHowever,\u00a0 after the retrial process, the Colombo Trial-at-Bar\u00a0 sentenced former Inspector General of Police (IGP) to death after finding him guilty on several charges, including criminal dereliction of duty for failing to take action to prevent the Easter Sunday terrorist attacks, despite receiving prior intelligence warnings."
  },
