    "language": "en",
    "md5": "19fbd5dd5345a6d0dd84374fffca372a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1697,
    "chunk_text": "To be fair to Trump\u2014even though he is rarely fair to anyone, not even himself\u2014he qualified his outrageous statement. He said he would annihilate Iran if no deal was reached to end the war, while adding in the same breath that an agreement could come soon.\n\nA nuclear strike is easier said than done. No world leader with a modicum of rationality would ever resort to it. But Trump being Trump, nothing can be ruled out. Moreover, he is said to suffer from narcissistic personality disorder. Its latest manifestation was his decision to ban CNN, MS Now, and Politico journalists from covering his events\u2014a form of punishment for their criticism of his administration. On Thursday, a federal judge issued a temporary ruling blocking White House officials from barring journalists from Trump events. The embarrassing blow came when Trump was according the visiting Chinese President Xi Jinping a welcome that he had not given any world leader before.\n\nAs Trump delivered his UN speech, the Iranian delegate in the audience appeared unmoved and defiant. Within hours, Iran responded, declaring that Tehran was in \u201cfull readiness to inflict more severe, crushing, and unpredictable blows on the aggressors.\u201d\n\nAmid the erratic nature of Trump\u2019s politics, US officials\u2014assisted by the shuttle diplomacy of Qatari mediators\u2014held talks with Iran\u2019s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi at the UN to explore ways to end the war.\n\nTrump claimed the talks US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner held with Araghchi made progress, but reports indicated Iran insisted that the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding be revived and its provisions honoured, adding that peace could be just days away."
  },
  {
