    "md5": "aa7f51f9ced9bcc059f96e237728f8dc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "Regulation or control? NGO Bill puts civic space under scrutiny: TPA\u2019s Barath\n\nColombo, Sep 26 (Daily Mirror) - The proposed NGO law could weaken organisations that help citizens challenge government decisions, defend their rights and obtain essential assistance, Democratic People\u2019s Front Vice President for International Affairs and TPA Politburo Member Barath Arullsamy warned.\n\nArullsamy said the circulated draft Non-Governmental Organisations (Registration and Supervision) law would extend compulsory registration across a broad range of civil society organisations, require renewal every three years and give a minister-appointed authority powers to inspect, suspend or deregister organisations. It also requires NGOs to \u201calign with the policies of the Government\u201d.\n\n\u201cConsider an organisation helping families challenge a government land decision. If it must align with government policy, can it defend those families without fearing consequences? That uncertainty alone can discourage people from speaking out,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cRepeated compliance costs could also leave small organisations with fewer resources for legal assistance, women\u2019s protection or disaster relief. Ultimately, the public could lose both practical support and an independent voice.\u201d\n\nThe NGO Secretariat\u2019s official July 24 statement says the Bill aims to strengthen regulation and reduce money-laundering, terrorist-financing and proliferation-financing risks. It also records consultations and an invitation for written civil society submissions.\n\nArullsamy said preventing financial crime and terrorist financing were legitimate regulatory objectives, but did not explain why independent organisations should be required to align with government policy."
