    "md5": "5fed771afe2a8d63f735db6a71eb8a73",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1988,
    "chunk_text": "Arullsamy said preventing financial crime and terrorist financing were legitimate regulatory objectives, but did not explain why independent organisations should be required to align with government policy.\n\n\u201cThe Government must identify what these existing laws cannot address and justify each additional power. If financial crime is the problem, why make agreement with government policy part of the solution?\u201d\n\nHe pointed out that the Financial Action Task Force\u2019s Recommendation 8 calls for proportionate measures based on identified terrorist-financing risks while protecting legitimate nonprofit activity.\n\nAt the Sri Lanka CSO FATF Network\u2019s September 17 event, UN Resident Coordinator Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche warned that blanket restrictions could undermine security and development. A Global NPO Coalition co-chair also warned that excessive banking caution could delay humanitarian funding.\n\n\u201cWe should not confuse effective financial regulation with blanket control of civil society. FATF itself has moved towards a targeted and proportionate approach. Sri Lanka should be careful not to go further than what genuine financial-security risks require,\u201d Arullsamy said.\n\nHe recalled the Defence Ministry\u2019s 2014 restrictions on NGO media activities, including press conferences, workshops, journalist training and the dissemination of press releases, on the basis that such activities were considered beyond their mandate, when Gotabaya Rajapaksa was Defence Secretary.\n\nDuring the good governance era, Mano Ganesan withdrew contested NGO amendments during his ministerial tenure.\n\n\u201cDuring Leader Mano Ganesan\u2019s ministerial tenure, many organisations, including Amnesty International, secured NGO registration after previously being refused under the earlier Rajapaksa administration. That was an opening of democratic space. Regulation must not become a mechanism through which governments decide what civil society may say, advocate or question,\u201d Arullsamy said."
