    "md5": "c539b2de9115913bda4957ac35e15d48",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1774,
    "chunk_text": "NMSJ urges lawmakers to avoid conflict of interest in proposed 22A\n\nColombo, Sep. 27 (Daily Mirror) - The National Movement for Social Justice (NMSJ) has urged lawmakers to carefully reconsider the proposed extension of the terms of judges of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal under the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution, warning that the reform could place the Supreme Court in a position where it has to hear a case concerning the tenure of its own judges.\n\nIn a statement, NMSJ General Secretary Sunil Jayasekara said the proposed amendment should be considered through a multi-party discussion involving all relevant stakeholders, with particular attention given to preserving judicial independence.\n\nThe Bill containing the proposed 22nd Amendment was gazetted and presented to Parliament on September 18. Several parties have indicated that they intend to seek judicial review of its constitutionality, the NMSJ said.\n\nThe organisation said this could create a situation where Supreme Court judges would have to determine an issue directly concerning the extension of their own terms of office.\n\nIt said this raised the legal principle nemo judex in causa sua \u2014 that no person should be a judge in his or her own case.\n\nThe NMSJ noted that the Supreme Court is empowered under the Constitution to examine the constitutionality of Bills and said the proposed amendment should therefore be drafted in a manner that avoids such a conflict.\n\nIt pointed to approaches adopted in other countries, including the use of recently retired judges or separate constitutional courts to hear cases involving similar issues.\n\nThe organisation said Sri Lanka currently has neither a separate constitutional court nor a panel of retired judges that could be used for such cases."
