    "md5": "f1428c0b26d3b91b90149471df5e3b6c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1174,
    "chunk_text": "It pointed to approaches adopted in other countries, including the use of recently retired judges or separate constitutional courts to hear cases involving similar issues.\n\nThe organisation said Sri Lanka currently has neither a separate constitutional court nor a panel of retired judges that could be used for such cases.\n\nThe NMSJ also said that if the Supreme Court determines that the proposed amendment requires approval at a referendum, the final decision would rest with the people and would not raise the same concern about judges deciding a matter affecting their own tenure.\n\nIt warned that extending the terms of sitting judges without a referendum could create perceptions that the administration of justice was not impartial, potentially affecting public confidence in the independence of the Judiciary.\n\nThe organisation urged lawmakers to exercise the sovereignty of the people in a manner that would not place the Supreme Court in a position where it has to hear its own case or weaken public confidence in the independence of the Judiciary.\n\nIt also called for constructive discussions with the Bar Association of Sri Lanka to find a solution to the issue."
