    "md5": "a514a5f5eaabd94021fcca5ba9d57cda",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1821,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018National Children\u2019s Day Week\u2019 from 25 September to 1 October\n\nCabinet approves week-long observance to precede Children\u2019s Day, aimed at raising awareness of children\u2019s rights amid growing digital-era risks\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved the implementation of special programs declaring a \u2018National Children\u2019s Day Week 2026,\u2019 to be observed from 25 September to 1 October, in the lead-up to Sri Lanka\u2019s national Children\u2019s Day commemoration on 1 October.\n\nThe country marks Children\u2019s Day annually with the objective of strengthening social attention toward protecting children\u2019s rights, education, health, and wellbeing.\n\nAddressing the weekly post-Cabinet meeting media briefing, Cabinet Spokesman and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa last week said it has been observed that the challenges facing children today have broadened well beyond traditional child protection concerns, now encompassing risks, psychological and social stress, negligence, and various social and economic vulnerabilities linked to internet use and digital technology.\n\nRecognising this shift, he said authorities identified a need to build awareness not only around children\u2019s rights, but also around the responsibilities of all parties consistently associated with children, including parents, teachers, public officials, service providers, and the general public.\n\n\u201cIn response, it was proposed to establish a dedicated National Children\u2019s Day Week alongside the national Children\u2019s Day commemoration, aimed at raising public awareness of children\u2019s rights, fostering greater social sensitivity towards children, and encouraging wider community involvement in child protection efforts,\u201d he added.\n\nThe proposal to this effect was submitted by Women and Child Affairs Minister Saroja Savithri Paulraj."
  },
