    "md5": "36caf6384eb924cce47fd7b336f22350",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1699,
    "chunk_text": "Trust is the ultimate currency of every profession: Capital Maharaja Group CEO\n\nFrom left: AAT Sri Lanka Chief Executive Officer Tishanga Kumarasinghe, President Dr. Chamara Bandara, Capital Maharaja Group CEO Suresh Srinivasan, Vice President Dayal Abhayasinghe, Chief Operating Officer Himali Dasanayake and Conference Committee Chairman and Council Member Isuru Kalhara\n\nTrust built on integrity is the ultimate currency of every profession, Capital Maharaja Group CEO Suresh Srinivasan said, urging accounting professionals to hold firm to their values as technology reshapes their work.\n\nSrinivasan was delivering the Chief Guest address at the AAT Sri Lanka Conference 2026, held under the theme \u2018Precision to Power\u2019 on 8 and 9 September at Waters Edge, Battaramulla.\n\nThe two-day conference brought together industry leaders, professionals and students for sessions on entrepreneurship, governance, data-driven decision-making and transformative leadership.\n\nWhile the pace of technological change will only accelerate, he noted that the fundamentals of the profession remain constant: integrity, objectivity, professional competence, confidentiality and professional behaviour.\n\n\u201cThese foundations build trust, which is the ultimate currency of every profession. That is how we move from precision to power,\u201d he stressed.\n\nTracing his roots to a modest family in southern India, where his father served as a government employee for 40 years, Srinivasan recalled that his earliest lessons in ethics came from home rather than the classroom.\n\n\u201cLong before I learned about integrity through a professional code of ethics, I learned it by watching my father live,\u201d he told delegates."
  },
