    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Trust-is-the-ultimate-currency-of-every-profession-Capital-Maharaja-Group-CEO/273-351230",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790554367.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-28-dailymirrorlk-0eb8cb74-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fe2bb391f0cabe69390fc700291c5b7d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1502,
    "chunk_text": "Tracing his roots to a modest family in southern India, where his father served as a government employee for 40 years, Srinivasan recalled that his earliest lessons in ethics came from home rather than the classroom.\n\n\u201cLong before I learned about integrity through a professional code of ethics, I learned it by watching my father live,\u201d he told delegates.\n\nA turning point came at Britannia, where he accepted an operational role others had declined, running packaging operations involving hundreds of employees and strong union representation.\n\n\u201cThat one \u2018yes\u2019 changed my career and taught me that leadership begins when we are willing to own the outcomes,\u201d he reflected.\n\nAt 40, while at CavinKare, a serious health challenge left him considering a university post at a fraction of his salary, until his boss persuaded him to stay and later entrusted him with the entire supply chain.\n\n\u201cAt a time when I was losing confidence in myself, someone else still believed in me,\u201d Srinivasan explained.\n\nAfter joining E.I.D. Parry in 2014, his team revived a dormant sugar refining business, taking it from significant losses to break-even within 22 months before expanding into value-added products such as brown, low-calorie and low-GI sugar.\n\n\u201cAt the end of our careers, we will all have a personal balance sheet showing assets and wealth, but there is one line no accounting standard will require us to disclose: how many people are better off because we were part of their journey?\u201d"
  },
