    "chunk_size_bytes": 1688,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cFor decades CA Sri Lanka has stood as the guardian of financial integrity and corporate governance in our nation. Today, by inaugurating this specialised faculty, we are sharpening our national defences against one of the most insidious threats of the modern era, which is financial crime, corruption and money laundering,\u201d he said.\n\nSpeaking further, Dayaratne emphasised that accountants, auditors and financial managers are the true gatekeepers of our economy.\n\nHe also called on the professionals to embrace their responsibility to report suspicious transactions and activities, noting that equipping the accountancy profession with specialised forensic skills would enable the auditors to move beyond their traditional role and develop the capabilities required to support investigations and deliver court-admissible financial evidence.\n\nDelivering his speech, Subasinghe said, \u201cBy upskilling and reskilling our own members and other stakeholders who are interested in following these particular programmes and developing the required expertise, we are sure that they will fight against corporate fraud and corruption, with the technical know-how that they are going to gain by following our programmes.\u201d\n\n\u201cI believe this combination of a professional qualification in forensic accounting, together with practical exposure to digital forensics, will provide an important foundation for developing the next generation of forensic professionals,\u201d Jayasekera said.\n\nThe faculty\u2019s Alternate Chairman Dr. Mudith Sujeewa highlighted the impact of employee fraud and the importance of behavioural red flags and forensic accounting education in strengthening fraud prevention."
