    "md5": "e604a2b3e2e0edd7420eb5ee80e5ee83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "CB carves top-rated foreign sovereigns out of large exposure limits as bank NFA rebuilds\n\nTop-rated foreign sovereign paper exempt from 25% single-borrower cap, subject to 50% of Tier 1 ceiling\n\nCommercial banks\u2019 NFA up 32% since December to Rs.467bn in June\n\nLicensed banks will be able to deploy up to half of their Tier 1 capital in the local-currency debt of any single top-rated foreign sovereign from October 1, carving such holdings out of the prudential large exposure regime, according to directions issued by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) last week.\n\nBanking Act Directions No. 05 of 2026 exempt instruments issued or guaranteed by foreign sovereigns and their central banks that attract a zero percent risk weight under Basel III capital rules. Such holdings will fall outside both the 25 percent single-borrower limit and the 55 percent cap on aggregate large exposures.\n\nHowever, exposure to any one sovereign and its central bank is capped at 50 percent of Tier 1 capital, twice the previous limit, and the instruments must be permitted by the CBSL and denominated in the issuer\u2019s domestic currency. With zero risk weights typically reserved for sovereigns rated AA- and above, dollar-denominated US Treasuries fall within scope, while hard-currency paper of lower-rated emerging market issuers is excluded.\n\nUntil now, holdings of a single foreign sovereign\u2019s paper beyond a quarter of Tier 1 capital would have breached the single-borrower limit. That left placements with correspondent banks, which carry counterparty risk and count against the same limits, as the main outlet for surplus dollar liquidity.\n\nNet foreign assets (NFA) of commercial banks rose to Rs.467.4 billion in June 2026, as per CBSL data. That is up 12.1 percent month-on-month and 32.2 percent above the end-December 2025 level. The position remains 16.0 percent below the April 2025 peak of Rs.556.5 billion, but marks a reversal from a net foreign liability of Rs.732.9 billion in April 2022."
  },
