    "md5": "ae9b3ac14bae5d4dec15d4da4cf47d03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "Net foreign assets (NFA) of commercial banks rose to Rs.467.4 billion in June 2026, as per CBSL data. That is up 12.1 percent month-on-month and 32.2 percent above the end-December 2025 level. The position remains 16.0 percent below the April 2025 peak of Rs.556.5 billion, but marks a reversal from a net foreign liability of Rs.732.9 billion in April 2022.\n\nForeign currency holdings and balances due from banks abroad rose to Rs.1.52 trillion in June, up 18.0 percent from end-December and 30.3 percent year-on-year, according to CBSL data on commercial bank assets. Offshore banking units\u2019 claims on non-resident non-bank entities, a category that includes foreign securities, climbed 22.1 percent in six months to Rs.787.3 billion, more than double the level at end-2024. By contrast, rupee Treasury bill holdings fell 40.1 percent year-on-year to Rs.1.64 trillion, while loans and advances expanded 24.7 percent to Rs.11.70 trillion.\n\nThe directions followed Fitch Ratings\u2019 upgrade of Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term foreign currency issuer default rating to \u2018B-\u2019 from \u2018CCC+\u2019 two days earlier, though the agency flagged modest reserve buffers against rising external debt service. Its end-2026 reserve projection of US$ 7.7 billion implies a further US$ 800 million build-up from the US$ 6.9 billion held at end-August.\n\nIn the US, the Federal Reserve raised its policy rate on September 16 for the first time since 2023, and the 10-year Treasury yield touched 5.22 percent on September 24, its highest since 2007. Elevated front-end yields improve the carry on short-dated bills. The long-end sell-off, however, exposes longer-duration holdings to mark-to-market losses, which would erode capital where securities are carried at fair value through other comprehensive income (FVOCI).\u00a0 (NF)"
