    "md5": "8b10860124ba91eb6d7e9a2d1574fe31",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1940,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. eyes AI platform to plug OTA commission leak from unregistered rooms\n\nSri Lanka could retain the 20 to 25 percent commission currently paid to global online travel agencies (OTAs) on tens of thousands of unregistered rooms by bringing the country\u2019s largely informal tourism sector onto an artificial intelligence-based platform, Deputy Minister of Tourism Prof. Ruwan Ranasinghe said.\n\nAddressing the national celebration of World Tourism Day 2026, hosted by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) at Taj Samudra, Colombo on Friday (25) under the theme \u2018Digital Agenda and Artificial Intelligence to Redesign Tourism\u2019, Deputy Minister pointed out that a large part of the industry remains informal and outside the direct purview of the regulator. \u201cWe can have a more regulated and even governed tourism sector if we absorb that into an AI platform. I think it is an investment that we need to work on,\u201d he told the gathering, adding that harnessing AI to take the industry to the next level must now find a place on SLTDA\u2019s agenda.\n\nHe argued that the commissions paid to OTAs for rooms not registered with the authority represent money the country need not lose. \u201cIf we can bring these onto that platform, we can save that money within our country in its entirety,\u201d he noted. On his figures, every US$ 100 of room revenue booked through such channels sends between US$ 20 and 25 out of the country.\n\nHowever, the Deputy Minister suggested the gains extend beyond commissions to pricing itself. Referring to an example from Dr. Subasinghe\u2019s keynote on AI in hospitality, in which an average daily rate (ADR) of about US$ 225 served as the starting point for a negotiated rate, he observed that Colombo\u2019s ADR sits at roughly US$ 100 to 125. The keynote benchmark is thus 80 to 125 percent above the Colombo range, a gap he believed AI-driven negotiation could help hotels narrow during the high season."
