    "chunk_text": "Thema Collection opens Deewara, plans silent-safari retreat to ease Yala crowding\n\nThema Collection has opened Deewara, its newest property on the Mawella coast, and is advancing a silent-safari retreat near Yala designed to ease crowding at the wildlife park, alongside new resort concepts for Kalpitiya and Ella, the family-owned hotel group\u2019s leadership revealed.\n\nSpeaking at a media gathering held in Colombo last Thursday, Thema Collection Founder and Chairman Chandra Wickramasinghe described Deewara as offering guests an entirely different experience on an untouched stretch of coastline beyond Tangalle. Acquired this year, the restored fisherman\u2019s cottage draws its character from the island\u2019s maritime heritage and features in the first episode of the relaunched Rendez-vous with Yasmin and Kumar, airing on 1 October. The Yala project addresses growing concern over congestion at the country\u2019s most visited national park. Some 2,000 hotel rooms ring its main entrance, the chairman pointed out, and around 100 jeeps a day pour in through it, a scene he likened to entering a city.\n\nBy contrast, fewer than eight jeeps a day use a quieter entrance which the government has already invested in opening, but which remains underused for want of accommodation nearby.\n\nThema plans to fill that gap with tree-house accommodation and silent safaris in electric vehicles, with tightly limited entries, while rehabilitating the surrounding buffer land.\n\nHowever, although the lease is now secured, the project must still obtain clearances from as many as 23 state institutions before construction can begin, alongside consultations with village societies and temple committees.\n\n\u201cThey collect 23 institutions again and again. This is the problem,\u201d he lamented, suggesting such approvals be completed within around six months, depending on the sensitivity of the land."
