    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "In Ella, the group is crafting a resort inspired by the Ravana legend woven into the surrounding hills, drawing on temple architecture and traditional havelis, a theme Maxime Wickramasinghe stressed would be interpreted with due reverence. Both projects remain at the design stage.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s more clips of storytelling, that\u2019s how it sells now,\u201d the chairman explained.\n\nAmba Yaalu in Kandalama, Sri Lanka\u2019s first hotel run entirely by women, and Wild Glamping Gal Oya, created with the Vedda community of Rathugala, both won Gold at the recent ICRT Indian Subcontinent Responsible Tourism Awards. Both now advance to the Global Responsible Tourism Awards in London in November. The group\u2019s carbon emissions are independently verified by Control Union.\n\nTurning to the wider industry, Maxime Wickramasinghe remarked that user-generated content and a younger generation of travellers were drawing guests beyond the classic circuit to places such as Sigiriya, Gal Oya and Wasgamuwa.\n\n\u201cThey want to go and explore, they want experiences, they want different things,\u201d he explained.\n\nSri Lankans account for over 30 percent of the group\u2019s clientele, he revealed, followed by the UK, France and Australia. He credited a diverse marketing mix with lifting India from about 0.5 percent to around 6 percent of group occupancy.\n\nThe chairman identified air connectivity as key to the industry\u2019s next phase of growth.\n\n\u201cWe need more airline seats,\u201d he emphasised, welcoming French Bee\u2019s planned Paris, Mal\u00e9 and Colombo service from December.\n\nHe also urged the private sector to take the lead in promoting the island.\n\n\u201cWe can\u2019t wait until government or tourist board or somebody is going to market us, so we have to market it ourselves,\u201d he asserted."
