    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka among world\u2019s ten fastest improvers in WEF tourism index\n\nScore up 4.3% to 3.85, more than double the global average gain\n\nClimbs four places to 69th, second in South Asia after India\n\nSri Lanka ranked among the 10 fastest-improving economies globally in the latest benchmark for tourism development conditions. It lifted its score by 4.3 percent to climb four places to 69th among 110 economies, narrowly ahead of India on the pace of improvement, according to World Economic Forum (WEF) data.\n\nSri Lanka was one of seven developing South and South-East Asian economies among the WEF\u2019s 10 fastest improvers between 2024 and 2026, and one of only two from South Asia alongside Nepal. Albania topped the list with a 7 percent gain, followed by Vietnam at 6.3 percent, Laos at 6.1 percent, Qatar at 6 percent and Malaysia at 5.8 percent. Thailand improved by 5.6 percent, the Philippines and Morocco by 5.5 percent each, and Nepal by 5.1 percent. India, which also posted a 4.3 percent gain on a rounded basis, narrowly missed the list.\n\nSri Lanka scored 3.85 out of 7 in the Travel & Tourism Development Index 2026. Its gain was more than double the 2.1 percent average improvement across all ranked economies, which the WEF described as the fastest pace since 2019. Even so, the country sits 0.33 points short of the top 50, where Israel holds 50th place with 4.18.\n\nThe WEF recalculated earlier editions under a revised framework covering 110 economies, which placed Sri Lanka 73rd in the comparable 2024 results. The 2024 edition, which assessed 119 economies, had originally ranked the country 76th, as reported by Mirror Business at the time.\n\nHowever, the recent momentum is yet to restore lost ground. Sri Lanka still sits four places below the 65th position it held in the recalculated 2019 results, and its score has grown by just 2.7 percent over the seven years. That is marginally behind the 2.9 percent average improvement across the index over the same period."
