    "md5": "93a1e4aba6827c177fc18d6509a16c6e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1715,
    "chunk_text": "However, the recent momentum is yet to restore lost ground. Sri Lanka still sits four places below the 65th position it held in the recalculated 2019 results, and its score has grown by just 2.7 percent over the seven years. That is marginally behind the 2.9 percent average improvement across the index over the same period.\n\nMeanwhile, South-East Asian competitors turned faster improvement into bigger jumps in rank. Malaysia surged 13 places to 26th, Thailand rose 10 places to 38th and Vietnam advanced seven places to 52nd, while Laos moved up 10 places to 75th. Sri Lanka\u2019s score is above the South Asian average of 3.71 but short of the Asia-Pacific average of 4.29, leaving it 13th among the 18 Asia-Pacific economies ranked. Among the 29 lower-middle-income economies in the index, based on the World Bank\u2019s classification, Sri Lanka ranked fifth, behind India, Vietnam, the Philippines and Egypt.\n\nWhile the report does not publish pillar scores for individual economies, its South Asia profile shows where the subregion\u2019s weaknesses lie. South Asia scored just 1.43 out of 7 on tourist services and infrastructure and 3.24 on business environment, the lowest of any subregion globally on both counts.\n\nBy contrast, it was the top-ranked subregion for price competitiveness, at 5.73, and for tourism demand sustainability, at 4.52. Its natural resources score rose by 5.1 percent, the third-largest subregional increase.\n\nThat pricing advantage stands out against a global squeeze. Price competitiveness declined by 3.1 percent on average between 2024 and 2026, and travel became less affordable in three out of four economies as higher labour, food and energy costs fed into prices, the WEF noted."
