    "md5": "8b8424ea09656dbe1603d269ddb1fbbe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "DS\u2019s Astuteness, Delivered in JR\u2019s Oratory: J. R. Spoke for Ceylon,  But Who Sent Him There?\n\nJ. R. Jayawardene spoke at the 1951 San Francisco Conference\n\nYet in his autobiography, \u2018Men and Memories\u2019, Jayewardene admitted something that recasts the episode. The idea of championing full Japanese independence, he confessed, was not his own; it belonged to his Prime Minister, D. S. Senanayake.\n\nAt San Francisco, Jayewardene\u2019s central question was deceptively simple: why were the peoples of Asia anxious that Japan should be free? His answer drew on history rather than diplomacy alone.\n\nIn September 1951, Ceylon\u2019s Finance Minister J. R. Jayewardene rose to speak at one of the most consequential diplomatic gatherings of the post-war world: the San Francisco Conference on the peace treaty with Japan. Representing a nation barely three years independent, he argued for restoring Japan\u2019s sovereignty, equality and dignity without qualification. The New York Times described the moment as one in which \u201cthe voice of free Asia\u201d dominated the proceedings. History would remember Jayewardene very differently after 1977, when his presidency left a legacy many Sri Lankans still regard as disastrous. But that day, he spoke as a young minister, extending \u201ca hand of friendship\u201d to a defeated former enemy.\n\nFor seven decades the speech has stood as Jayewardene\u2019s own achievement, the moment a small island found an outsized voice on the world stage. Yet in his autobiography, \u2018Men and Memories\u2019, Jayewardene admitted something that recasts the episode. The idea of championing full Japanese independence, he confessed, was not his own; it belonged to his Prime Minister, D. S. Senanayake. Jayewardene, by his own account, travelled to San Francisco to deliver a message on Senanayake\u2019s behalf."
