    "md5": "6abdaae4ff3ad9ab5d9117488d849f8f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "For seven decades the speech has stood as Jayewardene\u2019s own achievement, the moment a small island found an outsized voice on the world stage. Yet in his autobiography, \u2018Men and Memories\u2019, Jayewardene admitted something that recasts the episode. The idea of championing full Japanese independence, he confessed, was not his own; it belonged to his Prime Minister, D. S. Senanayake. Jayewardene, by his own account, travelled to San Francisco to deliver a message on Senanayake\u2019s behalf.\n\nTwo years earlier, in 1949, Britain had moved to draw Ceylon more tightly into the Sterling Assets Agreement. This arrangement left the island\u2019s dollar earnings sitting under London\u2019s control even after independence. Senanayake sent Jayewardene, with adviser John Exter, later the first governor of the Central Bank, to the Commonwealth Finance Ministers\u2019 Conference with a blunt brief: to tell the British plainly that Ceylon\u2019s earnings were its own, not funds to be managed from London\u2019s coffers, and that if Britain would not let Ceylon act independently over its own money, Ceylon would simply leave the Sterling Area.\n\nIt was a threat Whitehall could not brush aside, calculated from Colombo down to the last detail. San Francisco, two years on, repeated the same arrangement on a larger stage: the Prime Minister supplied the position, the finance minister supplied the voice.\n\nThe roots of Ceylon\u2019s position lay at the Colombo Conference of Commonwealth Foreign Ministers in January 1950, where the case for a fully independent Japan was first seriously advanced. Two ideas emerged there: that Japan should regain its sovereignty, and that South and South-East Asia needed economic development if their independence was to mean anything in practice. The second idea would eventually produce the Colombo Plan. The first would shape Ceylon\u2019s position at San Francisco the following year."
