    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "The roots of Ceylon\u2019s position lay at the Colombo Conference of Commonwealth Foreign Ministers in January 1950, where the case for a fully independent Japan was first seriously advanced. Two ideas emerged there: that Japan should regain its sovereignty, and that South and South-East Asia needed economic development if their independence was to mean anything in practice. The second idea would eventually produce the Colombo Plan. The first would shape Ceylon\u2019s position at San Francisco the following year.\n\nCeylon\u2019s foreign policy could not be separated from its economic vulnerabilities. Its comfortable living standard rested on a handful of exports, and when American wartime rubber stockpiles hit the market, prices collapsed. New markets were needed, and one arrived unexpectedly: China, whose purchases sustained the rubber industry and later funded a replantation programme, provided proof that survival did not always respect Cold War lines.\n\nAt San Francisco, Jayewardene\u2019s central question was deceptively simple: why were the peoples of Asia anxious that Japan should be free? His answer drew on history rather than diplomacy alone. Asia, he argued, shared long-standing cultural ties with Japan, the one Asian nation to have grown strong and independent while others remained under colonial rule. He went further into uncomfortable territory, acknowledging that Japan\u2019s wartime rhetoric of Asian \u201cco-prosperity\u201d had genuinely appealed to nationalists in Burma, India and Indonesia, some of whom had cooperated with Tokyo hoping a Japanese victory might hasten their own liberation.\n\nIt was an uneasy truth to voice so soon after the war, but it captured how differently Asian nationalists had experienced Japan\u2019s wartime challenge to colonial rule. Jayewardene was not defending wartime Japan; he was separating that old Japan from the independent nation Asia\u2019s future might require."
