    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "It was an uneasy truth to voice so soon after the war, but it captured how differently Asian nationalists had experienced Japan\u2019s wartime challenge to colonial rule. Jayewardene was not defending wartime Japan; he was separating that old Japan from the independent nation Asia\u2019s future might require.\n\nHe was equally forthright in criticising Soviet amendments concerning political freedom, expression, religion and assembly, observing with pointed sarcasm that these were freedoms the Soviet people themselves might well envy. He also endorsed Japan\u2019s right to organise its own defence, accepting the protection of a friendly power in the meantime, a position that aligned closely with Washington\u2019s own.\n\nCeylon\u2019s stance was therefore both principled and pragmatic: Buddhist ideals of forgiveness sat alongside hard Cold War calculation, and alongside a Prime Minister\u2019s quiet determination to secure new markets, allies and influence for a small nation newly arrived on the world stage.\n\nWhat Jayewardene\u2019s later admission makes clear is that this combination of idealism, strategy and timing was Senanayake\u2019s design as much as, if not more than, his own. The Prime Minister, absent from San Francisco just as he had been absent from London two years before, had shaped both outcomes from Colombo. His finance minister carried the message each time, delivering it with a rhetorical skill that made it his own in the world\u2019s eyes, and in history\u2019s memory, for seventy years.\n\nThe goodwill Ceylon earned that day did not fade with the men who created it. Japan went on to become one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most significant development partners, its grants and concessional loans underwriting decades of infrastructure and economic growth, a legacy owed as much to a Prime Minister\u2019s foresight in Colombo as to the eloquence of the minister who spoke his words abroad."
