    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "\u2019\u2019Sri Lanka emerges as key contender for Indian ocean blue economy hub\u2019\u2019\n\nColombo, Sep. 28 (Daily Mirror) -\u00a0For centuries, the waters around Sri Lanka were the silent engine of global commerce. Arab, Chinese, Indian, and later European traders rode the monsoon winds to our shores, turning ancient ports like Galle, Trincomalee, and Jaffna into bustling nodes of exchange. That historic maritime path never lost its strategic value it merely changed its cargo.\n\nImports: Roman coins, Persian and Chinese ceramics, glassware, and luxury textiles.\n\nMantai (Mahatittha): Located on the northwestern coast near Mannar, it was the island's premier trading emporium from the 5th century BCE to the 13th century CE, connecting ships from Rome, Arabia, Persia, and China.\n\nGodavaya (Godapavata): Situated on the southern coast near Hambantota, this bustling port served the ancient Ruhuna kingdom and featured an organized customs system. It is also home to an ancient shipwreck dating back to the 2nd century BCE.\n\nGokanna (Trincomalee): Located on the northeast coast, it provided one of the world's largest and safest natural deep-water harbors.\n\nJambukola: Located in the northern peninsula, it served as a key port for arrivals and departures to and from India, such as when the sacred Bodhi tree sapling was brought to Sri Lanka.\n\nKirinda: Positioned near the Kirindi Oya, it facilitated trade and connection for the southern inland hub of Tissamaharama.\n\nToday, the modern ports of Colombo and Hambantota stand as the direct inheritors of that legacy. They function as vital logistical hubs for refuelling and container shipping along the same historic sea routes, and their importance is only growing. With over 60% of the world's container traffic continuing to pass through the shipping lanes just off Sri Lanka's coast, the island is no longer a peripheral stop on the map. It is a pivotal junction in the architecture of global trade."
  },
