    "md5": "443f9bd62ebd650dbb8cdffd8f483cf8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1675,
    "chunk_text": "Today, the modern ports of Colombo and Hambantota stand as the direct inheritors of that legacy. They function as vital logistical hubs for refuelling and container shipping along the same historic sea routes, and their importance is only growing. With over 60% of the world's container traffic continuing to pass through the shipping lanes just off Sri Lanka's coast, the island is no longer a peripheral stop on the map. It is a pivotal junction in the architecture of global trade.\n\nThis geography has always been destiny. What is new is the scale and sophistication of the infrastructure built to serve it.\n\nThe Port of Colombo remains the crown jewel of Sri Lankan logistics. As South Asia's busiest transshipment hub, it handles millions of twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) each year, serving as a critical interchange for cargo destined for India, Bangladesh, Pakistan, and beyond. Its deep-water terminals, modern gantry cranes, and free-zone facilities allow it to compete with regional giants such as Singapore and Dubai.\n\nBut Colombo's success is not just about volume. It is about reliability. In an era when supply chains are fragile and delays cost millions, Colombo's ability to move containers efficiently from one vessel to another is a strategic asset\u2014not only for Sri Lanka but for the entire Indian Ocean rim.\n\nIf Colombo is the established anchor, Hambantota is the ambitious challenger. Located on the southern coast, just minutes from the main east-west shipping lane, Hambantota offers something Colombo cannot: proximity to the highway of global trade. Ships passing Sri Lanka need not deviate significantly to refuel, restock, or offload cargo."
  },
