    "md5": "7e1e9fc7245379f24e5926086d14e822",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1940,
    "chunk_text": "India-China Relations: He emphasized that regional peace and stability depend heavily on constructive ties between India and China. Improved relations between the two Asian giants benefit neighboring regions like Southeast Asia, which view friction between them with great anxiety.\n\nSri Lanka's Strategic Role: Yeo's remarks reinforced Sri Lanka's position as a pivotal junction in the architecture of global trade a nation whose geography has always been destiny, and whose modern ports are the inheritors of a rich maritime legacy.\n\nMany experts say, the real prize is not merely handling other countries' containers. It is building a maritime ecosystem that generates jobs, technology transfer, and industrial growth at home.\n\nBunkering and ship repair \u2013 servicing the thousands of vessels that pass daily.\u00b7 Logistics and warehousing \u2013 adding value to cargo before it moves on.\u00b7 Marine engineering and renewable energy tapping into offshore wind and ocean energy.\u00b7 Fisheries and aquaculture \u2013 modernising a traditional sector with sustainable practices.\n\nThese are not just economic opportunities; they are strategic imperatives. A port that only transships boxes is vulnerable to competition. A port that anchors an entire maritime cluster is indispensable.\n\nSri Lanka's geographic advantage is permanent. Its infrastructure and policy environment are not. To fully capitalise on the more than 60% of global container traffic passing its shores, the country must:\n\n1. Invest in digitalisation and automation at Colombo and Hambantota to match regional benchmarks.2. Streamline customs and trade facilitation to reduce turnaround times.3. Develop human capital in maritime logistics, engineering, and law.4. Pursue balanced diplomacy that protects national interests while attracting diverse international partners.5. Integrate port development with regional growth \u2013 ensuring that prosperity reaches beyond the harbour walls."
