    "md5": "db7a7e7dbcdedacb508c4a16b22cbe56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Driver demerit points system pilot project to begin October 1\n\nColombo, Sep. 28 (Daily Mirror) - A pilot project for introducing a driver demerit points system for traffic offences will commence on October 1, 2026, as part of the Government's efforts to improve road safety and strengthen the country's public transport system.\n\nThe six-month pilot project, which will continue until March 2027, is being implemented under the National Transport Action Plan to give drivers time to understand and adapt to the new safety-oriented system.\n\nThe initiative was announced at a special media briefing held today (28) at the Ministry of Transport, Highways and Urban Development auditorium at Maganeguma Mahamedura, Koswatta, Battaramulla, under the patronage of Transport, Highways and Urban Development Minister Bimal Rathnayake.\n\nThe pilot project is being jointly implemented by the Sri Lanka Police, Ministry of Digital Economy, Department of Motor Traffic, LankaPay and GovTech, under the guidance of the Ministry of Transport, Highways and Urban Development.\n\nOfficials said all drivers operating on expressways and highways will be required to comply with the system during the six-month pilot period.\n\nThe demerit points system is expected to be permanently implemented from March 2027 following the completion of the pilot phase. It will cover 33 specified traffic offences.\n\nMinister Bimal Rathnayake said the Government launched a sustainable programme to improve safety in the public transport sector in December 2024, stressing the need to address the more than 2,750 deaths caused by road accidents annually.\n\nHe said road accidents also cause significant damage to the country's economy and Gross Domestic Product (GDP), while resulting in a considerable loss of foreign exchange.\n\n\"A driving licence is not merely an asset or a right granted to a citizen, but also a responsibility,\" Rathnayake said."
  },
