    "chunk_text": "He said road accidents also cause significant damage to the country's economy and Gross Domestic Product (GDP), while resulting in a considerable loss of foreign exchange.\n\n\"A driving licence is not merely an asset or a right granted to a citizen, but also a responsibility,\" Rathnayake said.\n\n\"The impact of this system will be felt only by those who drive in disregard of the law,\" he said.\n\nThe entire demerit points system will operate through digital infrastructure, with the Government aiming to implement it nationwide by March 2027.\n\nMeanwhile, the Government has also streamlined the process of making online payments for traffic fines through Gov-Pay.\n\nThe online traffic fine payment system was initially launched as a pilot project in April 2025 on routes connecting Kurunegala and Anuradhapura. It was subsequently expanded islandwide in December 2025.\n\nDrivers can now settle traffic fines online through Gov-Pay using participating banks, financial institutions and fintech applications.\n\nAdditional Secretary to the Ministry of Transport, Highways and Urban Development Avanthi Senarathna, Deputy Inspector General of Police (Traffic Division) W.P.J. Senadheera, Advisor to the Ministry of Digital Economy Affairs Sumudu Rathnayake and LankaPay CEO Channa de Silva were among those present at the briefing."
