    "md5": "3be5e9203e1c17a3c18b020303701dc7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "US, Sri Lanka strengthen nuclear smuggling detection capabilities\n\nSep. 28 (Daily Mirror) - The United States and Sri Lanka have strengthened cooperation to detect and prevent the illicit trafficking of nuclear and radioactive materials following a five-day workshop conducted in Colombo from September 21 to 25.\n\nThe U.S. Embassy in Sri Lanka, through the U.S. Department of Energy\u2019s Office of Nuclear Smuggling Detection and Deterrence (NSDD), partnered with the Sri Lanka Navy to conduct the workshop, which focused on strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s capacity to identify and respond to nuclear smuggling threats.\n\nU.S. experts worked with Sri Lankan participants to develop standard operating procedures for nuclear smuggling detection operations. The procedures were developed with reference to international best practices while being tailored to Sri Lanka\u2019s existing legislative and regulatory framework.\n\nThe workshop also focused on improving coordination among Sri Lankan agencies involved in detecting and responding to the illicit movement of nuclear and other radioactive materials.\n\n\u201c Nuclear material in the wrong hands can have consequences far beyond any one country\u2019s borders,\u201d U.S. Ambassador Eric Meyer said.\n\n\u201cThrough this partnership, we\u2019re giving Sri Lankan agencies practical tools to detect illicit nuclear material and stop it before it can move through Sri Lanka\u2019s ports or waters. That strengthens security here, protects vital Indian Ocean trade routes, and helps keep Americans and our partners safer,\u201d he said.\n\nThe U.S. Embassy said the initiative was intended to ensure that the knowledge gained through the training would translate into practical procedures that Sri Lankan agencies could continue using after the workshop.\n\nThe programme is expected to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s long-term capacity to identify potential nuclear smuggling activities and improve the country's ability to respond to illicit trafficking through its ports and maritime areas."
  },
