    "chunk_id": "2026-09-28-dailymirrorlk-837ee49b-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a77e23c88a0b5532b5a270cf86e78435",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Rabies control has become a racket\u2019: AWC accuses Health Ministry of neglect\n\nColombo, Sep. 28 (Daily Mirror) - As Sri Lanka marks World Rabies Day today, the country\u2019s approach to controlling the deadly disease has come under sharp criticism, with the Veterinary Surgeon and Animal Welfare Coalition (AWC) alleging that authorities have placed greater emphasis on treating people after dog bites than preventing rabies at its source.\n\nA veterinary surgeon associated with the AWC alleged that billions of rupees were being spent on post-exposure treatment of people while insufficient attention was being given to mass vaccination of dogs, which remain the principal source of human rabies transmission.\n\nDescribing the situation as a \u201cracket,\u201d he alleged that low-quality vaccines were being used for dogs while costly vaccines were being imported and administered to people following suspected exposure.\n\n\u201cEvery day should be Rabies Day because the virus does not take holidays,\u201d he said, stressing that sustained, high-quality vaccination of dogs was essential to eliminating rabies.\n\nThe veterinary surgeon also alleged that the Ministry of Health had failed to properly implement a five-year rabies elimination programme developed in 2020 with the participation of the World Health Organization (WHO) and other stakeholders.\n\nHe claimed an assessment conducted in 2025 had found that the programme had not been adequately implemented.\n\nAccording to the AWC representative, Sri Lanka\u2019s rabies-control strategy had become disproportionately centred on post-exposure prophylaxis for people bitten or exposed to suspected rabid animals, instead of achieving adequate vaccination coverage among dogs.\n\nHe also raised concerns about the capacity of the field vaccination programme, claiming that Sri Lanka had only around 250 vaccinators, of whom at least 100 were not permanent employees."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-28-dailymirrorlk-837ee49b",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-837ee49b",
    "date_str": "2026-09-28",
    "description": "\u2018Rabies control has become a racket\u2019: AWC accuses Health Ministry of neglect",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/Rabies-control-has-become-a-racket-AWC-accuses-Health-Ministry-of-neglect/108-351286",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790588603.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-28-dailymirrorlk-837ee49b-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "80978c6d98ec6e654342a3d82f95f3ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "According to the AWC representative, Sri Lanka\u2019s rabies-control strategy had become disproportionately centred on post-exposure prophylaxis for people bitten or exposed to suspected rabid animals, instead of achieving adequate vaccination coverage among dogs.\n\nHe also raised concerns about the capacity of the field vaccination programme, claiming that Sri Lanka had only around 250 vaccinators, of whom at least 100 were not permanent employees.\n\n\u201cThe priority should be to vaccinate dogs in the field. Instead, the focus has been on importing vaccines at enormous cost and treating people after they are bitten,\u201d he alleged.\n\nThe veterinary surgeon also questioned the utilisation of funds allocated for dog population control.\n\nHe claimed that although Rs. 200 million had been allocated for dog population control in 2024, only around Rs. 100 million had been utilised, with the remainder returned to the Treasury.\n\nSimilarly, he claimed that of the Rs. 200 million allocated for the programme in 2025, only around Rs. 32 million had been spent.\n\nHe argued that the alleged under-utilisation of allocated funds raised questions about the effectiveness and commitment of the existing rabies-control mechanism.\n\nThe allegations come as the Government moves towards introducing legislation aimed at strengthening rabies control and regulating the dog population.\n\nUnder the proposed framework, responsibility for dog vaccination and population control would be transferred to the Department of Animal Production and Health, while local authorities would be responsible for dog registration.\n\nThe AWC representative alleged that the Health Ministry had become more active on the issue only after moves were initiated to establish the new institutional framework.\n\nHe also criticised what he described as attempts to present reductions in human rabies deaths as evidence of the overall success of the country's rabies-control programme."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-28-dailymirrorlk-837ee49b",
