    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "22574eb20748eb21e66e2072939def52",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1652,
    "chunk_text": "The AWC representative alleged that the Health Ministry had become more active on the issue only after moves were initiated to establish the new institutional framework.\n\nHe also criticised what he described as attempts to present reductions in human rabies deaths as evidence of the overall success of the country's rabies-control programme.\n\n\u201cThese claims of success need to be questioned. The public and politicians must know what is actually happening,\u201d he said.\n\nHe further alleged that vested interests linked to the procurement and use of human rabies vaccines could potentially be affected if greater responsibility for animal vaccination and dog-population management were transferred to veterinary authorities and local government institutions.\n\nThe allegations could not be independently verified, and the Ministry of Health and other agencies responsible for rabies control would need to respond to the claims.\n\nThe veterinary surgeon said World Rabies Day should not be reduced to ceremonies and awareness campaigns, arguing that the real measure of success should be the elimination of rabies from the country.\n\n\u201cIn my view, the day we can truly celebrate World Rabies Day is the day rabies is completely eradicated,\u201d he said.\n\nHe called for a coordinated national programme involving the Department of Animal Production and Health, local authorities and other relevant institutions, with emphasis on mass vaccination, dog-population control, registration and stronger measures to prevent zoonotic diseases.\n\nSri Lanka recorded 20 human rabies deaths in 2024, underscoring the continuing public-health threat posed by the disease."
  },
