    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka seeks to boost seafood exports to Russia\n\nColombo, Sep. 28 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka is seeking to expand exports of high-quality marine products, including tuna, prawns and crabs, to the Russian market, Fisheries Minister Ramalingam Chandrasekar said.\n\nHe made the remarks during a meeting with Russian Ambassador to Sri Lanka Levan S. Dzhagaryan at the Ministry of Fisheries, Aquatic and Ocean Resources today.\n\nThe meeting focused on strengthening bilateral cooperation in fisheries, maritime trade and economic relations, with particular attention to increasing access for Sri Lankan seafood products to the Russian market.\n\nThe two sides discussed international quality standards, food safety requirements and health certification procedures applicable to fish and seafood exports to Russia. They also explored practical measures to address challenges affecting bilateral maritime trade and commercial cooperation.\n\nMinister Chandrasekar highlighted the potential for Sri Lanka to use Russian technology, fishing vessels and specialised equipment to modernise its deep-sea fishing sector. He stressed the need for modern technology to reduce post-harvest losses and maintain the quality of marine catches.\n\nThe discussions also covered the possibility of attracting Russian investment and technical assistance to modernise fishing harbours, improve cold-storage facilities and strengthen infrastructure for handling and preserving marine products.\n\nAmbassador Dzhagaryan reaffirmed Russia\u2019s interest in expanding economic and trade cooperation with Sri Lanka, including greater market access for Sri Lankan seafood and increased investment and technical cooperation in the fisheries sector.\n\nThe two sides also discussed strengthening cooperation between research institutions to assess fish stocks within Sri Lanka\u2019s maritime boundaries, promote sustainable fishing practices and protect marine resources."
