    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e431de472f733f9dcf6b6e433045c21a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1735,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka pushes stronger social protection for migrant workers\n\nSri Lanka is stepping up efforts to strengthen social protection, safe migration pathways and long-term economic security for migrant workers, with Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism Acting Minister Arun Hemachandra calling for protection across the entire migration cycle.\n\nOpening the ILO STREAM introductory training on \u201cProtecting Those Who Move: Advancing Social Protection in the South Asia\u2013Gulf Migration Corridor\u201d in Colombo yesterday, Hemachandra said migration should be a pathway to opportunity rather than vulnerability.\n\nHe stressed that worker protection must begin before departure and continue through recruitment, overseas employment, access to social security, return and reintegration.\n\n\u201cProtecting those who move means ensuring that opportunities abroad translate into greater security for migrant workers and their families at home,\u201d Hemachandra said.\n\nThe program brought together representatives of countries of origin and destination to strengthen cooperation on the protection and social security of migrant workers, particularly along the South Asia\u2013Gulf migration corridor.\n\nHemachandra outlined several measures undertaken over the past two years, including increasing death compensation for eligible registered migrant workers to Rs. 2 million.\n\nHe also said work was progressing on a contributory pension scheme for migrant workers, while preparations were underway for a new Foreign Employment Bill aimed at strengthening the policy and regulatory framework governing overseas employment.\n\nThe Government is also preparing a mechanism to enable Sri Lankans living overseas to exercise their voting rights, he said."
  },
