    "chunk_text": "Hambantota could develop a deeper industrial and maritime ecosystem around its port rather than remaining trapped in an endless geopolitical argument about how it was financed. Tourism could move beyond counting arrivals towards attracting significantly more investment and expenditure per visitor through integrated resorts, wellness, marine tourism, high-quality urban hospitality and carefully planned destinations. Renewable energy could support both domestic competitiveness and new export-oriented industries. The north and east could be connected much more deliberately to India\u2019s enormous economic hinterland. None of these ideas is particularly novel. That is precisely the problem. Sri Lanka has discussed versions of them for years. The missing ingredient is not another vision document. It is execution architecture.\n\nSri Lanka should also resist one tempting conclusion from the Maldives announcement. The objective is not to find someone willing to announce an even larger number. Development value is not the same as foreign capital received. FDI is not synonymous with national prosperity. A megaproject is valuable only insofar as it produces productive activity, employment, exports, tax revenues, skills and opportunities that extend beyond the development boundary. The better question is therefore not: where is Sri Lanka\u2019s $ 20 billion deal? It is this: why does Sri Lanka not have a sufficiently deep pipeline of credible projects capable collectively of mobilising capital on that scale? That is a harder question because the answer cannot be outsourced to foreigners."
