    "md5": "6b60de3c67b22f7a958d829733990902",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "Hayleys installs solar in three Sri Lankan heritage hotels\n\nHayleys Solar has successfully completed solar power installations across three hospitality properties, Golden Star Beach Hotel (240 kWp), Tangerine Beach Hotel (780.57 kWp), and DoubleTree by Hilton Weerawila (280.25 kWp) supporting the hospitality sector\u2019s transition toward renewable energy and more sustainable operations.\n\nHaving welcomed generations of local and international travelers for decades, the transition to solar energy reflects each property\u2019s commitment to balancing exceptional guest experiences with environmental stewardship.\n\nTogether, the three installations are expected to offset approximately 1,356 tonnes of CO2 emissions annually equivalent to taking hundreds of vehicles off the road each year while reducing the properties\u2019 reliance on grid electricity.\n\nManaging Director of Hayleys Fentons Limited, Hasith Prematillake stated, \u201cThese installations demonstrate how renewable energy can support the hospitality sector in building more sustainable and efficient operations. We remain committed to enabling businesses across Sri Lanka to accelerate their transition to clean energy through reliable and innovative solar solutions.\u201d\n\nDeputy Managing Director \u2013 Energy of Hayleys Fentons Limited, Roshane Perera stated: \u201cThe hospitality sector plays a vital role in Sri Lanka\u2019s economy and global tourism appeal. By enabling these hotels to transition towards renewable energy, we are not only reducing their environmental footprint but also strengthening their long-term operational resilience. This milestone reflects our continued commitment to powering sustainable tourism through innovative solar solutions.\u201d\n\nWith this milestone, Hayleys Solar reaffirms its position at the forefront of Sri Lanka\u2019s renewable energy transition, continuing to partner with key sectors of the economy to build a more sustainable and resilient future."
  },
