    "time_ut": 1790656447.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-29-dailymirrorlk-674e01bc-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "84869628eda5d00dec19b58784f1f70a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1717,
    "chunk_text": "Prior to positioning the crane, the engineering teams executed extensive ground stabilisation, including aggregate base course preparation, soil compaction and rigorous load-bearing tests to ensure platform safety.\n\nHayleys Fentons Deputy Managing Director Energy Roshane Perera added, \u201cTurbine erection at this scale demands uncompromising safety standards, technical competence and disciplined execution. Our engineers, site supervisors and technical crews are Global Wind Organisation-certified, ensuring all operations strictly adhere to the globally benchmarked protocols for wind energy construction.\u201d\n\nHayWind One Chief Operating Officer Prashantha Amarasinghe said, \u201cEach lift is executed around dynamic ground and atmospheric conditions, strictly limited to wind speeds of 9.8 metres per second or below, in compliance with the OEM parameters. Ground stability and wind profiles are validated prior to every lift. Heavy-duty taglines ensure the lateral control of each section during hoisting, complemented by real-time visual wind indicators to maintain precision.\u201d\n\nUpon completion, the Mannar Wind Farm will comprise 10, 5MW turbines, delivering an aggregate capacity of 50MW.\n\nThe plant is projected to supply approximately 207 million units (kWh) of clean electricity annually to the national grid, under a 20-year Power Purchase Agreement, saving Sri Lanka an estimated Rs.100 billion in foreign exchange on fossil fuel imports over its operating lifecycle.\n\nWith the turbine installation advancing swiftly across the site, HayWind One remains firmly on schedule for commissioning and grid energisation by December 2026, marking another landmark stride in Sri Lanka\u2019s clean energy transition."
  },
