    "chunk_size_bytes": 2006,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s corporate debt swells to US $ 86.5bn as IIF flags data gaps\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s corporate debt climbs to 72% of GDP (US $ 86.5bn), highest among South Asian peers\n\nEstimate leans on domestic loan data, with no foreign currency breakdown available\n\nGovernment debt data to be updated from next issue, with restructuring about 94% complete\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s non-financial corporate debt is estimated to have risen to 72.0 percent of GDP or US $ 86.5 billion, by June 2026, the highest among the South Asian economies tracked, with the ratio rising in every quarter since early 2024, as per the data from Washington-based Institute of International Finance (IIF). The ratio has climbed three percentage points from 69.0 percent a year earlier and 6.5 percentage points from 65.5 percent in March 2024. In dollar terms, the estimate has grown 21.1 percent from US $ 71.4 billion in June 2025, close to twice the 11.2 percent increase in the emerging market corporate debt to US $ 42.8 trillion over the same period.\n\nThe sharpest move came in the first quarter of 2026, when the dollar figure jumped 12.6 percent from US $ 74.5 billion to US $ 83.8 billion. Sri Lanka\u2019s corporate leverage now sits well above India\u2019s 48.4 percent, Bangladesh\u2019s 30.3 percent and Pakistan\u2019s 10.7 percent and marginally above\n\nThailand\u2019s 70.3 percent, though it remains below the emerging market average of 89.1 percent and Malaysia\u2019s 88.7 percent.\n\nThe build-up contrasts with the household sector, where the debt ratio eased to 9.9 percent, from 11.2 percent a year earlier, leaving corporate debt more than seven times the size of household borrowing relative to output.\n\nResponding to the queries from Mirror Business, IIF Global Markets and Policy Director Emre Tiftik explained that the estimate combines the cross-border bank lending data from the Bank for International Settlements, outstanding bond data from Bloomberg and domestic lending data from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank."
