    "language": "en",
    "md5": "806e77f922efd466e26411978567f4c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1863,
    "chunk_text": "Responding to the queries from Mirror Business, IIF Global Markets and Policy Director Emre Tiftik explained that the estimate combines the cross-border bank lending data from the Bank for International Settlements, outstanding bond data from Bloomberg and domestic lending data from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank.\n\n\u201cGiven the limited availability of comprehensive underlying data, a currency breakdown of Sri Lanka\u2019s non-financial corporate debt is not readily available,\u201d he said.\n\nAs a result, the share of the corporate debt stock denominated in foreign currency and therefore exposed to the exchange rate and refinancing pressures, cannot be gauged from the IIF data.\n\nMeanwhile, the IIF confirmed that Sri Lanka\u2019s government debt figures for the quarters after end-2022 were carried forward in error. The database holds government debt at US $ 76.8 billion in every quarter from end-2022 to June 2026, while the report\u2019s country table prints an identical 115.9 percent of GDP for both June 2025 and June 2026.\n\n\u201cThe latest government debt figure for Sri Lanka in our database is for end-2022. The figures carried forward thereafter were the result of a data-entry error,\u201d Tiftik stated, adding that Sri Lanka has not been included in the IIF\u2019s aggregate emerging market government debt calculation since 2022 and that regular updates are expected to resume in the next issue as more official data become available.\n\nOn the sovereign side, Tiftik stressed that Sri Lanka\u2019s debt restructuring is not yet fully complete, with the agreements covering around 94 percent of external debt within the restructuring perimeter finalised, leaving roughly 6 percent still to be concluded.\n\n\u201cCompleting the remaining steps as soon as possible will be important to restore and deepen international market access,\u201d he observed."
