    "chunk_size_bytes": 1451,
    "chunk_text": "On the sovereign side, Tiftik stressed that Sri Lanka\u2019s debt restructuring is not yet fully complete, with the agreements covering around 94 percent of external debt within the restructuring perimeter finalised, leaving roughly 6 percent still to be concluded.\n\n\u201cCompleting the remaining steps as soon as possible will be important to restore and deepen international market access,\u201d he observed.\n\n\u201cWhile market access has improved, private capital is still trickling, not flowing at scale, to many frontier and developing economies,\u201d Tiftik pointed out, adding that stronger domestic capital markets are essential to attract international capital, as they improve market depth, transparency and liquidity.\n\nHe also highlighted timely and reliable information as a key constraint for the international investors, noting that Sri Lanka has made meaningful progress through more regular and substantive engagement with the creditors, as reflected in the IIF\u2019s latest investor-relations scores.\n\nGlobally, debt rose by more than US $ 10 trillion in the first half of 2026 to US $ 365.5 trillion, with non-financial corporate debt reaching a record US $ 105.1 trillion, up 6.1 percent from a year earlier, as per IIF data.\n\nAcross the emerging markets, excluding China, the governments and non-financial corporates accounted for most of the US $ 1.7 trillion rise in debt during the period, which lifted the total to a record US $ 38 trillion."
