    "md5": "ce9d70f8a413419ff7657d1097989b70",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "SpaceX\u2019s Starship makes orbital debut deploying Starlinks before early ending\n\nReuters\u00a0- SpaceX's (SPCX.O), giant Starship rocket reached orbit for the first time on Monday and deployed new Starlink satellites, achieving milestones in its 14th test flight despite an engine failure and a decision to cut \u200bthe mission short.\n\nThe uncrewed mission was scheduled to last 10 hours, but was reduced to three after one of Starship's three main engines shut down prematurely following separation from the spacecraft's \u200clower-stage Super Heavy booster rocket.\n\nIt is unclear how much of a setback Monday's engine failure, the latest glitch since Starship test flights began in 2023, will pose for the goal of Elon Musk's newly public company to put the powerful spacecraft into routine service later this year.\n\nUnlike past Starship flights, Monday's mission was both a high-stakes test of the next-generation rocket and an operational endeavor carrying millions of dollars' worth of Starlink satellites to orbit, representing Starship's first revenue-generating mission, even though its final design is still under \u200bdevelopment.\n\nSpaceX shares fell 2% on Monday. Much of SpaceX's market worth and continued investor confidence in the company \u2014 valued at $2 trillion as of Friday's close \u2014 ride on the success of Starship, which was designed as \u200bthe world's first fully reusable spacecraft.\n\nThe 40-story-tall vehicle, consisting of a Super Heavy booster rocket topped by the main upper-stage Starship vessel, launched from SpaceX's Starbase complex along \u2060the Gulf of Mexico near Brownsville.\n\nThe Super Heavy booster lofted the Starship upper stage into space before returning itself to Gulf waters for an engine-controlled splashdown, following hard landings for the booster on two previous missions. Some \u200bengines on that booster stopped working during its descent, SpaceX said."
  },
