    "md5": "d7a144cd05a8efbb33da357a78884aea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Middle East escalation threatens Sri Lanka on 6 economic fronts: ADB\n\nFlags risks to fuel, freight, fertiliser, food and subsidy costs\n\nWarns exports, remittances, tourism and reserves could come under pressure\n\nRemittances remain key buffer, rising 21.4% in first 7 months\n\nSri Lanka faces a multi-pronged economic threat from any further escalation of the Middle East conflict, the Asian Development Bank (ADB) said, as it warned that the fallout could simultaneously hit the import costs, exports, remittances, tourism, foreign reserves and the government\u2019s fiscal position.\n\nIn its Asian Development Outlook September 2026, the ADB said the risks to Sri Lanka\u2019s economic outlook remain tilted to the downside, with the Middle East conflict posing a particularly broad threat to an economy still rebuilding its external buffers.\n\n\u201cThe escalation of the Middle East conflict could raise the fuel, freight, fertiliser, food and subsidy costs and weigh on the exports, remittances, tourism and reserves,\u201d the ADB cautioned.\n\nThe warning indicates a potential double hit for Sri Lanka: the higher costs flowing into the economy through the energy, shipping and food channels, while the key sources of foreign exchange could weaken at the same time.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s external position has already deteriorated in 2026. The current account recorded a US $ 387 million deficit in the first seven months, reversing a US $ 1.7 billion surplus in the corresponding period of 2025.\n\nThe merchandise trade deficit widened 69 percent to US $ 6.5 billion, as the imports surged 25.8 percent while the exports grew by just 4.4 percent. Total goods and services exports rose 2.2 percent to US $ 12.3 billion but were insufficient to offset the higher import bill and a narrower services surplus.\n\nForeign reserves have also come under some pressure. The gross official reserves declined from US $ 6.8 billion at end-2025 to US $ 6.6 billion by end-July, equivalent to around 3.2 months of imports."
