    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "63ac1b05583d53cb43b5af63c9f5f963",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1881,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign reserves have also come under some pressure. The gross official reserves declined from US $ 6.8 billion at end-2025 to US $ 6.6 billion by end-July, equivalent to around 3.2 months of imports.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, the ADB stressed that \u201crebuilding reserves and sustaining investor confidence remain priorities\u201d, particularly with the public debt still high.\n\nThe Middle East risk is especially significant, given Sri Lanka\u2019s dependence on imported energy and its exposure to the Gulf economies through labour migration, aviation and tourism.\n\nThe ADB said the geopolitical pressures on the global energy markets have spread beyond crude oil, with disruption affecting the refined petroleum products and key shipping routes. The diesel and gasoline refining margins have risen sharply, while the higher security and insurance costs have kept the freight costs elevated.\n\nThis means relief in the international crude prices alone may not immediately translate into lower costs for the import-dependent economies. The ADB noted that the refined-product prices could remain elevated even if the crude prices ease, sustaining pressure on transport, industrial and agricultural costs.\n\nAcross Asia and the Pacific, economies have sought to diversify the crude supplies away from the Middle East but the region remains reliant on the Gulf oil and vulnerable to the disruptions along the major shipping routes.\n\nTourism is another channel through which the Middle East conflict is already being felt.\n\n\u201cThe conflict\u2019s impact on tourism in the region has been more evident than on the remittance flows so far,\u201d the ADB said, pointing to the airspace closures, flight rerouting, longer flying times, higher airfares and elevated jet fuel costs.\n\nThe impact has been particularly pronounced in South Asia because of its dependence on the Gulf carriers and transit hubs."
  },
