    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe conflict\u2019s impact on tourism in the region has been more evident than on the remittance flows so far,\u201d the ADB said, pointing to the airspace closures, flight rerouting, longer flying times, higher airfares and elevated jet fuel costs.\n\nThe impact has been particularly pronounced in South Asia because of its dependence on the Gulf carriers and transit hubs.\n\nThe remittances, however, have so far provided an important counterweight. The inflows increased 21.4 percent year-on-year during January-July, remaining the main buffer against the deterioration in the external account.\n\nThe ADB nevertheless warned that the risks to the remittance flows could increase if the Middle Eastern employment opportunities weaken or worker repatriation rises. Regionally, the remittance flows from the Middle East have remained resilient so far, despite the conflict.\n\nThe Middle East shock is also colliding with another emerging threat to Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery: a very strong El Ni\u00f1o.\n\nThe ADB said El Ni\u00f1o is already materialising, damaging crops and threatening the hydropower supply in Sri Lanka. Regionally, the phenomenon is expected to strengthen through late 2026 and persist into 2027, potentially adding further pressure on the food and energy prices.\n\nDespite these risks, the ADB retained Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP growth forecast at 4 percent for both 2026 and 2027, while projecting inflation at 6 percent this year and 5.2 percent next year.\n\nThe combination of geopolitical and weather-related shocks, however, leaves Sri Lanka navigating its recovery with less room for external slippage, particularly as the current account weakens and the country seeks to rebuild the reserves.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s target of attracting three million tourists in 2026 appears increasingly out of reach, according to the Asian Development Bank (ADB), due to the weaker arrivals and a sharper drop in earnings from the sector."
  },
