    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s target of attracting three million tourists in 2026 appears increasingly out of reach, according to the Asian Development Bank (ADB), due to the weaker arrivals and a sharper drop in earnings from the sector.\n\nThe tourist arrivals fell 1.8 percent year-on-year (YoY) during the first seven months of 2026, while the tourism earnings declined by a much steeper 11.5 percent, reflecting lower spending per visitor.\n\nThe divergence between the arrivals and earnings adds another concern for the industry, suggesting Sri Lanka is not only struggling to build visitor volumes fast enough to reach its annual target but is also generating less foreign exchange from tourists.\n\nThe weakness comes at a time when tourism is expected to play an important role in strengthening Sri Lanka\u2019s external buffers, following the economic crisis.\n\nThe ADB said the country\u2019s current account slipped into a US $ 387 million deficit during the first seven months of the year, against a US $ 1.7 billion surplus a year earlier, making the foreign exchange earnings from tourism and other services increasingly important.\n\nThe worker remittances have fared considerably better, rising 21.4 percent YoY during January-July, providing what the ADB described as the main buffer for the external account.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tourism industry is also navigating a more difficult external environment, particularly due to the continued instability in the Middle East.\n\n\u201cThe conflict\u2019s impact on tourism in the region has been more evident than on the remittance flows so far,\u201d the ADB said.\n\nThe airspace closures and flight rerouting have increased the travel times and airfares, while the higher jet fuel costs and weaker outbound travel from the Gulf economies have added to the pressure on tourism.\n\nThe ADB noted that the decline in tourist arrivals in April was the steepest in South Asia, which is particularly dependent on the Gulf carriers and transit hubs."
