    "md5": "bc0ac90c3ccc317f9ed46c2f758ab7d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "Do not politicise the Anojan issue\n\nNow, people attempt to\u00a0 gain political mileage out of this poor man\u2019s misery.\n\nSri Lanka has launched a diplomatic cum legal battle to save the life of one of its migrant workers who has been sentenced to death in Saudi Arabia over blasphemy against Islam.\n\nSivarasa Anojan, a 26-year-old Sri Lankan migrant worker from Kalmunai, faces a death sentence by a Saudi appellate court after prosecutors successfully appealed to have his initial cybercrime sentence upgraded under strict Sharia law.\n\nAnojan was working as a quantity surveyor in Saudi Arabia when he was arrested on July 2 over a Facebook comment deemed derogatory toward Islam and the Prophet Muhammad. On July 15, the Al-Ahsa Criminal Court initially convicted him under Saudi Arabia\u2019s Anti-Cyber Crime Law, sentencing him to five years in prison and a fine of 3 million Saudi riyals, the maximum punishment given under that law.\n\nWhile an appeal was lodged to reduce the penalty, the Saudi prosecution filed a parallel appeal arguing the offense warranted prosecution under strict Sharia law for blasphemy. An appellate court subsequently overturned the initial custodial sentence and reportedly handed down a death penalty.\n\nAccording to available reports, Anojan is the seventh Sri Lankan sentenced to death by Saudi Arabian courts. In the first case, four Sri Lankan youth, Ranjith de Silva, Victor Corea, Sanath Pushpakumara and Sermila Sangeeth Kumara were beheaded in 2007 for highway robbery of a Bangladeshi man in 2005.\n\nIn a second incident,\u00a0 \u00a0Saudi Arabia executed a Sri Lankan domestic worker, Rizana Nafeek for allegedly killing a baby in her care in 2005. Rizana was convicted in 2007 of murdering four-month-old baby Naif al-Quthaibi and executed in January, 2013, despite an eight year-long effort by international human rights organisations and the Sri Lankan government which made several pleas to the Saudi king for clemency."
  },
