    "md5": "58e20ae23eb4280916d725c4ce6715ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Why Are Our Politicians So Corrupt?\n\nOne can argue that it\u2019s the accelerated Mahaveli Programme which opened the floodgates of mega political corruption in Sri Lanka\n\nWe have always known this truth: Corruption was not just rampant; it was endemic and looked ineradicable. The sarcastic saying aimed at Lankans \u2013 in Sri Lanka, it\u2019s not what you know but whom you know that matters \u2013 was directly related to this corruption.\n\nBut getting rid of this destructive corruption element was not seen as a top political priority. Some people talked of Singapore, how Lee Kuan Yew eliminated it using draconian methods. Oh, but that\u2019s Singapore, they said. This is Sri Lanka, and the merry making went on and on.\n\nThere was, of course, a bribery commission. It\u2019s nothing new.\u00a0 The Bribery Act No. 11 of 1954 was introduced just six years after independence to establish comprehensive written laws against bribery. The original Bribery Commission was founded in 1958 to handle bribery and corruption investigations. Even in the \u2018good old days,\u2019 when there was no established tradition of bribing politicians, the need for an anti-corruption body was felt.\n\nPrime Minister S.W.R.D. Bandaranaike appointed the Parliamentary Bribery Commission led by Justice Walter Thalgodapitiya in 1959.The commission investigated allegations of bribery against members of parliament and government ministers. The final report was presented to Parliament on December 16, 1960, and\u00a0 found\u00a0 six\u00a0 politicians guilty of receiving bribes or corrupt practices: But\u00a0 \u00a0the government\u00a0 did not immediately punish those named in the 1960 report.\n\nIn 1965, Parliament passed the Imposition of Civic Disabilities (Special Provisions) Act (No. 14 of 1965).This act stripped the\u00a0 accused politicians of their civic rights and voting rights for a period of seven years."
  },
