    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "In 1965, Parliament passed the Imposition of Civic Disabilities (Special Provisions) Act (No. 14 of 1965).This act stripped the\u00a0 accused politicians of their civic rights and voting rights for a period of seven years.\n\nThat did not eradicate corruption. On the contrary, it only went to convince the \u2018untouchables\u2019 sitting in parliament or the plush chairs of top level bureaucracy that they could do as they please. One could also argue that high level corruption was less prior to 1977 simply because there was less money, and not due to the inherent decency or quality of those who held high office. But people of Dr. N. M. Perera\u2019s quality ended their careers with reputations untarnished by allegations of corruption. There were ministers who travelled by bus to parliament. But the Mahaweli development programme changed all that. It was initiated in 1969 under Prime Minister Dudley Senanayake\u2019s United UNP government and the first phase (the Polgolla Dam) was formally launched and commissioned by PM Dudley in February 1970, just before the 1970 general elections. This was meant to be a slow, 30 years programme. The subsequent United Front government (1970\u20131977) continued the initial construction phase (completing the Polgolla and Bowatenna headworks by 1976), but\u00a0 \u00a0J. R. Jayewardene after becoming president in 1977\u00a0 accelerated the master plan in late 1977 to be completed\u00a0 in just 6 years.\n\nThe total estimated capital cost for the initial 30-year programme, as formulated by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) and the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) in 1969, was Rs. 5,583 million (at 1968 prices). The initial estimated budget for the Accelerated Mahaweli Programme (AMP) in 1977 was a staggering Rs. 20,000 million (Rs. 20 billion) or approximately U.S. $610 million."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
