    "md5": "b6613f5b2376fc2bbbbd36e18ad5ef8b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "The total estimated capital cost for the initial 30-year programme, as formulated by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) and the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) in 1969, was Rs. 5,583 million (at 1968 prices). The initial estimated budget for the Accelerated Mahaweli Programme (AMP) in 1977 was a staggering Rs. 20,000 million (Rs. 20 billion) or approximately U.S. $610 million.\n\nThe anti-corruption drive is key to the NPP\u2019s political survival. That is not to say it isn\u2019t\u00a0 political theatre. In a boiling socio-economic situation where one would expect mass protests to erupt, people are holding their peace because this judicial process answers a long-cherished need to see the corrupt getting their due \u2013 facing humiliating arrests and convictions. Whatever the opposition claims, these are not victims of trumped up charges and kangaroo courts. But it also allows the government to provide a type of therapy to an otherwise extremely frustrated population. It\u2019s provides catharsis.\n\nWhat remains to be seen is whether this anti-corruption process will continue and become an established tradition without being thrown overboard in the post-NPP era. Or are we going to be treated to a new political soap opera where, whoever from the opposition gains power will render impotent the prosecuting bodies, offer pardons to jailed allies and start prosecuting the key figures behind the current judicial process?\n\nThis is going to be the real test of Sri Lankan political culture.\u00a0 The word \u2018culture\u2019 here is significant. One can argue that it\u2019s the availability of previously unavailable wealth (or cash)\u00a0 following economic liberalisation which unleashed the rush to become millionnaires (billionaires nowadays). But this phenomenon isn\u2019t uniquely Sri Lankan. It can be observed in all countries which underwent similar socio-economic metamorphosis."
  },
