    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "This is going to be the real test of Sri Lankan political culture.\u00a0 The word \u2018culture\u2019 here is significant. One can argue that it\u2019s the availability of previously unavailable wealth (or cash)\u00a0 following economic liberalisation which unleashed the rush to become millionnaires (billionaires nowadays). But this phenomenon isn\u2019t uniquely Sri Lankan. It can be observed in all countries which underwent similar socio-economic metamorphosis.\n\nMahinda Rajapakse was another propagandist for the importance of ancient royal traditions. During his presidency,\u00a0 state-sponsored nationalistic narratives frequently invoked symbolic and genealogical connections to ancient monarchs to solidify his political legitimacy. After\u00a0 defeating the LTTE in 2009, inscriptions on war monuments (such as the Jaffna war memorial) glorified Mahinda Rajapaksa as the \u201cLord of the Three Sinhala Countries\u201d (Tri-Sinhala), a title historically reserved for ancient Sri Lankan monarchs who successfully unified the island.\n\nSuch people always believe that they are above all institutions. Ancient kings ruled with absolute authority. When the king was good, the rule was good, too. When the king was bad, things went bad. It\u2019s like what Louis XIV of France said \u2013 \u2018L\u2019etat, c\u2019est moi\u2019 (The state, that\u2019s me). We had benign kings and wise kings. Other were stupid, power-grabbing and corrupt. They wasted the royal treasury. Modern, democratic Sri Lanka, too, offers examples of this \u2018the state, that\u2019s me\u2019 mindset.\n\nDespite the current frenzy to net the corrupt, we are not out of troubled waters yet."
  },
  {
