    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Former Singapore Foreign Minister George Yeo, who was in Sri Lanka, shared his views on current global developments and the way forward for small and medium powers such as Sri Lanka in the whirlpool of events. He was here at the invitation of the leading think tank \u2018Geopolitical Cartographer\u2019 founded by former President Ranil Wickremesinghe. It is a body dedicated to studying geopolitics and the growing role of the Indian Ocean in global political, security and economic spheres.\u00a0 From September 1988 to May 2011, he served 23 years in the Singapore Government and was Minister for Information and the Arts, Minister for Health, Minister for Trade & Industry and Minister for Foreign Affairs.\u00a0 Mr Yeo studied Engineering at Cambridge University on a President\u2019s Scholarship, graduating with a Double First in 1976, and became a Signals Officer in the Singapore Armed Forces. After graduating from the Singapore Command and Staff College in 1979, he was posted to the Republic of Singapore Air Force. He graduated with an MBA (Baker Scholar) from the Harvard Business School in 1985. He was appointed Chief-of-Staff of the Air Staff (1985-1986) and Director of Joint Operations and Planning in the Defence Ministry (1985-1988), attaining the rank of Brigadier-General.\u00a0 Mr Yeo was a member of the Pontifical Commission for Reference on the Economic-Administrative Structure of the Holy See in 2013-2014 and the Vatican Council for the Economy from February 2014 to July 2020. He said that Sri Lanka needs unity in domestic politics for the better advancement of its foreign policy. According to him, Sri Lanka should have a dynamic neutrality in its foreign policy. Excerpts\n\nWell, we are moving towards a multipolar configuration, where there are different centres of power, and the relationships are dynamic.\n\nI think the Europeans will begin to act more firmly in their own interests, and find some ways to move closer to China, and eventually reach an equilibrium in Russia."
