    "md5": "4000385e0cd376a2b22fbe7b5a2dae39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1731,
    "chunk_text": "Well, we are moving towards a multipolar configuration, where there are different centres of power, and the relationships are dynamic.\n\nI think the Europeans will begin to act more firmly in their own interests, and find some ways to move closer to China, and eventually reach an equilibrium in Russia.\n\nWell, we should be very clear about the way the world is going, and try to make sense of it as best as we can. There are a lot of uncertainties, of course.\n\nWe have very little say in what they do. We have very little influence on the outcomes. But what we can do is to decide for ourselves, in our position, that we have agency. Do I want to be closer to one or to the other? Do I strike a balance? That is within our own control. What do we do?\n\nQSo, for a country like Sri Lanka, does this foreign policy really amount to neutrality? Or does neutrality, in the practical sense, mean that Sri Lanka should develop strategic partnerships with different powers, such as India and China, based on different areas of mutual interest, while maintaining its strategic autonomy?\n\nI think for Sri Lanka, it\u2019s better to have a dynamic neutrality. So India, of course, will always be very important to you.\n\nBut you must have diversification, which means that you need relationships with the U.S., China and others so that you have more flexibility to deal with any one major power.\n\nIf you are locked into a static position, I think that\u2019s very uncomfortable, because the rest of the world is changing.\n\nQ Now, actually, we see India and China warming up to each other, with President Xi Jinping coming to New Delhi to attend the BRICS summits and all. So for a country like Sri Lanka, what are the takeaways from this warming up?"
  },
