    "md5": "f9c70a96ff4cf5ba217e4ea7cab39569",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "Q Now, actually, we see India and China warming up to each other, with President Xi Jinping coming to New Delhi to attend the BRICS summits and all. So for a country like Sri Lanka, what are the takeaways from this warming up?\n\nIn order not to choose, you must be cognisant of India\u2019s strategic interests- not to take direction from India, but to anticipate what the concerns are, so that you avoid hard rocks along your passage.\n\nQYou once mentioned that the strength of ASEAN (Association of Southeast Asian Nations) is its neutrality. What do you mean by that?\n\nWe don\u2019t have an alliance with one against the other. But of course, if any major power pushes too hard, it will find ASEAN shifting its weight to counterbalance the excessive pressure. Very dynamic.\n\nAnd our preference is always to retain neutrality, to retain balance.\n\nQ ASEAN is a missed opportunity for Sri Lanka because there were opportunities in the past for Sri Lanka also to be part of ASEAN as a member. What do you think?\n\nWell, I won\u2019t make too much of that because the world was very different at that time. I think looking ahead, Sri Lanka should move closer to ASEAN.\n\nWell, there are economic opportunities. Yes. It\u2019s good to huddle with like-minded people, especially when in the room there are big guys.\n\nQAll the Theravada Buddhist countries are part of ASEAN. Is that one reason?\n\nThere\u2019s a Theravada connection. There\u2019s a Muslim connection. There\u2019s a Christian connection. And Sri Lanka should not be overly Buddhist in its presentation, but should be multi-religious, multi-ethnic. I think Sri Lanka is.\n\nQ How do you see the importance of the Indian Ocean growing from security, political and economic perspectives?\n\nWell, the growth of India economically, it must be good for Sri Lanka. I mean, if India were a basket case, how would it help Sri Lanka? If India is a dynamic economy, there will be limitless opportunities for Sri Lanka. The question is the security dimension."
  },
