    "chunk_text": "Well, the growth of India economically, it must be good for Sri Lanka. I mean, if India were a basket case, how would it help Sri Lanka? If India is a dynamic economy, there will be limitless opportunities for Sri Lanka. The question is the security dimension.\n\nQ Now, looking at Sri Lanka\u2019s current positioning in the region and the wider international system, how would you assess the country\u2019s strategic position today? Sri Lanka, like Singapore and Dubai, has historically had considerable advantages arising from its location. Yet, despite these advantages, it has not fully realised its potential for economic transformation and development. In your view, what are the key factors that prevented Sri Lanka from translating its strategic location into sustained development, and does the current geopolitical environment offer Sri Lanka another opportunity to do so?\n\nI think for Sri Lanka, the most important concerns are domestic, not external. I think: get your domestic politics right, get your economy moving again, put your finances in good order. Without that internal unity, you can\u2019t navigate external turbulence.\n\nPeople may say that Sri Lanka should have a more active foreign policy. But if internally you\u2019re divided, a more active foreign policy doesn\u2019t help you.\n\nYou must get your act together domestically first. Now, there must be enough consensus in your elite structure that whatever political party you\u2019re from, whatever religion you espouse, you see a common destiny for all Sri Lankans. There are therefore limits to democratic competition. In the end, we\u2019re in the same boat. Don\u2019t rock that boat. We\u2019ve got to navigate this boat down dangerous rapids.\n\nQHow do you define this concept of non-alignment in the current context?\n\nWe have no natural inclination to align one against the other. If you align yourself against India, you\u2019re asking trouble for yourself. If you align yourself with India rigidly, you\u2019re self-optimising for yourself."
