    "md5": "803916b68d0b1d3dd2eae8008958a67b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "Cabinet approves continuation of education reforms for Grades 2 and 6 in 2027\n\nColombo, Sep. 29 (Daily Mirror) - The Cabinet has approved the continuation of Sri Lanka\u2019s education reform programme, with reforms for Grades 2 and 6 scheduled to be implemented in 2027, while the reforms introduced for Grade 1 in 2026 will continue.\n\nThe decision was taken based on the concept paper prepared by the National Education Commission, the National Curriculum Framework approved by the National Steering Committee for New Education Reforms, and the School-Based Evaluation and Assessment Guidelines.\n\nThe National Curriculum Framework and its implementation methodology, initiated by the National Institute of Education (NIE) in 2019, had been approved by Cabinet in March 2023.\n\nHowever, following the change of government in 2024, it was decided to introduce the new framework initially to Grades 1 and 6 in 2026, with appropriate adjustments to align it with the new Government's education policies. The introduction of vocational subjects from Grade 10 onwards was also proposed.\n\nA concept paper titled \u201cTransformational Change in General Education in Sri Lanka\u201d was subsequently submitted to the Parliamentary Sub-Committee on Education Reforms in October 2025.\n\nFollowing a Cabinet decision in January 2026, the implementation of reforms for Grade 6 was postponed to 2027, while reforms for Grade 1 proceeded in 2026.\n\nAs part of the latest preparations, the National Education Commission reviewed and redrafted the concept paper, incorporating policy recommendations.\n\nThe revised document is to be reviewed by the National Steering Committee for New Education Reforms and its sub-committees covering five key areas: curriculum reform, human resource development, education administration and infrastructure development, evaluation and assessment, and public awareness and promotion."
  },
