    "md5": "e15dfa48ee3af56e08fab2da5c3f95e0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1329,
    "chunk_text": "Child content for profit: Ministry issues warning\n\nColombo, Sep. 29 (Daily Mirror) - The Ministry of Women and Child Affairs has issued an urgent warning to parents, guardians and caregivers over the increasing number of photographs and videos of children being shared on social media platforms.\n\nThe Ministry said the expansion of monetisation features on platforms such as Facebook has contributed to a growing trend of individuals and organisations using children\u2019s photographs and videos for commercial purposes. Such content is sometimes presented as a way of showcasing children\u2019s talents or everyday activities, with or without the knowledge or consent of their parents or guardians.\n\nAuthorities warned that regularly posting videos showing children\u2019s daily routines, homes, schools and activities could expose them to serious risks to their privacy and personal safety.\n\nThe Ministry also raised concerns about the misuse of children\u2019s photographs and videos through artificial intelligence (AI) technology. Images shared online can potentially be manipulated or repurposed to create explicit or otherwise harmful content.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, the Ministry urged parents, guardians and all individuals responsible for children to exercise extreme caution when sharing content involving minors on social media."
  },
