    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9bb5e62f319414b5e7e4f624c8beac32",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "Three officers from the Ministry of Digital Economy are also to be assigned to coordinate the further development of the national system operated under the Ministry of Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government.\n\nThe officers will also identify shortcomings in existing systems and oversee necessary amendments and improvements.\n\nDigital mechanisms for obtaining permits and licences and systems introduced to provide prompt solutions to public grievances were also discussed.\n\nOfficials also examined ways of further developing digital payment systems such as GovPay under the programme.\n\nThe digitalisation programme at Divisional Secretariat level was also reviewed, with more than 300 services currently provided by Divisional Secretariats having been identified for the process.\n\nProgress in connecting services from the Grama Niladhari level through Divisional Secretariats to District Secretariats was also discussed.\n\nParticular attention was given to the digitalisation process being implemented in the Polonnaruwa District, including experiences gained from activities such as paddy purchases and the provision of disaster relief.\n\nPresident Dissanayake instructed officials to initially establish a fully equipped model for the Divisional Secretariat Digitalisation Programme in one selected district, incorporating all necessary facilities.\n\nThe meeting also reviewed the **GovHR** digital system, which is being developed to manage the entire administrative process relating to public officers, from their appointment until retirement.\n\nDeputy Minister of Digital Economy Eranga Weeraratne, Secretary to the President Dr Nandika Sanath Kumanayake, Senior Adviser to the President on Digital Economy Hans Wijayasuriya and senior officials from the relevant ministries and agencies attended the meeting."
  },
