    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6bd28d2ac1a071703d81b2c426814cd2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "Sajith accuses Govt., CBSL of using outdated data to mask poverty\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa yesterday accused the Government, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) and the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS) of painting a misleadingly positive picture of the country by using survey data up to a decade old to depict poverty.\n\nIn a special statement, Premadasa said the Sri Lanka Socio-Economic Data 2026 report, released on 28 September, used recent data for macroeconomic indicators, trade, prices, government finance, debt, labour and tourism, but relied only on 2016 to 2019 data for household income and expenditure, poverty, housing, energy use and food consumption.\n\nHe said the selective use of old data on poverty, inequality and living standards was a deliberate attempt to mislead the country\u2019s 22 million citizens. He called on the President, the Government, the CBSL and the DCS to account for the discrepancy, warned them against dismissing it as a printing error, and said he was ready for an open debate on the matter.\n\nPremadasa said no proper Household Income and Expenditure Survey (HIES) had been conducted and the poverty line had not been accurately identified since the country went bankrupt. He questioned whether the Budget, scheduled for 12 November, 2026, would be based on flawed data, and rejected as false the Government\u2019s position that a person could live on Rs. 17,315 a month.\n\nCiting a 2026 World Bank report, he said Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty rate stood at 65.4% under the World Bank\u2019s $ 8.30-a-day poverty line, which he put at Rs. 2,724, with 33% of households facing acute food insecurity. He said the rate was projected to ease only marginally to 63.4% by 2028.\n\nPremadasa demanded that a revised report based on accurate data be presented before the Budget."
  },
