    "chunk_text": "Citing a 2026 World Bank report, he said Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty rate stood at 65.4% under the World Bank\u2019s $ 8.30-a-day poverty line, which he put at Rs. 2,724, with 33% of households facing acute food insecurity. He said the rate was projected to ease only marginally to 63.4% by 2028.\n\nPremadasa demanded that a revised report based on accurate data be presented before the Budget.\n\nThe sections Premadasa criticised, covering household income, expenditure, housing, energy use, poverty and food consumption, are all sourced to the DCS HIES. The latest survey rounds shown are 2016 and 2019. The DCS conducts the HIES once every three years, and the survey provides the income and expenditure data used to measure living conditions. Unless a new round is published, these tables cannot be updated. The CBSL\u2019s 2023 and 2025 editions carried the same HIES tables, with the poverty headcount ratio series ending at 2019 in 2023, and the 2025 edition citing HIES 2016 and 2019 as sources.\n\nThe DCS carried out a fresh HIES in 2025, covering 25,000 households. It was the first since 2019, as the survey was skipped during the economic crisis. Its results, the first post-crisis measure of household income and poverty, have yet to be released."
  },
