    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Karu Jayasuriya at 86: Lesson of life in service\n\nThirty years is long enough for opinions about someone to solidify. It is more than enough time to identify the most important and character-defining qualities of an individual, provided, of course, the association has been close, frequent and consistent. As he celebrated his 86th birthday on 29 September, having known him at close quarters, I can truly say that Karu Jayasuriya stands out as a man of integrity, courage, unwavering loyalty and, most importantly, someone who never placed personal benefit of any kind above the public good.\n\nOne could add that, unlike many who engage in politics or even operate in corporate circles, Karu never let disagreements slip into enmity. He is civilised in the true sense of the word. He is a citizen who took citizenship and all it entails by way of responsible public conduct extremely seriously, and he is a human being who, in word and deed, adheres to and exemplifies the teachings of the Buddha pertaining to general conduct.\n\nKaru is a proud Anandian and an even prouder Sri Lankan. His patriotism was never noisy or theatrical -- he demonstrated it through service.\n\nEssays can be written about Karu with regard to all the many spheres he inhabited at one time or another, but that\u2019s a task, and an unenviable one, for a biographer. For me, he is a teacher whose lessons are written in deed rather than lecture. He is a friend who will go beyond the call of duty to stand by you in a crisis. He is the kind of politician that we rarely see in Parliament. He is the citizen we should aspire to be.\n\nWhen does Karu Jayasuriya ever stop? I have asked myself many times. The answer has always been: \u201cNever!\u201d Just a few days ago, when I called him on what would have been his late daughter Indira\u2019s 50th birthday, he was preparing to travel to Jaffna by train the following day."
  },
