    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6d72d388ffc3f2d6e5501bfd6b445e03",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "Channa Wijewardena named Diabetasol\u2019s Brand Ambassador\n\nHead of Nutrition at Kalbe, Kishan Canagasabey presenting a souvenir to Channa Wijewardena\n\nDiabetasol has announced Channa Wijewardena, Founder and Creative Director of Channa-Upuli Dance Troupe, as the face of its new campaign, \u201cKeep Life in Rhythm.\u201d\n\nThe idea behind the campaign is simple: everyone has a rhythm to their life \u2014 the passions, people and routines that make them who they are. A diagnosis of diabetes can feel like it puts all of that on hold. \u201cKeep Life in Rhythm\u201d sets out to challenge that, moving away from the idea that diabetes has to define or limit a person, and towards a more grounded message: that with the right balance, discipline and nutrition, life can go on much as it always has.\n\n\u201cHaving diabetes for me is about finding the right balance, being disciplined about my choices and taking care of myself,\u201d Wijewardena said. \u201cI still want to enjoy my work, my passions and my everyday life. Diabetes is something I manage, but it doesn\u2019t define who I am.\u201d\n\nNutrition sits quietly at the centre of that balance. Head of Nutrition at Kalbe, Kishan Canagasabey notes that the choice of nutrition matters as much as the decision to seek it out. \u201cWhen someone with diabetes considers a nutritional drink, the real question isn\u2019t just whether it\u2019s nutritious \u2014 it\u2019s whether it\u2019s the right choice for their condition,\u201d he said. \u201cResearch has moved well past the idea that one formula suits everyone. For someone managing diabetes, that distinction matters.\u201d\n\nDiabetasol has been formulated with that distinction in mind \u2014 a low glycemic index that allows its carbohydrates to be absorbed more slowly, along with high protein content, fibre, no added sucrose and essential vitamins and minerals, all designed around the everyday needs of people living with diabetes."
