    "description": "CSE, GGGI step up push for sustainable bonds",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/CSE-GGGI-step-up-push-for-sustainable-bonds/273-351418",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790735458.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-30-dailymirrorlk-57a763f1-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "89c7305e9010196a57c49197b20029fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "CSE, GGGI step up push for sustainable bonds\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s sustainable debt market is set for a fresh push, with the Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE) and Global Green Growth Institute (GGGI) opening a new technical assistance programme aimed at bringing more Green, Social, Sustainable and Sustainability-Linked (GSS+) bonds to the market.\n\nGSS+ bonds gain greater traction as an alternative source of long-term capital, with 12 issuances raising more than Rs. 92 billion since the CSE introduced the instruments in 2024.\n\nSignificantly, GSS+ instruments accounted for 40 percent of debt capital raised in 2025, underscoring their growing role in the domestic capital market.\n\nUnder the latest initiative, prospective issuers can apply for technical assistance from GGGI to support the development and eventual listing of GSS+ bonds. Applications will remain open until 26 October 2026.\n\nSelected issuers will receive needs-based support covering the development of GSS+ bond frameworks, coordination of Second Party Opinions (SPOs), post-issuance allocation and impact reporting during the first year, as well as capacity building.\n\nAll technical assistance under the programme, including the cost of obtaining a Second Party Opinion, will be provided free of charge.\n\nThe initiative could help lower some of the technical and preparatory barriers faced by companies and institutions considering thematic debt issuance, particularly the specialised requirements associated with establishing credible frameworks and demonstrating how proceeds are allocated and their impact measured.\n\nGGGI has already played a role in developing Sri Lanka\u2019s sustainable finance market. Through the Global Trust Fund on Sustainable Finance Instruments, funded by the Government of Luxembourg, it previously supported three thematic bond issuances in Sri Lanka, including the country\u2019s first blue bond."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
