    "description": "Control failures emerge from NDB\u2019s  Rs. 13.64bn fraud probe",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Control-failures-emerge-from-NDBs-Rs-13-64bn-fraud-probe/273-351419",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790735618.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-30-dailymirrorlk-92d7e5cc-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "65ce19d08636e872e6a5e9e4f10dd071",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "Control failures emerge from NDB\u2019s  Rs. 13.64bn fraud probe\n\nFinal assessment by Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu India LLP puts the fraud at Rs. 13.6397bn\n\nNDB says Deloitte had made recommendations relating to \u201ccontrol failures, technology lapses and governance improvements\u201d relevant to the incident\n\nThe Bank discloses it has enlisted an expert foreign agency specialising in tracing stolen funds and digital assets\n\nNational Development Bank PLC (NDB) yesterday disclosed it has received the final Deloitte forensic report into its multi-billion-rupee fraud, confirming losses of Rs. 13.64 billion and identifying control failures, technology lapses and governance weaknesses linked to the incident.\n\nThe final assessment by Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu India LLP puts the fraud at Rs. 13.6397 billion, Rs. 60 million higher than the Rs. 13.5797 billion estimated in its interim report in June. NDB had initially estimated the fraud at Rs. 13.2 billion in April. The additional Rs. 60 million will be recognised in accordance with applicable accounting standards.\n\nThe final report also placed the governance and control environment surrounding the fraud into sharper focus. NDB stated that Deloitte had made recommendations relating to \u201ccontrol failures, technology lapses and governance improvements\u201d relevant to the incident.\n\nIn a disclosure to the Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE), the bank said the recommendations were being acted upon and that the Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) would continue to be updated on the measures taken.\n\nMeanwhile, in a significant escalation of efforts to recover the money, NDB shared it has enlisted an expert foreign agency specialising in tracing stolen funds and digital assets.\n\n\u201cThe Bank is also pursuing all avenues available to it to recover the misappropriated funds including availing the services of an expert foreign agency specializing in tracing stolen money and digital assets,\u201d NDB said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-30-dailymirrorlk-92d7e5cc",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-92d7e5cc",
    "date_str": "2026-09-30",
    "description": "Control failures emerge from NDB\u2019s  Rs. 13.64bn fraud probe",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Control-failures-emerge-from-NDBs-Rs-13-64bn-fraud-probe/273-351419",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790735618.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-30-dailymirrorlk-92d7e5cc-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dcf6cb08bd6508fb0e720dab315ac0d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Bank is also pursuing all avenues available to it to recover the misappropriated funds including availing the services of an expert foreign agency specializing in tracing stolen money and digital assets,\u201d NDB said.\n\nNDB has also initiated and is pursuing internal action against individuals identified as responsible for collusion, lack of supervision and control lapses that facilitated the fraudulent activities.\n\nDeloitte\u2019s breakdown highlighted that the bulk of the fraud was concentrated in 2025. Of the final Rs. 13.64 billion, Rs. 9.617 billion, or about 70.5 percent, relatesld to the financial year ended 31 December 2025.\n\nA further Rs. 1.463 billion related to the period before 1 January 2025, while Rs. 2.560 billion was attributed to the quarter ended 31 March 2026.\n\nDeloitte was commissioned by NDB in consultation with and in line with recommendations from the CBSL\u2019s Director of Bank Supervision following detection of the fraud.\n\nUnder the agreed terms of reference, Deloitte was required to submit its report directly to the CBSL, with NDB kept on copy, a structure intended to ensure the independence of the forensic process.\n\nNDB stressed that no customer had suffered losses as a result of the fraud, with customer balances remaining intact. It also reiterated the bank remained well capitalised with sound levels of liquidity.\n\nWhile noting the final forensic report would not mark the end of the process, NDB said it viewed the findings as a step towards rebuilding credibility and strengthening its overall governance and control framework.\n\nThe bank said the measures arising from the investigation are expected to strengthen its ability to deal with existing and emerging threats facing financial institutions, including those stemming from technological developments."
  },
