    "chunk_text": "Small vehicles dominate Sri Lanka\u2019s auto demand as consumers turn selective\n\nTotal vehicle registrations fell 13.9% month-on-month to 45,811 in August from 53,221 in July\n\nElectric vehicle segment lost momentum in August, with total EV registrations falling 29% month-on-month to 5,084 from 7,170\n\nSri Lankan vehicle buyers are overwhelmingly gravitating towards smaller cars and compact crossovers, latest registration data show.\n\nData compiled by JB Securities showed that small cars accounted for 96.2 percent of brand-new car registrations in August, with 607 units falling below 1,000cc and 100kW.\n\nThe preference was even more pronounced in the pre-owned market, where 1,924 units, or 97.3 percent, were small cars. Suzuki led pre-owned car registrations with 681 units, including 523 Wagon Rs, followed by Toyota with 459, Nissan with 354 and Daihatsu with 350.\n\nA similar trend was evident in the SUV market, where small crossovers of up to 1,500cc accounted for 3,099 units, or 96 percent, of August registrations. Toyota led the segment with 1,186 registrations, driven largely by the Raize and Urban Cruiser, while Honda recorded 645, including 620 Vezels. Jetour followed with 306 and Suzuki with 284.\n\nTotal registrations fell 13.9 percent month-on-month to 45,811 in August from 53,221 in July, extending a decline from 62,776 in May and 58,151 in June.\n\nMotor-car registrations fell to 2,609 from 3,654 in July, while SUVs and crossovers declined to 3,226 from 4,439. Despite the monthly slowdown, total registrations remained nearly 20 percent higher year-on-year, compared with 38,240 units in August 2025.\n\nFinancing continued to play a sizeable role in purchases. The financing share for motor cars increased to 47.4 percent from 42.8 percent in July, while that for SUVs rose to 45 percent from 41.9 percent. Small-car financing stood at 42.3 percent for brand-new vehicles and 49 percent for pre-owned cars."
