    "md5": "6a16b01943618305c2fd270b8a3cc743",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1273,
    "chunk_text": "Dhanapala shared the government was working on the policy and regulatory environment, but infrastructure alone would not deliver that ambition. Sri Lanka also needed people capable of developing its own applications, rather than merely operating technology created elsewhere.\n\nMeanwhile, the immediate constraint is computing capacity, according to SLT Group Chairman Dr. Mothilal De Silva.\n\nDe Silva said SLT-MOBITEL would upgrade its data centres to support AI, while efforts were under way to attract investment in GPU infrastructure. Given the scale of spending by major technology companies overseas, Sri Lanka\u2019s better opportunity lay in smaller, lower-cost models tailored to its languages and practical needs, he said.\n\n\u201cFor Sri Lanka, the lesson is clear. We do not need to build the next GPT. We need practical, affordable AI solutions designed for our languages, infrastructure and budgets,\u201d De Silva said.\n\nAs Sri Lanka AI Week 2026 enters its final day today (30 September), the public are invited to visit the AI Exhibition located at the Monarch Imperial, Colombo, experience these innovations first-hand and discover how the theme of \u2018AI Amplified\u2019 is translating into real-world technologies and solutions shaping Sri Lanka\u2019s AI-powered future."
