    "chunk_text": "The Overstretch Doctrine: The price of being everywhere\n\nXi\u2019s state visit to Washington (23\u201325 September) was his first to the US capital since 2015, and the third in-person meeting between Xi and Trump in eleven months\n\nSeven months on, the war expanded past its original combatants.\u00a0 Iran has responded through a network of allied armed groups\n\nThe Houthis have simultaneously opened a new front in Yemen\u2019s civil war, displacing more than 100,000 people\n\nOn energy and trade exposure, Hormuz and Red Sea disruptions affect Sri Lanka\u2019s import bill and shipping insurance costs\n\nA war against Iran that has visibly drawn down US military stockpiles, a negotiated security agreement over Greenland that expands access without annexation, and a reciprocal state visit by Xi Jinping conducted from a position that analysts describe as one of greater Chinese confidence than his last such visit a decade ago\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the takeaway is that its principal regional partner, India, is simultaneously positioning as a Global South convener and managing its own balance between China and Washington, a dynamic worth factoring into Colombo\u2019s own planning, given Sri Lanka\u2019s economic and geographic dependence on India\n\nSpeaking at the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly, India\u2019s External Affairs Minister Subrahmanyam Jaishankar warned that unresolved conflicts are undermining peace, obstructing progress, and penalising innocent nations, urging the UN to renew its purpose as an instrument of justice and lasting peace.\u00a0 Jaishankar further observed that the inability to maintain peace is not just undermining security, but also threatening progress and retarding development. In a globalised world, the effects of war are felt worldwide; countries far from the theatre of conflict are penalised for no fault of their own. Those most impacted often have the least say. This Assembly, he stressed, must face up to the true state of affairs.\u201d"
