    "chunk_size_bytes": 1672,
    "chunk_text": "Speaking at the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly, India\u2019s External Affairs Minister Subrahmanyam Jaishankar warned that unresolved conflicts are undermining peace, obstructing progress, and penalising innocent nations, urging the UN to renew its purpose as an instrument of justice and lasting peace.\u00a0 Jaishankar further observed that the inability to maintain peace is not just undermining security, but also threatening progress and retarding development. In a globalised world, the effects of war are felt worldwide; countries far from the theatre of conflict are penalised for no fault of their own. Those most impacted often have the least say. This Assembly, he stressed, must face up to the true state of affairs.\u201d\n\nFormer US Defence Secretary Leon Panetta has described the current moment as \u201cdangerous,\u201d stating his view that the United States may no longer have the capacity to deter a future conflict in the South China Sea while remaining committed in the Middle East; one former official\u2019s assessment, not a settled fact. The figures cited alongside that assessment are notable: within under five months, reporting indicates the US had drawn down the majority of its Patriot interceptor stock, roughly 80 percent of its THAAD inventory, most of its PrSM (Precision Strike Missile) and ATACMS (Army Tactical Missile System) munitions, and close to half its global Tomahawk supply. The estimated military cost of the war has grown past $43.6 billion, and disruption to Red Sea and Hormuz shipping is a measurable factor in global trade and insurance costs, with knockon effects for import-dependent economies such as Sri Lanka\u2019s."
