    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "Former US Defence Secretary Leon Panetta has described the current moment as \u201cdangerous,\u201d stating his view that the United States may no longer have the capacity to deter a future conflict in the South China Sea while remaining committed in the Middle East; one former official\u2019s assessment, not a settled fact. The figures cited alongside that assessment are notable: within under five months, reporting indicates the US had drawn down the majority of its Patriot interceptor stock, roughly 80 percent of its THAAD inventory, most of its PrSM (Precision Strike Missile) and ATACMS (Army Tactical Missile System) munitions, and close to half its global Tomahawk supply. The estimated military cost of the war has grown past $43.6 billion, and disruption to Red Sea and Hormuz shipping is a measurable factor in global trade and insurance costs, with knockon effects for import-dependent economies such as Sri Lanka\u2019s.\n\nOn 19 September, Saudi air Defences intercepted a Houthi ballistic missile aimed at Riyadh the first such attack on the capital since hostilities escalated while reportedly intercepting additional strikes aimed at Bisha, a city in southwestern Saudi Arabia, in Asir Province, close to the Yemeni border, Taif a major city in Mecca Province, in the western highlands near Mecca. Farasan, and Yanbu a major port city on the Red Sea coast in Al Madinah Province, further north.\u00a0 The missile\u2019s approach disrupted flights at King Khalid International Airport and produced a visible plume of smoke near a fuel storage area; Saudi authorities reported no casualties.\u00a0 The Houthis stated the strikes targeted \u201csensitive sites\u201d in Riyadh and Saudi oil infrastructure a claim that remains independently unverified. Saudi Arabia has responded with intensified airstrikes on Houthiheld territory, the Houthi advance has continued."
