    "md5": "9dc7160bf424874ad4118c325093ce8f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "The Greenland settlement offers a contrasting case. Trump\u2019s earlier push to acquire the island, including remarks not ruling out military force, prompted eighteen months of transatlantic concern, French troop deployments to the island, new European consular presence in Nuuk, and strain within NATO discussed at the July Ankara summit. That approach did not produce a territorial transfer.\n\nFor states negotiating basing, port, or investment-screening arrangements with great powers, including in South Asia and the Indian Ocean, the Greenland agreement is a useful reference point for what \u201csovereignty-preserving\u201d arrangements concede in practice, and what they do not.\n\nTen days before Washington, Xi Jinping attended the 18th BRICS Summit in New Delhi (12\u201313 September), under India\u2019s 2026 chairmanship, alongside Russian President Vladimir Putin, Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian, and\u00a0UN Secretary-General Ant\u00f3nio Guterres. The summit adopted the New Delhi Declaration, a 140-paragraph document covering global governance, ongoing conflicts, trade, energy, climate, and technology. The declaration did not name the United States explicitly, despite the Iran war and US tariffs affecting several BRICS members consistent with a sequencing in which Global South positions are stated collectively before bilateral engagement with Washington.\n\nThe summit also surfaced an unresolved point relevant to the Saudi analysis above.\u00a0 India, as chair, described Saudi Arabia as a full BRICS member from January 2024, while Saudi Arabia continued to describe its own participation in terms of an invited country, sending its foreign minister rather than the crown prince. This is consistent with the broader pattern of Saudi hedging already noted military exposure, the Mecca pact, and Washington dependency now extended into multilateral diplomacy: Riyadh has not fully aligned with a bloc often read as counterweighting US influence, while maintaining relations with Beijing and Moscow."
