    "chunk_text": "The summit also surfaced an unresolved point relevant to the Saudi analysis above.\u00a0 India, as chair, described Saudi Arabia as a full BRICS member from January 2024, while Saudi Arabia continued to describe its own participation in terms of an invited country, sending its foreign minister rather than the crown prince. This is consistent with the broader pattern of Saudi hedging already noted military exposure, the Mecca pact, and Washington dependency now extended into multilateral diplomacy: Riyadh has not fully aligned with a bloc often read as counterweighting US influence, while maintaining relations with Beijing and Moscow.\n\nXi\u2019s state visit to Washington (23\u201325 September) was his first to the US capital since 2015, and the third in-person meeting between Xi and Trump in eleven months, following Busan (October 2025) and Beijing (May 2026); two further meetings are scheduled before year\u2019s end, at APEC in Shenzhen and the G20 in Miami. Analysts note that China\u2019s position has changed structurally since Xi\u2019s last Washington visit under President Obama: it now presents itself as a more direct technological competitor, citing narrowing gaps in AI development and citing the leverage demonstrated when Beijing threatened rare-earth export controls during the last major trade dispute. One assessment describes the relationship as now \u201cmore evenly matched\u201d a change from earlier characterizations of unambiguous US economic primacy, though this remains a contested and disputed characterisation rather than a consensus view.\n\nConcrete deliverables from the visit were limited to an agreement, reached 20 September, to establish a bilateral AI dialogue; reporting suggests both sides deliberately kept the summit\u2019s scope narrow. This is consistent with a preference, on both sides, for managing the relationship rather than resolving the underlying trade, technology, and security disputes."
  },
