    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Gotabaya Rajapaksa: Claims of disappearances were made to damage Sri Lanka\u2019s name\n\nColombo, Sep. 30 (Daily Mirror) - Former President Gotabaya Rajapaksa yesterday told the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court that allegations of abductions and disappearances of individuals allegedly committed during his tenure as Defence Secretary were completely false and that they had been made entirely with the intention of gaining political advantage and damaging the reputation of the country.\n\nRajapaksa made these remarks while giving evidence via Zoom from the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court in connection with a case being heard before the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court over the disappearance of social activists Lalith Weeraraj and Kugan Muruganandhan on December 10, 2011.\n\nThe complaint was filed before the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court by relatives of the missing persons, with Gotabaya Rajapaksa named as a witness in the case.\n\nThe former President had previously informed court that he was unable to travel to the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court to give evidence due to threats to his life, and that he was prepared to give evidence before any court other than the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court.\n\nSubsequently, when the case was taken up before the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court on February 6 this year, counsels representing Gotabaya Rajapaksa requested court to permit him to give evidence online.\n\nAccordingly, the court granted permission for the request on June 2.\n\nOn that occasion, the court ordered Gotabaya Rajapaksa to appear before the Colombo Chief Magistrate\u2019s Court today to give evidence.\n\nThe evidence was recorded from the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court on behalf of the prosecution under the direction of Counsel Nuwan Bopage.\n\nWhile giving evidence, Gotabaya Rajapaksa, responding to several questions put to him, said that he could not remember the relevant incidents as a considerable period of time had passed."
