    "chunk_size_bytes": 1788,
    "chunk_text": "New Bill to reform governance structure of Sri Lanka Cricket\n\nColombo, Sep. 30 (Daily Mirror) - The Government is preparing to introduce a new Bill aimed at completely reforming the governance structure of Sri Lanka Cricket, the Ministerial Consultative Committee on Youth Affairs and Sports was informed.\n\nThe matter was discussed at a recent meeting of the Committee, chaired by Youth Affairs and Sports Minister Sunil Kumara Gamage. It was revealed that arrangements were underway to gazette the proposed legislation and present it to Parliament.\n\nThe proposed governance structure will include independent directors, while District Cricket Associations will no longer participate directly in decision-making with Sri Lanka Cricket. Instead, they will operate through Provincial Cricket Associations.\n\nThe Committee was also informed that Sri Lanka Cricket allocates around Rs. 900 million annually, along with equipment, for school cricket.\n\nPlans are also being formulated in consultation with the Ministry of Education, Higher Education and Vocational Education to formalise the recruitment of coaches and the player selection process.\n\nThe Committee reviewed progress on implementing recommendations made by the Auditor General concerning sports associations. Of the associations reviewed, 33 have completed the relevant recommendations, while reports are awaited from another 33 associations.\n\nMeanwhile, the Ministry has instructed that fees charged for the use of walking tracks and sports complexes for public health and exercise activities be suspended with immediate effect.\n\nThe Committee was told that sports facilities should be maintained as a government responsibility to promote public health and community welfare, rather than being operated as profit-making ventures."
