    "md5": "4051eda31f8969388e864ec1d3b23d87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "SLC targets transparency, professional management\n\nColombo, Sep. 30 (Daily Mirror) - The Sri Lanka Cricket (SLC) Transformation Committee is pursuing a series of reforms aimed at restoring public trust, strengthening financial accountability and establishing professional governance within the country's cricket administration, committee Chairman Eran Wickramaratne said.\n\nWickramaratne said that cricket belongs to the public and not to any individual or administrative board, adding that all stakeholders must operate within the law and established regulations.\n\nA key element of the proposed new constitutional framework is to place SLC administration under the principle of public trusteeship. Under the proposed structure, elected board members would be responsible for policy-level decisions, while day-to-day operations would be handled by an independent and professional management team.\n\n\"We need to create an independent and expert management... We take policy decisions, while we build the management independently so they remain continuously, providing continuity and longevity,\" he said.\n\nHe said separating policy-making from operational management would ensure greater continuity and support the long-term development of cricket, while also reflecting established corporate governance practices.\n\nWickramaratne also emphasised the need for strict financial oversight, saying SLC is subject to national auditing and financial regulations. He rejected previous misconceptions that the cricket administration was exempt from transparency requirements, including the Right to Information Act.\n\nThe proposed reforms would also require affiliated bodies, including district associations, provincial bodies and clubs, to comply with financial and governance regulations. Wickramaratne said there would be no tolerance for financial or administrative irregularities."
  },
