    "chunk_size_bytes": 1570,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018\u2026It is by now public knowledge that an Indian agency passed on highly credible intelligence which, had it been acted upon, could not only have failed the murder conspiracy but also led to the arrests of the bombers. Sadly, the inputs received were not followed up. No amount of planning by the terrorists and no amount of overt or covert help they allegedly received from anyone within Sri Lanka or outside could have succeeded in killing people en masse if the warnings had been heeded\u2026\u2019\n\nIt wasn\u2019t just one person who ignored or delayed in acting on the Indian intelligence alerts. It was several. They all worked for the State, in similar or related agencies. They belonged to broadly the same category of occupation. If it was only one officer it could have been coincidental, but the chain or clustering suggests conspiracy.\n\nWhy did several intelligence officers behave in the same way, which overrode their training, experience and instincts? Where could they have\u00a0 caught the \u2018delay disease\u2019 from?\n\nTheir names are all known from previous inquiries. Suresh Sallay isn\u2019t one of them. His name seems only to crop up in Azad Maulana\u2019s clip in the TV program. Is he a scapegoat or red herring? He has the wrong profile--not have enough seniority and personal authority to be the mastermind; too junior, youthful, lightweight. Shouldn\u2019t the mastermind have been a heavyweight; some former covert operations chieftain committed to a certain political outcome, who once had authority over them all; someone who remained and remains in the shadows?"
  },
