    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "98377cd2eae541116da6b3a3745fa62a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "The roadshow is a direct response to the momentum generated by Port City Colombo\u2019s Gulf outreach program this year, and the specific appetite from Abu Dhabi\u2019s distinct institutional and sovereign investment community for a more focused and substantive engagement with Sri Lanka\u2019s investment proposition.\n\nChief Guest DMCC Deputy CEO and Chief Operating Officer Feryal Ahmadi, shared her perspective on the shifting dynamics of global trade and the role free trade zones will play in that evolution. \u201cResearch shows that global trade is moving towards South-South \u2014 35% of global trade in the next ten years will be South-South versus 25% for North trade,\u201d she said. \u201cThis means that free trade zones in Dubai, Singapore, and Colombo will be extremely important as connectors for businesses moving forward, acting as launchers that enable businesses to access new markets.\u201d\n\nColombo Port City Economic Commission Chairman Harsha Amarasekera PC, drew a compelling parallel between Abu Dhabi\u2019s rise as a global investment hub and Sri Lanka\u2019s own strategic positioning.\n\n\u201cAbu Dhabi\u2019s rise as a premier global trading and investment hub is driven by its strategic location at the crossroads of East and West, its massive sovereign wealth, and its progressive regulatory framework,\u201d he said. \u201cIn many ways, Sri Lanka shares some of the same strategic advantages, such as its location in the heart of the Indian Ocean and its natural deep-water ports. It is this shared geographic strength that creates a strong foundation for collaboration between our two countries and investors. Port City Colombo is not a mere development project. It is a strategic national initiative designed to position Sri Lanka as a neutral, wholly connected platform for businesses, linking South Asia with the Middle East, Asia, and beyond.\u201d"
  },
