    "chunk_size_bytes": 1945,
    "chunk_text": "Child protection system to be strengthened through new laws, policies\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - The Government is taking steps to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s child protection system by integrating relevant institutions and introducing necessary policies and laws, Deputy Minister of Women and Child Affairs Dr. Namal Sudarshana said.\n\nIn a message issued to mark World Children\u2019s Day, the Deputy Minister said the Government\u2019s primary objective was to protect children from all forms of abuse and violence while safeguarding their rights.\n\nHe said a stronger child protection mechanism was being developed by coordinating various institutions involved in preventing abuse and violence against children.\n\n\u201cWe are taking the necessary legal steps to further strengthen these measures,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Deputy Minister said the Government was translating its decisions into policies and laws aimed at protecting children and ensuring their rights.\n\nHowever, he stressed that the objective should not simply be to increase the number of laws or programmes relating to children.\n\n\u201cThe aim is to create a country where a child feels safe, a society where they feel their dreams have value, and a world where they can believe that opportunities exist for them, regardless of who they are,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said providing children with quality education and proper nutrition from early childhood was also an important investment in their future.\n\nAmong the measures highlighted were the introduction of a National Early Childhood Development Curriculum Framework for preschools and increasing the allowance for children\u2019s morning meals.\n\nThe Deputy Minister said children growing up free from fear, with the freedom to dream and the support of society when they face difficulties, would be better prepared to shape their own futures.\n\n\u201cEvery step we take to uphold a child's rights is a step taken for the future of humanity,\u201d he said."
