    "md5": "376b6bfcebdf4378aaa529131c54a8fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1826,
    "chunk_text": "801 reusable plastic bottles seized in Pettah raid\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - The Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) has seized 801 reusable plastic bottles during special raids conducted at trading establishments in the Pettah area.\n\nThe bottles were taken into custody as case productions from shops found to be selling reusable plastic bottles, the CAA said.\n\nThe Authority said investigations into the relevant establishments are currently underway and legal action will be instituted before the appropriate Magistrate\u2019s Courts upon completion of the investigations.\n\nAccording to the CAA, reusable plastic bottles intended for carrying drinkable liquids are subject to the SLS 1616 standard.\n\nReusable plastic bottles manufactured in Sri Lanka are required to bear the SLS Product Certification Mark issued by the Sri Lanka Standards Institution (SLSI).\n\nFor imported bottles, however, displaying the SLS Mark is not mandatory. Such products must instead have been inspected and approved for sale under the SLSI Import Inspection Scheme, the CAA said.\n\nThe Authority urged traders to ensure that all applicable legal and standards requirements are met before purchasing or selling reusable plastic bottles.\n\nTraders have also been advised to properly maintain bills, invoices and supplier information relating to the purchase and supply of such products, as these records are important for establishing the source of products and tracing the supply chain.\n\nConsumers purchasing reusable plastic bottles have been advised to check whether locally manufactured products carry the SLS Product Certification Mark. In the case of imported products, consumers can request information from traders regarding the relevant approval.\n\nThe CAA also urged consumers to obtain and retain bills or receipts as proof of purchase."
  },
