    "md5": "38a87445bdbaedbb1e1742b274bd7085",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "Johnston: Government targeting opponents through legal action\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - Former Minister and Sri Lanka Podujana Peramuna (SLPP) Head of Operations Johnston Fernando alleged that the Government was targeting those who oppose or challenge it through legal action, including remanding suspects and filing new cases.\n\nSpeaking to journalists near the Fort Magistrate\u2019s Court, Fernando said the SLPP had expected such developments and claimed that remanding and imprisoning government opponents had become a policy of the present administration.\n\n\u201cWe expected things like this. Remanding and imprisoning those who oppose or challenge the government is this administration\u2019s policy,\u201d he said.\n\nFernando alleged that the Government was using such cases as a means of generating publicity, while claiming that it had little to show in terms of development projects or measures to reduce the cost of living.\n\n\u201cThey have no news to share about building roads or bridges. They have no news about constructing a port or an airport. So, the only news they have is about remanding people,\u201d he said.\n\nHe also alleged that individuals who obtained bail were subsequently faced with new cases.\n\nFernando said the SLPP would hold its next rally in Matara and indicated that the party would continue its political activities despite what he described as pressure from the Government.\n\nHe further alleged that certain officials were abusing the law and fabricating evidence to serve the Government\u2019s agenda.\n\nHowever, Fernando said his criticism was not directed at the judiciary, stressing that he had confidence in the courts and an independent judicial system.\n\n\u201cWe have never had a conflict with the courts. The problem lies with certain officials,\u201d he said.\n\nFernando also criticised what he described as political interference in law enforcement, saying politicians should not take the enforcement of laws into their own hands."
