    "date_str": "2026-10-01",
    "description": "BOC marks World Children\u2019s Day with focus on preparing young Sri Lankans for future",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/BOC-marks-World-Childrens-Day-with-focus-on-preparing-young-Sri-Lankans-for-future/273-351498",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790830619.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-01-dailymirrorlk-0956aeea-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "53a92584b724cbbfc489b967d491f5b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Children\u2019s Day is an opportunity to think not only about what we can give our children today but also about how well we are preparing them for tomorrow. Teaching children to understand the savings habit, to save towards something they want and to make responsible choices are lessons that can remain relevant throughout their lives. Through Ran Kekulu, Haritha Kekulu, Diyawara Kekulu and PFC Minor for children, BOC aims to support both children and parents as they build that foundation,\u201d said BOC Senior Deputy General Manager and Head of Development and Branch Banking Badra Kalyanapriya Gurusinghe.\n\nThat relationship can continue as children become teenagers and begin exercising greater independence. BOC 14 Plus provides a savings option for teenagers, while BOC 18+ Youth Savings caters to young adults as they move into higher education, employment and greater responsibility for managing their own finances.\n\nThe progression from Ran Kekulu to 14 Plus and subsequently to 18+ reflects the changing financial needs of a young person. What begins as savings, managed largely with the involvement of parents, can gradually become money set aside independently for education, a personal goal or a first major purchase.\n\nFor BOC, the Children\u2019s Day therefore provides an opportunity to look beyond celebrating childhood itself and consider the knowledge, habits and opportunities that children will carry into adulthood.\n\nA savings account alone cannot teach a child everything about money. However, involving children in saving, allowing them to see their own progress and helping them understand why money is being set aside can provide a practical place to start.\n\nBOC invites all parents and guardians to take the first step towards a brighter future for their children by joining the BOC Smart Ran Kekulu account. With BOC as a trusted, steady and reliable banking partner, saving for your child can become a long-term investment in their education, dreams and future."
  },
