    "chunk_size_bytes": 1827,
    "chunk_text": "Liquidator files preliminary report on Daya Group\u2019s Bimputh; seeks directors\u2019 statements of affairs\n\nBimputh Lanka Investments PLC, formerly Bimputh Finance and part of former Minister Daya Gamage\u2019s Daya Group, has disclosed that its liquidator has filed a preliminary report on the winding up and called for statements of affairs from its directors, with the liabilities exceeding the assets by close to Rs.2.2 billion, according to a stock filing.\n\nG.K. Sudath Kumar, who was appointed as liquidator on June 26, 2025, \u201chas since called for statements of affairs from the company\u2019s directors and has filed his preliminary report on the conduct of the liquidation\u201d, the filing noted.\u00a0 The company disclosed the appointment to the market only this week, some 15 months after it took effect.\n\nBimputh\u2019s appeal against the Commercial High Court\u2019s winding-up order of February 14, 2025, remains pending before the Supreme Court.\n\nHDFC Bank had filed the petition after Bimputh defaulted on the loans exceeding Rs.350 million.\n\nAs at December 31, 2024, the liabilities of Rs.4.64 billion exceeded the assets of Rs.2.46 billion, leaving the shareholders\u2019 funds at minus Rs.2.18 billion. This deficit was nearly 16 times the Rs.136.7 million recorded in March 2022.\n\nThe accumulated losses more than tripled over the same period to Rs.2.92 billion, a figure equivalent to 4.7 times the company\u2019s stated capital. According to its 2021/22 annual report, Bimputh\u2019s capital adequacy ratio stood at 7.5 percent, against the regulatory minimum of 10.5 percent, while a promised Rs.1.5 billion capital infusion never materialised.\n\nThe Central Bank froze deposit-taking and lending and then cancelled the licence, with effect from September 01, 2023, citing deficient capital, poor asset quality and continuous losses."
