    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "U.S. issues damning report on investment climate in Sri Lanka\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - In what appeared to be a damning report , the U.S. Department of State, in its latest report, says the country\u2019s investment climate remains challenging despite recovery from the 2022 economic crisis with a five percent growth last year exceeding expectations.\n\nThe report\u2019 2026 Investment Climate: Sri Lanka\u2019 , the Department says the sweeping electoral victories of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake and his National People\u2019s Power (NPP) parliamentary coalition in late 2024 had provided political stability.\n\nHowever, it says the NPP\u2019s commitment to the country\u2019s $3 billion, four-year (2023-2027) Extended Fund Facility IMF programme reassured investors, but many remain wary given the NPP leadership\u2019s mixed messages on the openness of the country\u2019s market.\n\nThe U.S. embassy in Colombo shared the online version of the report with the Sri Lankan media.\n\nForeign direct investment (FDI) in 2025 was $1.06 billion, approximately 1 percent of GDP, significantly below the three to four percent commonly seen in emerging economies. The sectors that attracted the most foreign investment in 2025 were: manufacturing, port development, tourism, information technology and business process outsourcing and real estate.\n\nAccording to the report , investment experts cite the following areas as requiring greater focus and improvement on areas such as policy stability, regulatory reform, availability of skilled labor, access to industrial land, efficient logistics and streamlined trade procedures to meet the FDI targets set by the government.\n\nInvestors view access to preferential export markets through FTAs as an important factor in investment decisions, as such agreements improve export competitiveness and reduce the cost of imported raw materials and intermediate goods, the report says."
