    "md5": "d4933de08a374d0e34b31bff01a1256b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Investors view access to preferential export markets through FTAs as an important factor in investment decisions, as such agreements improve export competitiveness and reduce the cost of imported raw materials and intermediate goods, the report says.\n\nHowever, regulatory unpredictability, bureaucratic hurdles, and selective transparency continue to limit broader participation, it says.\n\nThe government\u2019s institutional capacity to encourage an open investment environment remains limited despite positive rhetoric. Overall, investors report that doing business remains difficult, frequently citing concerns about project reversals, regulatory shifts, slow decision making and inadequate support for established businesses.\n\nThe IMF and local business chambers stress the need for comprehensive structural reforms, including trade facilitation, digitization, and stronger governance mechanisms.\n\nThese country reports are published by the respective U.S. embassies across the world covering over 170 countries.\n\nAsserting that Sri Lanka\u2019s implementation of foreign investment policies is inconsistent, the report says the Board of Investment (BOI) , as the principal investment promotion agency, struggles to function as a \u201cone-stop shop\u201d due to fragmented authority across multiple government departments, creating lengthy approval processes that frustrate potential investors.\n\nInvestors report challenges in having a consistent and open dialogue with the BOI. In May 2026, the BOI launched the Ready to invest digital platform with sector-specific investment opportunities. Key impediments include unnecessary regulations, legal uncertainty, and poor bureaucratic responsiveness. Inefficient state-owned energy enterprises, notably the Ceylon Electricity Board, hinder development of cost-effective energy supplies crucial for industrial operations. Foreign investors consistently report high transaction costs, unpredictable policies, and opaque procurement procedures, according to the report."
