    "chunk_text": "Investors report challenges in having a consistent and open dialogue with the BOI. In May 2026, the BOI launched the Ready to invest digital platform with sector-specific investment opportunities. Key impediments include unnecessary regulations, legal uncertainty, and poor bureaucratic responsiveness. Inefficient state-owned energy enterprises, notably the Ceylon Electricity Board, hinder development of cost-effective energy supplies crucial for industrial operations. Foreign investors consistently report high transaction costs, unpredictable policies, and opaque procurement procedures, according to the report.\n\nThe report has cited the non-implementation of US $ 3.7 billion Sinopec investment committed by the President in January, 2025 and withdrawal from the US $ 400 wind farm project by the Adani group as examples.\n\n\u201cJanuary 2025, President Dissanayake committed to finalizing a $3.7 billion Sinopec oil refinery project, the largest FDI project in Sri Lankan history, to be located adjacent to Chinese-controlled Hambantota International Port. As of June 2026, the project remains pending due to disagreements between the government and Sinopec. In February 2025, Indian firm Adani Green Energy withdrew from a proposed $400 million, 484 MW wind farm project in northern Sri Lanka, citing Sri Lankan government efforts to renegotiate a previously awarded contract. In December 2025 the government quietly ended negotiations with China Harbour Engineering Company on a new floating LNG terminal just days before the contract was to be signed. The NPP government also ceased the planned privatization of many SOEs, choosing to implement turnaround reforms instead. Some investors report concerns regarding mixed public messaging. While the government has sought to attract foreign investment, certain senior officials have publicly advocated a larger role for state-owned enterprises and state participation in economic activity,\u201d the report says."
