    "chunk_text": "September arrivals edge back into growth as Japan, Australia gain ground\n\nIsland welcomed 143,623 tourists in first 27 days of September, up 0.4% when compared to same period last year\n\nHolidaymakers from Japan and Australia have already outnumbered their full September 2025 totals, while arrivals from Germany, Spain and Bangladesh trail well behind\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tourist arrivals rose 0.4 percent in the first 27 days of September when compared to the same period last year, as a strong growth from Japan and Australia put the month on course to end three consecutive months of decline, as per the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority data.\n\nThe island welcomed 143,623 tourists between September 01 and 27, against 143,085 over the same days last year.\n\nTo surpass last September\u2019s 158,971, it needs a further 15,348 arrivals over the final three days, fewer than the 15,886 it welcomed over the same three days in 2025. That would mark its first monthly increase since May.\n\nJapan sent 5,187 holidaymakers, already exceeding last September\u2019s full-month total of 4,137 and rose to sixth from 10th among the source markets.\n\nAustralia likewise surpassed last September\u2019s 9,105, with 10,067 arrivals, climbing to third from fifth, following the launch of Jetstar\u2019s direct low-fare Melbourne-Colombo service on August 25. China moved up to second, with 10,081 arrivals.\n\nHowever, Germany recorded 6,745 arrivals, against 9,344 over the whole of September last year, while Spain drew 3,163, against 4,841 and Bangladesh 3,923, against 4,953.\n\nThe Netherlands, ninth last September with 4,433 arrivals, dropped out of the top 10. The cumulative arrivals of 1,678,745 by September 27 were 30,863 or 1.8 percent, lower when compared to the same period last year.\u00a0 Tourist arrivals grew 12.9 percent in January and February but have declined 7.8 percent since March, as the West Asia conflict disrupted air connectivity to the island."
