    "chunk_text": "The Netherlands, ninth last September with 4,433 arrivals, dropped out of the top 10. The cumulative arrivals of 1,678,745 by September 27 were 30,863 or 1.8 percent, lower when compared to the same period last year.\u00a0 Tourist arrivals grew 12.9 percent in January and February but have declined 7.8 percent since March, as the West Asia conflict disrupted air connectivity to the island.\n\nThe Indian arrivals are already 14.9 percent above the first nine months of last year, at 431,162, whereas the Russian arrivals are 32.3 percent lower, at 82,733.\n\nMeanwhile, air connectivity is set to improve ahead of the peak winter season. British Airways will resume London Gatwick-Colombo flights on October 23, ending an absence of more than 11 years, while Edelweiss will increase its seasonal Zurich-Colombo service to three weekly flights from October 26. French Bee is also set to launch Paris Orly-Colombo flights on December 19.\n\nThe Russian carriers are set to bring close to 50,000 winter seats from eight Russian cities into Mattala and Colombo between October and April, as reported earlier by Mirror Business, which could revive a market whose 39,411 decline in arrivals this year exceeds the island\u2019s overall shortfall.\n\nThe added capacity will be critical to the 2.5 million year-end target, which requires 821,255 arrivals between September 28 and December 31. That would mean a growth of 25.8 percent when compared to the same period last year, more than double the 12.2 percent growth recorded over the same stretch in 2025, when the island welcomed 652,913 tourists, against 581,744 a year earlier."
