    "chunk_text": "The external sector remained resilient, with the current account estimated to have recorded a surplus in August, after four consecutive months of deficits. The moderating merchandise imports and improved tourism earnings and workers\u2019 remittances supported the turnaround.\n\nThe gross official reserves rose to US $ 6.9 billion at end-August, supported by the CBSL\u2019s net foreign exchange purchases. The figure includes the swap facility from the People\u2019s Bank of China.\n\nColombo\u2019s inflation held at 8.0 percent in September for a second straight month, masking a shift in the cost-of-living squeeze, as the price pressures increasingly extend beyond the volatile food items into transport, housing and services.\n\nThe Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI) edged up just 0.1 percent month-on-month (MoM)\u00a0 to 209.1 points in September, from 208.8 in August, suggesting that the fresh price momentum remained relatively contained despite the elevated annual inflation rate, the data from the Census and Statistics Department showed.\n\nFood inflation, however, accelerated to 8.7 percent year-on-year, from 8.5 percent in August, while non-food inflation remained at 7.7 percent. Headline inflation was also unchanged at 8.0 percent. The non-food items accounted for 5.16 percentage points of September\u2019s 8.0 percent annual inflation, compared with 2.83 percentage points from food, pointing to a broader cost increase that is less dependent on the short-term swings in the agricultural prices. Transport alone contributed 2.38 percentage points to annual inflation, making it the largest non-food driver. Within the category, petrol accounted for 1.31 percentage points, while diesel, bus fares, vehicle prices, servicing costs and school transport also added to the increase."
