    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Food inflation, however, accelerated to 8.7 percent year-on-year, from 8.5 percent in August, while non-food inflation remained at 7.7 percent. Headline inflation was also unchanged at 8.0 percent. The non-food items accounted for 5.16 percentage points of September\u2019s 8.0 percent annual inflation, compared with 2.83 percentage points from food, pointing to a broader cost increase that is less dependent on the short-term swings in the agricultural prices. Transport alone contributed 2.38 percentage points to annual inflation, making it the largest non-food driver. Within the category, petrol accounted for 1.31 percentage points, while diesel, bus fares, vehicle prices, servicing costs and school transport also added to the increase.\n\nThe food prices increased 0.15 percent MoM, while the non-food prices added just 0.04 percent. Fresh fruits, big onions and milk powder were among the largest upward contributors, while the lower sea fish, green chilli and vegetable prices provided some relief.\n\nThe transport prices also fell on the month, largely reflecting the lower petrol prices, helping contain the overall index despite the increases in education, health and restaurant-related costs.\n\nThe annual comparison, however, remains considerably less benign. Selected retail prices show petrol was about 34 percent higher than in September 2025, while diesel was also 34 percent higher. The big onion prices were up 90 percent, dried chillies 61 percent and eggs 36 percent.\n\nThe divergence between the subdued monthly price movements and elevated annual inflation partly reflects the sharp increase in the price level over the past year.\n\nColombo inflation stood at only 1.5 percent in September 2025, before accelerating through 2026. It rose from 2.3 percent in January to 5.4 percent in April, 6.8 percent in June and 7.3 percent in July, reaching 8.0 percent in August and remaining there in September."
