    "chunk_size_bytes": 1574,
    "chunk_text": "Quest for accountability should no longer lead to dead-ends\n\nThis was his response when Bopege questioned him about the abduction and attack on journalist Keith Noyahr. He had said that he doesn\u2019t remember Noyahr sustaining injuries following the attack.\n\nAccountability has been a buzz word for many lawmakers that Sri Lanka produced. It had been a promise that was rarely fulfilled on the political stage. This is why people rejected corrupt leaders and their associates. People rejected political nepotism, dynastic politics and demanded transparency and accountability.\n\nSri Lanka has surpassed turbulent periods in history. From insurrections to a prolonged armed conflict,\u00a0 and unfortunate events such as the Easter Sunday attacks and the economic crisis, Sri Lankans have survived the best and worst of times. In each one of these incidents, there is the truth, the rumours, the fake narratives and a long list of questions and doubts that remain unanswered.\n\nNo party has claimed authorship for carrying out the 1971, 1987-1989 insurrections despite popular secrets. Claims of the Batalanda torture chambers were seen by some to be unfounded. Tamil communities in the North and East including the diaspora claim that the armed forces engaged in war crimes during the final stages of the conflict. But in his speech at the United Nations General Assembly in 2010, the then President Mahinda Rajapaksa defended his military campaign as a humanitarian operation. To date, many frontline army officials reject war crime allegations during the final stages of the war."
