    "md5": "168576f36134658f98d9f8889e3ac4b3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1968,
    "chunk_text": "No party has claimed authorship for carrying out the 1971, 1987-1989 insurrections despite popular secrets. Claims of the Batalanda torture chambers were seen by some to be unfounded. Tamil communities in the North and East including the diaspora claim that the armed forces engaged in war crimes during the final stages of the conflict. But in his speech at the United Nations General Assembly in 2010, the then President Mahinda Rajapaksa defended his military campaign as a humanitarian operation. To date, many frontline army officials reject war crime allegations during the final stages of the war.\n\nWho is responsible for this crime and what was their purpose, they question.\n\nBut apart from such major incidents, journalists were being abducted, attacked and even murdered in a previous era.\n\nQuestions regarding accountability have once again come to the fore with former President Gotabaya Rajapaksa\u2019s responses before the Colombo Magistrate\u2019s Court on Tuesday. \u201cI can\u2019t remember\u201d, was his response to many of the questions raised by lawyer Nuwan Bopege. This was his response when Bopege questioned him about the abduction and attack on journalist Keith Noyahr. He had said that he doesn\u2019t remember Noyahr sustaining injuries following the attack.\n\nRajapaksa further said that he had not been involved in any operation on the ground,\u00a0 and that his duty as Defence Secretary was to convey policies adopted by the Government to those institutions. When asked whether he was aware of abductions and disappearances that happened in the country,\u00a0 he said that these were \u201cmatters presented with the intention of gaining political advantage and that there is no truth in them.\u201d He said that these were said with the intention of damaging the reputation of the country.\n\nPeople including victims have been longing for answers. But when former political leaders respond in such a manner, the quest for accountability becomes a series of dead-ends."
