    "md5": "73466530cad471ad8a564b553b2ce31a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "Will any leader undo the 22A in future?\n\nDespite opposition leader Sajith Premadasa\u2019s promise, his party will need a two thirds majority to carry it out\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government states that it is increasing the retirement age to retain the experience of senior judges for the judiciary for a little longer. The government may have other motives as well.\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa claims that he would repeal the recently adopted 22nd Constitutional Amendment once he takes office someday. Yet, we don\u2019t believe he will do so.\n\nFirstly, Premadasa\u2019s party, the Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) must have the two-thirds parliamentary majority to roll back this Constitutional Amendment. Yet, given the political antics staged by the Opposition parties -- including the SJB --\u00a0 we cannot imagine a day when the SJB would secure a two-thirds parliamentary majority, or when it would come to power at all.\n\nIn Sri Lankan political history, a single party or a coalition has assumed office with a two-thirds majority on only three occasions - in the years of 1970, 1977, and 2024. The alliances led by Mahinda Rajapaksa won 144 and 145 seats respectively in 2010 and 2020, and secured a two-thirds majority only with the support of several minor political parties.\n\nTherefore, it is difficult to believe that even if SJB comes to power, it will obtain a two-thirds majority capable of altering the constitution.\n\nBesides, the 22nd Constitutional Amendment was introduced precisely to increase the retirement age of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges and it is generally believed that the appellate court judges also prefer to serve longer.\u00a0 Therefore, if it is repealed in the future, it would cause significant dissatisfaction among the judiciary."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-10-01-dailymirrorlk-594587a0",
