    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7618840b7a2189f4f960926988abec57",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "Besides, the 22nd Constitutional Amendment was introduced precisely to increase the retirement age of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges and it is generally believed that the appellate court judges also prefer to serve longer.\u00a0 Therefore, if it is repealed in the future, it would cause significant dissatisfaction among the judiciary.\n\nThe most critical question is why the 22nd Constitutional Amendment should be repealed at all. One could argue that this amendment will not expedite court trials in the manner the government claims. However, the opposition\u2019s argument that increasing the retirement age of judges compromises the independence of the judiciary is completely baseless.\n\nIt is customary for politicians to declare, as Sajith Premadasa does, \u201cWe will come to power and change the course of history.\u201d However, most of the time it is mere rhetoric. Only on a few rare occasions in history have politicians reversed the course of history. The undoing of 17th, 18th, 19th and the 20th Constitutional Amendments can be cited as examples of this.\n\nPrior to 2001, the Presidents directly appointed whomever they wished to high state posts and as judges of the superior courts. They also removed individuals from those posts at will. Consequently, the courts and the state machinery operated under constant fear of the Executive.\n\nTo reverse this situation, the 17th Constitutional Amendment was introduced in 2001 during Chandrika Kumaratunga\u2019s administration. In fact, Kumaratunga also kept delaying its passage. It was passed back then only because of pressure from the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), which wanted to get it done by supporting the Kumaratunga\u2019s government when it had lost its parliamentary majority."
  },
  {
