    "md5": "ebe6ee8b479e848170e3f694157e4c36",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "To reverse this situation, the 17th Constitutional Amendment was introduced in 2001 during Chandrika Kumaratunga\u2019s administration. In fact, Kumaratunga also kept delaying its passage. It was passed back then only because of pressure from the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP), which wanted to get it done by supporting the Kumaratunga\u2019s government when it had lost its parliamentary majority.\n\nThe Constitutional Council is a body comprising members of parliament from both the ruling and opposition parties, as well as respected members of civil society. Through this mechanism, the absolute power of the President was somewhat checked.\n\nIn 2010, the United People\u2019s Freedom Alliance led by Mahinda Rajapaksa assumed office after winning 144 parliamentary seats. With the support of other minor parties, Rajapaksa increased his parliamentary strength to a two-thirds majority and introduced the 18th Constitutional Amendment. Through this, the President was once again empowered to directly appoint whoever he wished to high state offices. The Constitutional Council was downgraded to a powerless Parliamentary Council. Besides, the 18th Amendment removed the rule limiting an individual to two presidential terms, allowing a person to contest for the presidency any number of times.\n\nFollowing this, the Maithripala Sirisena-Ranil Wickremesinghe government came to power in 2015, promising to reintroduce checks on the presidency and to abolish the Executive Presidency. In April of that year, the 19th Constitutional Amendment was enacted, reviving the mechanism to check the President\u2019s actions. The term of office for both the President and Parliament was reduced to five years. The legal provision restricting an individual to two presidential terms was also restored. Surprisingly, Rajapaksa loyalists who had backed the previous two opposing amendments, voted in favour of this one as well."
  },
