    "md5": "03c6500fe816b1112c398ff5e6b4a5ed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "Following this, the Maithripala Sirisena-Ranil Wickremesinghe government came to power in 2015, promising to reintroduce checks on the presidency and to abolish the Executive Presidency. In April of that year, the 19th Constitutional Amendment was enacted, reviving the mechanism to check the President\u2019s actions. The term of office for both the President and Parliament was reduced to five years. The legal provision restricting an individual to two presidential terms was also restored. Surprisingly, Rajapaksa loyalists who had backed the previous two opposing amendments, voted in favour of this one as well.\n\nWhen the economic crisis reached its peak, Gotabaya tried various means to win back public favour. Accordingly, in 2022, he himself came forward to repeal the 20th Amendment he had introduced and enacted the 22nd Amendment to restrict the powers of the presidency. The concession extended to dual citizens was also revoked.\n\nIn a striking display of unprincipled politics, Rajapaksa loyalists voted for every constitutional amendment from the 17th straight through to the 22nd, despite the fact that these laws alternately negated each other.\n\nApart from these shifts, no leader who claimed they would alter the course of political history actually did so. The best example of this is the repeated promise made by various parties to completely abolish the Executive Presidency. Chandrika Kumaratunga was the first to give such a promise in 1994. However, she did not possess the required two-thirds majority. Although the then United National Party (UNP) leader Gamini Dissanayake stated at one stage that he was ready to offer the opposition support to pass it, Chandrika did not follow through. To this day, that system of governance remains in practice."
