    "md5": "b57a382821800c1b2a1ee9ab60fcd1cf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2032,
    "chunk_text": "Children\u2019s rights: My day, my rights, my future\n\nChildren\u2019s rights are not rewards for being well-behaved or successful. They are fundamental human rights\n\nListening does not mean allowing children to make every decision. It means respecting their views according to their age and maturity,\u00a0 and ensuring those views are considered in decisions affecting their lives\n\nEvery child has the right to grow up healthy, safe, loved and respected. Children have the right to learn, play, dream and express their views. Yet,\u00a0 millions continue to face poverty, conflict, violence, displacement, malnutrition and unequal access to education and healthcare.\n\nThis year, Universal Children\u2019s Day observed in Sri Lanka on October 1 carries the message \u201cMy Day. My Rights. My Future.\u201d The theme calls on children to speak about their lives, rights and aspirations, while urging adults and leaders to listen and act.\n\nUniversal Children\u2019s Day commemorates important milestones in the history of children\u2019s rights. On November 20, 1959, the United Nations adopted the Declaration of the Rights of the Child. Thirty years later, on November 20, 1989, it adopted the Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC).\n\nThe CRC recognises childhood as a special period of life and establishes rights including survival and development, health, education, protection from violence and exploitation, and participation in decisions affecting children.\n\nSri Lanka signed the Convention in 1990 and ratified it in 1991, committing to uphold and protect children\u2019s rights. While Sri Lanka celebrates Children\u2019s Day on October 1, November 20 is observed internationally as Universal Children\u2019s Day.\n\nChildren\u2019s rights are not rewards for being well-behaved or successful. They are fundamental human rights.\n\nEvery child has the right to adequate nutrition and healthcare, regardless of family income. Every child has the right to education and protection from physical, emotional and sexual abuse. Children also have the right to be heard."
