    "md5": "0f06aca4cffd170b214f770aafb38b15",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Every child has the right to adequate nutrition and healthcare, regardless of family income. Every child has the right to education and protection from physical, emotional and sexual abuse. Children also have the right to be heard.\n\nThese principles should guide parents, teachers, healthcare professionals, communities and governments when making decisions that affect children.\n\nChallenges facing childrenThe world has made significant progress in child survival, vaccination, education and access to basic services. However, poverty, conflict, climate-related disasters and inequality continue to threaten these gains.\n\nUNICEF identifies child poverty as a major threat to children\u2019s rights. Poverty can mean inadequate nutrition, poor housing, limited healthcare, unsafe water and sanitation, and fewer educational opportunities. UNICEF estimates that 412 million children worldwide live in extreme monetary poverty.\n\nClimate-related disasters also place children at particular risk. Floods, droughts and heat waves can disrupt education, damage health services, cause displacement and increase the risks of infectious diseases and malnutrition. Armed conflicts similarly expose children to devastating consequences despite their having no responsibility for the conflicts themselves.\n\nSri Lanka has made considerable progress in child health and education. Childhood immunisation is widely available, under-five mortality has declined and primary school attendance is high.\n\nHowever, challenges remain. Some children continue to experience poverty and unequal access to essential services, while vulnerable communities face difficulties related to nutrition, healthcare, sanitation and education. Child abuse, neglect and exploitation also require continued vigilance."
