    "chunk_text": "However, challenges remain. Some children continue to experience poverty and unequal access to essential services, while vulnerable communities face difficulties related to nutrition, healthcare, sanitation and education. Child abuse, neglect and exploitation also require continued vigilance.\n\nPerhaps the most important word in this year\u2019s theme is \u201cMy.\u201d Children should not simply be spoken about; they should be listened to. They may have concerns about bullying, safety, examinations, friendships, social media, climate change or their future.\n\nListening does not mean allowing children to make every decision. It means respecting their views according to their age and maturity,\u00a0 and ensuring those views are considered in decisions affecting their lives.\n\nUniversal Children\u2019s Day should go beyond celebrations and competitions. It should be an opportunity for meaningful action.\n\nParents can create homes where children feel safe to speak without fear. Teachers can make schools safe and inclusive. Healthcare professionals can advocate for children\u2019s health, development and protection. Communities can support vulnerable children and families. The media can give children a voice while protecting their privacy and dignity.\n\nDecision-makers, meanwhile, have a responsibility to ensure that policies, programmes and budgets place children\u2019s best interests at the centre.\n\nChildren are not merely the adults of tomorrow. They are citizens and rights-holders today.\n\nOn Universal Children\u2019s Day 2026, let us move beyond speaking about children and start listening to them.\n\nLet every child have the opportunity to learn, play and feel safe. Let every child know their rights and have those rights respected. And let every child imagine a future\u2014and receive the support needed to achieve it.\n\nMy Day. My Rights. My Future. The message is simple. The responsibility belongs to all of us.\n\n(Dr. Maheshaka Wijayawardena is\u00a0 Consultant Paediatrician at the Base Hospital,\u00a0 Marawila)"
