    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "More than half of Sri Lanka\u2019s older persons have chronic diseases\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - More than half of Sri Lankans aged 60 and above are affected by at least one chronic disease, highlighting the growing healthcare needs of the country\u2019s rapidly ageing population, according to the 2024 Census of Population and Housing.\n\nThe Department of Census and Statistics (DCS) said non-communicable diseases (NCDs) were more prevalent among older people and women.\n\nAccording to the Census, 19.2% of Sri Lanka\u2019s total population, or around 4.18 million people, were reported to be suffering from non-communicable diseases in 2024.\n\nAmong the NCDs reported, high blood pressure was the most prevalent at 10.1%, followed by diabetes at 8.5%. The DCS noted that these conditions were more widespread among females and older persons.\n\nThe Census specifically found that more than half of the population aged 60 years and above had at least one chronic disease.\n\nThe figures come as Sri Lanka undergoes a significant demographic shift, with people aged 60 and above accounting for 18% of the country's population, according to the 2024 Census.\n\nPopulation Association of Sri Lanka President and University of Colombo Professor of Demography Prof. Manori Kaluthantiri Weeratunga said longer life expectancy did not necessarily mean that older people would live without illness.\n\nShe said some older persons had to manage several health conditions at the same time, alongside age-related changes in mobility, hearing, vision or memory.\n\nProf. Weeratunga said Sri Lanka therefore needed healthcare services that could support older people continuously and help them navigate the different services they required.\n\nShe identified strong primary healthcare, regular reviews of medicines, rehabilitation, mental health support, hearing and vision care, and assistance with daily activities as important areas requiring attention."
  },
