    "md5": "1ff71af8dd2ef32616078059115edb7c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1337,
    "chunk_text": "She identified strong primary healthcare, regular reviews of medicines, rehabilitation, mental health support, hearing and vision care, and assistance with daily activities as important areas requiring attention.\n\n\u201cHealthcare should ask more than \u2018What illness do you have?\u2019 It should also ask, \u2018What do you want to continue doing?\u2019\u201d she said.\n\nWith appropriate treatment, assistive devices and a supportive environment, many older people can continue to remain active while managing chronic conditions, Prof. Weeratunga said.\n\nThe Census findings also highlight the importance of adapting healthcare services to the needs of an ageing population rather than focusing only on treating individual illnesses.\n\nSri Lanka recorded a total population of 21,781,800 in the 2024 Census, which was conducted with the census moment set at midnight on December 18, 2024. The final Census report was released by the DCS in April 2026.\n\nThe findings come ahead of the International Day of Older Persons 2026, which focuses internationally on \u201cThe Age of Longevity: Rethinking Systems for Longer Lives.\u201d\n\nThe data underlines the need for Sri Lanka to consider not only how long people live, but also how healthcare and support systems can help older people maintain independence and quality of life while living with chronic diseases."
  },
