    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "Fuel companies to get Rs. 70 per litre diesel subsidy\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - The government will provide Rs. 70 per litre of diesel as part of a Rs. 41 billion fuel subsidy approved by the Cabinet to cushion consumers and fuel distributors from the impact of surging global oil prices, Energy Minister Anura Karunathilaka said.\n\nThe subsidy is intended to prevent the full impact of rising international fuel prices from being passed on to consumers.\n\nKarunathilaka said fuel distribution companies have reported varying levels of losses under the current pricing structure amid the sharp increase in global fuel prices.\n\nAccording to the Minister, companies have cited losses ranging from Rs. 120 to Rs. 247 per litre of diesel, with figures of Rs. 135, Rs. 247, Rs. 192 and Rs. 120 per litre reported by different companies.\n\nThe government will release the Rs. 41 billion subsidy over three months, allocating Rs. 15 billion in October, Rs. 13.5 billion in November and Rs. 12.5 billion in December.\n\nThe subsidy will be calculated based on the volume of diesel sold by each fuel distribution company during the respective month.\n\nKarunathilaka acknowledged that the Rs. 70-per-litre subsidy will not fully cover the losses reported by fuel companies, meaning distributors will continue to absorb part of the increased costs.\n\nHe said the measure was aimed at easing pressure on the fuel market and preventing a sharp price shock for consumers.\n\nThe Minister said some fuel distribution companies had begun restricting diesel supplies, placing additional pressure on the Ceylon Petroleum Corporation (CPC).\n\nDespite the increased burden on the CPC, Karunathilaka expressed confidence that the corporation would not record an overall financial loss by the end of the year.\n\nHe said revenue from the CPC\u2019s wider operations, including petrol, diesel and aviation fuel sales, as well as refinery operations, would help offset the additional costs."
