    "md5": "cb6ea939758b6bba48d6202d696075c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1740,
    "chunk_text": "Africa\u2019s industrial future takes shape in Kenya with US$16 Billion oil refinery investment\n\nOct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - A significant chapter in East Africa\u2019s industrial transformation opened yesterday with the groundbreaking of the US$16 billion Dangote East Africa Petroleum Refinery and Petrochemicals Special Economic Zone at Mokowe in Lamu County, Kenya.\n\nThe landmark ceremony brought together Kenyan President William Ruto, Ugandan President Yoweri Kaguta Museveni, Benin President Romuald Wadagni, Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed, Togo President Jean-Lucien Savi de Tov\u00e9 and former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo, alongside Africa\u2019s richest person and leading Nigerian industrialist Aliko Dangote and other senior dignitaries.\n\nThe proposed refinery by Dangote with a planned processing capacity of 700,000 barrels of crude oil per day, represents one of the most ambitious industrial investments undertaken in the region. The project is designed to serve not only Kenya but the wider East African market, reducing dependence on imported refined petroleum products, strengthening regional energy security and promoting greater value addition within Africa.\n\nDangote Petroleum Refinery in Lagos, Nigeria\u2014Africa\u2019s largest oil refinery, with a processing capacity of about 650,000 barrels per day\u2014demonstrating the scale of industrial investment that African private enterprise is increasingly capable of delivering.\n\nPresident Ruto described the project as a generational undertaking and placed it within a much broader vision for African industrialisation. He argued that Africa\u2019s vast natural resources should become the foundation of African industry rather than simply being exported for processing elsewhere."
  },
