    "chunk_text": "President Ruto described the project as a generational undertaking and placed it within a much broader vision for African industrialisation. He argued that Africa\u2019s vast natural resources should become the foundation of African industry rather than simply being exported for processing elsewhere.\n\nDangote, meanwhile, described the refinery as another demonstration that Africa has the capacity to conceive, finance and execute industrial projects of global scale. Drawing on the experience of his refinery in Nigeria, he said the Lamu project should help reduce East Africa\u2019s dependence on imported petroleum products while creating employment, transferring skills and building industrial capacity. He pledged to deliver the refinery within 40 months.\n\nUganda\u201ds President Museveni welcomed the investment but stressed that East Africa would require several refineries as its economies and populations expand. He said the Dangote refinery should complement planned refinery developments elsewhere in the region, including Uganda and Tanzania, rather than compete with them.\n\nMuseveni\u2019s broader message was equally significant: Africa must increasingly process its own natural resources instead of continuing the traditional economic model of exporting raw materials and importing finished products. Regional integration, industrialisation and value addition, he argued, are essential if Africa is to retain more of the wealth generated from its resources.\n\nThe scale of the gathering itself carried an important message. The presence of leaders from East and West Africa demonstrated that major investments of this magnitude are increasingly being viewed not merely as national projects, but as part of a wider African economic transformation.\n\nFormer Sri Lankan Ambassador and businessman Kananathan, who attended the groundbreaking ceremony, said the development should also carry an important message for Sri Lanka."
