    "md5": "9200bf070e158c61ae592219ab863fe0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1748,
    "chunk_text": "Six-year labour pact promises better jobs and worker protection - but can Sri Lanka deliver?\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has signed a six-year labour programme promising better employment opportunities, stronger worker protection and expanded social protection, but the success of the initiative will ultimately depend on how effectively its commitments are implemented.\n\nThe Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) 2026\u20132032 was signed yesterday by the Government, the International Labour Organization (ILO), employers\u2019 organisations and trade unions, establishing a common framework for cooperation in the labour and employment sector.\n\nThe programme identifies productive employment, workers\u2019 rights, social protection, labour governance and social dialogue as key priorities.\n\nHowever, translating these broad commitments into tangible improvements for workers will require sustained implementation, effective labour institutions and continued cooperation among the Government, employers and trade unions.\n\nThe programme comes at a time when Sri Lanka\u2019s labour market is undergoing significant changes amid evolving economic conditions. Against this backdrop, the new framework places particular emphasis on creating better employment opportunities, strengthening labour market institutions and improving access to social protection.\n\nIt also seeks to promote a safe, fair and inclusive working environment.\n\nThe DWCP was developed through an extensive consultation process that began in 2024 with a review of the previous programme, followed by consultations with key stakeholders. The National Labour Advisory Council (NLAC) was also used as a platform to obtain the views of workers\u2019 and employers\u2019 representatives."
  },
