    "md5": "2bc120a05352738aa0e56d9b1d44e680",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1548,
    "chunk_text": "The DWCP was developed through an extensive consultation process that began in 2024 with a review of the previous programme, followed by consultations with key stakeholders. The National Labour Advisory Council (NLAC) was also used as a platform to obtain the views of workers\u2019 and employers\u2019 representatives.\n\nLabour Minister Anil Jayantha Fernando, addressing the signing ceremony, stressed that economic growth and decent work should progress together.\n\nHe said the Government, employers and workers have a collective responsibility to create a productive, inclusive and safe working environment.\n\nThe parties have also reaffirmed their commitment to implementing the programme through continued social dialogue, consultation and monitoring of progress.\n\nThe challenge, however, will be ensuring that the commitments contained in the six-year framework translate into practical outcomes for workers, particularly in terms of employment opportunities, labour protection and access to social protection.\n\nThe programme is therefore expected to serve not merely as a policy framework but as a mechanism for measuring progress in Sri Lanka\u2019s labour sector through 2032.\n\nUN Resident Coordinator Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche, ILO Country Director for Sri Lanka and the Maldives Joni Simpson, Deputy Labour Minister Mahinda Jayasinghe, Secretary to the Ministry of Labour S. M. Piyatissa and Commissioner General of Labour Nadeeka Wataliyadda were among those present at the signing ceremony, along with employer and employee representatives of the NLAC."
