    "md5": "481dade157a94be3e2d546df43a80418",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1943,
    "chunk_text": "63 endemic snakes in spotlight at global congress\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka\u2019s 63 endemic snake species have come under the spotlight as more than 400 international and local experts gathered in Kandy today for the world\u2019s first international conference dedicated entirely to snakes.\n\nThe \u201c1st World Congress on Snakes \u2013 2026,\u201d held under the theme \u201cSnakes, Science, Species Survival & Sri Lanka,\u201d commenced today at The Grand Kandyan Hotel under the patronage of Environment Minister Dammika Patabendi.\n\nExperts, researchers, environmentalists and field specialists representing 42 countries are participating in the four-day congress, which focuses on snake diversity, conservation, scientific research and species survival.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s snake biodiversity is a major focus of the conference, with 112 of the 411 snake species recorded worldwide found on the island.\n\nOf these, 63 species are endemic to Sri Lanka, accounting for 56.3% of the country\u2019s recorded snake species.\n\nThe high level of endemism places particular importance on protecting Sri Lanka\u2019s snake populations, as the loss of an endemic species could mean the loss of that species from the entire world.\n\nHowever, many of Sri Lanka\u2019s snake species are facing increasing threats from habitat loss and fragmentation, agricultural expansion, urbanisation and climate change.\n\nExtreme weather events and other environmental disruptions have also contributed to increased encounters between wildlife and human communities, adding another dimension to the conservation challenge.\n\nThe congress is also addressing the issue of snakebites, which remains a serious public health concern in Sri Lanka and internationally.\n\nNearly 10,000 snakebite cases are reported in Sri Lanka annually, with hundreds of deaths recorded. Globally, approximately 138,000 people die each year from snakebites, while many survivors suffer permanent disabilities."
  },
