    "md5": "5b52602679b5c8a56dfbd1c5a9af658a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1188,
    "chunk_text": "Nearly 10,000 snakebite cases are reported in Sri Lanka annually, with hundreds of deaths recorded. Globally, approximately 138,000 people die each year from snakebites, while many survivors suffer permanent disabilities.\n\nThe conference aims to bring together international expertise to strengthen research and conservation efforts while identifying practical measures to protect snake species and reduce human-snake conflict.\n\nAddressing the inaugural session, Minister Patabendi said agencies affiliated with the Ministry were working to implement national biodiversity priorities under the National Biodiversity Strategy and Action Plan for 2026\u20132030.\n\nHe said the conference should not end with academic discussions alone but should generate actionable knowledge that could ultimately help save both human and wildlife lives.\n\nSri Lanka, the United States and Australia are key partners of the congress, with participants from countries including India, Germany, Pakistan, Poland and Malaysia.\n\nEight new books on snakes are also scheduled to be launched during the event, while the Department of Posts plans to issue a commemorative stamp marking the first World Snake Conference."
