    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "PM urges stronger safeguards for children\n\nColombo, Oct. 1 (Daily Mirror) - Prime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya said ensuring children\u2019s emotional security, mental well-being and self-confidence has become increasingly important due to the social and technological challenges facing childhood, stressing that the Government has made child protection a priority.\n\nSpeaking at the National Children\u2019s Day celebration held at the University of Sri Jayewardenepura today (Oct. 1), under the theme \u201cMentally Strong, Children Empowered,\u201d the Prime Minister said the Government was taking steps to strengthen legal and protective frameworks to ensure children could benefit from digital technology and artificial intelligence while being protected from cyberbullying, harmful content and other online pressures.\n\nShe said reforms were also being introduced to prevent physical punishment and psychological abuse of children and promote disciplinary practices that respect their dignity.\n\nAmarasuriya said measures had also been taken under the Women, Young Persons and Children\u2019s Employment Act to increase the fine for employing children in hazardous work from Rs. 10,000 to Rs. 100,000, as part of efforts to prevent their economic exploitation.\n\nShe further said the Government was working to bring preschool education under a single national standard, introduce registration and regulatory provisions for preschools, and make professional qualifications and training mandatory for preschool teachers.\n\nThe Prime Minister stressed that during the preschool years, greater emphasis should be placed on developing children\u2019s social skills and personalities through sports rather than forcing them to focus primarily on writing letters and numbers.\n\n\u201cEvery child has the right to live safely and with self-confidence, irrespective of language, religion or geographical differences,\u201d she said, adding that ensuring this right was a shared responsibility of the Government, parents, teachers and society."
  },
