    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e7107427cb7c5952cf8c17be057b8271",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "He made these comments in the presence of Guest of Honour Deputy Inspector General of Police (DIG), Financial Crimes Investigation Division (FCID), M. D. P. Dayaratne, as well as CA Sri Lanka\u2019s President Tishan Subasinghe, Vice President Anoji de Silva, Forensic Accounting and Investigation Committee Chairman Ashane Jayasekera among others.\n\n\u201cI am warmly commending CA Sri Lanka for taking this forward-thinking approach to create a platform dedicated to fostering expertise, knowledge sharing, and professional collaboration in Forensic Investigation. May this initiative play an important role in ensuring transparency, accountability, and financial security across Sri Lanka,\u201d the IGP added.\n\nAs part of its inaugural offering, the Faculty will introduce two programs in October 2026: the Certified Professional Forensic Investigator (CPFI) and Digital Forensics for Finance Professionals, both designed to build specialised capabilities in investigating financial crime and addressing the growing complexity of digital evidence.\n\nCA Sri Lanka President Tishan Subasinghe described the launch of the Faculty as one of the Institute\u2019s most significant recent milestones.\n\n\u201cFor decades, our profession has stood as the custodian of financial integrity in this country. Today, that responsibility has grown more complex because fraud no longer confines itself to ledgers and invoices but moves through networks, mobile devices, cloud systems and cross-border transactions more rapidly than controls can contain it. This faculty is our answer to that shift as it brings together the technical, legal, behavioural and ethical disciplines required to investigate financial crime with rigour and credibility, producing professionals capable of standing before a court, a regulator or a tribunal with evidence that holds,\u201d he said."
  },
