    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cFor decades, our profession has stood as the custodian of financial integrity in this country. Today, that responsibility has grown more complex because fraud no longer confines itself to ledgers and invoices but moves through networks, mobile devices, cloud systems and cross-border transactions more rapidly than controls can contain it. This faculty is our answer to that shift as it brings together the technical, legal, behavioural and ethical disciplines required to investigate financial crime with rigour and credibility, producing professionals capable of standing before a court, a regulator or a tribunal with evidence that holds,\u201d he said.\n\nAshane Jayasekera said that the need of the hour is for professionals who can go beyond traditional accounting and auditing.\u00a0 \u201cWe need professionals who can analyse financial statements, identify anomalies, investigate suspected wrongdoing, evaluate evidence, follow the money trail, and communicate their conclusions with clarity and credibility. This is the role of the modern forensic accountant.\u201d\n\nSpeaking about the programs the Faculty offers, he explained that the Certified Professional Forensic Investigator qualification, which is a three-level qualification covering Foundation, Corporate and Professional levels, is designed to develop precisely these capabilities.\n\nHe noted that modern financial investigations increasingly rely on digital evidence found across computers, mobile devices, email systems and cloud platforms. Against this backdrop, the second program, Digital Forensics for Finance Professionals programme will provide practical exposure to the fundamentals of digital forensics, helping bridge the gap between financial investigations and digital forensic capabilities."
