    "chunk_text": "My interest in this subject and writings on account thereof, go back decades. In an article titled Country Risk, Media Freedom and a Role for Civil Society, pursuant to an apparent attack on a high profile private electronic media organisation in Sri Lanka, I wrote in my column, The Thought Leadership Forum, (which had been launced in November 2005) in the then Daily Mirror Financial Times, the predecessor to today\u2019s Daily FT, as follows:\n\nBecause the issue questions a nation\u2019s ability to maintain law and order; reduce or eliminate organised crime; prevent political intimidation; ensure for our people that Sri Lanka is indeed a safe and secure place to live, work and bring up children in. Certain organisations probably have several committees for these and much more- (For example the Law & Order Committee of the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce). These are inherent expectations of Governance in a nation that has pledged to build and maintain a disciplined society\"\n\n\"My expectation of professions and business is that they can, through an increased level of alertness and activism, be an effective deterrent to violations of law and order, political intimidation, organised crime, human rights violations etc., just as much as a structure and system of good corporate governance, with effective audit committees, with or without codes, manuals or check lists, can serve as a deterrent to poor accounting, reporting and disclosure, accounting irregularities, fraud and corruption and so on. An essential potent partner, in the hands of civil society, is a responsible media. Civil Society must then help protect and defend a responsible media rather than only derive benefit from it or simply put \u2013 Be sweet to the media when it suits it\"- End of Extract of Daily Mirror Financial Times, 25, April 2007."
