    "chunk_text": "\"My expectation of professions and business is that they can, through an increased level of alertness and activism, be an effective deterrent to violations of law and order, political intimidation, organised crime, human rights violations etc., just as much as a structure and system of good corporate governance, with effective audit committees, with or without codes, manuals or check lists, can serve as a deterrent to poor accounting, reporting and disclosure, accounting irregularities, fraud and corruption and so on. An essential potent partner, in the hands of civil society, is a responsible media. Civil Society must then help protect and defend a responsible media rather than only derive benefit from it or simply put \u2013 Be sweet to the media when it suits it\"- End of Extract of Daily Mirror Financial Times, 25, April 2007.\n\nPower must have boundaries: The real lesson - institutional design\n\nPower must have boundaries. Rules must be sufficiently clear. Reasons should be capable of scrutiny. Affected parties should receive procedural fairness where the circumstances require it. Independent institutions must be able to exercise their professional judgement, and courts must remain capable of reviewing the exercise of public power. None of these principles requires hostility towards Government. None requires hostility towards the media. They are safeguards precisely because Governments, journalists, regulators, corporations and courts are all human institutions capable of error. The 24 September order is only an interim step. The administration may appeal, and the underlying litigation remains unresolved. The constitutional issues will continue to be tested against the facts, the applicable precedents and the Government's national-security arguments. But the case has already given us an important opportunity to reflect."
