    "md5": "84eb31385e3351723480ab265b418d1c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "World Children\u2019s Day: We cannot say we didn\u2019t know\n\nAcross the country, children will be on stages, in classrooms, at events, in photographs and on social media.\n\nThere will be art exhibitions, concerts, celebrations, gifts, ice cream, special offers, colourful campaigns and beautiful messages about children. And there is nothing wrong with celebrating children.\n\nChildren deserve joy. They deserve celebration. They deserve to laugh, play, sing and simply be children.\n\nChild protection is not the celebration. It is what happens when the celebration is over.\n\nIt is what happens when an eight-year-old tells an adult something that no eight-year-old should ever have to know.\n\nIt is what happens when a child comes to school carrying something far heavier than their schoolbag.\n\nIt is what happens when a child tells us that someone they trusted has hurt them.\n\nAnd I know this because these are not hypothetical situations to us.\n\nThese are children who have told us things that are difficult to repeat, difficult to process and, sometimes, almost impossible to comprehend.\n\nChildren as young as eight are telling us that their 12 year old brother is raping them at home.\n\nChildren as young as 11 are being groomed by teachers to sexual activity and being told \u201cthis is good for you\u201d\n\nChildren are living through nightmares during absolute horrific violence at home at the hands of their caregivers.\n\nChildren as young as 7 years old stating that they are completely addicted to watching sexually explicit content.\n\nChildren as young as 4 who are completely addicted to devices watching adult content.\n\nI will not put the worst of what we hear into a social media post.\n\nBut I wish every adult who speaks about child protection could sit in a room and listen to even one of these children. Because once you have heard them, child protection stops being a campaign.\n\nSri Lanka ratified the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child in 1991. That was 35 years ago."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-10-02-dailyftlk-256dc875",
