    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "28fc055ba0f24ec3c483f27c10669164",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "But I wish every adult who speaks about child protection could sit in a room and listen to even one of these children. Because once you have heard them, child protection stops being a campaign.\n\nSri Lanka ratified the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child in 1991. That was 35 years ago.\n\nAnd yet we still have children who cannot access even the most basic protection they are entitled to.\n\nOur Penal Code, for example, specifically recognises cruelty to children and criminalises wilful assault, ill-treatment, neglect and abandonment of a person under 18 in circumstances likely to cause suffering or injury, including psychological or mental trauma.\n\nBut a law existing on paper is not the same as a child being protected in reality.\n\nThey are asked to repeat what happened over and over and over again.\n\nThey can be exposed to processes that do not sufficiently account for trauma.\n\nAnd sometimes the very systems that are supposed to protect a child can become another part of the child\u2019s ordeal.\n\nThat is where we need to be honest with ourselves. We cannot keep measuring child protection by the number of programs conducted. We cannot measure it by how many children attended an event. We cannot measure it by how many photographs were taken. We cannot measure it by how many pledges were made at a press conference. And we certainly cannot measure it by how impressive our Children\u2019s Day activity report looks at the end of the year.\n\nDid someone listen? Did someone believe them enough to act? Did someone know what to do? Was the child kept safe? Was the child treated with dignity? Did the system move? Did justice become accessible? Did the child receive the support needed to heal?\n\nAnd most importantly, did we prevent another child from experiencing the same thing?\n\nAnd organisations that work in the field of child protection have a responsibility to ensure that their work does not end with a programme, a publication, a pledge or a photograph."
  },
