    "md5": "fdeaa2721d687b3c787c47ad96064c94",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1620,
    "chunk_text": "Child protection in Sri Lanka: Root causes, scale, response, and the case for reform\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s child protection system has built substantial machinery \u2014 school committees, a police bureau, national statistics, a housing program for care-leavers. Yet, vulnerability drivers; poverty, family breakdown, migration, stigma \u2014 go largely unaddressed until they produce a crisis. This article combines the institutional record, current statistics, a Ministry of Women\u2019s Affairs coordination discussion, and IHR\u2019s reform proposal to the Prime Minister, all pointing toward earlier, joined-up intervention.\n\nOnly 18\u201319% of children in residential institutional care have lost both parents; 50% have one living parent and 31% have both living. Institutionalisation is overwhelmingly a response to parental inability to cope, not orphanhood, along a connected chain: economic precarity (single mothers face ~5x the poverty risk) pushes some women into labour migration abroad, leaving children unguarded, and others into sex work, with exposure to violence, arrest and STIs. Rising adolescent HIV/STI rates compound household instability. Stigma against children of unmarried, sex-worker, or HIV-positive mothers excludes them from schools and extended family support. Economically stretched families see more underage pregnancy and exploitation, restarting the cycle a generation down \u2014 surfacing as institutionalisation, while the mother stays invisible to policy and data. No published Sri Lankan data disaggregates children by parental sex-work or HIV/STI status, making targeted services impossible to design."
