    "chunk_text": "Only 18\u201319% of children in residential institutional care have lost both parents; 50% have one living parent and 31% have both living. Institutionalisation is overwhelmingly a response to parental inability to cope, not orphanhood, along a connected chain: economic precarity (single mothers face ~5x the poverty risk) pushes some women into labour migration abroad, leaving children unguarded, and others into sex work, with exposure to violence, arrest and STIs. Rising adolescent HIV/STI rates compound household instability. Stigma against children of unmarried, sex-worker, or HIV-positive mothers excludes them from schools and extended family support. Economically stretched families see more underage pregnancy and exploitation, restarting the cycle a generation down \u2014 surfacing as institutionalisation, while the mother stays invisible to policy and data. No published Sri Lankan data disaggregates children by parental sex-work or HIV/STI status, making targeted services impossible to design.\n\n5,523 complaints Jan.-Jun. 2026 (1,100 cruelty, 213 sexual harassment, 112 cyber violence). Cumulative 2015\u20132025: 108,581 complaints, averaging ~9,800\u201310,800/year.\n\nA June 2026 Health Ministry briefing described a \u201ctriple burden\u201d affecting over one in five schoolchildren: stunting 10.1%, underweight 16.1%, wasting 8.1%, overweight 0.5%. Under-five trends worsened 2021\u21922024: underweight 12.2%\u219217.0%, stunting 7.4%\u219210.5%, wasting 8.2%\u21929.3%, reversing prior decades of improvement (underweight fell from 38% in 1993 to 29% in 2000) \u2014 linked to the 2022 economic crisis. Severe acute malnutrition affects 1.2% of under-fives (~16,000 children); Nuwara Eliya is worst-affected district (24.6% underweight); tea-estate children show ~1 in 3 underweight/stunted versus ~1 in 10 in urban areas. 10% of children lived in poverty as of June 2023."
