    "chunk_size_bytes": 1893,
    "chunk_text": "A June 2026 Health Ministry briefing described a \u201ctriple burden\u201d affecting over one in five schoolchildren: stunting 10.1%, underweight 16.1%, wasting 8.1%, overweight 0.5%. Under-five trends worsened 2021\u21922024: underweight 12.2%\u219217.0%, stunting 7.4%\u219210.5%, wasting 8.2%\u21929.3%, reversing prior decades of improvement (underweight fell from 38% in 1993 to 29% in 2000) \u2014 linked to the 2022 economic crisis. Severe acute malnutrition affects 1.2% of under-fives (~16,000 children); Nuwara Eliya is worst-affected district (24.6% underweight); tea-estate children show ~1 in 3 underweight/stunted versus ~1 in 10 in urban areas. 10% of children lived in poverty as of June 2023.\n\nThe Student Ambassador National Program (NCPA/Education Ministry) trains senior prefects, plus a university strand, in child protection and ethical leadership across Government, private and international schools and pirivens. Surakum Pawwa aims to place tripartite (parent-teacher-child) committees in all 9,700 government schools, backed by community \u201cJana Pavura\u201d bodies (Grama Niladhari, Public Health Nurse, five civil-society representatives). A defined complaint pipeline follows: police investigate within 48 hours, an NCPA Field Officer within 14 days, with video evidence and psychosocial support. Rollout has lagged \u2014 full coverage was still a forward plan in 2020, a decade after launch.\n\nThe Bureau for the Prevention of Abuse of Children and Women runs a 24-hour desk (Emergency 109; Hotline 011-2444444) with ~300 district child protection officers; CID investigates, the AG\u2019s Department prosecutes, and the Children and Women\u2019s Bureau enforces child-labour law. A 2024 International Reporting Portal lets the public report online CSAM. Police note home-front conflict pushes children toward early relationships, and that victims/guardians often avoid pursuing protection orders."
