    "chunk_text": "The Bureau for the Prevention of Abuse of Children and Women runs a 24-hour desk (Emergency 109; Hotline 011-2444444) with ~300 district child protection officers; CID investigates, the AG\u2019s Department prosecutes, and the Children and Women\u2019s Bureau enforces child-labour law. A 2024 International Reporting Portal lets the public report online CSAM. Police note home-front conflict pushes children toward early relationships, and that victims/guardians often avoid pursuing protection orders.\n\nOn 17 September 2026, police warned that creating, possessing, distributing or viewing AI-generated CSAM is a criminal offence regardless of whether a real child was involved, driven by easy-to-use AI tools; detection is hampered by encrypted apps and dark-web channels, prompting international cooperation and penalties including imprisonment. This extends NCPA\u2019s traditional contact-abuse focus to AI-specific risks, consistent with rising cyberbullying/counselling figures in Section 3.1.\n\n4,421 abuse cases remain pending, coordinated across 47 Police Child and Women Protection Bureaus; nine provincial video evidence recording units are under discussion to spare children court appearances.\n\nNCPA\u2019s staffing gap (284/618 vacant) and the decade-long SCPC rollout lag show design has outpaced field capacity."
