    "chunk_size_bytes": 2020,
    "chunk_text": "4,421 abuse cases remain pending, coordinated across 47 Police Child and Women Protection Bureaus; nine provincial video evidence recording units are under discussion to spare children court appearances.\n\nNCPA\u2019s staffing gap (284/618 vacant) and the decade-long SCPC rollout lag show design has outpaced field capacity.\n\nIHR Chairman Jeevan Thiagarajah wrote to PM Dr. Harini Amarasuriya (3 July 2026) arguing that fragmented agencies \u2014 clinics, police, children\u2019s homes, Probation and Childcare Services \u2014 each see only one piece of a family\u2019s situation, so intervention comes only after breakdown. Proposed reforms, under a standing multi-disciplinary authority:\n\nA national automatic multi-agency referral registry (under Probation and Childcare Services), triggered by clinic pregnancies, police reports involving minors, or care-home admissions.\n\nNamed case coordinators at District/Divisional level, reporting to the registry.\n\nTargeted cash/livelihood support for at-risk single mothers via Samurdhi/Divisional Secretariat infrastructure.\n\nConfidential district-level services for sex workers and STI-affected parents, linked to the registry.\n\nA recognised child-led early-warning network (schools, youth clubs) as a fifth trigger \u2014 children as sentinels only, never handling case files.\n\nA standing multi-disciplinary cluster (Health, Police, Probation, Education, Women and Child Affairs, Provincial Councils, civil society) empowered to act on cases raised.\n\nJoint implementation circulars within the financial year, with benchmarks cascading to town level.\n\nThe letter does not seek to reopen sex-work legalisation, framing itself narrowly around families already living this reality. It cites Sri Lanka\u2019s 2019 removal of marital status from birth certificates as a precedent for tackling stigma directly.\n\nCommon thread: any state contact should automatically register a person of concern into a shared case file and trigger family-level support, not just single-agency or removal-point action."
