    "num": "dailymirrorlk-876ec96f",
    "date_str": "2026-10-02",
    "description": "US troops leave Iraq: A sovereignty party without sovereignty",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/US-troops-leave-Iraq-A-sovereignty-party-without-sovereignty/172-351575",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790916820.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-876ec96f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fe437fcb3a2313e09c2263800124f3e9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "US troops leave Iraq: A sovereignty party without sovereignty\n\nThere was celebration in Iraq as the government declared Sovereignty Day and a two-day national holiday after the last American troops left the country on Wednesday, marking the second such withdrawal in 23 years.\n\nUS President Donald Trump hailed it as the conclusion of \u201ca very expensive excursion into Hell\u201d and a victory for both countries.\n\nHowever, the occasion was also a time for reflection on Iraq\u2019s lost sovereignty during the US occupation, an experience not dissimilar to the loss of sovereignty under British rule. Like the United States, Britain had two episodes in Iraq: it left in 1932, only to return in 1941 during World War II, before departing again in 1947, while continuing to prop up a puppet monarchy until a coup brought it to an end.\n\nThe casus belli for the US invasion of Iraq in 2003 was ostensibly Iraq\u2019s non-existent weapons of mass destruction (WMDs). Not a single WMD was found during the US occupation, which was declared illegal by then UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan. But does the US care about the illegality of its actions or its violations of international law? Its record is appallingly poor. No amount of shaming can shake its conscience.\n\nThe occupation was made possible by a web of deception. Iraq, which remained secular under the tyrannical rule of Saddam Hussein, had nothing to do with the 9/11 attacks or with Islamic radical groups, including Al-Qaeda. Yet a majority of Americans believed otherwise. President George W. Bush was reportedly preoccupied with plans for Iraq from the outset of his presidency. It is said that when the 9/11 attacks occurred, he wanted to strike Iraq immediately, and that only after much persuasion did he agree to invade Afghanistan first, thereby setting in motion the Project for the New American Century\u2019s plan to militarily dominate the world and control its resources."
  },
