    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "The occupation was made possible by a web of deception. Iraq, which remained secular under the tyrannical rule of Saddam Hussein, had nothing to do with the 9/11 attacks or with Islamic radical groups, including Al-Qaeda. Yet a majority of Americans believed otherwise. President George W. Bush was reportedly preoccupied with plans for Iraq from the outset of his presidency. It is said that when the 9/11 attacks occurred, he wanted to strike Iraq immediately, and that only after much persuasion did he agree to invade Afghanistan first, thereby setting in motion the Project for the New American Century\u2019s plan to militarily dominate the world and control its resources.\n\nThose who challenged or exposed these falsehoods were hounded. In the United States, officials who disputed the administration\u2019s narrative were victimised. In Britain, a senior government scientist was found dead in a wooded area days after questioning Blair\u2019s claims in a BBC interview. The official verdict was suicide. Yet in the deadly world of politics, a suicide can also mean a murder.\n\nThe war was deadly. No sooner had the ground invasion begun, with support from the Kurds in the north and the Shiites in the south, than the Iraqi Army was in tatters. According to some accounts, more than a million Iraqis perished as a result of the war. Allegations of war crimes against the US-led coalition forces followed.\n\nAs Iraqis celebrate the departure of US troops, they remember Abu Ghraib prison, where detainees were photographed naked, sexually abused and humiliated. They remember Fallujah, where resistance to the US occupation began when Iraqi parents were reportedly massacred for demanding that American troops vacate their children\u2019s schools. They also remember children born with severe deformities that research attributed to the use of depleted uranium munitions by US forces."
  },
