    "chunk_text": "As Iraqis celebrate the departure of US troops, they remember Abu Ghraib prison, where detainees were photographed naked, sexually abused and humiliated. They remember Fallujah, where resistance to the US occupation began when Iraqi parents were reportedly massacred for demanding that American troops vacate their children\u2019s schools. They also remember children born with severe deformities that research attributed to the use of depleted uranium munitions by US forces.\n\nA war within a war emerged. Iraqis fought Iraqis. Iran backed the Shiites, the Gulf countries the Sunnis; and Israel the Kurds. Israel favoured the Balkanisation of Iraq, or at least the creation of an independent Kurdistan. That plan failed because Turkiye opposed it. Instead, an autonomous Kurdistan Regional Government, complete with its own president and prime minister, was established under a federal arrangement that many regarded as an erosion of Iraq\u2019s sovereignty.\n\nAs sectarianism dominated the headlines, the economic plunder of the country continued. US oil giant Kellogg Brown & Root was among the principal beneficiaries, securing contracts worth $39.5 billion. Bechtel, ExxonMobil and Chevron also dipped into the honeypot. So too did Blackwater, a security company that faced numerous allegations of war crimes. Arms manufacturers such as General Electric, Lockheed Martin and Northrop Grumman also made a killing. War was business then, as it is now, and as it always will be.\n\nIn 2005, Iraqis experienced democratic elections for the first time since 1958. The United States was compelled to hold elections as it had craftily shifted its justification for the war from the search for WMDs to the promotion of democracy in a nation ruled by a tyrant.\n\nShiites assumed power for the first time since the creation of Iraq in 1920\u2014following the Ottoman Empire\u2019s defeat in World War I\u2014under a parachuted monarch, King Faisal, a British acolyte imported from Hejaz, which is now under Saudi Arabia."
