    "chunk_size_bytes": 1210,
    "chunk_text": "Shiites assumed power for the first time since the creation of Iraq in 1920\u2014following the Ottoman Empire\u2019s defeat in World War I\u2014under a parachuted monarch, King Faisal, a British acolyte imported from Hejaz, which is now under Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe ISIS was widely viewed as a byproduct of the US occupation and was nurtured by a network of Western and regional intelligence agencies working to topple Bashar al-Assad\u2019s regime in Syria, a key ally of Iran. ISIS, which had become a law unto itself, much like Frankenstein\u2019s monster, disobeyed its masters, established its own caliphate and unleashed terror on Iraq\u2019s Yazidi community, raping women and killing men. The US was forced to intervene, largely through an air campaign, while Iraqi forces backed by pro-Iranian militias fought the ground war.\n\nThe 2003 US invasion was originally dubbed Operation Iraqi Liberation but was subsequently renamed Operation Iraqi Freedom because the acronym of the former, OIL, let the cat out of the bag.\n\nAs Iraqis celebrated Sovereignty Day, the question remains whether Iraq has truly regained its sovereignty. Far from it. Washington still exerts considerable influence over Iraq through economic pressure."
