    "md5": "aa9c8cc2110e53e79139891bca45fb19",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1520,
    "chunk_text": "Investment climate: Sri Lanka cannot afford mixed signals\n\nSri Lanka has spent the past few years trying to rebuild an economy battered by the worst crisis in its post-independence history. The country has stabilised, growth has returned and the Government has remained committed to the International Monetary Fund programme. Yet, the latest U.S. assessment of Sri Lanka\u2019s investment climate carries an important message: Economic recovery alone will not be enough to convince investors that Sri Lanka has become a predictable place to do business.\n\nThe U.S. Department of State, in its 2026 Investment Climate Statement on Sri Lanka, recognises the political stability that followed the sweeping electoral victories of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake and the National People\u2019s Power Government in 2024.\n\nForeign investment is ultimately about confidence. Investors commit billions of dollars not merely because a country has attractive locations, a skilled workforce or strategic geography. They need to know that the rules under which they make an investment today will not be substantially altered tomorrow, that taxes will be predictable and government agencies will operate according to transparent procedures.\n\nSri Lanka has struggled with these issues for decades. The problem, therefore, predates the present administration. The 2024 U.S. assessment similarly identified inconsistent foreign investment policies, governance problems, bureaucratic delays and weak transparency as impediments to investment."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
