    "num": "dailymirrorlk-bc35b954",
    "date_str": "2026-10-02",
    "description": "Investment climate: Sri Lanka cannot afford mixed signals",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Investment-climate-Sri-Lanka-cannot-afford-mixed-signals/172-351577",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790917020.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-bc35b954-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "32001f9de6593f25ed831c044aac7c3c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1892,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has struggled with these issues for decades. The problem, therefore, predates the present administration. The 2024 U.S. assessment similarly identified inconsistent foreign investment policies, governance problems, bureaucratic delays and weak transparency as impediments to investment.\n\nThe country cannot expect to transform itself into a competitive investment destination while investors have to navigate layers of approvals across multiple institutions. Nor can Sri Lanka aspire to become a regional production and logistics hub if access to land, customs procedures, taxation and regulatory approvals remain difficult or unpredictable.\n\nThe Government has a particularly important opportunity here. Its political mandate was built partly around the promise of cleaner governance and an end to the practices associated with the old political culture. The administration has also publicly promoted foreign investment while maintaining 100 per cent foreign ownership in most sectors.\n\nThe report\u2019s reference to major projects that have stalled or been withdrawn should therefore not be viewed merely through the prism of individual investment decisions. The cases involving the proposed Sinopec refinery and the Adani wind-power project have become examples of the broader question of whether Sri Lanka can provide investors with sufficient certainty once a project enters the complicated stages of negotiation, approval and implementation.\n\nAt the same time, Sri Lanka should not interpret every failed negotiation as proof that the investor must always be accommodated. A sovereign government has every right to protect the national interest, renegotiate terms where necessary and ensure that public resources are not committed under unfavourable conditions. The real test is whether such decisions are taken through transparent, consistent and clearly understood procedures."
  },
