    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-bc35b954-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8663966329a9de40c17f22057f96a8b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1202,
    "chunk_text": "At the same time, Sri Lanka should not interpret every failed negotiation as proof that the investor must always be accommodated. A sovereign government has every right to protect the national interest, renegotiate terms where necessary and ensure that public resources are not committed under unfavourable conditions. The real test is whether such decisions are taken through transparent, consistent and clearly understood procedures.\n\nSri Lanka is therefore facing a credibility test. The next phase of economic recovery must be about building institutions that make investment decisions easier, faster and more predictable. Cutting unnecessary approvals, improving inter-agency coordination, ensuring transparent procurement, strengthening contract enforcement and maintaining policy continuity would do more for the investment climate than more\u00a0 investment promotion campaigns.\n\nThe Government should consequently treat the U.S. report as an opportunity for introspection rather than confrontation. Sri Lanka has repeatedly announced ambitious FDI targets. The harder task is creating an environment in which investors voluntarily choose to put their money here and, more importantly, reinvest it."
