    "md5": "58f72e9f0b2bc92dfca7618158ad4279",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "The CJP called the protest after last week's report in The Indian Express that CEC Kumar's two fellow election commissioners, Sukhbir Singh Sandhu and Vivek Joshi, had formally objected at least 14 times over 10 months to decisions and orders they said had been issued without their knowledge.\n\nThe Election Commission says the exercise is intended to remove duplicate and ineligible entries - and so far, 130 million names have been struck off the voters' list.\n\nBut opposition parties and activists have alleged that many of those are eligible voters and that the process is being manipulated by CEC Kumar to favour Prime Minister Narendra Modi's Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) during elections. Both the BJP and the Election Commission have rejected the accusations.\n\nLast week's Indian Express report said the two commissioners had also been raising questions over the integrity of the voter database, changes to the way new voters are enrolled and the security of the electoral rolls itself.\n\nThe EC defended its way of working and said in a statement that \"differing views\" were part of the \"decision-making process\" and that all the decisions were taken unanimously.\n\nBut the revelations sparked a furore, with opposition leaders demanding Kumar's resignation.\n\nLeader of the opposition and senior Congress party leader Rahul Gandhi has repeatedly accused the commission of \"stealing votes from the opposition\" and said those officers who were indulging in \"vote theft\" were \"traitors\".\n\nAnother senior opposition leader, Mamata Banerjee, has demanded the CEC's arrest.\n\nOn Wednesday, the INDIA alliance - an opposition grouping that includes nearly two dozen parties and has both Gandhi and Banerjee as members - said they would also organise protest rallies across the country in the coming days. On 6 October, all opposition MPs plan to march to the Election Commission office in Delhi, they added.\n\nMany of the opposition parties have also pledged support to the protest in Mumbai."
