    "language": "en",
    "md5": "77d6e66563521a92fac80834faeea68b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1025,
    "chunk_text": "The CPPTOA Chairman stressed that fuel distribution was an essential service and said tanker owners had continued operating despite mounting costs in an effort to prevent disruptions to the country's fuel supply.\n\nHe warned that if the dispute was not resolved, fuel deliveries to filling stations could be halted across the country from tomorrow.\n\nAccording to Adhikari, several private fuel bowsers had already stopped transporting fuel, which he said had contributed to queues at a number of filling stations.\n\nThe Association is expected to decide on further action depending on the outcome of today's(2) discussions with the CPC Chairman.\n\nMeanwhile, Adhikari also referred to the recent fuel supply situation, claiming that concerns over a possible fuel price increase had resulted in delays in placing fuel orders and contributed to shortages experienced in some areas.\n\nFuel crisis looms Friday as tanker owners threaten to stop deliveries\n\nCPC still incurs losses on diesel, petrol despite price increase and subsidy"
