    "description": "Gunda Power backs proposed night-time tariff for Sri Lanka\u2019s solar sector",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Gunda-Power-backs-proposed-night-time-tariff-for-Sri-Lankas-solar-sector/215-351596",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790923824.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-6cb67564-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "085bddf3b519ed759c51ddedb597af8e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "Gunda Power backs proposed night-time tariff for Sri Lanka\u2019s solar sector\n\nGunda Power Director Tracy Cao, Solis Country Manager Mark Gong and Solis Pakistan General Manager Aftersales and Operations Hamza Sajid\n\nSays hybrid battery storage could let solar customers supply power to grid after dark, not just during daylight\n\nGunda Power has welcomed Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed night-time feed-in tariff, calling it a potential turning point for the country\u2019s solar sector.\n\nThe company says the policy could let rooftop solar move beyond daytime generation into round-the-clock energy delivery.\n\nRooftop solar has let homes and businesses cut costs and feed clean power into the grid but the demand peaks after sunset, when the panels stop producing. Meeting that gap currently means leaning on imported fossil fuels, adding pressure to the energy costs and foreign exchange reserves.\n\nA night-time feed-in tariff would let the stored solar energy be released when demand is the highest. Gunda Power argues this shifts solar from simple generation to active energy management.\n\nHybrid systems, which combine solar generation with battery storage and intelligent energy management, are central to that shift. Excess daytime energy can be stored for later use or under the right regulatory framework, sent to the grid during peak hours.\n\nAt a recent Solis Storage seminar, the Solis team presented EverCore ESS, an integrated inverter and battery storage solution. The installers and customers examined its safety, reliability and backup capabilities.\n\n\u201cThe question could shift from asking how much a solar system can save, to how much it can generate, store and deliver,\u201d said Gunda Power Managing Director Nirash Goonewardena.\n\n\u201cThat is a fundamental change in how we think about renewable energy.\u201d\n\nGoonewardena said success depends on a regulatory framework that is practical, transparent and commercially sustainable, with clear standards for metering, grid connection, safety and payment."
  },
