    "md5": "da5bde729017fafd11db7a413fb4bb36",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "Lead agencies convene to accelerate implementation of National Export Development Plan 2026\u20132030\n\nThe Sri Lanka Export Development Board (EDB), in collaboration with the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development, convened a coordination meeting with the Lead Implementing Agencies of the National Export Development Plan (NEDP) 2026\u20132030 this week, at the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development.\n\nThe meeting brought together representatives of key government institutions designated to lead and support the implementation of the NEDP 2026\u20132030, with the objective of strengthening inter-agency coordination and ensuring the effective and timely implementation of the Plan.\n\nThe session followed by remarks by the Secretary, Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development, who highlighted the importance of coordinated action among government institutions in translating the national export development agenda into tangible outcomes.\n\nThe Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of the EDB delivered a comprehensive presentation on the NEDP 2026\u20132030, outlining its strategic direction, key objectives, priority sectors and implementation framework. The presentation also highlighted the roles and responsibilities of Lead Implementing Agencies in advancing the initiatives identified under the Plan.\n\nThe NEDP 2026\u20132030 provides a national framework to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s export competitiveness, diversify export products and markets, enhance trade facilitation and logistics, improve access to trade finance and investment, strengthen quality and standards, and promote innovation, skills development and sustainable export growth.\n\nThe Plan has set an ambitious target of increasing Sri Lanka\u2019s exports to approximately US$36 Bn by 2030, comprising merchandise and services exports. Achieving this target requires coordinated implementation across government institutions, the private sector and other stakeholders."
  },
