    "md5": "d5d86400490453cd237009d2579b27e6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "SL must take disability hiring out of CSR box to catch up: HR expert\n\nSri Lanka must move disability employment out of the corporate social responsibility (CSR) and welfare box and onto an economic agenda if persons with disabilities are to secure dignified and productive work, with the country still lagging on workplace access, DFCC Bank Chief Human Resources Officer Padmasiri Senanayake urged.\n\nAddressing a session on partnerships for social inclusion and equity at the 49th Annual Meeting of the Association of Development Financing Institutions in Asia and the Pacific (ADFIAP), hosted by DFCC Bank in Colombo this week, Senanayake argued that employability, more than access to credit, offers the most sustainable route to inclusion for women, youth and persons with disabilities. \u201cPersons with disability, if I take Sri Lanka, we are still a bit backward in this area and the journey is much further,\u201d he told delegates, observing that support for the community varies widely across Asia.\n\nAdvocata Institute Chief Executive Officer Dhananath Fernando told an earlier session that 1.8 million young people would join the labour force over the next few years. Senanayake estimated that around 45 percent of them would be women and 4 to 5 percent persons with disabilities. That would place between 72,000 and 90,000 persons with disabilities in the cohort, and the question he raised was how many of them are actually employable. His estimate sits just above the national picture. As per DCS census data for 2024, 727,293 people aged five and above were identified as having significant disabilities, a national rate of 35 per 1,000, or 3.5 percent. A wider group of 3,223,413, or 157 per 1,000, reported at least one physical or mental difficulty. Sri Lanka enacted the Protection of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act No. 28 of 1996 and ratified the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities in February 2016."
  },
