    "md5": "2cca51dfaee52b13f525f84c1dd83d51",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "Advocata Institute Chief Executive Officer Dhananath Fernando told an earlier session that 1.8 million young people would join the labour force over the next few years. Senanayake estimated that around 45 percent of them would be women and 4 to 5 percent persons with disabilities. That would place between 72,000 and 90,000 persons with disabilities in the cohort, and the question he raised was how many of them are actually employable. His estimate sits just above the national picture. As per DCS census data for 2024, 727,293 people aged five and above were identified as having significant disabilities, a national rate of 35 per 1,000, or 3.5 percent. A wider group of 3,223,413, or 157 per 1,000, reported at least one physical or mental difficulty. Sri Lanka enacted the Protection of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act No. 28 of 1996 and ratified the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities in February 2016.\n\nSenanayake traced the gap to corporate mindsets, putting the question directly to the audience. \u201cIf I ask any of you in this audience under which agenda would your employing of the disabled come into play, in most cases you would say it might be a CSR agenda or it might be a welfare agenda,\u201d he remarked.\n\nHiring under such an agenda, according to him, becomes a numbers exercise. \u201cRight now, being a welfare agenda, we look at how we bring in somebody and there are percentages of staff given under the CSR agenda, so there\u2019s no major value addition which comes in,\u201d he observed.\n\n\u201cThe day the country or an organisation moves from a welfare agenda to an economic agenda, our focus on building these areas would be much stronger,\u201d he stressed, adding that the shift would begin breaking down barriers within organisations and across the country."
  },
  {
