    "md5": "af1048f0b75f461a2ec4f12130af67da",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe day the country or an organisation moves from a welfare agenda to an economic agenda, our focus on building these areas would be much stronger,\u201d he stressed, adding that the shift would begin breaking down barriers within organisations and across the country.\n\nAssistive technology, in his experience, has already shown what is possible. \u201cThere are organisations I\u2019ve seen and worked in where even the blind community have different technology which enables them to provide a lot of value to the organisation,\u201d he recalled. \u201cSo the priority has to be access, opportunity and the chance to contribute.\u201d\n\nHowever, he cautioned that attitudes remain as formidable a barrier as infrastructure. \u201cIf you look at the elephant in the room, it is the element of unconscious bias. We start stereotyping people,\u201d he warned. In his view, true inclusion rests on whether marginalised groups feel accepted, connected and valued, and whether they get a fair chance to progress.\n\nJobs and talent both exist, he noted, yet the two are poorly matched, since many from under-represented groups lack the education, skills and avenues to become employable. With most employment concentrated in urban centres, he reasoned that building those skills would also strengthen rural communities. The resulting independence and earnings would, in turn, deliver the financial inclusion development banks pursue.\n\nSenanayake called on development finance institutions (DFIs) to look beyond concessionary lending and fund the education and skill-building channels that create such talent. \u201cIt is very important that we also support those organisations to move this agenda from a social or a charity-based or welfare-based agenda to an economic agenda,\u201d he insisted."
