    "description": "Melwa readies Rs.6bn Seeduwa cement plant, secures key ticket to European steel market",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Melwa-readies-Rs-6bn-Seeduwa-cement-plant-secures-key-ticket-to-European-steel-market/215-351612",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790925451.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-7407d93d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fe7cbd36f52c9976f11edceb465b5ad1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "Melwa readies Rs.6bn Seeduwa cement plant, secures key ticket to European steel market\n\nMelwa Group\u2019s Rs.6bn cement plant in Seeduwa is 90 percent complete, with first bags due within two to three months.\n\nThe plant can produce around 30,000 bags a day, and the company expects to recover its investment in about five years.\n\nMelwa\u2019s steel arm has secured CARES certification, which opens the door to European exports.\n\nMelwa Group will begin rolling out cement from its Rs.6 billion grinding and blending plant in Seeduwa within the next two to three months, extending its building materials portfolio beyond steel as it positions itself for a pipeline of state infrastructure projects, company officials said.\n\nAccording to Melwa Cement Chief Operations Manager Chinthaka Thuduwewatta, civil structures at the plant have been completed, leaving only the remaining mechanical, process and laboratory work before production begins.\n\nThe company expects to recover the investment within about five years, which implies average annual returns in the region of Rs.1.2 billion. However, officials declined to disclose how the project was financed, noting that the matter rested with top management.\n\nDaily output is expected to be around 30,000 bags, depending on the product mix. At 50 kg a bag, this works out to roughly 1,500 tonnes a day, or close to 550,000 tonnes over a full year. That would be equivalent to nearly 12 percent of the 4.71 million tonnes of domestic cement demand reported by Tokyo Cement last year.\n\nUnlike integrated cement plants, the Seeduwa facility will neither produce clinker nor operate a kiln.\n\nRaw materials imported in containers through the Colombo Port will be stored, proportioned into a mixer, separated by particle size and held in three vertical silos before being packed. Thuduwewatta explained that the plant handles only the final stage of the conventional cement process, which accounts for about 20 percent of the full cycle."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-7407d93d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7407d93d",
    "date_str": "2026-10-02",
    "description": "Melwa readies Rs.6bn Seeduwa cement plant, secures key ticket to European steel market",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Melwa-readies-Rs-6bn-Seeduwa-cement-plant-secures-key-ticket-to-European-steel-market/215-351612",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1790925451.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-10-02-dailymirrorlk-7407d93d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5cac6c8fe3fa439055dd325f309b95c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "Raw materials imported in containers through the Colombo Port will be stored, proportioned into a mixer, separated by particle size and held in three vertical silos before being packed. Thuduwewatta explained that the plant handles only the final stage of the conventional cement process, which accounts for about 20 percent of the full cycle.\n\nIn the initial phase, the company plans to release three product categories, including blended hydraulic cement under SLS 1247 and Portland composite cement under SLS 1697. These will be sold both in 50 kg bags and in bulk through bowsers of 40 to 60 tonnes. The process is aligned with ISO 14001 and ISO 9001, while the plant has been designed to European best available standards with a working life of around 40 years in mind.\n\nThe site was chosen mainly for its proximity to the Colombo Port and the Katunayake Expressway, which the company believes will considerably ease the movement of its imported materials.\n\nMelwa Group Head of Sales and Brand Malik Kannangara was asked why the company was entering a market already dominated by established international players. In response, he drew a parallel with the group\u2019s steel business.\n\n\u201cWhen we came to the market in 2002, there were so many players who had been running for over 40 to 50 years. But within a short period, when we came out with our 2018 steel mill, we became Sri Lanka\u2019s market leader and the biggest manufacturer,\u201d he recalled.\n\nKannangara sees demand building on the back of the government\u2019s move to award tenders to local construction companies. He pointed to projects such as the Kandy tunnel, multimodal transport hubs, water projects and the long-awaited Central Expressway, as well as the group\u2019s own construction requirements at Port City Colombo.\n\n\u201cThe opportunities are coming. The industry is getting opportunities as well as we are getting them. So we are ready for that, and this is something targeting all these projects,\u201d he stressed."
  },
