    "chunk_size_bytes": 2035,
    "chunk_text": "Easing sovereign risks lift BOC\u2019s credit rating\n\nFitch Ratings has upgraded Bank of Ceylon\u2019s (BOC) long-term foreign- and local-currency ratings to \u2018B-\u2019 from \u2018CCC+\u2019, with a Stable Outlook, citing easing sovereign risks and stronger prospects for Sri Lanka\u2019s largest bank.\n\nThe October 1 rating action follows Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign upgrade to \u2018B-\u2019 with a Stable Outlook on September 22, reflecting the close link between the bank\u2019s credit strength and the government\u2019s financial position.\n\nFitch also raised BOC\u2019s Viability Rating, which assesses its standalone credit profile, to \u2018b-\u2019 from \u2018ccc+\u2019, and its Short-Term Issuer Default Rating to \u2018B\u2019 from \u2018C\u2019.\n\nThe bank\u2019s National Long-Term Rating of \u2018AA-(lka)\u2019 with a Stable Outlook was not considered in the review.\n\nFitch said structural reforms had eased Sri Lanka\u2019s external financing risks and improved the economy\u2019s resilience to shocks, supporting greater financial-market stability and the bank\u2019s business and financial prospects.\n\n\u201cThe sovereign credit profile is a key constraint on the bank\u2019s ratings,\u201d the agency said.\n\nHowever, BOC\u2019s substantial exposure to the state remains central to its risk profile. Fitch estimated that sovereign bonds and loans to state-owned entities accounted for about 60 percent of the bank\u2019s total assets at the end of the first quarter of 2026, including Treasury securities held for liquidity purposes.\n\nThe improved sovereign credit profile prompted Fitch to raise its assessments of BOC\u2019s risk profile, asset quality and capitalisation, reflecting reduced risks from these exposures. It also expects credit risks outside the sovereign portfolio to ease as stronger economic conditions feed through to borrowers.\n\nBOC\u2019s market position remained a rating strength. The bank accounts for around 22 percent of banking-sector assets and deposits, while its extensive branch network and deposit franchise provide an advantage in generating business at acceptable risk, Fitch said."
