    "md5": "26d8b75af9621fab0fef825ea6dec4ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "Nearly 10,000 snakebites reported annually in Sri Lanka\n\nColombo, Oct. 2 (Daily Mirror) - Nearly 10,000 snakebite cases are reported in Sri Lanka each year, with hundreds of people losing their lives, as local and international experts gathered in Kandy yesterday for the world\u2019s first international conference dedicated to snakes.\n\nThe \u201c1st World Congress on Snakes \u2013 2026,\u201d held under the theme \u201cSnakes, Science, Species Survival & Sri Lanka,\u201d commenced yesterday at The Grand Kandyan Hotel with the participation of more than 400 scholars, researchers, environmentalists and field experts representing 42 countries.\n\nSnakebite and its impact on human health are among the key issues being addressed at the four-day congress.\n\nGlobally, approximately 138,000 people die each year from snakebites, while many survivors suffer permanent disabilities, including limb loss, disfigurement, kidney failure and severe psychological trauma.\n\nSri Lanka also faces a significant snakebite burden, with nearly 10,000 cases reported annually. A considerable number involve envenomation, while hundreds of victims die as a result.\n\nHowever, experts at the congress are also highlighting an important distinction: the vast majority of Sri Lanka\u2019s snake species are harmless to humans, with only a very small proportion posing a serious medical threat.\n\nThe issue is particularly significant as Sri Lanka is home to 112 of the 411 snake species recorded worldwide. Of these, 63 species are endemic to the island.\n\nAt the same time, snakes face growing threats from habitat loss and fragmentation, agricultural expansion, urbanisation and climate change.\n\nRecent extreme weather situations have also increased encounters between wildlife and human communities, potentially raising the likelihood of human-snake encounters.\n\nThe four-day congress aims to bring together global expertise on snake biology, diversity, conservation and medical research, while examining possible future measures based on scientific data."
