    "md5": "faddb6e5dc7f86dd04a3d317d1017ef8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s trade gap hits US$7.2bn\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s import bill grew more than seven times as fast as merchandise export earnings in the first eight months of 2026, widening the trade deficit to US$ 7.18 billion as higher fuel costs increased pressure on the country\u2019s external finances.\n\nImports rose 24.1 percent to US$ 16.56 billion during January-August, while merchandise exports increased 3.3 percent to US$ 9.38 billion, according to the Central Bank\u2019s latest external sector report.\n\nThe mismatch added US$ 2.92 billion to the trade deficit compared with the corresponding period of 2025, when the gap stood at US$ 4.26 billion. Stronger workers\u2019 remittances helped cushion the deterioration, although weaker tourism earnings and a smaller services surplus reduced support from other foreign exchange sources.\n\n\u201cThe current account recorded a cumulative deficit of US$ 291 million during January-August 2026, reflecting pressures on the external sector stemming from the escalation of the Middle East conflict,\u201d the Central Bank said.\n\nThe eight-month current account deficit compared with a surplus of US$ 2.05 billion a year earlier, marking a substantial reversal in the balance of the country\u2019s trade, services, income and transfer flows.\n\nFuel accounted for a sizeable part of the increase in import expenditure. The January-August fuel bill rose 61.6 percent year-on-year to approximately US$ 4 billion, despite monthly expenditure declining for the fourth consecutive month in August.\n\nVehicle imports totalled a further US$1.68 billion over the eight months. Spending on personal and commercial vehicle imports fell 24.2 percent year-on-year to US$189 million in August.\n\nThe Central Bank said the terms of trade deteriorated in both August and the first eight months, as import prices increased faster than export prices."
  },
