    "chunk_text": "Vehicle imports totalled a further US$1.68 billion over the eight months. Spending on personal and commercial vehicle imports fell 24.2 percent year-on-year to US$189 million in August.\n\nThe Central Bank said the terms of trade deteriorated in both August and the first eight months, as import prices increased faster than export prices.\n\nHowever, the monthly trade gap remained wider than a year earlier. Merchandise imports rose 12.8 percent to US$ 1.91 billion in August, while exports fell 3.3 percent to US$ 1.24 billion, leaving a deficit of US$ 673.5 million against US$ 414.2 million in August 2025.\n\nGrowth in combined goods and services exports was also modest. Total receipts increased 1.5 percent to US$ 14.08 billion during January-August, with services earnings declining 1.8 percent to US$ 4.70 billion.\n\nThe services account surplus contracted 21.4 percent to US$ 2.10 billion as receipts weakened and payments overseas rose 22.9 percent to US$ 2.60 billion.\n\nTourism earnings fell 10 percent to US$2.06 billion during the eight months, while arrivals declined 2 percent to 1.54 million. The earnings estimates reflect the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority\u2019s revised methodology, introduced in May and applied retrospectively from January 2026.\n\nComputer and IT/BPO services receipts declined 16.5 percent to US$ 459.9 million over the same period, including a 56.3 percent year-on-year fall in August to US$ 31.4 million. The report did not explain the decline.\n\nSea and air transport receipts increased 4.6 percent to US$ 1.33 billion during January-August, providing some support to services earnings.\n\nOn the expenditure side, spending on travel abroad rose 62.1 percent to US$ 815.2 million, while payments for sea and air transport services increased 20.2 percent to US$ 800.1 million."
