    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f9436825a8d97da82d7d28332ea565d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Customs cuts paper trail in push for faster trade\n\nAbout 1,144 importers have registered for the system, according to the official announcement\n\nUnder the new requirements, paperless declarations at Customs\u2019 Long Room are mandatory, with importers and declarants required to submit declarations and supporting documents bearing valid digital signatures\n\nSri Lanka began cutting the paper trail at its borders yesterday, launching digitally signed customs declarations in a bid to speed up cargo clearance and tighten oversight, with importers accounting for over 60 percent of total imports already registered.\n\nThe Paperless Customs Declaration System takes a key stage of border clearance online, advancing a wider effort to ease trade procedures and connect government agencies through a National Single Window.\n\nSome 1,144 importers have registered for the system, according to the official announcement, giving the rollout substantial coverage at its launch on October 1.\n\nUnder the new requirements, paperless declarations at Customs\u2019 Long Room are mandatory, with importers and declarants required to submit declarations and supporting documents bearing valid digital signatures. Manual submissions will no longer be accepted under this process, Customs said.\n\nThe immediate change removes the need for traders and customs brokers to visit declaration counters to hand over bundles of paperwork. Declarations, invoices, packing lists, bills of lading, permits and other supporting documents are submitted electronically through ASYCUDA, where officers review them and raise any queries.\n\nAuthorities expect the shift to make clearance faster and more transparent. Digital signatures also allow changes made to declarations after signing to be identified, providing a safeguard against document manipulation and helping reduce opportunities for fraud and corruption."
  },
