    "md5": "fa6ec2e5257617172be26d4466ea7f4f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1593,
    "chunk_text": "Authorities expect the shift to make clearance faster and more transparent. Digital signatures also allow changes made to declarations after signing to be identified, providing a safeguard against document manipulation and helping reduce opportunities for fraud and corruption.\n\nThe launch follows sustained pressure to resolve weaknesses in Customs\u2019 digital infrastructure.\n\nEarlier in July, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake called for urgent procedural reforms, legislative updates and faster digital integration after a review of persistent inefficiencies and technological bottlenecks.\n\nThat review also examined the National Single Window, with concerns over slow adoption and gaps in the ability of agencies\u2019 systems to work together.\n\nThe latest rollout follows a paperless declaration pilot introduced in August 2025 for selected Authorised Economic Operators and a declarant, allowing digitally signed documents to be submitted through ASYCUDA World without physical submission at the Declaration Directorate.\n\nAn earlier step involved obtaining cargo information through ASYHUB, which connects shipping lines, freight forwarders, ship operators, port authorities and Customs. Registration of cargo documents through that platform became mandatory on May 7, 2025, enabling Customs to receive container information before, or close to, arrival at the port, according to the launch announcement.\n\nThe declaration system was implemented jointly by Sri Lanka Customs, Lanka Sign and the Revenue Administration, Reforms and Innovation Bureau attached to the Presidential Secretariat."
