    "md5": "2d86527419a09e75526042b484f1a000",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "About 400 French schools closed as some student protests turn violent\n\nReuters\u00a0- About 400 schools in France are expected to remain \u200bclosed on Friday after student protests over education-related grievances turned violent in some \u200cplaces.\n\nThe protests against teacher shortages and dilapidated school buildings erupted in the Paris region last month but have turned increasingly violent as they spread nationwide, with dozens injured and police making \u200bhundreds of arrests.\n\n\"There is an active and violent minority (participating in the protests) ... \u200bWe're not entirely sure that they are even high-school students themselves\", Justice \u2060Minister Gerald Darmanin told RTL radio on Friday, adding that 2,000 people had \u200bbeen arrested since the protests began, with about two-thirds placed in police custody.\n\n\"We have \u200bgone from high-school student demonstrations to a public-order disturbance. When people target buses and set schools on fire, it is no longer simply a protest issue\", he also said.\n\nIn an interview with \u200bbroadcaster France 2, French Education Minister Edouard Geffray said on Thursday evening that \"a \u200blittle over 400 schools and other educational establishments\" would remain closed on Friday.\n\nAccording to a source \u200cclose \u2060to Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu, intelligence agencies told a crisis meeting on Thursday that the protest movement had been initiated by politicians from the hard left, including members of La France Insoumise (LFI).\n\nLFI lawmaker Louis Boyard denied the claim in comments to \u200bFrench broadcasters.\n\nStudent protesters started \u200bfires in at \u2060least two high schools on Thursday, while teachers were inadvertently splashed with petrol at a third in the southern city of \u200bMarseille, officials said. At least one car was torched in \u200bStrasbourg in \u2060the east and riot police clashed with youths in Toulouse in the southwest."
